-- Passage 2: Passive Acoustic Monitoring in Urban Biodiversity Management
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'afcde447-ffa2-5c1f-a6f3-02ea3ac887c1', 'c9d29b91-75d1-58ad-bb23-31a0a3db1863', 2, 'Passive Acoustic Monitoring in Urban Biodiversity Management',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The rapid expansion of metropolitan landscapes presents one of the most pressing ecological challenges of the modern era, resulting in widespread habitat fragmentation and significant biodiversity loss. Urban ecologists and city planners have traditionally relied on manual biological surveys—such as point-count visual assessments for birds and hand-net trapping for bats—to gauge the health of urban wildlife communities. However, these conventional survey techniques are heavily constrained by logistical bottlenecks. They demand substantial human labour, depend entirely on the subjective identification skills of individual observers, and are typically limited to brief daytime observation windows, leaving nocturnal and elusive taxa severely underrepresented.

B. To overcome these observational deficits, environmental managers are increasingly deploying Passive Acoustic Monitoring (PAM). This non-invasive surveying approach utilizes unattended, automated sound-recording units stationed permanently or semi-permanently throughout municipal parks, green corridors, and urban nature reserves. These acoustic sensors operate autonomously around the clock, continuously or periodically capturing the surrounding soundscape—including biophony (biological sounds such as bird song, anuran calls, and bat echolocation pulses), anthrophony (human-generated noises like vehicular traffic and industrial machinery), and geophony (natural physical sounds such as wind and rainfall).

C. The sheer volume of continuous audio data captured by PAM networks creates an analytical challenge that manual human listening cannot resolve. An array of twenty audio sensors deployed across an urban park network over a single season can generate thousands of hours of audio recordings. To interpret this vast acoustic deluge, conservation researchers employ computational acoustic indices alongside machine-learning classification algorithms. Acoustic indices mathematically measure the distribution, entropy, and complexity of sound frequencies across time, providing a proxy measurement of total community biodiversity without requiring the identification of individual species. Simultaneously, deep learning models, particularly Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) trained on visual spectrogram representations of animal calls, automatically identify and enumerate distinct species with remarkable accuracy.

D. Despite its distinct advantages, the implementation of passive acoustic surveillance in densely populated environments faces substantial technical obstacles. Foremost among these is the pervasive acoustic interference generated by anthropogenic noise. Low-frequency traffic rumblings, construction machinery, and aircraft overflights frequently mask or distort the acoustic signatures of vocalizing wildlife. This phenomenon, known as acoustic masking, can cause automated algorithms to generate false-negative results by missing genuine animal calls buried within background noise. Furthermore, physical urban structures—such as concrete high- rises and glass facades—reflect and scatter sound waves, creating complex echoes that can degrade recording quality and skew estimates of species abundance.

E. In response to these acoustic distortions, hardware engineers and bioinformaticians are developing increasingly sophisticated mitigation strategies. Modern acoustic sensors now incorporate dynamic noise-filtering algorithms that attenuate predictable urban background noise before the audio file is written to memory. Advanced post-processing software utilizes adaptive background subtraction and spectral filtering to isolate wildlife vocalizations from sporadic urban disturbances, such as sirens or car horns. Furthermore, researchers are calibrating detection radiuses by conducting controlled sound-propagation tests within diverse urban micro- environments, ensuring that variations in vegetation density and building geometry are mathematically accounted for during population modeling.

F. The integration of PAM-derived ecological intelligence into municipal urban planning is already yielding tangible conservation outcomes. By tracking subtle shifts in species presence and daily calling patterns, municipal authorities can evaluate the genuine ecological efficacy of urban greening initiatives, such as the creation of green roofs, pocket parks, and wildlife corridors. For example, continuous acoustic monitoring along highway margins can reveal whether newly installed vegetated noise barriers successfully restore natural avian communication. Furthermore, acoustic monitoring of bat activity enables city managers to optimize municipal lighting schedules, dampening light pollution in specific urban zones during peak foraging hours to protect nocturnal insectivorous species.

G. Looking to the future, the integration of PAM networks with Internet of Things (IoT) architecture promises to transform urban biodiversity management into a real-time, responsive enterprise. Edge-computing audio nodes, capable of processing acoustic data directly on-chip without uploading massive raw audio files to external servers, will allow instantaneous alerts regarding the arrival of invasive species or the unexpected absence of vulnerable indicator organisms. As acoustic monitoring infrastructure matures and costs decline, these automated auditory sentinels will become standard components of smart-city infrastructure, systematically embedding ecological accountability into the fabric of 21st-century urban governance.'
);

