-- Passage 1: The Vitrified Hillforts of Scotland
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '7796b1cb-3a1d-51fb-a2db-65c469275845', '5e700130-cfd7-50a0-b89f-a020acadc91a', 1, 'The Vitrified Hillforts of Scotland',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the rugged landscapes of northern and western Scotland stand the enigmatic remnants of more than a hundred Iron Age enclosures known to archaeologists as vitrified hillforts. Dating predominantly from the first millennium BCE to the early centuries of the first millennium CE, these defensive structures were typically erected atop prominent hills and coastal promontories. What distinguishes these sites from standard prehistoric drystone fortifications is the dramatic physical alteration of their masonry. Portions of their thick stone ramparts appear fused, melted, and recooled into jagged, glassy masses of black scoria and porous slag, resembling the aftermath of volcanic activity or modern industrial smelting.

B. The structural design of these forts originally followed a construction technique known as murus gallicus (Gallic wall) or timber-laced masonry. Ancient builders assembled dual parallel outer walls using large blocks of local metamorphic or igneous stone, such as basalt, granite, or gneiss. The internal space between these stone facades was packed with a dense core of smaller rubble and interlaced with a robust framework of transversal and longitudinal timber beams. This hybrid configuration granted initial structural elasticity and resilience against battering rams or earthworks. However, it also introduced a latent vulnerability: huge quantities of combustible organic material embedded permanently within a mineral matrix.

C. The physical phenomenon of vitrification requires sustained, extraordinary thermal exposure. For typical siliceous rocks to soften, melt, and fuse into a coherent amorphous mass, temperatures within the wall core must reach between 1,000°C and 1,200°C. In open-air bonfires, such extreme temperatures are virtually impossible to attain because heat dissipates rapidly into the surrounding atmosphere. In a timber-laced wall, however, the combustion of the internal wooden timbers acts like a self-feeding blast furnace. As the wood slowly burns in an environment with restricted oxygen airflow, the timber channels create a natural draught system, sucking in air and driving temperatures up to the melting point of the adjacent stone blocks.

D. The first systematic scientific account of Scottish vitrified forts was published in 1777 by the English civil engineer and geologist John Williams. After conducting close inspections of sites such as Craig Phadrig near Inverness and Knockfarrel near Dingwall, Williams posited that the fusion was the deliberate outcome of an ingenious prehistoric engineering method. He argued that ancient builders intentionally set fire to the wooden framework or added chemical fluxes to weld loose rubble into a solid, monolithic barrier, thereby enhancing the rampart''s structural defense against hostile incursions and severe weather.

E. Throughout the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, Williams’s "deliberate construction" hypothesis was subjected to vigorous experimental testing and engineering critiques. Modern structural analysis revealed that vitrification actually undermines rather than reinforces stone ramparts. Heating crystalline rocks to their melting points causes thermal shock, shattering the crystalline lattice and creating widespread fractures. Upon cooling, the resulting glassy slag is far more brittle and prone to catastrophic collapse under mechanical stress than unheated drystone masonry. Furthermore, vitrification is rarely uniform across an entire fort perimeter; it typically appears in irregular, localized patches where timber density and wind conditions favored intense localized combustion.

F. Consequently, prevailing archaeological consensus has shifted toward alternative explanations. The most widely supported model attributes vitrification to hostile destruction by rival clans or invading forces. Under this scenario, attacking armies deliberately set fire to the exposed timber superstructures of captured forts, resulting in the uncontrolled burning of the internal framework and the incidental melting of the surrounding stonework. A secondary hypothesis suggests that vitrification may have been the result of post-abandonment ritual desecration, where conquered strongholds were systematically torched to symbolize the political extinction of a defeated lineage and render the site spiritually uninhabitable.

G. Recent archaeological investigations utilizing thermoluminescence dating and high- resolution geophysical surveys continue to refine our understanding of these ancient monuments. Rather than representing a uniform engineering tradition or a single wave of coordinated destruction, vitrified hillforts reflect a complex, prolonged history of construction, episodic burning, and occasional rebuilding across centuries. While questions regarding the precise motives behind every individual burning event remain open, these stone citadels stand as enduring testaments to the complex interplay between human architectural ambition and the transformative power of fire.'
);

