-- Passage 3: Epistemic Injustice and Deliberative Democratic Institutions
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '79ce8243-eb8c-5c6f-80fa-e9b93d57490e', '5e700130-cfd7-50a0-b89f-a020acadc91a', 3, 'Epistemic Injustice and Deliberative Democratic Institutions',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. Modern deliberative democratic theory rests upon the premise that political decisions attain legitimate moral authority when they emerge from inclusive, reasoned dialogue among free and equal citizens. Proponents of institutional deliberative forums—such as citizens’ assemblies, participatory budgeting panels, and consensus conferences—argue that structured public deliberation elevates policy-making above the raw aggregation of self-interested votes. By assembling demographically representative cohorts of non-specialist citizens to interrogate expert witnesses, evaluate complex evidence, and debate policy trade-offs, these institutions aim to produce outcomes that reflect informed collective judgment. However, political philosophers and social epistemologists have increasingly identified a profound structural flaw within this deliberative ideal: the widespread presence of epistemic injustice.

B. The concept of epistemic injustice, systematically formulated by philosopher Miranda Fricker, refers to the distinct forms of harm inflicted upon individuals specifically in their capacity as knowers, conveyers of knowledge, and interpreters of social experience. Fricker demarcates this phenomenon into two primary dimensions: testimonial injustice and hermeneutical injustice. Testimonial injustice occurs when prejudice causes a hearer to assign a deflated level of credibility to a speaker’s word. Hermeneutical injustice occurs at an earlier, systemic stage, when a lack of collective interpretive resources puts someone at an unfair disadvantage when trying to make sense of their social experiences, typically because marginalized groups have been historically excluded from shaping dominant cultural and political vocabularies.

C. Within deliberative democratic arenas, testimonial injustice manifests in subtle yet pernicious ways. Even when deliberative bodies achieve perfect descriptive demographic parity through stratified random sampling (sortition), formal inclusion does not guarantee communicative equality. Empirical analyses of mini-public deliberations reveal that participants possessing high socioeconomic status, formal academic credentials, or dominant social identities are systematically granted higher credibility by their peers. Conversely, testimony delivered by marginalized individuals—particularly when articulated through non-academic vernacular, emotional appeals, or personal narrative—is frequently dismissed as anecdotal, irrational, or irrelevant. This credibility deficit distorts the deliberative process, ensuring that the perspectives of privileged participants disproportionately steer final policy recommendations.

D. Hermeneutical injustice presents an even more intractable challenge to deliberative integrity. In standard deliberative setups, the framing of policy problems, the categorization of admissible evidence, and the agenda of discussion are predominantly established by policy elites, institutional organizers, and accredited technical experts. When marginalized participants attempt to articulate grievances arising from unique systemic oppressions, they often find that the official deliberative vocabulary lacks the conceptual tools required to render their lived experiences intelligible to mainstream attendees. Consequently, their contributions are either misconstrued or filtered out of the analytical framework, leaving underlying structural inequities unaddressed.

E. Defending the orthodox deliberative paradigm, some political theorists contend that epistemic distortions can be adequately mitigated through procedural refinements. They argue that skilled, impartial facilitators can regulate speaking times, enforce rational discourse norms, and translate subjective narratives into objective policy propositions. Furthermore, they maintain that the formal requirement of providing reasons accessible to all citizens—the principle of public reason—serves as an egalitarian filter that weeds out biased assertions and idiosyncratic prejudices, preserving the epistemological purity of the deliberative outcome.

F. This procedural defense, however, misunderstands the deeply embedded nature of epistemic power imbalances. Impartial facilitation cannot neutrally level the playing field when the very standards of "rationality," "coherence," and "civility" enforced by moderators are themselves culturally specific constructs that privilege dominant communicative styles. Requiring participants to strip emotional resonance and personal storytelling from their contributions does not eliminate bias; rather, it disenfranchises those whose claims to justice are intrinsically rooted in visceral, experiential harm. When public reason demands that arguments conform strictly to technocratic discourse, it effectively silences those who lack formal training in bureaucratic rhetoric.

G. To achieve genuine epistemic equality, deliberative democratic institutions must undergo a fundamental conceptual and structural overhaul. Deliberative bodies must expand their epistemological boundaries to recognize multiple modes of communication—including testimonio, storytelling, affective testimony, and visual rhetoric—as legitimate forms of public reason. Furthermore, institutional organizers must implement proactive hermeneutical scaffolding, collaborating with marginalized communities prior to formal deliberations to develop shared conceptual vocabularies that accurately reflect diverse social realities. Only by dismantling structural credibility deficits and diversifying interpretive frameworks can deliberative democracy fulfill its promise of fair and just governance.'
);

