-- Passage 1: The Discovery and Domestication of the Cassava Root
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '5cb985d0-eb23-5ed4-934c-de40589a13b0', '02e7efd6-50a2-599c-97df-3713c90a6f6d', 1, 'The Discovery and Domestication of the Cassava Root',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Cassava (Manihot esculenta), a woody shrub belonging to the Euphorbiaceae (spurge) family, represents one of the most remarkable agricultural triumphs of prehistoric humankind. Flourishing in drought-prone, nutrient-poor acidic soils where traditional cereal crops such as maize and wheat routinely perish, this hardy tropical cultigen currently serves as the primary dietary source of carbohydrates for over eight hundred million people across Latin America, sub- Saharan Africa, and Southeast Asia. Yet, beneath its starchy, energy-dense tuberous roots lies a lethal evolutionary defense mechanism: high concentrations of cyanogenic glucosides.

B. The botanical ancestor of domesticated cassava is Manihot esculenta subspecies flabellifolia, a wild shrub naturally distributed along the southern periphery of the Amazon Basin in South America. Botanical and molecular studies suggest that human populations began domesticating this wild progenitor at least 8,000 to 10,000 years ago. Early lowland agriculturalists recognized that the plant’s swollen roots provided an extraordinarily reliable subterranean cache of calories. Furthermore, because cassava can be propagated easily through stem cuttings rather than delicate seeds, and its mature tubers can remain preserved safely underground for up to two years without decaying, it offered indigenous communities an indispensable buffer against seasonal famines.

C. Despite these agronomic strengths, consuming raw cassava can cause acute poisoning or chronic paralytic disorders such as konzo. The plant synthesizes two primary cyanogenic compounds—linamarin and lotaustralin—within its cellular vacuoles. When the plant tissue is crushed or masticated, these compounds come into direct contact with the endogenous enzyme linamarase located in the cell walls. This enzymatic interaction rapidly hydrolyzes the glucosides into acetone cyanohydrin, which subsequently degrades into highly volatile and toxic hydrogen cyanide (HCN). Based on cyanide concentrations, cultivars are broadly categorized into "sweet" varieties, which contain less than 50 milligrams of cyanide per kilogram of fresh root and can be rendered edible by simple boiling, and "bitter" varieties, which often exceed several hundred milligrams per kilogram and require sophisticated multi-step detoxification.

D. Paradoxically, indigenous Amazonian societies preferentially cultivated bitter varieties over sweet ones. Bitter cassava yields higher quantities of starch and exhibits pronounced natural resistance against herbivores, burrowing rodents, and insect pests. To exploit this abundant food source safely, native cultures developed a brilliant suite of bio-mechanical detoxification technologies. The foundational tool of this ancient chemical engineering was the tipití, a long, flexible, diagonally woven tubular basket woven from the fibers of the jacitara palm (Desmoncus species) or wild reeds.

E. The traditional processing sequence begins by peeling the fibrous outer bark of the tubers and grating the wet pulp using wooden boards embedded with sharp quartz fragments or fish teeth. The wet, cyanogenic mash is then packed tightly into the tipití. The basket is suspended vertically from a sturdy roof beam or wooden tripod, while a heavy stone weight or an operator sitting on a lever attached to the bottom loop applies immense tensile force. As the flexible tube stretches downward, its internal diameter constricts dramatically, exerting tremendous lateral pressure that squeezes out the toxic liquid runoff (known in Brazil as tucupi).

F. The mechanical extraction of the cyanogenic juice does not remove all toxins; residual cyanide is eliminated through subsequent thermal processing. The squeezed, compacted cylindrical cake of cassava meal is broken up, sieved into fine grains, and continuously roasted on large, shallow earthenware griddles. The applied heat simultaneously volatilizes the remaining hydrogen cyanide and gelatinizes the starch, yielding a dry, shelf-stable coarse flour known across the Amazon as farinha. Meanwhile, the poisonous liquid runoff is not wasted: indigenous cooks boil it vigorously for several hours to break down the volatile toxins, creating a safe, savory seasoning base rich in umami.

G. Following European contact with the Americas in the late fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, Portuguese maritime traders recognized the utility of cassava farinha as an ideal, non-perishable shipboard ration. They subsequently transported the plant across the Atlantic Ocean, introducing it to coastal West Africa and the Congo River Basin in the late 1500s, and later to maritime Southeast Asia. In these adopted environments, cassava adapted effortlessly to local soils and climates, transforming into an indispensable famine-reserve crop that underpinned demographic survival and economic development across the tropical world.'
);

