-- Passage 1: The Evolution of Marine Navigation: From Astrolabes to the Marine Chronometer
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '27b0eaea-223f-525e-9770-6357da682dbd', '9e9c600b-0e13-5c69-b72e-30358c7f9915', 1, 'The Evolution of Marine Navigation: From Astrolabes to the Marine Chronometer',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For centuries of oceanic seafaring, the greatest scientific and commercial challenge confronting European navigators was the accurate determination of a ship’s position at sea. While calculating latitude—a vessel’s north-south coordinate relative to the equator—was relatively straightforward using ancient celestial instruments like the astrolabe, quadrant, or cross-staff to measure the altitude of the midday Sun or the North Star (Polaris), determining longitude—a ship’s east-west position—proved extraordinarily elusive. Without an accurate method to establish longitude, entire fleets regularly lost their bearings on open oceans, resulting in disastrous shipwrecks, extended voyage durations, rampant scurvy, and staggering losses of merchant cargo and naval personnel.

B. The fundamental geometry of longitude is inherently linked to time. Because the Earth completes one full rotation of 360 degrees on its axis every 24 hours, each single hour corresponds precisely to 15 degrees of longitude (or one degree every four minutes). Consequently, if a mariner could simultaneously know both the local solar time at their ship''s current position (easily measured by observing the moment the Sun reaches its highest point at solar noon) and the precise reference time at a known prime meridian (such as Greenwich, London), calculating the vessel’s longitude required simple arithmetic. The difference between the two times in hours multiplied by fifteen yields the exact angular distance in degrees east or west of the reference line.

C. In theory, the solution was remarkably simple: carry an accurate mechanical clock aboard the ship set to reference time. In practice, eighteenth-century horological technology was entirely incapable of functioning at sea. Conventional pendulum clocks relied on the steady gravitational pull on a swinging pendulum, which was rendered useless by the violent rolling, pitching, and yawing of ocean vessels. Furthermore, maritime conditions subjected delicate clock mechanisms to severe thermal variations, high humidity, corrosive sea salt, and shifting barometric pressures. Temperature fluctuations caused metal balance springs and clock components to expand or contract, drastically altering their oscillation rates and causing clocks to lose or gain several minutes per day—an error margin that translated into navigational inaccuracies of dozens of miles.

D. The urgency of the problem escalated dramatically following the Scilly Naval Disaster of October 1707. A British fleet of twenty-one warships under the command of Admiral Sir Cloudesley Shovell struck coastal rocks off the Isles of Scilly due to severe navigational error, resulting in the loss of four naval vessels and nearly two thousand sailors. In direct response to this catastrophe, the British Parliament passed the landmark Longitude Act of 1714. The legislation established the Board of Longitude and offered an astronomical financial reward: £20,000 (equivalent to millions of pounds today) to anyone who could invent a reliable method to determine longitude at sea to within half a degree (30 nautical miles) after a six-week voyage to the West Indies.

E. Prominent astronomers, including Sir Isaac Newton and the Astronomer Royal Nevil Maskelyne, believed that mechanical clocks could never achieve the necessary precision under harsh oceanic conditions. Instead, they championed the astronomical "lunar distance method." This technique involved using a marine octant or sextant to measure the angular distance between the Moon and prominent celestial stars, then comparing the observed angle with complex mathematical tables published in the Nautical Almanac. While theoretically sound, the lunar method was severely hampered by practical limitations: it required extensive mathematical calculations taking up to four hours to complete, and it was entirely useless whenever overcast skies or daytime conditions obscured the Moon and stars.

F. Defying the prevailing astronomical consensus, a self-educated Yorkshire carpenter and clockmaker named John Harrison dedicated his life to developing a mechanical timekeeper capable of withstanding the marine environment. Harrison pioneered revolutionary frictionless mechanisms, including the "grasshopper" escapement, and invented the bimetallic gridiron pendulum, which used alternating rods of brass and steel to automatically cancel out thermal expansion. Between 1735 and 1759, Harrison constructed a succession of marine timekeepers: the massive, spring-counterbalanced H1, followed by the refined H2 and H3.

G. Harrison’s ultimate breakthrough arrived with his fourth creation, the H4, completed in 1759. Departing radically from the large, heavy brass housings of his earlier designs, the H4 was a compact, silver-cased pocket watch approximately 13 centimeters (5 inches) in diameter. It incorporated high-speed balance wheels, diamond pallets to minimize friction, and an ingenious bimetallic temperature-compensation curb. Tested on a transatlantic voyage to Jamaica aboard HMS Deptford in 1761–1762, the H4 performed with staggering accuracy, losing just 5.1 seconds over an 81-day voyage—far exceeding the accuracy threshold mandated by the Longitude Act. Despite bureaucratic resistance from the Board of Longitude, Harrison’s mechanical masterpiece revolutionized maritime navigation, transforming global trade and securing safe passage across the oceans.'
);

