-- Passage 2: Microplastics Remediation in Constructed Wetland Ecosystems
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '407571c2-4239-599b-b8de-9bacdb334fe7', '9e9c600b-0e13-5c69-b72e-30358c7f9915', 2, 'Microplastics Remediation in Constructed Wetland Ecosystems',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The ubiquitous contamination of aquatic environments by microplastics—synthetic polymer particles measuring less than five millimeters in diameter—represents a critical global environmental challenge. Originating from the degradation of macroplastic debris, synthetic textile laundry effluents, cosmetic microbeads, and tire wear abrasion, microplastics enter municipal stormwater networks and wastewater streams in staggering volumes. Conventional wastewater treatment plants (WWTPs), originally engineered to remove biological oxygen demand and dissolved nutrients, are not fully equipped to capture microscopic synthetic particulates. Consequently, millions of plastic microfibers and pellets bypass primary and secondary treatment stages, discharging directly into fragile freshwater and estuarine environments.

B. To intercept this synthetic contaminant flux, environmental engineers are increasingly deploying constructed wetlands (CWs) as sustainable, low-energy bio-filtration buffers. Constructed wetlands are engineered natural systems that replicate the hydrological, biological, and physicochemical processes of natural swamps and marshes. Typically configured as surface- flow or subsurface-flow gravel beds planted with emergent vegetation, CWs have demonstrated remarkable removal efficiencies for suspended solids and heavy metals. Recent empirical studies indicate that these artificial ecosystems can also capture between 75 and 98 percent of incoming microplastic particles, functioning as vital tertiary filtration barriers before treated water reaches natural waterways.

C. The capture of microplastics within constructed wetlands involves a complex combination of physical, biological, and chemical mechanisms. The primary physical removal pathway is sedimentation. As water flows through the dense network of plant stems and porous substrate media (such as gravel, sand, or biochar), hydraulic velocity drops drastically. This loss of kinetic energy allows heavier, high-density plastic polymers—such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and polyvinyl chloride (PVC)—to settle out of the water column and deposit into the benthic sediment layers. Lighter, low-density polymers, such as polypropylene (PP) and polyethylene (PE), are mechanically strained and trapped by the fine root matrices of wetland macrophytes.

D. Beyond mechanical entrapment, biological processes significantly enhance microplastic retention. Within wetland aquatic environments, submerged plant roots and gravel substrates are coated in a complex, sticky biological film known as a biofilm (a matrix of bacteria, algae, and extracellular polymeric substances). As suspended microplastics come into contact with this adhesive matrix, they become bio-fouled. This colonization by microorganisms increases the physical density of the synthetic particles, causing normally buoyant low-density polymers to aggregate, lose buoyancy, and sink into the substrate. Furthermore, emergent wetland plants such as the common reed (Phragmites australis), broadleaf cattail (Typha latifolia), and yellow iris (Iris pseudacorus) physically stabilize deposited sediments, preventing settled plastics from being resuspended during heavy storm surges.

E. Despite their impressive remediation efficacy, constructed wetlands present distinct ecological risks when acting as microplastic sinks. The sustained accumulation of synthetic polymers in benthic sediments exposes wetland fauna to acute physical and toxicological hazards. Benthic invertebrates, detritivores, and larval amphibians readily ingest microplastic fragments, mistaking them for natural organic detritus or food particles. This ingestion can cause internal abrasions, pseudo-satiation (where the digestive tract is filled with non-nutritive plastic, leading to starvation), and blocked intestines. Furthermore, microplastics readily adsorb hazardous hydrophobic pollutants—including polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) and heavy metals—from surrounding waters, potentially transferring concentrated chemical toxins up the trophic food chain to wetland waterfowl and mammalian predators.

F. Managing the long-term sustainability of constructed wetlands requires rigorous maintenance and material disposal protocols. Because synthetic polymers are exceptionally resistant to biological degradation, trapped microplastics remain indefinitely within the wetland sediment matrix unless physically extracted. Over decades of operation, accumulated sediments can reach saturation points, diminishing the wetland''s hydrological conductivity and filtration capacity. Facility managers must periodically dredge benthic sediment layers and harvest senescent plant biomass. However, disposing of this plastic-laden sediment poses an additional challenge, necessitating controlled high-temperature incineration or secure landfill containment to prevent the contaminants from re-entering the wider environment.

G. Ongoing research is exploring innovative methods to enhance the degradative capacity of constructed wetlands. Scientists are isolating and inoculating wetland beds with specialized plastic-degrading bacterial strains, such as Ideonella sakaiensis, alongside ligninolytic fungi capable of producing enzymes that break down recalcitrant carbon-carbon polymer bonds. By combining optimized plant-microbe consortia with advanced carbonaceous substrates like engineered biochar, the next generation of constructed wetlands will evolve beyond simple physical sinks into active, bio-regenerative microplastic degradation systems.'
);

