-- Passage 3: Semantic Drift, Conceptual Metaphor, and Cognitive Framing
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'ae82e383-de84-5a7c-b97e-780b281bc79f', '9e9c600b-0e13-5c69-b72e-30358c7f9915', 3, 'Semantic Drift, Conceptual Metaphor, and Cognitive Framing',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. For much of the twentieth century, formal linguistic philosophy and classical cognitive science operated under an objectivist, truth-conditional model of human language. Under this traditional paradigm, language was conceptualized as an autonomous, abstract formal system: words served as arbitrary linguistic symbols that corresponded directly to objective, mind- independent realities in the external world. Meaning was deemed strictly propositional, governed by formal logic and truth values. Metaphor, within this orthodox perspective, was relegated to the periphery of linguistics—viewed as a decorative rhetorical flourish, an ornamental stylistic device utilized by poets and orators, but entirely secondary to literal, truth-evaluable speech.

B. This classical objectivist view was fundamentally upended in 1980 by cognitive linguists George Lakoff and Mark Johnson in their seminal work, Metaphors We Live By. Lakoff and Johnson introduced Conceptual Metaphor Theory (CMT), arguing that metaphor is not merely an ornamental linguistic device, but a fundamental cognitive mechanism that structures how human beings perceive, conceptualize, and navigate reality. According to CMT, the human conceptual system is intrinsically metaphorical. Because abstract concepts (such as time, morality, emotional states, and political systems) lack physical, tangible substance, human cognition makes sense of them by systematically mapping properties from concrete, embodied source domains onto abstract target domains.

C. These conceptual mappings are deeply anchored in human sensorimotor experience and physical embodiment. For example, in the conceptual metaphor "ARGUMENT IS WAR," everyday linguistic expressions—such as defending a position, attacking a weak point, shooting down an opponent’s claims, or winning an argument—are not accidental figures of speech. Instead, they reflect a coherent conceptual framework where the abstract activity of intellectual debate is structured around the embodied physical realities of physical combat. Similarly, the ubiquitous conceptualization of "TIME IS MONEY" (spending time, investing minutes, running out of time) reflects a historical and economic mapping where a temporal continuum is understood through the quantifiable metrics of a commercial commodity.

D. The structural power of conceptual metaphors is particularly evident in cognitive framing and sociopolitical discourse. When politicians, policy makers, or media organizations frame a complex social phenomenon through a specific metaphorical lens, they unconsciously activate a corresponding network of inferential associations while systematically suppressing others. In an influential empirical study conducted by cognitive psychologists Paul Thibodeau and Lera Boroditsky, participants were presented with policy reports describing crime in a fictional city. When crime was metaphorically framed as a "beast" preying upon the community, participants overwhelmingly favored punitive, enforcement-heavy responses, such as increasing police forces and implementing harsher prison sentences. Conversely, when the exact same statistical data was framed through the metaphor of crime as a "virus" infecting the city, participants predominantly endorsed systemic, rehabilitative remedies, such as improving educational infrastructure, social services, and public health initiatives.

E. Over extended historical epochs, widespread conceptual metaphors drive a profound linguistic phenomenon known as semantic drift—the gradual transformation and evolution of word meanings over time. As metaphorical associations become deeply embedded and conventionalized within a linguistic community, the original literal roots of words frequently fade from conscious awareness, a process known as "dead metaphor" or lexicalization. For instance, the English verb comprehend derives from the Latin comprehendere, meaning literally "to physically grasp or seize." Over centuries of cognitive mapping based on the conceptual schema "IDEAS ARE PHYSICAL OBJECTS," the physical act of seizing migrated to signify intellectual understanding, completely shedding its tactile physical requirement in modern usage.

F. Despite its explanatory power, Conceptual Metaphor Theory has faced vigorous critiques from discursive psychologists, formal semanticists, and corpus linguists. Critics such as Raymond Gibbs and John Barnden point out that early CMT relied heavily on decontextualized, cherry-picked linguistic examples constructed by theorists themselves, rather than analyzing real-time, spontaneous discourse. Furthermore, linguistic anthropologist Paul Kay argues that CMT often overstates the degree to which metaphorical language reflects active, unconscious cognitive mappings in the speaker''s brain. When an individual uses the phrase wasting time, critics argue, they may simply be retrieving a conventionalized lexical idiom from memory without performing an active, real-time cognitive mapping from monetary commerce to temporal duration.

G. In response to these critiques, contemporary cognitive linguistics has evolved beyond rigid, unidirectional mapping models toward more dynamic, multi-modal frameworks. Modern research incorporates neural imaging, eye-tracking experiments, and vast digitized linguistic corpora to map how metaphorical framing dynamically interacts with working memory, emotional affect, and cultural narratives. Far from being a mere decorative ornament of speech, metaphor is recognized as an indispensable cognitive bridge—a foundational architecture of the human mind that allows us to transform raw sensorimotor embodiment into the heights of abstract thought, cultural expression, and political reality.'
);

