-- Passage 1: The Biology and Social Organization of Weaver Ants
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'beba1144-ba91-507c-9b6e-2816abdd7667', '7990107c-c793-5952-96da-48851bec9cd4', 1, 'The Biology and Social Organization of Weaver Ants',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Weaver ants, primarily belonging to the genus Oecophylla, represent one of the most socially sophisticated and ecologically dominant groups of predatory insects in the tropical canopies of Africa, South Asia, and northern Australia. Comprising two extant species—Oecophylla longinoda, distributed across the equatorial forests of sub-Saharan Africa, and Oecophylla smaragdina, widespread across the Indo-Australian tropics—these arboreal insects are celebrated for their cooperative nest construction. Unlike ground-dwelling ant species that excavate subterranean chambers, weaver ants construct intricate, globular nests high in the canopy by binding living tree leaves together with biological adhesive.

B. The architecture of a weaver ant nest is a triumph of collective bio-mechanics. Construction begins when scout workers identify suitable clusters of flexible, green foliage. To bridge gaps between distant leaves, workers form living physical chains. One ant grips the edge of a leaf with its mandibles, while a second ant grasps the first ant’s petiole or hind legs, and a third mounts the second, repeating the sequence until a flexible chain spanning several insects is formed. Working in coordinated unison, hundreds of ants exert joint tensile force to pull opposing leaves together, bending pliable stems into spherical enclosures.

C. Once the leaves are drawn into contact, other workers arrive carrying late-stage larvae from existing nests. These larvae possess hypertrophied, highly specialized labial glands capable of secreting liquid silk composed of fibroin proteins. Gently manipulated by adult workers using tactile antennal stimulation, the larvae exude sticky silk threads across the leaf junctions. Workers maneuver the larvae back and forth in a weaving motion, effectively stapling and gluing the foliage margins together. While this continuous silk donation exhausts the larvae''s supply— preventing them from spinning individual cocoons for their own pupation—it serves the colonial good, creating a weatherproof, structurally resilient micro-habitat.

D. A single weaver ant colony exhibits a decentralized, polydomous social structure that can span across dozens of adjacent trees. A mature super-colony may encompass over a hundred individual leaf nests and contain more than five hundred thousand worker ants, all ruled by a single founding queen residing in a centrally located, heavily fortified nest. Sterile female workers are divided into two distinct morphological castes: major workers and minor workers. Major workers, measuring 8 to 10 millimeters in length, manage external duties, including foraging, aggressive territorial defense, leaf-pulling, and bridge construction. Minor workers, roughly half the size of majors, remain almost exclusively inside the nests, tending to the queen, nurturing the brood, and milking mutualistic honeydew-producing scale insects.

E. Territoriality among weaver ants is exceptionally fierce and systematically organized. Workers continuously patrol their arboreal territories and the forest floor beneath their trees, marking boundaries with specialized chemical trail pheromones. When an alien insect or a competing ant colony crosses these invisible borders, major workers mobilize rapidly, releasing alarm pheromones (specifically hexanal and 1-hexanol) that trigger mass recruitment. Rather than relying on venomous stings, weaver ants immobilize prey and adversaries through cooperative physical pinning. Multiple workers seize the limbs of an intruder and pull outward in opposite directions, stretching the victim completely flat while other ants spray formic acid into open wounds or structural joints.

F. The aggressive predatory efficiency of Oecophylla has long been harnessed by human agriculturalists. Historical records demonstrate that citrus growers in southern China used weaver ants as biological control agents as early as 304 CE, as documented in Ji Han’s botanical treatise Nanfang Caomu Zhuang. This practice represents the earliest recorded use of biological pest control in human history. Citrus farmers collected wild weaver ant nests from forest trees, suspended them in orange orchards, and constructed bamboo poles connecting tree canopies to facilitate the ants'' predatory patrols, successfully eliminating destructive caterpillars, beetles, and fruit-boring insects without chemical pesticides.

G. Contemporary agro-ecological research has confirmed the economic and environmental validity of this ancient farming technique. Modern trials across tropical Asia and Australia show that weaver ant colonies protect economically vital tree crops—such as cashew nuts, mangoes, and citrus—just as effectively as synthetic chemical sprays, while costing farmers significantly less. In addition to pest control, weaver ant larvae and pupae serve as a valuable high-protein delicacy in Southeast Asian cuisine, providing farmers with a lucrative secondary harvest. By merging ancient ecological wisdom with modern integrated pest management, weaver ants continue to demonstrate their enduring value to sustainable agriculture.'
);

