-- Passage 2: The "Quiet Quitting" Phenomenon and Modern Psychological Contracts
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'bef28ca1-0b2d-5df3-a7f2-6b0cdc733b7d', '7990107c-c793-5952-96da-48851bec9cd4', 2, 'The "Quiet Quitting" Phenomenon and Modern Psychological Contracts',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the wake of the global disruptions to conventional workplace environments prompted by the COVID-19 pandemic and the widespread transition to remote and hybrid work models, a new term captured organizational discourse: "quiet quitting." Contrary to its literal phrasing, quiet quitting does not refer to the outright resignation of an employee from their corporate position. Rather, it describes an employee''s conscious decision to perform solely the prescribed duties outlined in their official job description, strictly refusing to undertake uncompensated overtime, answer off-hours communications, or volunteer for extra-role organizational tasks. While popularized via social media platforms, organizational psychologists recognize this behavior as a modern manifestation of a fundamental shift in workplace dynamics: the renegotiation or breakdown of the psychological contract.

B. The concept of the psychological contract, originally introduced by organizational theorist Chris Argyris and later expanded by Denise Rousseau, encompasses the unwritten, subjective set of mutual expectations, beliefs, and obligations existing between an employer and an employee. Unlike formal employment contracts, which define explicit terms such as base salary, job title, and standard working hours, psychological contracts govern intangible exchanges. Traditionally, employees implicitly agreed to contribute "organizational citizenship behaviors" (OCBs)—such as mentoring colleagues, staying late during project crises, and showing organizational loyalty— in exchange for implicit organizational rewards, including job security, informal recognition, internal career progression, and perceived managerial care.

C. Organizational psychologists identify the widespread emergence of quiet quitting as a direct behavioral response to perceived psychological contract breach. When employees perceive that their organization has failed to fulfill its implicit promises—manifested in stagnant real wages, precarious employment conditions, lack of promotional pathways, or chronic corporate indifference to mental wellbeing—they experience cognitive dissonance and emotional exhaustion. To re-establish psychological equity and prevent personal burnout, workers systematically calibrate their input to match the bare minimum contractual requirements, formally withdrawing their discretionary citizenship behaviors while retaining their employment status.

D. The structural shift toward distributed remote work environments has significantly accelerated this psychological detachment. In traditional office spaces, physical proximity fostered spontaneous social interactions, informal mentoring, and a shared corporate culture that subtly reinforced emotional commitment to the firm. Remote working arrangements, while offering undeniable flexibility and reducing commuting burdens, have largely stripped employment of its relational, communal dimensions, reducing the worker-employer dynamic to a purely transactional digital exchange. Without the social capital and affective bonds forged through face-to-face interactions, employees increasingly view their jobs through a strictly contractual lens.

E. Corporate responses to quiet quitting have diverged sharply, reflecting competing managerial philosophies. Traditionalist management structures have responded with heightened surveillance, deploying digital tracking software, keystroke monitors, and active-time monitoring algorithms to enforce productivity metrics. Workplace sociologists warn that such coercive surveillance tactics are deeply counterproductive. Far from restoring genuine employee engagement, algorithmic monitoring signals systemic managerial distrust, further eroding relational psychological contracts and entrenching defensive, compliance-only work mentalities among staff.

F. Conversely, progressive organizational leadership recognizes that resolving quiet quitting requires a systemic modernization of workplace psychological contracts. This entails establishing transparent performance metrics, establishing rigid operational boundaries around digital communication during non-work hours, and formally compensating or recognizing extra- role contributions rather than treating them as assumed obligations. Furthermore, forward- thinking enterprises are shifting away from presence-based evaluation metrics toward outcome- based management, granting employees greater autonomy over their schedules while prioritizing workload sustainability and mental health support.

G. Ultimately, the quiet quitting debate illuminates an overdue recalibration of the relationship between labor, identity, and personal life in 21st-century society. Rather than viewing the phenomenon merely as a wave of employee apathy or a decline in professional work ethic, modern enterprises must interpret it as an urgent signal that old, paternalistic models of uncompensated corporate devotion are obsolete. In an increasingly digital and volatile economic landscape, organizations that foster authentic transparency, mutual trust, and reciprocal psychological contracts will secure enduring loyalty and sustainable productivity.'
);

