-- Passage 1: The Restoration and Architecture of Classical Stepwells in Western India
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '3fe1323d-d19d-5871-99f2-bc8157c39efc', '1db694d8-027a-5cde-8e12-5fa8924844f6', 1, 'The Restoration and Architecture of Classical Stepwells in Western India',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the arid and semi-arid landscapes of western India—predominantly across the modern states of Gujarat and Rajasthan—stand hundreds of subterranean architectural monuments known locally as baolis, vavs, or kunds. Constructed primarily between the 7th and 19th centuries CE, these classical stepwells represent an extraordinary convergence of practical hydrological engineering, climate-responsive architectural design, and elaborate stone sculpture. In a geographic zone characterized by stark climatic extremes—where brief, torrential summer monsoon downpours are followed by nine to ten months of relentless heat and drought—these structures were engineered to secure a perennial, reliable water supply for agricultural communities, urban populations, and weary travelers traversing long-distance desert caravan routes.

B. The basic operational principle of a traditional stepwell was straightforward yet brilliant. Rather than attempting to draw water vertically using buckets and rope from a narrow circular borehole, builders excavated deep into the Earth to expose the underlying water table. They then constructed a sequence of tiered stone steps leading down directly to the fluctuating water level. As the subterranean water table rose during the monsoon deluge, villagers gathered water from the uppermost platforms. As the dry season progressed and the reservoir receded, users simply walked down successive flights of stairs to access the water at lower depths.

C. Over several centuries, these utilitarian access shafts evolved into grand, multi-storied architectural complexes. Stepwells were typically designed along a linear subterranean axis, featuring one, three, or four entry pavilions that descended through multiple subterranean storeys. Each subterranean tier was stabilized by intricate stone colonnades, carved pillars, and vaulted cross-beams that acted as structural buttresses, resisting the immense lateral earth pressure exerted by the surrounding soil. At the deepest end of the axis lay the circular or octagonal well cylinder, which tapped directly into the aquifer.

D. Beyond their primary hydrological role, stepwells functioned as sophisticated climate- buffering gathering places. Subterranean earth shelter provided substantial thermal insulation: subterranean chambers remained significantly cooler than the scorching surface environment, with temperatures often dropping by as much as 5 to 6 degrees Celsius in the lower recesses. The presence of large bodies of evaporating water further cooled the subterranean air. Consequently, stepwells served as vital communal refuges, particularly for women, who gathered daily at the wells not only to collect household water, wash laundry, and bathe, but to socialize and perform religious rituals away from the fierce afternoon sun.

E. Stepwells were also deeply embedded in the sacred and philanthropic traditions of ancient and medieval India. Because water was revered as an indispensable giver of life, building a stepwell was regarded as an act of supreme spiritual merit and civic piety. Historical epigraphic inscriptions carved into the stonework reveal that many of the most magnificent stepwells were commissioned by royal queens and wealthy female patrons. A premier example is the 11th- century Rani ki Vav ("Queen''s Stepwell") in Patan, Gujarat, commissioned by Queen Udayamati in memory of her deceased husband, King Bhima I. Featuring over 500 major religious sculptures depicting Hindu deities, celestial nymphs, and mythological scenes, Rani ki Vav stands as a subterranean temple celebrating the sanctified nature of water.

F. The advent of British colonial administration in the 19th and early 20th centuries marked the rapid decline of stepwell culture. Colonial health authorities, viewing the shared open water reservoirs as unsanitary vectors for waterborne diseases such as cholera and guinea worm, decommissioned many stepwells or installed mechanical water pumps and enclosed pipe networks. Deprived of municipal maintenance and rendered obsolete by modern canal systems and diesel-powered tube wells, hundreds of stepwells fell into disrepair. Many dried up entirely as intensive agricultural irrigation lowered regional water tables, leaving these grand structures clogged with domestic silt, debris, and vegetative overgrowth.

G. In recent decades, however, the worsening freshwater crisis in northern India has ignited a powerful resurgence of interest in traditional stepwell engineering. Government conservation agencies, such as the Archaeological Survey of India (ASI), alongside non-governmental organizations and international heritage bodies, have initiated ambitious desilting and structural restoration projects. Restored stepwells are now being integrated into modern rainwater harvesting systems, serving as decentralized percolation basins that capture seasonal monsoon runoff to recharge severely depleted aquifers. By blending ancient indigenous engineering wisdom with contemporary water management, these stone sanctuaries are reclaiming their historical role as life-giving centers in drought-prone landscapes.'
);

