-- Passage 1: The Traditional Paper-Making Arts of Washi in Japan
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '4521d050-0acf-5577-8ffa-a7564052d4e5', 'c5a9a619-f098-57df-a7ec-9c6dce026104', 1, 'The Traditional Paper-Making Arts of Washi in Japan',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For more than thirteen centuries, the traditional craft of producing washi—handmade Japanese paper—has been maintained as one of Japan’s most refined cultural and technical traditions. Recognized in 2014 on UNESCO’s Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, washi is celebrated worldwide for its remarkable physical resilience, translucent optical warmth, and extraordinary longevity. While mass-produced Western wood- pulp papers, developed during the nineteenth-century industrial era, typically become brittle, yellow, and disintegrate within a few decades due to residual acidic compounds, preserved classical washi manuscripts crafted over a millennium ago remain supple, pristine, and mechanically sound.

B. The secret behind washi’s physical durability lies in the unique botanical raw materials selected for its manufacture. Unlike modern industrial papermaking, which relies on pulverized, short-fiber timber cellulose, traditional Japanese papermakers utilize the inner bast fibers of specific deciduous shrubs. The primary and most prestigious plant utilized is kozo (the paper mulberry, Broussonetia papyrifera), whose bast fibers average over ten millimeters in length. When interlocked, these exceptionally long, tough fibers create an interwoven matrix of immense tensile strength. Secondary fibers include mitsumata (Edgeworthia chrysantha), which imparts a delicate, golden, silk-like smoothness favoured for official banknotes and fine calligraphy, and gampi (Diplomorpha sikokiana), a rare, wild mountain shrub yielding an insect- resistant, highly lustrous paper surface.

C. The production of authentic washi is an arduous, multi-stage craft traditionally governed by rigorous seasonal rhythms. Harvesting takes place exclusively during the coldest winter months, when the shrub branches contain the optimal moisture concentration and freezing stream waters inhibit biological bacterial decay. Workers harvest young, straight shoots, cut them into uniform lengths, and steam them in massive wooden cauldrons to soften the outer layers. Once steamed, the dark outer bark is peeled away by hand. Papermakers then carefully scrape off the coarse black and green exterior layers using specialized drawknives, isolating only the pure white inner bast tissue known as shirokawa.

D. The extracted shirokawa bast fibers must subsequently undergo chemical purification and physical beating. To dissolve non-cellulosic impurities—such as lignins, pectins, and waxy starches—the fibers are gently boiled in open cauldrons containing a mild alkaline solution derived from natural wood ash or soda ash. Unlike the harsh sulfuric acid baths utilized in Western paper mills, this alkaline cooking process leaves the natural cellulose polymer chains completely undamaged. Following boiling, the softened fibers are rinsed in running river water and subjected to chiri-tori (speck-picking), an exacting manual task where artisans submerge the fibers in cold water basins and pick out every microscopic bark fragment and discoloured fleck by hand. The cleansed fibers are then placed onto solid hardwood or granite blocks and pounded rhythmically with heavy wooden mallets (nagashi-uchi) to tease apart individual filaments without fracturing their structural length.

E. Sheet formation represents the technical pinnacle of the papermaking art, requiring years of tactile apprenticeship to master. The beaten kozo fibers are suspended in a large vat of pure, cold water alongside a crucial organic additive called neri, a viscous mucilage extracted from the crushed roots of the tororo-aoi plant (a variety of wild hibiscus). The neri serves two vital functions: it acts as a deflocculant, preventing the dense, long fibers from clumping or tangling together in the vat, and it increases the viscosity of the liquid, slowing drainage so the artisan can manipulate the pulp suspension across the screen.

F. To form a sheet, the papermaker dips a suketa—a specialized wooden deckle frame holding a finely woven, removable bamboo screen (su)—into the vat. In a continuous, rhythmic rocking motion known as nagashi-suki, the craftsman scoops the slurry, undulating the frame horizontally and vertically. This fluid motion causes the excess water to wash over the edges while the long fibers cross and intertwine in multiple axes across the bamboo lattice. Once the desired sheet thickness is achieved, the wet sheet is couched onto a wooden platform, layered between cloth separators, pressed under screw-presses to expel excess water, and individually brushed onto flat ginkgo or pine drying boards to cure under the sun.

G. Historically integrated into everyday Japanese architecture (as sliding shoji screens), domestic lighting (andon lanterns), ritual Shinto origami, and classical calligraphy, washi is today experiencing a vibrant global renaissance. Contemporary conservators and museum curators in major international galleries rely almost exclusively on thin kozo paper to restore fragile Western illuminated manuscripts, prints, and oil paintings, capitalizing on the paper’s acid-free neutrality and enduring structural stability. By bridging centuries-old agrarian craft with high-level conservation science, washi continues to flourish as a living art form.'
);

