-- Passage 2: The Psychology of Gamification in Higher Education STEM Pedagogy
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '84e8b232-2d63-57de-be9e-6682d0edd1f4', 'c5a9a619-f098-57df-a7ec-9c6dce026104', 2, 'The Psychology of Gamification in Higher Education STEM Pedagogy',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Higher education institutions globally are grappling with high attrition rates and disengagement in undergraduate Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics (STEM) disciplines. Foundational STEM coursework—characterized by abstract theoretical models, rigorous mathematical formalisms, and heavy cognitive workloads—frequently fosters passive learning habits and low academic self-efficacy among novice students. To invigorate classroom engagement and reverse dropout trends, university educators have increasingly implemented gamification: the integration of game-design mechanics, structural game dynamics, and playful design principles into non-game academic environments.

B. The psychological foundation underpinning educational gamification draws heavily from Self-Determination Theory (SDT), formulated by psychologists Edward Deci and Richard Ryan. SDT posits that optimal human motivation, cognitive development, and psychological wellbeing require the fulfillment of three basic psychological needs: autonomy (the perception of control and agency over one’s actions), competence (the sense of mastery and efficacy in achieving desired goals), and relatedness (the experience of meaningful connection and belonging within a social group). Proponents argue that properly structured gamified learning environments stimulate high student motivation by directly satisfying this psychological triad.

C. In practical STEM pedagogy, gamified systems typically deploy a core architecture of mechanical elements, commonly summarized as the "PBL triad": Points, Badges, and Leaderboards. Points provide immediate, granular feedback on problem-solving attempts, transforming delayed grading cycles into real-time reinforcement. Badges serve as digital credentials signaling specific competence milestones—such as mastering a complex coding algorithm or debugging an electrical circuit simulation—which reinforces academic competence and digital identity. Leaderboards introduce competitive social visibility, ranking students against cohort peers to drive achievement orientation.

D. However, empirical educational research indicates that the motivational outcomes of gamification are far more complex and double-edged than early enthusiasts claimed. While mechanical game elements can generate short-term spikes in student engagement, they rely predominantly on extrinsic motivators—external incentives that reward performance through tokenistic status markers. Psychological studies demonstrate that the heavy over-reliance on extrinsic rewards can trigger the "overjustification effect," a behavioral phenomenon where external incentives undermine and crowd out a student’s pre-existing, spontaneous intrinsic interest in the academic subject itself. Once the artificial rewards are removed, baseline student interest frequently drops below its original level.

E. Furthermore, competitive mechanics such as public leaderboards have been shown to produce severe adverse psychological impacts on diverse student demographics. While top- performing students may experience heightened motivation from prominent ranking positions, low-performing or underrepresented students frequently experience "leaderboard demotivation," heightened evaluation anxiety, and acute threats to their perceived academic competence. In highly competitive STEM cohorts, persistent visibility of low rankings induces learned helplessness, causing struggling students to withdraw completely from collaborative problem- solving exercises rather than risk public academic exposure.

F. To avoid these psychological pitfalls, educational theorists advocate a fundamental shift from mechanical, reward-driven gamification toward "meaningful gamification." Grounded in meaningful learning theories, this advanced pedagogical design de-emphasizes competitive rankings and artificial point economies. Instead, it prioritizes immersive narrative framing, branching exploratory quests, collaborative team-based challenges, and low-stakes "fail-forward" feedback loops. In a fail-forward paradigm, academic errors are framed not as punitive, grade- lowering events, but as natural, low-cost iterations in an experimental learning loop—analogous to losing a life in a video game and immediately restarting to refine strategy.

G. As digital learning management systems become increasingly sophisticated, the future of gamification in STEM education will rely on adaptive, personalized pedagogical architectures. Rather than enforcing a rigid, one-size-fits-all game design onto an entire lecture cohort, intelligent gamified platforms utilize learning analytics to tailor game mechanics to individual student motivational profiles. By balancing intrinsic curiosity with constructive, low-stress scaffolding, thoughtful educational game design can transform demanding STEM curricula into deeply engaging, empowering intellectual journeys.'
);

INSERT INTO test_questions (id, section_id, qnumber, position, type, prompt, options)
