-- Passage 3: Behavioral Economics, Hyperbolic Discounting, and Intertemporal Choice
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '3056d30e-1adf-57be-b9b7-bc0e3f94e428', 'c5a9a619-f098-57df-a7ec-9c6dce026104', 3, 'Behavioral Economics, Hyperbolic Discounting, and Intertemporal Choice',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the late nineteenth and twentieth centuries, neoclassical economics was anchored by the axiomatic assumption of the Homo economicus—a perfectly rational economic agent endowed with unlimited computational capability, complete information, and stable, self- interested preferences. When modeling intertemporal choices (decisions involving trade-offs between costs and benefits occurring at different points in time, such as retirement savings, educational investments, or climate mitigation expenditures), orthodox economic theory relied on the Discounted Utility Model pioneered by Paul Samuelson in 1937. Samuelson’s framework assumed that human decision-makers discount future utility at a constant, invariant rate across time, mathematically formalized as an exponential decay function.

B. The defining mathematical property of exponential discounting is dynamic consistency. Under an exponential discount curve, an individual’s relative preference between two prospective rewards depends solely on the absolute temporal distance between them, remaining entirely invariant to the passage of time. For example, if a rational agent prefers receiving $100 today over $110 tomorrow, dynamic consistency dictates that they must maintain the exact same relative preference when choosing between $100 in 365 days and $110 in 366 days. In standard economic theory, humans evaluate the distant future with the same dispassionate, proportional trade-offs they apply to the immediate present.

C. Over the past four decades, however, the foundational assumptions of exponential discounting have been systematically shattered by behavioral economics and neuroeconomics. Empirical experiments conducted by cognitive psychologists like George Ainslie, Daniel Kahneman, and Richard Thaler have repeatedly proven that real-world human intertemporal choice violates exponential consistency. When presented with the choice between $100 today and $110 tomorrow, the vast majority of human subjects exhibit an overwhelming preference for the immediate $100, prioritizing immediate gratification over a greater delayed return. Yet, when the exact same financial trade-off is shifted twelve months into the future—choosing between $100 in 365 days and $110 in 366 days—subjects overwhelmingly reverse their choice, calmly opting to wait the extra day for the larger $110 reward.

D. To mathematically capture this systematic violation of rationality, behavioral economists formulated hyperbolic discounting (and its simplified two-parameter derivative, quasi-hyperbolic or "beta-delta" discounting). Unlike a flat, steady exponential decay, a hyperbolic discount curve drops precipitously in the immediate present before flattening out across longer time horizons. This creates a strong present bias: human beings place a disproportionately high premium on instant consumption, but evaluate future delays with dispassionate rationality. As a future delay draws closer to the present moment, an individual''s subjective discount rate surges, triggering predictable, irrational preference reversals.

E. Neuroimaging research has identified the biological roots of hyperbolic discounting within competing neural substrates in the human brain. Using functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI), neuroeconomists such as Samuel McClure and David Laibson demonstrated that intertemporal choices activate two distinct neural systems operating in an antagonistic dynamic. Immediate rewards trigger intense activation within the evolutionarily ancient limbic and paralimbic structures (including the ventral striatum and medial prefrontal cortex)—the neural networks heavily driven by dopamine and associated with impulsive emotional processing. Conversely, evaluating delayed rewards predominantly recruits the lateral prefrontal cortex and posterior parietal cortex—the brain regions responsible for executive control, working memory, and deliberative planning. Present bias is the direct cognitive byproduct of the emotional limbic system hijacking decision-making when immediate rewards are within reach.

F. The societal ramifications of hyperbolic discounting and present bias are profound and pervasive. It explains why individuals chronically undersave for retirement despite knowing the long-term mathematical necessity of compound interest, why populations struggle with personal debt and chronic health lifestyle choices, and why democratic political systems routinely fail to invest in proactive climate change mitigation. When costs are experienced in the immediate present (such as paying carbon taxes or saving income) while benefits are deferred to a distant, abstract horizon, the hyperbolic discount function systematically suppresses the subjective value of future wellbeing in favor of short-term comfort.

G. To counteract the systemic failures induced by present bias, behavioral economists propose the deliberate design of "choice architecture" and commitment devices. Championed by Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein in their "Nudge" framework, soft paternalistic interventions help individuals align their immediate actions with their long-term welfare without restricting personal freedom. A celebrated real-world application is the "Save More Tomorrow" (SMarT) retirement savings program. Under SMarT, employees commit in advance to allocate a portion of their future salary raises toward retirement savings. Because the increased financial contribution is scheduled to take effect in the future, the hyperbolic present bias is bypassed, enabling individuals to make rational, forward-looking financial decisions.

H. The transition from exponential utility models to hyperbolic discounting marks a decisive milestone in economic science. By replacing abstract, idealized models of human omniscience with empirically verified psychological and neurological realities, modern economics has acquired the capacity to understand why humans systematically act against their own long-term interests. Developing policy frameworks that accommodate this biological vulnerability is essential to securing human welfare, financial resilience, and ecological stability in an uncertain world.'
);

