-- Passage 1: The Geological Formation and Exploration of Cenotes in the Yucatán Peninsula
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '1f1f965d-3939-5833-bd37-d531cab1fff5', '778746c8-41d7-5a17-9ef1-bb79feb184e4', 1, 'The Geological Formation and Exploration of Cenotes in the Yucatán Peninsula',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The Yucatán Peninsula of southeastern Mexico comprises an expansive, low-lying platform formed almost entirely of carbonate bedrock, primarily limestone, dolomite, and evaporites deposited over millions of years in shallow marine environments. One of the most distinctive geographical features of this vast karst landscape is the near-total absence of surface rivers, lakes, and streams. Because carbonate rocks are naturally porous and soluble, rainwater does not accumulate on the surface; instead, it rapidly infiltrates the ground, draining vertically through fissures and joints into an intricate, continent-spanning labyrinth of submerged subterranean conduits, flooded caverns, and flooded river systems.

B. The primary surface windows into this immense subterranean hydrological network are sinkholes known locally as cenotes, a Spanish phonetic transliteration of the Yucatec Maya word ts’ono’ot, meaning "deep water-bearing cavity." Cenotes are formed through the chemical dissolution of calcium carbonate by mildly acidic water—a process known to geologists as karstification. Rainwater absorbs atmospheric carbon dioxide and decomposing organic matter from the thin tropical topsoil, transforming into a weak carbonic acid ($H_2CO_3$). As this acidic groundwater migrates along fractures in the limestone, it steadily dissolves the calcite minerals, gradually excavating vast hollow chambers beneath the jungle floor.

C. The structural evolution of cenotes was heavily accelerated by the dramatic sea-level fluctuations of the Pleistocene epoch (from approximately 2.6 million to 11,700 years ago). During periods of intense global glaciation, massive quantities of Earth''s water were locked into continental ice sheets, causing regional sea levels to drop by as much as 100 to 120 meters below modern levels. During these lowstand intervals, the subterranean caverns drained completely, becoming dry, air-filled subterranean vaults. Deprived of the hydrostatic buoyancy previously exerted by water against the limestone ceiling, weakened cave roofs suffered massive structural collapse. When the glaciers melted at the onset of the Holocene epoch, rising sea levels flooded the subterranean caves once again, creating deep, water-filled pits exposed directly to the sky.

D. The geographic distribution of cenotes across the northern tip of the Yucatán exhibits an extraordinary spatial pattern known as the "Ring of Cenotes." This semicircular arc of densely clustered sinkholes forms a nearly perfect semicircle roughly 180 kilometers in diameter, centered near the coastal town of Chicxulub Puerto. Geoscientists in the late twentieth century confirmed that this arc traces the buried rim of the massive Chicxulub impact crater, generated approximately 66 million years ago when an asteroid collided with the Earth. Although the original impact crater was eventually buried under hundreds of meters of Tertiary sediment, the intense structural fractures in the underlying impact melt rock created zones of high hydraulic permeability, preferentially guiding groundwater flow and focusing karst collapse along the crater’s rim.

E. Within the subterranean waters of coastal cenotes, a remarkable hydrological phenomenon occurs: the formation of a distinct, sharp boundary between fresh and saltwater layers, known as a halocline. Because freshwater has a lower physical density than seawater, rainwater filtering through the limestone floats on top of the heavier, marine saline water that infiltrates horizontally from the adjacent Caribbean Sea. In the zone where these two water masses meet, a visible optical distortion appears—resembling a shimmering, liquid mirror. The chemical mixing zone within the halocline creates an environment of accelerated carbonate dissolution, carving out extensive horizontal cave networks precisely at the interface between the two water layers.

F. To the ancient Maya civilization, cenotes were not merely critical hydrological infrastructure, but sacred portals to the supernatural realm. In an arid landscape lacking surface rivers, cenotes served as the sole permanent sources of drinking water, dictating the spatial placement of major urban centers like Chichén Itzá and Mayapán. Culturally, the Maya viewed cenotes as physical portals to Xibalba, the mythical underworld inhabited by the rain deity Chaac and death gods. Archaeological dredging and underwater excavations within the Sacred Cenote at Chichén Itzá have recovered thousands of ritual offerings cast into the depths, including jade jewelry, carved obsidian blades, copper bells, gold plaques, and the skeletal remains of sacrificed individuals.

G. In modern times, the exploration of the Yucatán’s flooded cave networks has transformed speleology and environmental monitoring. Cave divers utilizing advanced mixed-gas rebreathers, underwater scooters, and continuous guideline protocols have mapped hundreds of kilometers of flooded passages, establishing the Sac Actun and Ox Bel Ha systems as the longest continuous underwater cave networks on Earth. Beyond exploration, cenotes serve as indispensable hydrogeological sensors, providing scientists with real-time indicators of regional groundwater health and helping environmental managers protect the peninsula''s fragile aquifer from the growing threats of urban contamination and tourist overdevelopment.'
);

