-- Passage 3: The Extended Mind Thesis and Cognitive Artifacts
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '7c3e76ef-bcdf-59df-bc22-11cefb095767', '778746c8-41d7-5a17-9ef1-bb79feb184e4', 3, 'The Extended Mind Thesis and Cognitive Artifacts',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. For centuries, Western philosophical orthodoxy and classical cognitive science operated under an unquestioned assumption regarding the physical locus of human thought: cognition is strictly confined within the biological boundary of the individual organism. Under this internalist paradigm, the mind is viewed as coextensive with the brain (or at most the central nervous system), safely enclosed within the protective shell of the skull. Information processing, reasoning, memory storage, and problem-solving were deemed purely neurocentric phenomena occurring across synapses and neural assemblies. External instruments—such as notebooks, counting beads, maps, and digital calculation devices—were categorized merely as inert tools or passive memory aids that feed sensory data into the cognitive engine, but never form part of the thinking process itself.

B. This classical intracranial consensus was radically challenged in 1998 by philosophers Andy Clark and David Chalmers in their landmark paper, The Extended Mind. Clark and Chalmers formulated the Extended Mind Thesis (EMT), positing an active externalism: the thesis that human cognitive processes are not bounded by the skull and skin, but can literally extend into the surrounding technological and social environment. According to EMT, when external artifacts are coupled to the human biological brain in a sufficiently reliable, transparent, and direct manner, those physical objects do not merely assist cognition from the outside; they become constitutive components of the cognitive system itself.

C. To establish their philosophical argument, Clark and Chalmers introduced the "Parity Principle." The principle states: if, as we confront some task, a part of the world functions as a process which, were it done in the head, we would have no hesitation in recognizing as part of the cognitive process, then that part of the world is part of the cognitive process. To illustrate this principle, they proposed a famous thought experiment comparing two individuals: Inga and Otto. Inga wishes to visit the Museum of Modern Art in New York; she recalls its location on 53rd Street by accessing her biological biological memory stored in her cerebral cortex. Otto, who suffers from mild Alzheimer’s disease, relies on a carefully organized notebook that he carries everywhere. When Otto decides to visit the museum, he looks up the address in his notebook and walks to 53rd Street.

D. Clark and Chalmers argue that there is no principled epistemological distinction between Inga’s biological memory retrieval and Otto’s notebook lookup. In both cases, the information is readily accessible, reliably consulted, and automatically endorsed as true before guiding action. The notebook plays the exact functional role for Otto that biological neural circuits play for Inga. Therefore, denying cognitive status to Otto’s notebook simply because it is made of paper and ink rather than organic neurons is an arbitrary biological prejudice—an error Clark and Chalmers label "bioprejudice."

E. The Extended Mind Thesis has provoked vigorous resistance from traditional philosophers of mind and orthodox cognitive scientists. Prominent critics, such as Kenneth Aizawa and Fred Adams, argue that EMT falls victim to a fundamental philosophical error known as the "coupling-constitution fallacy." Aizawa and Adams contend that merely demonstrating that an external object is causally coupled to a cognitive agent does not prove that the object is constitutive of cognition. For instance, while a biological lung is causally dependent on external atmospheric oxygen to execute respiration, nobody claims that the surrounding atmosphere is part of the human respiratory organ. By confusing a causal connection with an internal constitution, critics argue, EMT stretches the definition of "mind" to an absurd degree, risking a conceptual collapse where everything in the universe becomes part of an individual''s cognition.

F. Furthermore, orthodox internalists argue that external artifacts lack the "mark of the cognitive"—specifically, intrinsic, non-derived intentionality and specialized representational dynamics. Biological brain states, critics assert, possess innate intentionality (they are naturally "about" things in the world) and undergo rapid, sub-symbolic computations. In contrast, the words in Otto’s notebook or the pixels on a digital screen possess only derived intentionality— arbitrary symbols that mean nothing until an internal, conscious human brain interprets them. Without an active biological observer to decipher the marks, the notebook is merely cellulose and carbon, lacking any cognitive agency of its own.

G. In response to these critiques, "second-wave" and "third-wave" extended mind theorists have refined the original parity argument, shifting the focus from functional parity to cognitive integration and complementarity. Rather than demanding that external tools behave identically to inner neural circuits, integration theorists like John Sutton and Richard Menary emphasize that the true power of extended cognition lies in its complementary differences. Biological brains are exceptional at pattern recognition, emotional valence, and sensorimotor coordination, but notoriously unreliable at long-term archival precision, numerical calculations, and error-free indexing. External cognitive artifacts (such as mathematical notation systems, spreadsheets, and networked smartphones) complement these biological limitations by providing stable, high- capacity, non-biological storage.

H. In the contemporary digital era, characterized by ubiquitous cloud computing, algorithmic search engines, and artificial intelligence interfaces, the boundaries of human cognition are shifting more rapidly than ever before. Humans do not merely use digital technology; we systematically offload our working memory, spatial orientation, and analytical tasks to external digital scaffolds. Accepting that the human mind is an intrinsically hybrid entity—an open, porous biological-technological system—forces a fundamental transformation in educational design, legal definitions of personal privacy, and our understanding of human identity in a hyper- connected world.'
);

