-- Passage 1: The Origins and Technology of Medieval Stained Glass
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '41714b34-8ac5-512e-93e0-255f8f2767d2', 'f98595e8-d906-5613-86a0-2b6d87f2033b', 1, 'The Origins and Technology of Medieval Stained Glass',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the high and late Middle Ages, the emergence of Gothic architecture transformed the sacred landscapes of Western Europe. Characterized by soaring pointed arches, ribbed vaulting, and external flying buttresses, Gothic cathedrals replaced the dark, thick-walled enclosures of earlier Romanesque architecture with luminous, towering walls of glass. Far from being merely decorative apertures, the monumental stained glass windows of cathedrals such as Chartres, Sainte-Chapelle, and York Minster were engineered as theological and artistic instruments. Known as "the poor man’s Bible," these vibrant, translucent installations translated complex theological doctrines into accessible visual narratives for predominantly illiterate congregations, while suffusing sacred spaces with an otherworldly, colored illumination that medieval theologians equated with the divine presence.

B. The manufacture of medieval stained glass was an exacting pyrotechnological craft requiring precise control of mineral chemistry and furnace aerodynamics. The raw base material for medieval glass was created by fusing silica (derived from crushed quartz pebbles or clean river sand) with an alkaline flux to lower the melting point of the quartz from over 1,700°C to an achievable 1,100°C–1,200°C. In classical antiquity and early Islamic glassmaking, natron—a naturally occurring mineral soda salt—was the standard flux. However, by the ninth and tenth centuries, the disruption of Mediterranean trade routes forced European artisans north of the Alps to substitute local forest plant ash. Medieval glassmakers burned beechwood, bracken ferns, or oak timber to produce potash (potassium carbonate), yielding a distinctive "potash-lime" glass formulation.

C. To introduce the intense, radiant hues that defined medieval church glazing, master glassmakers added minute quantities of metallic oxides directly to the molten glass bath within ceramic crucibles. Cobalt oxide produced deep, luminous blues; cuprous (copper) oxide yielded ruby reds or rich greens depending on furnace oxidation states; manganese dioxide generated purples or acted as a decolouriser to neutralize iron impurities; and iron oxide imparted yellow, amber, or pale green tones. Ruby red glass presented a distinct technical challenge: because the copper red colorant was so intensely saturated that a standard-thickness sheet appeared opaque and black, artisans developed "flashed glass." This involved fusing a paper-thin layer of ruby- colored glass onto a thicker backing sheet of clear or pale-tinted base glass, allowing light to pass through while displaying a rich red hue.

D. Transforming the molten, colored glass into flat sheets was accomplished through one of two primary glassblowing methods: the "cylinder" (or "muff") method and the "crown" method. In the cylinder technique, an artisan gathered a glob of molten glass on the tip of a hollow iron blowpipe, inflated it, and swung it rhythmically within a floor trench to elongate it into a hollow cylinder. The blower then severed the rounded ends, sliced the cylinder lengthwise with hot shears, and transferred it to an annealing oven, where wooden tools flattened it into a rectangular sheet. In the crown technique, the inflated bubble was transferred to a solid iron pontil rod, pierced, and spun rapidly in front of the furnace heat. Centrifugal force spread the glass outward into a flat, circular disc with a thick, central "bullseye" or crown.

E. Once cooled and annealed, the glass sheets were transported to the glazier’s workshop. The master glazier first drafted a full-scale compositional design, known as a cartoon, drawn in charcoal or chalk directly onto a whitewashed wooden trestle table. Artisans placed individual colored glass pieces over the cartoon and scored the shapes using a heated iron rod, snapping the glass along the thermal fracture line. To achieve precise, curved contours, workers used a notched iron instrument called a grozing iron to nibble away small flakes along the perimeter of each glass piece until it conformed perfectly to the cartoon guide.

F. Intricate facial features, drapery folds, and architectural details were rendered using "vitreous paint" (or grisaille), a dark brown or black paste compounded from powdered iron oxide or copper rust, pulverized glass frit, and an organic binder like vinegar or gum arabic. After the painter applied the pigment using fine badger-hair brushes, the decorated glass segments were returned to a low-temperature kiln. Firing at approximately 600°C–650°C melted the glass frit in the paint, fusing the metallic pigment permanently into the glass surface.

G. The individual glass segments were subsequently assembled using a network of flexible, H- shaped lead strips known as cames. The glazier inserted the glass edges into the parallel lateral channels of the cames and soldered the lead intersections together using an alloy of lead and tin. To render the sprawling structural mosaic watertight and rigid against gale-force coastal winds, the artisan rubbed a semi-liquid putty—a mixture of chalk, linseed oil, and carbon soot—into every joint between the glass and the lead cames. Finally, the completed panels were mounted into the stone window frames and secured with iron tie-bars and saddle-bars, establishing monumental translucent mosaics that have endured for centuries.'
);

