-- Passage 2: Mass Timber Construction and the Decarbonization of Commercial Skylines
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'e9220b63-ddb9-54d1-ae91-e1a724ae3cd1', 'f98595e8-d906-5613-86a0-2b6d87f2033b', 2, 'Mass Timber Construction and the Decarbonization of Commercial Skylines',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The global building and construction sector is currently responsible for nearly 40 percent of energy-related carbon dioxide emissions worldwide. A significant portion of this environmental footprint arises from "embodied carbon"—the greenhouse gas emissions generated throughout the extraction, manufacturing, refining, transport, and assembly of primary structural materials. The modern construction industry has historically relied on two carbon-intensive commodities: structural steel and reinforced Portland cement concrete. The chemical calcination of limestone in cement kilns, combined with the extreme heat required for blast-furnace steel manufacturing, releases gigatons of carbon dioxide annually into the atmosphere, making deep decarbonization of the built environment virtually impossible under conventional engineering methods.

B. To mitigate this structural emissions crisis, architectural engineers, municipal planners, and structural designers are turning to mass timber construction as a renewable, carbon-sequestering alternative. Unlike traditional light-frame timber construction, which is restricted to low-rise residential homes, mass timber refers to an advanced family of engineered wood products designed for high-load-bearing, multi-story structural applications. The cornerstone of modern mass timber engineering is Cross-Laminated Timber (CLT). First developed in Austria and Germany during the 1990s, CLT consists of multiple layers of kiln-dried dimensional lumber boards stacked perpendicularly to adjacent layers at 90-degree angles, glued under immense hydraulic pressure with high-performance structural adhesives.

C. This alternating, orthogonal lamination architecture imparts extraordinary structural properties to CLT panels, granting two-way structural span capabilities and mechanical strength- to-weight ratios that rival reinforced concrete and structural steel. Complementary mass timber products—such as Glued Laminated Timber (Glulam), Laminated Veneer Lumber (LVL), and Nail-Laminated Timber (NLT)—allow engineers to construct massive, load-bearing timber columns, beams, and sheer walls capable of supporting commercial office buildings and residential skyscrapers exceeding twenty stories in height.

D. The primary ecological advantage of mass timber over conventional mineral materials lies in its dual carbon benefit: direct substitution and long-term biological sequestration. Trees naturally capture atmospheric carbon dioxide through photosynthesis, converting carbon into stable biogenic cellulose and lignin that constitute the physical structure of wood. When timber is harvested from certified, sustainably managed forests and manufactured into durable building components, that stored biogenic carbon is permanently locked within the architectural fabric of the city for decades or centuries. Simultaneously, substituting mass timber for concrete slabs and steel girders prevents the massive fossil-fuel emissions that would have been generated in the production of those mineral components.

E. Despite these environmental virtues, the widespread adoption of mass timber in high-density commercial construction faces persistent skepticism regarding structural fire performance. Public perception often assumes that a wooden skyscraper represents an inherent fire hazard. However, fire engineering research demonstrates that large-dimension mass timber behaves predictably and safely under extreme thermal exposure. When exposed to fire, the outer surface of a thick mass timber panel chars at a slow, calibrated rate of approximately 0.65 millimeters per minute. This dense, carbonaceous char layer forms an insulating, oxygen-deprived barrier that protects the inner wood core, allowing the structural member to retain its structural load-bearing capacity for several hours, often outperforming bare structural steel, which loses its tensile yield strength and undergoes sudden catastrophic buckling at temperatures above 500°C.

F. Significant financial, regulatory, and supply-chain hurdles continue to impede mainstream adoption. In many jurisdictions, outdated municipal building codes enforce rigid height limits on combustible construction materials, requiring costly, lengthy administrative variance approvals and fire-testing certifications for tall timber proposals. Furthermore, while prefabricated mass timber panels accelerate on-site assembly timelines—reducing construction noise, dust, and on- site labor costs by up to 30 percent—the lack of localized mass timber manufacturing mills in certain global regions inflates transportation logistics costs and exposes projects to international freight volatility.

G. As international building codes evolve to embrace engineered timber frameworks and institutional real estate investors prioritize environmental, social, and governance (ESG) metrics, mass timber is poised to redefine urban architecture. Transforming metropolitan commercial skylines from carbon emitters into renewable, carbon-storing urban forests represents a viable path toward net-zero urban development. In the sustainable metropolis of the twenty-first century, engineering ingenuity and natural ecology will converge to construct the cities of the future.'
);

