-- Passage 1: Roman Hydraulic Concrete and Maritime Harbours
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'd624c635-2721-5219-8e63-1911f55eaa24', 'f4b544e3-e994-5fd1-a099-8efcc7fd7d64', 1, 'Roman Hydraulic Concrete and Maritime Harbours',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the expansion of the Roman Empire across the Mediterranean basin, the construction of massive maritime infrastructure—including monumental breakwaters, piers, quays, and seawalls—played an indispensable role in securing naval supremacy and expanding mercantile trade networks. What astounds modern materials scientists and civil engineers is that dozens of these coastal structures, such as those at Caesarea Maritima, Portus, and Baiae, remain remarkably intact today, having survived more than two millennia of continuous exposure to violent ocean waves and corrosive saltwater. In contrast, modern marine structures fabricated from standard Portland cement reinforced with steel rebar frequently suffer severe chemical degradation, microcracking, and structural failure within fifty to one hundred years of deployment.

B. The extraordinary longevity of Roman maritime works is rooted in their mastery of an innovative composite material known as opus caementicium, or Roman hydraulic concrete. Unlike modern concrete, which is prepared as a fluid slurry and poured into temporary wooden forms, Roman concrete was installed by hand as a dense, compacted composite. Roman builders constructed submerged wooden caissons or cofferdams across the seabed, into which they manually placed large angular aggregate stones (caementa)—typically volcanic tuff, pumice, or dense limestone fragments measuring up to twenty centimeters across. They then packed the voids between these stones with a stiff hydraulic mortar composed of quicklime (calcium oxide) and specialized reactive volcanic ash.

C. The secret ingredient that imparted hydraulic properties—the ability of mortar to set and harden underwater without contact with atmospheric air—was a specific volcanic deposit mined from the Gulf of Naples near the town of Puteoli (modern Pozzuoli). Termed pulvis puteolanus (pozzolana), this ash was produced by catastrophic pyroclastic eruptions from the Phlegraean Fields volcanic complex. Pozzolana is rich in reactive aluminosilicates—amorphous silica ($SiO_2$) and alumina ($Al_2O_3$). When mixed with slaked lime ($Ca(OH)_2$) and seawater, the pozzolana triggered a vigorous chemical pozzolanic reaction, precipitating dense interlocking networks of calcium-aluminum-silicate-hydrate (C-A-S-H) binder minerals that bound the aggregate stones into a monolithic rock.

D. The historical authority on classical Roman engineering, the architect and military engineer Marcus Vitruvius Pollio, documented this remarkable phenomenon in his first-century BCE treatise De Architectura. Vitruvius observed: "There is also a kind of powder which from natural causes produces astonishing results. It is found in the neighborhood of Baiae and in the country belonging to the towns round about Mount Vesuvius. This substance, when mixed with lime and rubble, not only lends strength to buildings of other kinds, but even when piers of it are constructed in the sea, they set hard under water." Pliny the Elder later echoed this in his Naturalis Historia, writing that Roman maritime concrete became an impregnable barrier that grew stronger with each passing day.

E. For decades, modern geochemists struggled to understand why ancient concrete did not dissolve under the constant chemical attack of magnesium, sulfates, and chloride ions present in seawater. Recent high-resolution investigations utilizing synchrotron X-ray microdiffraction and scanning electron microscopy have finally deciphered the mineralogical mechanism. When seawater slowly percolates through microscopic pores in the Roman concrete matrix, it dissolves remnants of volcanic glass and lime components, making the internal pore solution highly alkaline. This alkaline seawater environment triggers a secondary, post-curing mineral reaction that precipitates a rare, highly durable mineral known as aluminous tobermorite ($Al\text{- tobermorite}$) alongside the fibrous zeolite mineral phillipsite.

F. In nature, tobermorite is an exceptionally rare hydrothermal mineral that typically requires high-temperature, high-pressure geological conditions to crystallize. In Roman marine concrete, however, the continuous circulation of low-temperature seawater over centuries promotes the slow, steady growth of interlocking, blade-like crystals of $Al\text{-tobermorite}$ within the relict lime clasts and interfacial transition zones. Instead of degrading the material, incoming seawater actively strengthens the concrete fabric: the flexible, interlocking crystals reinforce the matrix against shear stress, seal internal microcracks, and arrest fracture propagation, granting the concrete a self-healing, dynamic chemical resilience unmatched by modern synthetic formulations.

G. The rediscovery of the chemical architecture of Roman hydraulic concrete offers profound opportunities for contemporary civil engineering and coastal management. Modern Portland cement production is an energy-intensive industrial process responsible for approximately 8 percent of global carbon dioxide emissions. Furthermore, as rising global sea levels and intensifying storm surges threaten contemporary coastal cities, the need for durable, low-carbon marine infrastructure has become urgent. By synthesizing sustainable pozzolanic cements incorporating natural volcanic ash, fly ash, and geothermal byproducts, modern materials scientists are developing next-generation eco-concretes that combine ancient Roman multi- millennial longevity with modern structural standards.'
);

