-- Passage 3: Structural Realism and Ontological Commitment in Quantum Field Theory
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'dbfd88ea-322d-5a52-b1aa-1f5c8fa90589', 'f4b544e3-e994-5fd1-a099-8efcc7fd7d64', 3, 'Structural Realism and Ontological Commitment in Quantum Field Theory',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of the philosophy of science, the debate over scientific realism has centered on a fundamental question: do our best, mature scientific theories provide a true, literal description of an objective, mind-independent external world? Scientific realists advance the "No-Miracles Argument," famously articulated by philosopher Hilary Putnam, which asserts that realism is the only philosophy that does not make the empirical success of modern science an inexplicable miracle. If our predictive theories about subatomic particles, genetic sequences, and gravitational waves were entirely false, their extraordinary capacity to forecast novel physical phenomena and underpin advanced technologies would be utterly incomprehensible. Therefore, realists argue, the theoretical entities posited by mature sciences must genuinely exist.

B. Conversely, anti-realists and instrumentalists undermine this position through the "Pessimistic Meta-Induction" (PMI) argument, championed by Larry Laudan. A historical survey of scientific progress reveals that the history of science is a vast graveyard of past mature theories that were empirically successful in their day, yet were subsequently shown to be fundamentally false and had their core theoretical entities discarded. The caloric theory of heat, the luminiferous ether of nineteenth-century wave optics, and phlogiston chemistry all enjoyed predictive success, yet science eventually discarded their central postulated entities. If past successful theories turned out to be radically mistaken about their ontological posits, anti-realists argue, we have no rational inductive warrant to believe that current theoretical entities (such as quarks, dark matter, or spacetime curvature) will survive future scientific revolutions.

C. In 1989, philosopher John Worrall proposed a revolutionary middle path designed to break this epistemological deadlock: Structural Realism (SR). Worrall argued that both traditional realism and anti-realism capture vital truths about science, but both commit the error of conflating theoretical entities with theoretical structure. When scientific revolutions occur, the postulated physical objects (such as the mechanical ether) are indeed discarded, but the mathematical equations and relational structures that describe physical phenomena (such as Fresnel’s mathematical equations for light refraction) are preserved and subsumed within the successor theory (such as Maxwell’s electromagnetic equations). Structural realism contends that we should not be realist about the unobservable entities themselves, but rather about the relational mathematical structures that govern the physical universe.

D. In contemporary philosophy of science, structural realism has bifurcated into two primary schools: Epistemic Structural Realism (ESR) and Ontic Structural Realism (OSR). ESR, originally formulated by Worrall, maintains a cautious epistemological stance: there may well be underlying, self-subsisting physical objects or "things-in-themselves" that possess intrinsic natures, but all human science can ever know are the structural relations between these entities. In contrast, Ontic Structural Realism (OSR), championed by philosophers James Ladyman, Don Ross, and Steven French, makes a far more radical metaphysical claim: there are no underlying, self-subsisting individual objects at all. In OSR, reality is relational structure "all the way down." Physical structures do not exist between objects; rather, what we conventionally call "objects" are merely nodes or intersections within a fundamental web of mathematical relations.

E. The most compelling empirical and theoretical motivation for Ontic Structural Realism arises from modern quantum physics, particularly Quantum Field Theory (QFT) and quantum statistics. In classical Newtonian physics, physical objects are conceptualized as distinct, self-identical individuals possessing sharp spatio-temporal trajectories and intrinsic non-relational properties (such as mass and charge). In quantum mechanics, however, identical particles (such as electrons or bosons) violate classical individuality. Quantum statistics (Fermi-Dirac and Bose-Einstein distributions) demonstrate that permutations of identical particles do not result in a new physical state—a phenomenon known as permutation indistinguishability. Permuting two identical electrons does not create a distinct arrangement, proving that quantum particles lack "primitive thisness" (haecceity) or individuality.

F. Furthermore, Quantum Field Theory dissolves the classical concept of particles into relational field excitations. In QFT, the fundamental physical ontology is not composed of tiny, billiard-ball-like particles traversing empty space, but rather continuous, underlying quantum fields. What physicists detect as a "particle" is merely a localized, quantized excitation of a field. In curved spacetimes, the Unruh effect and Hawking radiation demonstrate that the very particle count of a system is observer-dependent: an accelerating observer will detect a thermal bath of particles in what an inertial observer measures as a completely empty vacuum. If the existence and number of particles depends entirely on the state of motion of the observer, particles cannot be regarded as fundamental, self-subsisting physical substances.

G. Despite its mathematical elegance and alignment with modern physics, Ontic Structural Realism faces severe philosophical challenges. Critics, such as Stathis Psillos and Anjan Chakravartty, raise the "relations without relata" objection. They argue that the concept of a "relation" is logically and conceptually dependent on the existence of objects between which the relation holds. Positing pure relational structure without any underlying entities is dismissed by critics as an incoherent metaphysical fiction—analogous to asserting the existence of a marriage without any spouses, or the grin of the Cheshire Cat remaining without the cat.

H. Proponents of OSR respond by rejecting this classical metaphysical intuition as a cognitive bias derived from human macroscopic experience. They argue that our linguistic and cognitive habits, evolved in a macroscopic world of solid medium-sized objects, have misled us into imposing an unnecessary "substance-attribute" ontology onto fundamental reality. By embracing gauge symmetries, group theory, and mathematical invariant relations as the true ontological bedrock of physics, Ontic Structural Realism offers a coherent, modern framework for scientific realism in the quantum age. In the grand view of contemporary physics, the universe is not an assembly of isolated things, but a magnificent, unbroken tapestry of structural relations.'
);

