-- Passage 1: The History and Ecology of Cork Oak Silviculture in the Iberian Peninsula
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '994db904-a22b-51be-9e2c-d64dc6341321', 'b4c20e95-ea48-51cb-acf3-92c39473c8b4', 1, 'The History and Ecology of Cork Oak Silviculture in the Iberian Peninsula',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Stretching across the sun-drenched plains and rolling hills of southwestern Europe, the ancient agroforestry landscapes of the Iberian Peninsula represent one of the most ecologically balanced and economically sustainable agricultural systems in the world. Known in Portugal as the montado and in Spain as the dehesa, these expansive, open woodlands are dominated by the cork oak (Quercus suber), an evergreen oak species native to the western Mediterranean basin. For centuries, these semi-natural habitats have integrated low-intensity forestry, livestock grazing, and cereal cultivation, forming a unique cultural landscape that sustains rural human populations while functioning as a critical biodiversity hotspot. Today, these regions yield more than half of the world''s commercial cork supply, supporting a multi-billion-euro global industry rooted in centuries-old silvicultural traditions.

B. The remarkable biological properties of Quercus suber derive primarily from its exceptional outer bark, which serves as a specialized evolutionary adaptation against the frequent wildfires and prolonged summer droughts characteristic of the Mediterranean climate. The bark is composed of phellem tissue—a thick layer of dead, suberized cellular structures arranged in a dense honeycomb matrix. Each cubic centimeter of cork bark contains upwards of forty million microscopic, fourteen-sided cells called tetrakaidecahedrons. The interior of each cell is filled with a gas mixture almost identical to air, while the cell walls are heavily impregnated with suberin, an impermeable, waxy organic polymer, alongside lignin and ceroids. This cellular geometry makes the bark virtually impermeable to liquids and gases, highly elastic, resistant to microbial decay, poor at conducting thermal energy, and naturally fire-retardant.

C. Unlike almost any other commercial tree species, the cork oak can endure the complete stripping of its outer bark without suffering irreversible physiological damage or mortality. However, the manual extraction of this material is a delicate, physically demanding craft that relies entirely on traditional human labor and sharp hand axes. Harvesting occurs strictly during a narrow summer window, typically spanning from late May to August, when high ambient temperatures cause the tree to generate new vascular cells rapidly, making the outer bark separate easily from the delicate underlying phellogen (the cork cambium or living inner bark). If a harvester accidentally cuts into or fractures the phellogen, the tree will lose its regenerative ability, suffer fungal infection, or die. Consequently, mechanized tools have largely failed to replace the precision of experienced generational cork strippers (descortiçadores).

D. The silvicultural lifecycle of a commercial cork oak is characterized by remarkable patience and generational stewardship. A newly planted sapling must mature for approximately twenty- five years before its trunk reaches the minimum statutory circumference of seventy centimeters required for its maiden harvest. This initial harvest yields "virgin cork" (cortiça virgem), a rough, irregular, and highly fractured material that lacks the density and structural uniformity needed for premium wine stoppers. Instead, virgin cork is ground into granules and used for thermal and acoustic insulation boards, footwear components, or specialized construction materials. The tree must then be left undisturbed for a mandatory legal resting period of at least nine years to regenerate its suberized bark before a second harvest can take place.

E. Even the second extraction, yielding "secunda cork," is generally deemed unsuitable for high-grade natural stoppers due to lingering structural irregularities. It is only at the third harvest—when the tree is approximately forty-three to forty-five years old—that the bark achieves the requisite elasticity, density, and aesthetic uniformity required to manufacture high- end, punched natural wine bottle stoppers, a grade known as amadia cork. Thereafter, the tree continues to be harvested systematically every nine to twelve years throughout its productive lifespan, which frequently extends from two hundred to two hundred and fifty years. Over its multi-century lifetime, a single healthy cork oak may be harvested between fifteen and eighteen times, sequestering immense volumes of carbon while sustaining local agrarian economies.

F. Beyond its timber and forestry value, the montado ecosystem provides an exceptional range of ecological services, serving as a haven for endangered Mediterranean wildlife. The mosaic of open grasslands, scattered canopy trees, and dense understory scrub supports some of Europe’s most threatened vertebrate species, including the critically endangered Iberian lynx (Lynx pardinus), the Spanish imperial eagle (Aquila adalberti), and the Eurasian black vulture (Aegypius monachus). Millions of migratory waterfowl and cranes rely on Iberian cork woodlands as vital winter feeding grounds, foraging on the sweet, high-calorie acorns produced by the oaks. Furthermore, traditional rotational grazing of indigenous livestock breeds—such as the famous black Iberian pig (cerdo ibérico), which fattens on autumn acorns to produce high- value cured ham—prevents overgrowth, enriches topsoil nutrient cycling, and curbs wildfire transmission.

G. In recent decades, however, this ancient agrarian equilibrium has encountered grave socioeconomic and environmental threats. The global wine industry''s increasing adoption of synthetic plastic closures and aluminum screw caps has reduced international demand for natural cork stoppers, which account for approximately 70 percent of the economic revenue generated by cork forestry. When cork forests lose their financial viability, rural landowners often abandon active management, plant fast-growing monoculture eucalyptus plantations, or convert traditional woodlands into intensive mechanized agriculture, which rapidly degrades topsoils. Coupled with the intensifying effects of anthropogenic climate change—manifested in prolonged megadroughts and catastrophic megafires—the preservation of traditional cork silviculture has become a major conservation priority, demonstrating that the protection of biological biodiversity is inextricably linked to the economic survival of historic craft traditions.'
);

