-- Passage 1: Ancient Ice Harvesting and the Engineering of Persian Yakhchāls
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '0644a913-cd3c-57c0-a633-2eded88ca681', '67b88ccf-be09-57f3-8905-d378e15a7689', 1, 'Ancient Ice Harvesting and the Engineering of Persian Yakhchāls',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the arid plateau regions of ancient and medieval Persia (modern-day Iran), summer temperatures routinely exceed 40 degrees Celsius, creating an environment where the preservation of perishable provisions and the availability of chilled refreshments posed profound logistical challenges. In the absence of modern electrical refrigeration, ancient Persian architects and hydrologists engineered an extraordinary passive cooling complex known as the yakhchāl (literally "ice pit"). Dating back as early as 400 BCE, these monumental conical structures allowed desert communities to manufacture, harvest, and store hundreds of tons of solid ice throughout the scorching summer months. Operating entirely without mechanical energy or artificial fuel inputs, the yakhchāl stands as a masterwork of ancient thermodynamics, materials science, and hydrological engineering.

B. The operation of a yakhchāl complex was based on an ingenious combination of nocturnal radiative cooling and subterranean insulation. A standard installation consisted of three integrated architectural components: a long, shallow freezing channel; a massive east-west shade wall; and a domed subterranean storage repository. During clear winter nights, water was systematically diverted from local underground aqueducts, known as qanāts, into the shallow freezing channel excavated along the northern side of the tall shade wall. The wall, often standing over ten meters high and stretching for hundreds of meters, served a dual purpose. During the day, it cast a continuous shadow over the channel, preventing winter sunlight from heating the earth; during the night, it sheltered the shallow water from warm southern breezes, allowing the surface water to radiate its thermal energy directly into the dry, cloudless night sky.

C. This nocturnal sub-cooling effect allowed surface water to freeze solid even when the ambient air temperature hovered slightly above the freezing mark, typically between 1 and 3 degrees Celsius. Because outer space acts as an effective heat sink, the thin layer of water radiated long-wave infrared energy toward the cosmos faster than the surrounding ambient air could warm it via convection. Before dawn, workers harvested the newly formed sheets of ice, broke them into uniform blocks, and transferred them into the adjacent subterranean holding chamber before the morning sun could initiate melting. This seasonal process was repeated night after night throughout the coldest winter months, gradually accumulating vast monolithic blocks of ice within the underground pit.

D. The subterranean holding pit was excavated deep into the ground, frequently reaching depths of ten to fifteen meters to capitalize on the natural thermal inertia of the earth. The walls of the pit and the soaring conical dome above it were constructed from a specialized, waterproof mortar known as sarooj. Developed by Persian master builders, sarooj was an engineered composite material formulated by meticulously blending sand, clay, egg whites, lime, goat hair, and wood ash in precise empirical proportions. Once cured, sarooj exhibited remarkable compressive strength, complete impermeability to liquid water, and extraordinarily low thermal conductivity, forming a thermal barrier that prevented outside heat from penetrating into the underground vault.

E. Above the subterranean pit rose a towering, beehive-shaped conical dome featuring an opening at its apex. This conical geometry served as an essential aerodynamic element of the passive thermodynamic cooling system. Because warm air naturally becomes less dense and rises, any convective heat that penetrated the interior migrated upward along the curved inner walls of the dome and escaped through the central ventilation hole at the top. Simultaneously, the heavy, chilled air remained pooled at the bottom of the deep subterranean pit. The massive thickness of the dome’s base—frequently exceeding two meters—shielded the interior from direct solar radiation during the height of summer, ensuring that internal temperatures remained near or below freezing year-round.

F. To further regulate the subterranean microclimate, ancient Persian engineers linked the yakhchāl to active bādghīrs (traditional windcatchers) and subterranean qanāt channels. The bādghīr, a vertical turret with directional vents, captured high-altitude desert breezes and channeled them downward across the cool, moist subterranean qanāt waters. As the dry wind passed over the subterranean water flow, it underwent evaporative cooling, dropping in temperature before entering the lower chambers of the yakhchāl. The continuous displacement of warm air by this chilled, incoming airstream maintained internal humidity and prevented the ice from sublimating or melting.

G. To prevent the stored ice from fusing into an impenetrable, solid mass of frozen rock over months of storage, workers layered the blocks with insulating straw, woven reeds, or felt blankets. A network of narrow trenches at the base of the pit captured any minor meltwater, funneling it through drainage channels to prevent pooled liquid from transferring heat to the remaining ice cache. Through this integrated matrix of desert engineering, yakhchāls supplied ice throughout the blazing summer for royal courts, public water fountains, and the preservation of food and traditional iced desserts such as faloodeh. Today, though rendered obsolete by industrial refrigeration, surviving yakhchāls across the Iranian plateau remain enduring monuments to the power of sustainable, zero-energy environmental architecture.'
);

