-- Passage 1: The Agronomy and Cultural Spread of the Breadfruit Tree
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '87d0c3eb-eab1-58c5-8d66-91a9097835dd', '5b080670-15ae-5369-b2fc-b8a60c4ad6c1', 1, 'The Agronomy and Cultural Spread of the Breadfruit Tree',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the tropical archipelagos of the South Pacific, few botanical species have played as vital a role in human settlement and demographic survival as the breadfruit tree (Artocarpus altilis). A majestic evergreen tree belonging to the Moraceae (mulberry) family, the breadfruit reaches heights of up to twenty to thirty meters, bearing large, glossy, pinnately lobed leaves. Its true value, however, resides in its prolific yield of massive, starchy syncarps—composite fruits that can weigh between one and four kilograms each. When roasted, baked, or boiled, the fibrous, cream-colored pulp of a mature, unripened breadfruit exhibits a culinary texture and mild savory flavor remarkably reminiscent of freshly baked wheat bread or roasted potatoes. For millennia, this resilient cultigen has served as the foundational staple carbohydrate for indigenous Oceanic civilizations, yielding enormous caloric abundance with minimal agricultural labor.

B. The botanical ancestry of domesticated breadfruit is rooted in the tropical lowland rainforests of Southeast Asia and the western Pacific. Evolutionary geneticists and ethnobotanists have traced its primary wild ancestor to Artocarpus camansi (known natively as camansi or seeded breadfruit), a wild species indigenous to New Guinea, the Maluku Islands, and the Philippines. Over generations of selective cultivation, ancestral horticulturalists selected naturally occurring mutant specimens that possessed greatly reduced seed counts and enlarged, fleshy edible receptacles. Through continuous vegetative cloning, early farmers developed the triploid, seedless variety (Artocarpus altilis), which is almost entirely sterile and depends strictly on human intervention for vegetative propagation via root suckers, root cuttings, or air-layering.

C. The geographical dispersal of the breadfruit is inextricably intertwined with the great maritime migrations of the Austronesian-speaking voyagers across Oceania. Beginning approximately three thousand years ago, seafaring voyagers associated with the Lapita cultural complex embarked on open-ocean voyages across thousands of kilometers of the Pacific Ocean in double-hulled sailing canoes (waʻa kaulua). Recognizing that remote volcanic atolls and coral islands possessed few native edible plants, these intrepid explorers transported a curated selection of vital domesticated plants and animals, a portable ecosystem known to modern anthropologists as "canoe plants." Carefully wrapped in protective damp moss, leaves, and woven coconut-fiber matting, sterile breadfruit root cuttings were shielded from ocean saltwater spray during weeks at sea and planted immediately upon landing on newly colonized islands throughout Melanesia, Micronesia, and Polynesia.

D. In the tropical volcanic soils of Polynesian island groups such as Samoa, Tahiti, and Hawaii, the breadfruit flourished, integrating into highly sophisticated agroforestry systems. A single mature tree can produce between one hundred and five hundred fruits annually, continuing its high-yield productive output for well over fifty to eighty years with virtually no requirement for annual soil tillage, chemical fertilizers, or intensive weeding. Under the shade of its expansive canopy, traditional farmers practiced multi-strata cropping, planting complementary understory species such as taro (Colocasia esculenta), bananas, and medicinal herbs. This multi-layered forest agriculture conserved topsoil moisture, minimized erosion on steep volcanic slopes, and created a self-sustaining ecological buffer against tropical cyclones and droughts.

E. Because breadfruit production is intensely seasonal—typically yielding one or two massive harvest gluts each year—Oceanic societies developed ingenious, large-scale biotechnology to preserve surplus harvests against seasonal scarcity and catastrophic typhoons. Throughout eastern Polynesia and Micronesia, communities excavated massive subterranean fermentation pits lined with banana and ti leaves. Hundreds of peeled, cored breadfruits were packed into these anaerobic earthen silos, covered with heavy stones, and left to undergo lactic acid fermentation. Over several weeks, microorganisms transformed the starchy pulp into a pungent, paste-like preserved substance known as mahi in Tahiti or mā in the Marquesas. Tightly sealed from oxygen, this fermented food cache could remain edible and nutritionally viable for years, and in some recorded cases, several decades, providing an emergency famine reserve.

F. The immense agronomic productivity of the breadfruit eventually attracted the attention of eighteenth-century European colonial empires. Following reports from circumnavigators such as Captain James Cook and botanist Sir Joseph Banks, who observed that Tahitian islanders secured an abundant year-round carbohydrate supply with almost no physical toil, the British Crown recognized breadfruit as an ideal, low-cost food source to sustain enslaved populations working on sugarcane plantations in the British West Indies. In 1787, the British Admiralty commissioned Lieutenant William Bligh to command HMS Bounty on an expedition to collect live breadfruit saplings from Tahiti and transport them to the Caribbean.

G. Although Bligh''s initial voyage ended in the infamous mutiny on the Bounty in 1789, where mutineers tossed hundreds of potted breadfruit plants into the sea, Bligh successfully completed the botanical transfer on a second voyage aboard HMS Providence in 1793, delivering thousands of living plants to St. Vincent and Jamaica. While Caribbean plantation workers initially rejected the unfamiliar, alien fruit, breadfruit gradually integrated into local culinary traditions, evolving into an indispensable cultural staple across Jamaica and the wider Caribbean basin. In the contemporary era, international agronomic organizations and global food-security initiatives are re-evaluating breadfruit as a powerful, climate-resilient crop to combat chronic malnutrition and enhance food sovereignty across drought-prone equatorial nations.'
);

