-- Passage 1: The Architecture and Construction of Medieval Windmills in Northern Europe
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '6b90f812-38e5-5b73-8b25-efe4be02f0d4', '9749457f-ed2c-5471-9ce9-eea127e428d2', 1, 'The Architecture and Construction of Medieval Windmills in Northern Europe',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the early medieval period, the agricultural economy of Western Europe relied almost exclusively on human labor, draught animals, and watermills to grind cereal grains into flour. However, in low-lying, flat coastal landscapes—most notably across eastern England, the Low Countries, and northern France—slow-moving rivers and tidal estuaries frequently lacked the hydraulic gradient required to power conventional waterwheels. Furthermore, severe winter freezes regularly immobilized river currents, while summer droughts reduced stream flows to a trickle. In response to these geographical and hydrological limitations, medieval engineers during the late twelfth century developed an extraordinary prime mover that harnessed the kinetic energy of the atmosphere: the vertical-axis windmill.

B. The earliest and most widespread technological manifestation of this innovation was the "post mill" (or trestle mill), which emerged across the plains of northern Europe around 1180 CE. The foundational engineering challenge of capturing wind energy lies in its directional volatility; unlike river currents that flow along fixed geological channels, winds constantly shift across the compass. To address this unpredictability, the entire wooden superstructure of the post mill—a massive, timber-framed box containing the heavy grinding stones, gearing mechanisms, and grain hoppers—was suspended and balanced upon a single, massive vertical oak pillar known as the main post.

C. The structural stability of this entire elevated assembly depended upon an ingenious timber sub-structure known as the "trestle" or "roundhouse." The massive main post, carved from the trunk of a single mature oak tree measuring up to a meter in diameter, was supported at its base by two massive horizontal cross-trees arranged at right angles. Four diagonal timber struts, termed quarter-bars, extended upward from the ends of the cross-trees to mortise into the vertical post, forming a rigid pyramidal foundation that prevented the heavy mill body from toppling under violent wind gusts. To rotate the mill toward shifting wind directions, a long wooden tailpole projected outward from the rear of the buck, extending down toward the ground. The miller pushed this heavy lever manually, or hitched a horse to its lower end, walking the entire multi-ton mill housing in a circle around the central post until the sails faced directly into the wind.

D. Inside the buck, the aerodynamic power generated by the turning sails was translated into rotational mechanical force through a sophisticated system of wooden gearwheels. The central horizontal windshaft, typically fashioned from seasoned oak or pine, carried a massive wooden wheel called the brake wheel. Lined with sturdy cogs carved from dense, wear-resistant hardwoods such as hornbeam, applewood, or beech, the brake wheel engaged a smaller vertical gear called the wallower. This configuration transformed the slow, horizontal rotation of the sails into high-speed vertical rotation, driving a heavy iron spindle that spun the upper millstone (the "runner stone") against a stationary lower stone (the "bedstone") at speeds exceeding one hundred revolutions per minute.

E. Operating a medieval windmill was an arduous, highly skilled task that required continuous meteorological vigilance. Early windmill sails were constructed as open wooden frameworks covered with heavy canvas cloth. To regulate the mill''s operating speed across fluctuating wind velocities, the miller had to stop the mill, climb onto each individual sail frame, and manually adjust the canvas spread—unfurling full sail during light breezes or reefing the cloth during stiff gales to prevent runaway rotation. If a sudden tempest struck and the wooden friction brake on the brake wheel failed to halt the rotating machinery, the extreme frictional heat generated between moving wooden components could ignite the dry timber frame, resulting in catastrophic structural fires.

F. By the late fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, the inherent physical and spatial constraints of the post mill spurred the development of the "tower mill" (and its Dutch timber variant, the "smock mill"). Instead of rotating the entire multi-story wooden building around a central timber pillar, the tower mill featured a stationary, cylindrical tower constructed of stone or brick masonry. Only a lightweight timber roof structure, known as the cap, was rotated atop the stone tower on a track of greased iron rollers. This architectural breakthrough allowed millers to build substantially larger, multi-story processing facilities containing multiple sets of grinding stones, extensive grain storage lofts, and living quarters, vastly expanding industrial capacity across the agrarian landscape of pre-industrial Europe.

G. Technological refinement continued throughout the early modern era, culminating in two major automated inventions during the eighteenth century: the fantail and the shuttered patent sail. Invented by English blacksmith Edmund Lee in 1745, the fantail was a small auxiliary windwheel mounted at right angles to the main sails behind the cap; when the wind shifted, the fantail rotated, driving a series of reduction gears that automatically turned the cap back into the wind without human intervention. Simultaneously, the invention of spring-loaded wooden shutters eliminated the dangerous manual task of reefing canvas sails. By converting volatile atmospheric forces into dependable mechanical power, medieval and early modern windmills laid the technological foundations for European industrialization, standing as timeless testaments to historical renewable engineering.'
);

