-- Passage 1: The Discovery and Excavation of the Terracotta Army
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '2181c0c6-362b-581a-b019-6953ae308db5', '7716f566-0584-52e8-b60f-456d69489684', 1, 'The Discovery and Excavation of the Terracotta Army',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In March 1974, during a period of severe regional drought, a group of local farmers drilling an agricultural well near the city of Xi''an in Shaanxi Province, China, struck an extraordinary subterranean deposit. Beneath layers of hard clay and silt lay fragments of life-sized terracotta figures, bronze arrowheads, and broken terracotta horses. Unbeknownst to the well-diggers, they had punctured the outer defensive perimeter of the sprawling necropolis of Qin Shi Huang (259– 210 BCE), the visionary and ruthless first emperor of unified China. Subsequent excavations conducted by the Shaanxi Provincial Archaeological Team revealed that the figures formed part of a massive subterranean military garrison—now known universally as the Terracotta Army— interred over two millennia ago to guard the emperor''s spirit in the afterlife.

B. The scale of the necropolis complex surrounding the imperial burial mound at Mount Li is breathtaking in its architectural ambition. Covering an estimated fifty-six square kilometers, the funerary compound was constructed over a thirty-six-year period by a mobilized labor force exceeding seven hundred thousand conscripted laborers and convicts, as recorded by the Han dynasty historian Sima Qian in his Shiji (Records of the Grand Historian). The subterranean terracotta army itself is situated approximately 1.5 kilometers east of the central burial tumulus, distributed across four massive underground vaults or pits. Pit 1, the largest enclosure, spans an area of over 14,000 square meters and houses an estimated six thousand infantry soldiers arranged in precise battle formation, supported by wooden war chariots and armored cavalry.

C. What distinguishes the terracotta warriors from standard mass-produced funerary effigies of antiquity is their astonishing degree of individualization and modular engineering. The army comprises diverse military ranks and functional specializations, including front-line vanguard crossbowmen, heavily armored heavy infantry, kneeling archers, chariot drivers, cavalry riders, and high-ranking generals. While early observers hypothesized that each figure was a sculpted portrait of an actual living soldier from Qin Shi Huang’s imperial guard, archaeological and technical analyses demonstrate that the figures were manufactured through a sophisticated modular assembly-line system. Artisans used specialized clay molds to produce eight standardized facial shapes, onto which individual clay features—such as ears, noses, beards, and distinct hair knots—were manually applied and sculpted before firing in high-temperature kilns.

D. The assembly process required precise coordination among imperial state workshops and local pottery guilds. Raw loess clay, abundant in the surrounding Shaanxi plains, was washed, sieved, and mixed with fine sand to prevent shrinkage and thermal cracking during firing. Craftsmen constructed the hollow torsos using coiled clay techniques, onto which solid cast legs, arms, hands, and heads were affixed using wet slip as an adhesive. Epigraphic stamps and inscribed calligraphic marks discovered on the bodies of the warriors reveal the names of master potters and their specific administrative workshops, establishing an early, rigorous system of state quality control: if an individual statue suffered structural defects or cracked during kiln firing, the responsible artisan was held legally accountable and penalized.

E. When originally installed in their subterranean timber-roofed corridors, the terracotta warriors were not the monochrome, terracotta-brown statues seen by museum visitors today; they were vibrantly painted in radiant, polychrome pigments. Artisans applied one or two base coats of raw Chinese lacquer (qi), a natural sap tapped from the toxic lacquer tree (Toxicodendron vernicifluum), followed by layers of mineral and synthetic colorants. Clothing and armor were painted with cinnabar (bright red), malachite (green), azurite (blue), bone white, and a rare synthetic pigment known to materials scientists as Han Purple (barium copper silicate). However, the underlying lacquer layer is exceptionally sensitive to atmospheric changes; when an excavated figure is exposed to the dry air of modern excavation trenches, the lacquer dehydrates and contracts within fifteen to thirty seconds, peeling away from the clay and taking the fragile pigment layers with it. Modern conservation teams now deploy specialized moisture-retaining polymers like polyethylene glycol (PEG) to consolidate the delicate paint layers before excavation proceeds.

F. In addition to the ceramic infantry, the necropolis pits have yielded an astonishing armory of real, functional Bronze Age weaponry, including over forty thousand bronze arrowheads, halberds (ge), lances, spears, and double-edged swords (jian). Metallurgical testing revealed that many of these weapons remained razor-sharp and remarkably free from corrosive rust after lying buried in damp earth for more than twenty-two hundred years. Early scientific researchers hypothesized that Qin metalsmiths had developed an ancient form of chromium-conversion anti- rust coating. However, modern high-resolution scanning electron microscopy and energy- dispersive X-ray spectroscopy have disproven this theory, demonstrating that the microscopic chromium traces were actually environmental contaminants originating from the lacquer coatings applied to wooden scabbards and shafts. The extraordinary preservation of the bronze weapons was instead the result of the alkaline, tightly compacted burial soil and the high tin content of the bronze alloy.

G. Despite five decades of continuous archaeological excavation, the central imperial burial tumulus containing Qin Shi Huang’s actual coffin remains unexcavated. Sima Qian’s historical chronicles describe an inner subterranean palace featuring miniature rivers and seas of flowing liquid mercury circulating through mechanical pumps, with the ceiling encrusted with pearls and gemstones to mirror the celestial constellations. Modern geochemical soil core surveys around the central mound have detected anomalous mercury concentrations up to one hundred times higher than background levels, strongly corroborating the historical accounts. Fearing that modern excavation techniques cannot adequately protect the delicate organic contents of the central tomb from environmental degradation, Chinese state heritage authorities have resolved to leave the emperor''s ultimate resting place sealed until non-invasive scanning technologies and conservation methods mature.'
);

