-- Passage 3: Enactivism and the Sensorimotor Foundations of Perception
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'b0c9c867-1455-5393-90b1-64bcecc2d440', '7716f566-0584-52e8-b60f-456d69489684', 3, 'Enactivism and the Sensorimotor Foundations of Perception',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. For more than half a century, the dominant conceptual framework in cognitive psychology and mainstream neurobiology has been computationalism—the classical thesis that the human brain operates as an information-processing digital computer. Under this orthodox cognitivist paradigm, perception is conceptualized as an internal, passive computational process. The external physical world presents raw sensory signals—such as photons striking retinal photoreceptors or acoustic vibrations displacing cochlear hair cells—which the nervous system translates into symbolic neural codes. The brain’s central processing modules then perform algorithmic computations upon these internal inputs, generating a rich, three-dimensional mental representation of the outside world within the subjective theatre of consciousness. In standard computational cognitive science, the mind is strictly identical to these internal neural models, while the physical body and external environment serve merely as peripheral input-output hardware.

B. In the early 1990s, however, a radical philosophical and cognitive counter-movement emerged to challenge this representational orthodoxy: the "4E Cognitive Science" framework, which asserts that cognition is Embodied, Embedded, Enacted, and Extended. At the cutting edge of this movement stands Enactivism, a theoretical paradigm formulated by cognitive scientist Francisco Varela, philosopher Evan Thompson, and psychologist Eleanor Rosch in their 1991 foundational work, The Embodied Mind. Enactivism rejects the premise that perception consists of recovering pre-existing, mind-independent objective realities through internal neural mirroring. Instead, enactivists argue that perception is fundamentally enacted: the mind does not passively construct internal representations of an external world; rather, a living organism "brings forth" or enacts a meaningful world of experience through its dynamic, goal-directed sensorimotor interactions with its physical environment.

C. In 2001, philosophers J. Kevin O’Regan and Alva Noë crystallized this framework into the Sensorimotor Contingency Theory of perception. O’Regan and Noë advanced the radical proposition that perceiving is not something that occurs inside the brain; perceiving is a skillful physical activity executed by the whole organism. According to their model, visual perception consists of mastering the lawful, systematic patterns of sensorimotor contingencies—the implicit rules that govern how sensory inputs change in response to physical bodily movements. For example, when an observer moves their head to the left, the retinal projection of a three- dimensional sphere shifts, rotates, and expands according to predictable geometric laws. Perceiving an object is not possessing a static neural snapshot of that object in the visual cortex; it is having practical, skillful mastery of how one''s sensory inputs will transform if one moves one''s eyes, shifts one''s posture, or reaches out a hand.

D. The most compelling empirical evidence supporting the sensorimotor enactive model arises from experimental work with sensory substitution devices (SSDs), originally pioneered by neuroscientist Paul Bach-y-Rita. In the late 1960s, Bach-y-Rita developed Tactile-Visual Sensory Substitution (TVSS) systems to assist congenitally blind individuals. A TVSS system converts visual imagery captured by a head-mounted video camera into an array of vibrating plastic pins or electrical micro-currents pressed against the skin of the subject''s back, abdomen, or tongue. Crucially, if a blind participant sits passively while an experimenter moves the camera, the participant experiences only meaningless, confusing tactile sensations of vibration or tickling on their skin. However, the moment the participant is given physical control of the camera—allowing them to actively pan, tilt, zoom, and physically explore the environment—a profound perceptual transition occurs within hours. The tactile tingling on the skin fades from conscious awareness, and the user begins to experience externalized, three-dimensional visual perception, perceiving objects as located out in physical space, estimating distances, and recognizing complex shapes.

E. Furthermore, developmental experiments in animal cognition provide striking behavioral confirmation of the enactive premise. In the famous "Kitten Carousel" experiment conducted by developmental psychologists Richard Held and Alan Hein in 1963, pairs of newborn kittens were raised in complete darkness, exposed to light only during daily training sessions inside a cylindrical apparatus with high-contrast vertical stripes. One kitten (the "active" kitten) walked freely, exploring the environment while hitched to a mechanical gondola. The second kitten (the "passive" kitten) was placed inside the gondola, carried along the exact same spatial trajectory and exposed to identical visual stimulation without executing any independent motor movements. When tested after several weeks, the active kitten developed normal, mature visual- spatial perception, exhibiting depth avoidance and blinking at approaching objects. The passive kitten, despite having received the exact same photons on its retinas, was functionally blind, bumping into walls and failing basic visual cliff tests. Without active motor coupling, sensory input fails to generate coherent perception.

F. Despite its revolutionary insights, enactivism has encountered vigorous resistance from orthodox computational neuroscientists and representational philosophers of mind, such as David Marr’s followers, Jerry Fodor, and Andy Clark. Critics raise the "scaling-up problem": while sensorimotor enactivism provides a plausible account of basic online perceptual-motor coordination (such as catching a baseball or navigating a room), it struggles to explain "offline" abstract cognitive capabilities that operate in the total absence of immediate sensory feedback. How can an enactive model account for abstract mathematical reasoning, counterfactual historical reflection, daydreaming, or contemplating theoretical physics concepts like black holes, where the mind operates disconnected from real-time physical movement? Critics contend that abstract, offline thought necessarily requires internal symbolic representations, demonstrating that the mind cannot be reduced entirely to physical sensorimotor loops.

G. Contemporary enactivists, such as Shaun Gallagher and Daniel Hutto, have responded by developing "radical enactivism" and multi-tiered models of embodied cognition. They argue that higher-order abstract reasoning is not an un-embodied computational process, but an internalized, culturally scaffolded extension of basic sensorimotor habits. Abstract thought is mediated by internalized linguistic practices, gesture, physical writing tools, and cultural artifacts that allow humans to simulate and manipulate sensorimotor dynamics offline. By linking the biological autonomy of living organisms (autopoiesis) with skillful sensorimotor exploration and cultural scaffolding, enactivism offers a profound paradigm shift. Perception is not an internal calculation performed within the dark enclosure of the skull; it is an open, embodied dance between a living being and its world.'
);

