-- Passage 1: The History and Ecology of Great Barrier Reef Coral Spawning
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '108fca2c-0676-58f7-b790-1ca3dd7f7dbc', 'b75daf5e-65b7-5246-b05a-ef8cf2e456de', 1, 'The History and Ecology of Great Barrier Reef Coral Spawning',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For centuries of biological investigation, the reproductive mechanisms of scleractinian (stony or reef-building) corals remained one of the most baffling enigmas of marine natural history. Until the early 1980s, prevailing marine biological doctrine held that the vast majority of coral species reproduced via "brooding." In brooding species, male colonies release sperm into the water column, which is subsequently drawn into female polyps to fertilize eggs internally. The female polyps then nurture the resulting microscopic, ciliated larvae (planulae) inside their gastrovascular cavities before releasing mature, free-swimming planulae directly into the surrounding reef environment. Because planula release was observed to occur in small, staggered increments across several months, scientists assumed that all coral reefs reproduced through continuous, low-intensity internal fertilization.

B. This classical biological consensus was dramatically shattered in November 1981 by a team of young marine biologists from James Cook University—including Peter Harrison, Russell Babcock, Gordon Bull, James Oliver, and Carden Wallace—conducting field research at Magnetic Island near Townsville, along Australia’s Great Barrier Reef. During night-time diving surveys undertaken a few nights after the late-spring full moon, the researchers witnessed an unprecedented natural spectacle: dozens of distinct coral species across multiple taxonomic families simultaneously expelled millions upon millions of brightly colored egg-and-sperm bundles into the ocean. The buoyant gamete clusters floated upward through the water column, transforming the dark sea into an underwater blizzard of pink, red, orange, and turquoise spheres that coalesced into vast slicks on the ocean surface.

C. This extraordinary phenomenon, now known globally as synchronous mass coral spawning, represents the largest synchronized reproductive event on Earth. Subsequent ecological surveys across the 2,300-kilometer expanse of the Great Barrier Reef established that mass spawning is not an isolated local anomaly, but a universal reproductive strategy utilized by more than 130 species of scleractinian corals, including dominant reef-building genera such as Acropora, Montipora, Porites, and Favites. Rather than brooding internally, these species are broadcast spawners: they synchronously release male and female gametes externally into the open sea, where cross-fertilization, embryonic development, and larval morphogenesis occur entirely in the pelagic zone.

D. The evolutionary advantages of synchronized broadcast spawning are rooted in the ecological dynamics of predator swamping and fertilization kinetics. By concentrating their entire annual reproductive output into a single, coordinated temporal window lasting only a few hours on two or three consecutive nights, coral populations flood the surrounding marine ecosystem with a superabundance of nutritious gametes. While local planktivorous reef fish, nocturnal crustaceans, and filter-feeding invertebrates feed voraciously on the floating banquet, the sheer volume of eggs and sperm far exceeds the collective digestive capacity of the local predator community. Consequently, millions of surplus gametes survive predation to achieve successful cross-fertilization, maximizing reproductive yields while minimizing per-capita mortality risks.

E. Achieving such exquisite multi-species synchrony across hundreds of kilometers of open ocean requires an intricate, multi-tiered hierarchy of environmental cues that align physiological maturation with external physical rhythms. The broad seasonal timing of gametogenesis is regulated by rising seawater temperatures and lengthening photoperiods during late spring, which accelerate the cellular development of eggs and testes within the polyps. The specific night of spawning is dictated by the lunar cycle: spawning almost universally occurs four to six nights after the full moon in October or November (and sometimes December in offshore reef sectors), when nocturnal tidal amplitudes are minimal. Finally, the precise hourly trigger on the night of spawning is set by the solar cycle: the rapid drop in ambient light levels following sunset initiates a hormonal cascade that prompts polyp contraction and simultaneous gamete release between 7:30 PM and 10:30 PM.

F. Once released, the positively buoyant gamete bundles float to the surface, where wave action ruptures their protective membranes, allowing sperm to locate and fertilize compatible eggs from neighboring colonies. Within twenty-four to forty-eight hours post-fertilization, the zygotes develop into ciliated planula larvae capable of directed swimming. These planulae drift with oceanic currents for several days to weeks, dispersing across regional reef systems. When a planula detects favorable chemical settlement cues—specifically biochemical signals emitted by crustose coralline algae (CCA) and specialized microbial biofilms on clean limestone substrates—it settles, metamorphoses into a primary polyp, secretes an external calcium carbonate skeleton, and begins budding to establish a new colonial reef matrix.

G. In contemporary marine science, understanding the dynamics of coral spawning has become vital for reef conservation in the face of anthropogenic climate change. Ocean warming events and marine heatwaves induce coral bleaching, disrupting gamete development and suppressing annual reproductive output. To counter these declines, marine conservationists have developed larval propagation techniques (known as "coral IVF"): collecting millions of wild gamete bundles during mass spawning events, culturing them in floating nursery enclosures protected from surface predators, and mass-settling lab-reared juvenile corals onto degraded reef sectors. By harnessing the natural power of synchronous mass spawning, scientists are pioneering innovative restoration tools to safeguard the future of global coral reefs.'
);

