-- Passage 3: The Thermodynamics of Computation: Landauer’s Principle and Maxwell’s Demon
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '6b792595-5a90-542a-92ef-90ef7e5a64f2', 'b75daf5e-65b7-5246-b05a-ef8cf2e456de', 3, 'The Thermodynamics of Computation: Landauer’s Principle and Maxwell’s Demon',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the year 1867, the Scottish physicist James Clerk Maxwell proposed a famous thought experiment that appeared to threaten the absolute authority of the Second Law of Thermodynamics. The Second Law asserts that the total entropy (a mathematical measure of microscopic disorder or unavailable energy) of an isolated physical system can never decrease over time; it must always remain constant or increase ($\Delta S \ge 0$). Maxwell envisioned a sealed container divided into two isolated compartments, $A$ and $B$, separated by an impermeable partition containing a microscopic, frictionless trapdoor. The entire chamber is initially filled with a gas in thermal equilibrium at a uniform temperature. Controlling the door is an intelligent, microscopic entity—subsequently christened "Maxwell’s Demon."

B. By monitoring the velocities of individual gas molecules as they approach the partition, the Demon operates the trapdoor without expending physical work: it opens the door to let fast, high-kinetic-energy molecules pass from $A$ to $B$, while allowing slow, low-energy molecules to pass from $B$ to $A$. Over time, compartment $B$ becomes progressively hotter while compartment $A$ becomes progressively colder, establishing a temperature gradient across the container. This temperature differential could then be harnessed to drive a heat engine and generate mechanical work, all without adding external energy to the system. By sorting molecules based on information, Maxwell’s Demon appeared to achieve the impossible: decreasing the total entropy of an isolated system, thereby permitting a perpetual motion machine of the second kind.

C. For nearly a century, theoretical physicists struggled to resolve the paradox of Maxwell''s Demon. Early attempts, notably by Hungarian-American physicist Leó Szilárd in 1929 and French physicist Léon Brillouin in the 1950s, focused on the physics of measurement. Szilárd and Brillouin argued that to distinguish fast molecules from slow ones, the Demon must interact with the particles using light signals (photons). The thermodynamic entropy generated by the light source and the physical act of measurement, they posited, would always equal or exceed the entropy reduction achieved by sorting the gas molecules, thereby rescuing the Second Law.

D. However, this measurement-based resolution was fundamentally overturned in 1961 by the German-American physicist Rolf Landauer working at IBM Research. Landauer demonstrated that the act of measurement and data acquisition can, in principle, be performed reversibly with zero thermodynamic dissipation. Instead, Landauer identified the true thermodynamic cost of computation at the opposite end of the information lifecycle: information erasure. Landauer established that whenever a computer or physical agent performs a logically irreversible operation—such as deleting a bit of memory, resetting a register to zero, or merging two computational paths into one—there is an unavoidable, fundamental physical consequence.

E. Known universally as Landauer’s Principle, this law states that the erasure of a single bit of binary information from a physical memory register always dissipates a minimum quantity of heat into the surrounding thermal environment, given by the fundamental thermodynamic equation: $$Q_{\min} = k_B T \ln 2$$ where $k_B$ is the Boltzmann constant ($1.3806 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K}$), $T$ is the absolute temperature of the thermal reservoir in Kelvin, and $\ln 2$ is the natural logarithm of 2 (approximately 0.693). At standard room temperature (293 K or 20°C), this Landauer limit equates to an infinitesimal energy dissipation of approximately $2.8 \times 10^{-21}$ joules per erased bit. While infinitesimal on human scales, this quantity represents an irreducible physical lower bound imposed by the laws of quantum statistical mechanics.

F. The final, definitive exorcism of Maxwell''s Demon was achieved in 1982 by IBM physicist Charles Bennett, building directly upon Landauer’s Principle. Bennett demonstrated that a physical Demon can measure and sort gas molecules without dissipating any energy, but to continue operating, the Demon must store the measurement results (e.g., "molecule was fast" or "molecule was slow") within its own internal physical memory. Because the Demon’s memory register is finite, it must eventually erase previous records to make room for new measurements. It is precisely during this mandatory information erasure step that the Demon dissipates heat into the environment, increasing external thermodynamic entropy by an amount exactly equal to or greater than the entropy reduction achieved inside the gas container. Information is not an abstract, ethereal entity; information is physical.

G. In modern experimental physics, Landauer’s Principle has transitioned from theoretical deduction to direct laboratory confirmation. In 2012, an international team of experimental physicists led by Antoine Bérut utilized optical tweezers to trap a microscopic silica bead within a bistable dual-well laser potential, creating a physical one-bit memory. By manipulating the laser barriers to perform a physical reset operation, the researchers measured the heat released into the surrounding water bath, confirming with breathtaking precision that energy dissipation asymptotically approaches the exact Landauer bound ($k_B T \ln 2$) as the reset speed approaches thermodynamic equilibrium.

H. The profound fusion of thermodynamics and information theory carries transformative implications for the future of computer engineering. Contemporary commercial semiconductor microprocessors operate far above the Landauer limit, dissipating thousands of times more energy per logic gate operation due to parasitic electrical resistance and capacitive charging. However, as silicon transistors approach fundamental atomic scale limits, managing thermal dissipation has become the primary bottleneck in microchip architecture. To bypass the Landauer erasure barrier, computer scientists are developing "reversible computing" architectures— computational paradigms that execute logical operations without ever erasing intermediate data bits, holding the revolutionary promise of ultra-dense microprocessors that compute with near- zero heat generation.'
);

