-- Passage 1: The Development of the Printing Press and Early European Typography
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '2fc3baee-a55f-51c0-967f-7265a1389000', '39cd2a80-c8fb-5d8b-8146-69e767dd7bd7', 1, 'The Development of the Printing Press and Early European Typography',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the mid-fifteenth century, Western Europe stood on the threshold of an unprecedented intellectual, religious, and scientific transformation. For centuries, the preservation and transmission of written culture had been the exclusive domain of monastic scriptoria and secular scribal guilds. Every individual manuscript—from illuminated ecclesiastical Bibles to classical legal treatises—demanded arduous manual transcription using quill pens and animal skins (vellum or parchment). Because producing a single comprehensive folio volume could require the labor of a skilled scribe for several months to a year, books remained extraordinarily rare, luxury commodities accessible only to the wealthiest feudal aristocrats, royal courts, and high ecclesiastical authorities. The resulting scarcity of texts created a severe intellectual bottleneck across medieval European civilization.

B. The definitive technological breakthrough that dissolved this bottleneck occurred in the German city of Mainz between 1440 and 1455, spearheaded by a skilled goldsmith, merchant, and venture capitalist named Johannes Gensfleisch zur Laden zum Gutenberg. While wooden block printing (xylography) and movable ceramic and metal type systems had been developed centuries earlier in East Asia—notably in Song Dynasty China and Goryeo Dynasty Korea— Gutenberg’s singular genius was synthesizing multiple distinct mechanical and metallurgical innovations into a unified, high-speed mass-production system capable of industrial replication.

C. The cornerstone of Gutenberg’s mechanical system was the invention of the adjustable hand mold for casting precision metal type. Previous experiments with individual letter stamps made of wood, soft clay, or sand molds failed because the materials wore down rapidly or produced irregular, misaligned letterforms. Gutenberg, drawing upon his professional metallurgical training as a gem cutter and goldsmith, carved individual letter shapes in high relief onto the ends of hard steel punches. The punch was then hammered into a softer bar of copper to create a matrix—an inverted, deep impression of the character. This copper matrix was fitted into a two- part adjustable hand mold constructed of wood-jacketed steel, into which molten metal was poured.

D. The metallurgical formulation of the type alloy was another critical engineering triumph. Gutenberg formulated a specialized ternary alloy consisting of approximately 80 to 85 percent lead, 10 to 15 percent antimony, and a small fraction of tin. Pure lead was too soft and deformed under repetitive mechanical pressure, while other common metals had excessively high melting points that made casting slow and dangerous. The inclusion of antimony was brilliant: unlike almost all other metals, antimony expands slightly as it solidifies from a liquid state into a crystal lattice. This thermal expansion forced the molten alloy into every microscopic groove and hairline contour of the copper matrix, yielding razor-sharp letter outlines. Furthermore, the alloy possessed a relatively low melting point (around 300°C) and cooled almost instantaneously upon contact with the mold, enabling an artisan to cast several thousand uniform, interchangeable type pieces in a single working day.

E. Standard, water-based inks utilized by contemporary scribes proved entirely unsuitable for metallic type; water-based preparations beaded up on the greasy metal surfaces, creating blurred, illegible smudges on the paper. Gutenberg formulated a viscous, oil-based printing ink compounded from boiled linseed oil (varnish), turpentine, and fine walnut soot or lampblack. This thick, black paste adhered evenly to the metallic type faces and transferred cleanly onto damp rag paper under pressure without bleeding across the fibers. To apply the pressure, Gutenberg adapted the mechanical design of the heavy wooden screw presses traditionally used in the Rhineland for pressing olives and extracting juice from harvested wine grapes. Mounted on a rigid vertical timber frame, the great wooden screw allowed a pressman to pull a long lever, lowering a flat wooden platen to exert immense, uniform downward pressure across a large sheet of paper.

F. The culmination of Gutenberg’s technical experimentation was the publication of his famous 42-Line Bible (commonly known as the Gutenberg Bible or Mazarin Bible), completed in Mainz around 1454–1455. Printed in double columns with forty-two lines per page, the work was published in an edition of approximately 180 copies—around 135 printed on high-grade imported Italian rag paper and 45 on calfskin vellum. To compete directly with the aesthetic prestige of high-end illuminated manuscripts, Gutenberg designed a dense, ornate Gothic typeface known as Textura Quadrata, incorporating over 290 distinct typographic characters, including complex ligatures, abbreviations, and variable-width letters to achieve perfectly justified right-hand margins.

G. Despite the artistic and technical triumph of the 42-Line Bible, Gutenberg faced financial ruin. To finance his costly decade-long development process, he had borrowed substantial sums from a wealthy Mainz financier named Johann Fust. Before the distribution of the Bible could yield profits, Fust foreclosed on the unpaid loans, taking legal possession of Gutenberg’s workshop, presses, and completed types. Fust subsequently partnered with Gutenberg’s brilliant foreman, Peter Schöffer, to commercialize the printing enterprise under the firm Fust & Schöffer. Nevertheless, the technology of typographic printing—dubbed the "Divine Art"—spread uncontrollably across Europe. By 1500, printing presses operated in more than 250 European cities, producing an estimated twenty million books during the incunabula period (the first fifty years of print). This technological revolution democratized access to information, fueled the Protestant Reformation, and laid the intellectual foundations for modern scientific inquiry.'
);

