-- Passage 3: Social Choice Theory and Arrow’s Impossibility Theorem
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '4a50d7a6-c6a7-5440-8889-4647f94e604f', '39cd2a80-c8fb-5d8b-8146-69e767dd7bd7', 3, 'Social Choice Theory and Arrow’s Impossibility Theorem',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. The philosophical foundation of democratic governance rests upon an intuitive and appealing ideal: collective public decisions should faithfully reflect the aggregated will and preferences of individual citizens. In modern democratic societies, institutional voting procedures—whether used to elect national political leaders, approve legislative referenda, or allocate municipal budgets—are presumed to serve as fair, objective mechanisms for transforming diverse individual preference rankings into a coherent collective social choice. However, in the mid- twentieth century, mathematical political economy and social choice theory exposed a profound, inescapable paradox at the mathematical heart of democratic voting systems, proving that the ideal of a perfectly fair, rational, and consistent collective decision-making system is mathematically impossible.

B. The historical roots of this mathematical dilemma trace back to the late eighteenth century and the work of French mathematician and philosopher Nicolas de Condorcet. In 1785, Condorcet identified what is now known as the "Condorcet Paradox" (or the voting paradox). Consider a simple committee of three voters choosing among three distinct candidates: A, B, and C. Voter 1 ranks their preferences: A≻B≻C (prefers A to B, and B to C). Voter 2 ranks their preferences: B≻C≻A. Voter 3 ranks their preferences: C≻A≻B. When these preferences are aggregated through pairwise majority votes, a startling cyclical contradiction emerges: A majority (Voters 1 and 3) prefers A to B. A majority (Voters 1 and 2) prefers B to C. Yet, a majority (Voters 2 and 3) prefers C to A. Even though every individual voter maintains completely rational, transitive preferences, the resulting collective group preference is non-transitive and circular (A≻B≻C≻A), meaning there is no clear majority winner.

C. In 1951, American economist and mathematician Kenneth J. Arrow generalized Condorcet’s specific paradox into a universal mathematical theorem in his landmark doctoral dissertation, Social Choice and Individual Values. Arrow sought to determine whether it was possible to design an idealized social welfare function—a formal aggregation rule that takes the ranked preferences of all individual voters and produces a single, complete, and transitive social ordering—that simultaneously satisfies a modest set of foundational, uncontroversial criteria of democratic fairness.

D. Arrow established five minimal, universally endorsed fairness conditions that any reasonable democratic aggregation system ought to satisfy: 1. Unrestricted Domain (Universality): The voting system must accommodate any conceivable set of rational individual preference rankings across all available alternatives. 2. Non-Dictatorship: There must be no single individual ("dictator") whose personal preferences automatically determine the collective social choice regardless of the preferences of all other voters. 3. Pareto Efficiency (Weak Unanimity): If every single individual voter strictly prefers candidate X over candidate Y, the collective social choice must also strictly prefer X over Y. 4. Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives (IIA): The collective social ranking between any two candidates, X and Y, should depend strictly on voters'' preferences between X and Y alone, remaining completely unaffected by the introduction, withdrawal, or ranking of a third, irrelevant candidate Z. 5. Transitivity (Rationality): The collective outcome must be logically consistent; if the group prefers X to Y and Y to Z, it must prefer X to Z.

E. Through a rigorous axiomatic mathematical proof using set theory and symbolic logic, Arrow demonstrated his devastating result: Arrow’s Impossibility Theorem (also known as the General Possibility Theorem). Arrow proved that whenever a voting system involves three or more distinct candidates and two or more voters, it is mathematically impossible to construct a ranked social choice function that satisfies all five conditions simultaneously. The only way to guarantee a transitive, consistent social outcome that avoids Condorcet cycles while maintaining Pareto efficiency and IIA is to allow the system to collapse into a dictatorship. Fair aggregation is not merely politically difficult; it is logically self-contradictory.

F. The core vulnerability that destabilizes real-world democratic voting systems is the violation of the Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives (IIA). In standard plurality voting systems (such as the "first-past-the-post" systems utilized in the United States and the United Kingdom), the entry of a third-party or minor "spoiler" candidate can dramatically alter the election outcome without having any chance of winning themselves. A classic historical illustration is the 2000 United States presidential election in the state of Florida: third-party candidate Ralph Nader secured nearly 100,000 votes, predominantly drawing support from left-leaning voters who overwhelmingly preferred Al Gore over George W. Bush. Nader''s presence on the ballot siphoned critical votes away from Gore, allowing Bush to win the state by a razor-thin margin of 537 votes and capture the presidency, despite the possibility that a majority of the electorate may have preferred Gore to Bush in a direct pairwise contest.

G. Social choice theorists and electoral reform advocates have proposed alternative voting methods to mitigate Arrow’s constraints, including Ranked Choice Voting (Instant-Runoff Voting), the Borda Count, and Approval Voting. However, secondary mathematical theorems— most notably the Gibbard-Satterthwaite Theorem of 1973—demonstrated that every deterministic voting system involving three or more candidates is inherently susceptible to strategic (tactical) voting. Under any non-dictatorial system, there will always be situations where a voter can achieve a preferred political outcome by misrepresenting their true preferences rather than voting sincerely.

H. Arrow’s Impossibility Theorem represents one of the most profound intellectual milestones of the twentieth century, carrying radical philosophical implications for political theory. It demolishes the simplistic populist belief that an election outcome reveals an undisputed, singular "will of the people." In any complex society with diverse preferences, the collective outcome is inextricably bound to the specific mathematical voting algorithm used to aggregate the ballots. Recognizing these mathematical limits does not mean abandoning democracy; rather, it demands that citizens and constitutional architects understand the trade-offs inherent in electoral design, ensuring that institutions are built with transparent awareness of their inevitable mathematical imperfections.'
);

