-- Passage 1: The Evolution of Alpine Skiing Technology
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'd74d10a5-30a4-5356-957b-337afea709e5', 'ef991073-b598-5685-b4a1-5cd70e2457f7', 1, 'The Evolution of Alpine Skiing Technology',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For thousands of years across the snowbound landscapes of Scandinavia, northern Russia, and Central Asia, skiing served strictly as an essential mode of winter transportation, hunting, and military mobility. Archaeological evidence—most notably the 9,000-year-old wooden ski fragments discovered near Lake Sindor in Russia and prehistoric rock carvings in Rødøy, Norway—reveals that ancient Nordic hunters utilized long, solid wooden planks strapped to soft leather boots to distribute their body weight across deep snowbanks. Throughout this prolonged utilitarian epoch, skiing was predominantly cross-country (Nordic), characterized by a free-heel binding system that allowed the user to glide forward across undulating terrain or propel themselves up steep gradients.

B. The foundational transformation of skiing from a subsistence transport method into a dynamic downhill mountain sport began during the mid-nineteenth century in the Telemark region of southern Norway. In the 1860s, a visionary Norwegian carpenter and skier named Sondre Norheim introduced two revolutionary equipment innovations: the sidecut ski and the willow-root heel binding. Traditional wooden skis had straight, parallel edges that required enormous physical exertion to pivot and frequently skidded unpredictably during high-speed descents. Norheim shaved the waist of the ski narrower than the shovel (tip) and tail, creating an hourglass geometry known as a parabolic sidecut. When an arched wooden ski is tilted on its edge, this curvature forces the ski to flex into a smooth arc, allowing skiers to execute clean, carving turns—the famous Telemark and Christiania turns—without excessive braking.

C. Norheim’s flexible willow-band binding wrapped around the heel of the leather boot, providing lateral stiffness while preserving vertical heel elevation. However, as skiing migrated south to the steep, icy vertical drops of the European Alps in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, the shortcomings of Nordic equipment became glaringly evident. Alpine descents demanded rigid torsional control and precise edge pressure to prevent catastrophic falls on sheer icy precipices. In the 1890s, Austrian mountaineer Mathias Zdarsky designed the first rigid steel binding that locked the boot to a metal plate, shortening skis from nearly three meters down to less than two meters to enhance downhill maneuverability.

D. The definitive bifurcation between Nordic and Alpine skiing was finalized in the 1930s with the invention of the Kandahar cable binding and the integration of segmented steel edges. Invented in 1928 by Austrian racer Rudolf Lettner, segmented steel edges screwed directly into the wooden ski base allowed skiers to bite into rock-hard ice and hard-packed glacial snow without dulling the soft wood. By the 1930s, Sir Arnold Lunn and Hannes Schneider had popularized the Arlberg technique, demanding bindings that clamped the skier’s heel rigidly to the ski to ensure direct kinetic energy transfer from the lower legs directly to the metal edges.

E. While rigid downhill clamping provided unprecedented speed and control, it introduced a severe medical epidemic: catastrophic lower-limb orthopedic injuries. When a skier fell while moving at high speed, the rigid binding acted as a long lever, transferring severe rotational torque directly to the skier''s tibia and fibula. Spiral leg fractures became notoriously commonplace in alpine resorts. The solution arrived in 1939 with Norwegian engineer Bjarne Tøien’s early release mechanisms, followed in 1950 by the landmark invention of the Look Nevada toe piece by French engineer Jean Beyl, and Hjalmar Hvam’s Saf-Ski binding. Modern safety bindings incorporated spring-loaded release mechanisms calibrated to disengage the boot automatically whenever sudden rotational torque or forward impact exceeded safe biological thresholds.

F. The materials revolution of the post-World War II era fundamentally redefined ski fabrication, transitioning manufacturing from solid organic timber to multi-layered composite laminates. In 1950, American aerospace engineer Howard Head introduced the "Head Standard," the first commercially successful composite ski. Head constructed a sandwich structure featuring a lightweight plywood or balsa wood core enclosed between high-tensile aircraft-grade aluminum alloy sheets, bonded with plastic adhesives and edged with continuous steel strips. The Head Standard dampened vibrations, resisted warping from moisture, and turned effortlessly, democratizing alpine skiing for millions of recreational enthusiasts worldwide. Subsequent material breakthroughs during the 1960s and 1970s replaced aluminum with high- modulus fiberglass laminates, carbon fibers, and ultra-high-molecular-weight polyethylene (P- Tex) sintered bases, producing skis with extraordinary torsional rigidity, dampening stability, and gliding velocity.

G. In the early 1990s, the ski industry underwent a second geometric renaissance with the widespread commercial introduction of the "super-sidecut" or shaped carving ski, pioneered by Slovenian manufacturer Elan with their SCX model. By radically exaggerating the parabolic sidecut and shortening overall ski length, shaped skis allowed even novice skiers to execute clean, high-angle carved turns at modest speeds without skidding the tails. Today, contemporary ski engineering represents a sophisticated intersection of computational finite element modeling, carbon nanotube materials, and piezoelectric vibration-dampening electronics. From primitive Nordic hunting planks to aerospace-engineered composite carving blades, the evolution of ski technology highlights the dynamic interplay between human athletic ambition and advanced materials engineering.'
);

