-- Passage 2: Circular Economy Models in Lithium-Ion Battery Recycling
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '8468433d-ef46-5694-a39f-618cbc96f84a', 'ef991073-b598-5685-b4a1-5cd70e2457f7', 2, 'Circular Economy Models in Lithium-Ion Battery Recycling',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The global transition toward clean energy and sustainable transportation has triggered an unprecedented surge in the production and adoption of electric vehicles (EVs) and stationary grid-scale energy storage systems. Central to this technological revolution is the lithium-ion battery (LIB), an electrochemical device prized for its high energy density, long cycle life, and rapid discharge capabilities. However, this global electrification drive has generated a massive looming waste management crisis. An estimated eleven million metric tons of spent lithium-ion battery packs are projected to reach the end of their operational lifespans by 2030. Disposing of spent batteries in municipal landfills is an environmental catastrophe: damaged cells pose severe thermal runaway and fire hazards, while toxic electrolytes and heavy metals can leach into regional soil and groundwater aquifers.

B. Beyond environmental contamination risks, the rapid depletion of primary mineral resources underscores the urgent economic necessity of closed-loop recycling. The manufacturing of lithium-ion battery cathode chemistries relies heavily on critical raw minerals—predominantly battery-grade lithium (Li), cobalt (Co), nickel (Ni), and manganese (Mn). The mining and refining of these virgin minerals are plagued by geopolitical vulnerabilities, concentrated supply chains, acute water depletion in arid regions, and severe human rights violations in artisanal mining sectors. Establishing robust circular economy frameworks—where critical cathode minerals are recovered from spent battery cells at purity levels exceeding 99.5 percent and directly reintegrated into new cell manufacturing—is essential to mitigate supply volatility and achieve industrial sustainability.

C. The industrial recycling of lithium-ion batteries begins with mechanical pre-treatment. Spent battery modules are systematically discharged to eliminate residual electrical energy, deactivated in inert gas environments or aqueous salt baths to prevent combustion, and fed into high-torque mechanical shredders. The shredded material is subsequently processed through a sequence of magnetic separation, density classification, and air-elutriation steps. This physical separation yields distinct fractions: copper and aluminum foil fragments, plastic polymer casings, and a fine, dark powder known to industrial recyclers as "black mass." The black mass contains the concentrated value of the battery: the active cathode metal oxides (Li, Co, Ni, Mn) mixed with anode graphite particles.

D. Recovering pure, battery-grade metal salts from black mass is currently achieved through two primary chemical engineering pathways: pyrometallurgy and hydrometallurgy. Pyrometallurgy involves feeding spent cells or black mass into high-temperature smelting blast furnaces operating between 1,200°C and 1,500°C. In the furnace, organic binders, separators, and graphite burn off as thermal fuel, while the metals reduce into a molten metallic alloy (matte) containing cobalt, nickel, and copper. While pyrometallurgical smelting is technologically mature and can process heterogeneous battery chemistries without extensive disassembly, it suffers from severe ecological drawbacks: it releases substantial greenhouse gases, consumes vast quantities of thermal energy, and completely loses lithium, aluminum, and graphite into an unrecoverable slag phase.

E. In contrast, hydrometallurgical processing operates at substantially lower temperatures (typically below 100°C) and utilizes aqueous chemical leaching to dissolve metals from black mass into solution. Mineral acids—such as sulfuric acid (H2SO4) paired with hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) as a reducing agent—are injected into the slurry to achieve metal dissolution rates exceeding 98 percent. The resulting multi-metallic leachate solution then undergoes a sequence of pH-adjusted solvent extraction, chemical precipitation, and crystallization steps to sequentially separate and recover individual metal salts, such as lithium carbonate (Li2CO3), cobalt sulfate (CoSO4), and nickel sulfate (NiSO4). Hydrometallurgy boasts significantly lower carbon emissions, lower energy consumption, and high recovery rates for all critical metals, including lithium.

F. To further optimize circular efficiency, chemical engineers are developing "direct cathode recycling." Unlike pyrometallurgy and hydrometallurgy, which break cathode chemistries all the way down into their constituent elemental precursors, direct recycling aims to recover and restore the active cathode material crystal lattice intact. By separating the intact cathode particles from the aluminum foil using mild solvents, followed by hydro-thermal re-lithiation to replenish lost lithium ions, direct recycling rejuvenates degraded cathode structures without requiring energy-intensive smelting or aggressive chemical dissolving. This closed-loop approach drastically slashes chemical reagent consumption, cuts greenhouse gas emissions by up to 70 percent, and significantly reduces recycling operating expenses.

G. Realizing a global circular economy for battery manufacturing requires harmonizing technological innovation with international regulatory frameworks. The European Union’s landmark 2023 Battery Regulation has established mandatory minimum recycled content targets for new batteries (specifying rising quotas for recovered cobalt, lead, lithium, and nickel) and mandated digital "battery passports" to track material provenance across global supply chains. By holding manufacturers legally accountable for the entire lifecycle of their products through Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) mandates, international governance can accelerate the transition from a linear "extract-use-dispose" paradigm to a resilient, closed-loop industrial ecosystem.'
);

