-- Passage 3: Radical Constructivism and the Epistemology of Scientific Models
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '70b20611-fcf8-5511-a1df-797b085de6e9', 'ef991073-b598-5685-b4a1-5cd70e2457f7', 3, 'Radical Constructivism and the Epistemology of Scientific Models',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. For centuries, orthodox Western epistemology and mainstream scientific practice have operated under the unexamined philosophical framework of scientific realism. Scientific realism rests upon a straightforward metaphysical premise: there exists an objective, mind-independent external reality that possesses definite structures and properties, and the primary objective of scientific inquiry is to discover, map, and describe this reality with increasing accuracy. Under this classical correspondence theory of truth, a scientific model or empirical law is deemed "true" if and only if its theoretical constructs correspond directly to the real, ontological entities and causal mechanisms operating in the external universe. Scientific progress is conceptualized as a cumulative, asymptotic convergence toward an objective, photographic representation of the cosmos.

B. In the late twentieth century, however, this classical realist consensus was radically upended by the emergence of Radical Constructivism, an epistemological paradigm pioneered by philosopher, cybernetician, and cognitive scientist Ernst von Glasersfeld. Drawing upon the developmental psychology of Jean Piaget, the cybernetic feedback loops of Heinz von Foerster, and the skeptical philosophy of the ancient Pyrrhonists and Immanuel Kant, von Glasersfeld advanced a revolutionary proposition: human knowledge does not, and logically cannot, represent an objective external reality "as it is in itself." Instead, knowledge is an active cognitive construction created by human observers to organize, coordinate, and make sense of their ongoing experiential world.

C. To clarify this radical departure from traditional realism, von Glasersfeld formulated two fundamental epistemological principles: 1. Active Construction: Knowledge is not passively received through sensory transmission or mechanical recording; rather, knowledge is actively built up by the cognizing subject through experiential interactions and cognitive reflection. 2. Adaptive Viability: The function of cognition is adaptive, serving the organization of the subject''s experiential world, not the discovery of ontological reality.

D. The cornerstone of radical constructivist epistemology is the crucial distinction between "truth" (ontological correspondence) and "viability" (functional fit). To illustrate this distinction, von Glasersfeld utilized the metaphor of a key and a lock. A burglar or locksmith possesses a key that successfully opens a door lock. Does the key "match" the lock in the sense of being an exact physical mirror of the lock’s internal chamber? No; the key merely needs to fit the lock’s internal obstacles well enough to turn the bolt. There may be dozens of radically different key shapes that can successfully open the exact same lock. Similarly, when a scientific model (such as Newtonian mechanics or Einsteinian relativity) successfully forecasts empirical observations, it does not prove that the model reflects the actual ontological fabric of the universe; it merely proves that the model is "viable"—that it functions successfully within the specific constraints of human experience without hitting an experiential obstacle.

E. Radical constructivists point to the history of scientific revolutions to demonstrate that multiple, mutually incompatible theoretical models can be equally viable within specific observational domains. For instance, in classical celestial mechanics, the Ptolemaic geocentric model (with its complex geometric system of deferents and epicycles) predicted planetary positions and eclipses with remarkable mathematical accuracy for over fourteen centuries. When the Copernican heliocentric model superseded Ptolemy, it did not replace a "false" mirror of reality with a "true" one; it introduced an alternative conceptual framework that was more mathematically economical and viable across a broader range of celestial and terrestrial observations. Science does not uncover absolute reality; it expands the repertoire of viable conceptual instruments.

F. Despite its explanatory power and alignment with quantum mechanics and cognitive cybernetics, Radical Constructivism has provoked fierce condemnation from orthodox scientific realists, analytical philosophers, and evolutionary naturalists, such as Michael Devitt and John Searle. Critics argue that constructivism collapses into solipsism—the absurd philosophical position that only the individual self exists and that reality is an arbitrary fantasy invented by the mind. Furthermore, realists contend that constructivism reduces science to mere instrumentalism or relativism, robbing scientific theories of their objective authority and opening the door to anti- scientific skepticism: if all models are merely viable constructions, how can society defend scientific truths (such as climate change or vaccine efficacy) against politically motivated pseudoscientific alternatives?

G. Von Glasersfeld and contemporary constructivists vigorously reject the charge of solipsism by emphasizing the concept of "experiential constraint." Radical Constructivism does not assert that human beings can construct reality arbitrarily or that anything goes. While we cannot know the ontological world directly, we constantly collide with its non-negotiable boundaries whenever our conceptual models fail. If an individual attempts to walk through a solid concrete wall, their conceptual model will instantly encounter an insurmountable experiential obstacle (a collision). Reality manifests not by revealing what it is, but by telling us what our models cannot do. Viable scientific theories survive because they successfully navigate the narrow channels of experiential possibility without crashing into physical barriers.

H. In synthesizing the philosophy of scientific modeling, Radical Constructivism provides an empowering, human-centered vision of science. Science is not a cold, detached transcription of an alien universe, but an extraordinary creative enterprise—a living dialogue between human imagination, empirical experimentation, and operational constraints. By recognizing that our scientific models are tools for living rather than photographs of ultimate reality, we liberate science from dogmatic absolutism, fostering an open, self-reflective epistemological culture capable of continuously adapting to the complex challenges of an evolving world.'
);

