-- Passage 1: The Agricultural Engineering of Ancient Incan Andenes (Terraces)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '659b620c-70d5-5f89-a080-affe9581c1b8', '0ac18286-0afa-55aa-980c-850bbc194fb3', 1, 'The Agricultural Engineering of Ancient Incan Andenes (Terraces)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The rugged topography of the central Andes of South America presents one of the most formidable and ecologically challenging agricultural environments on Earth. Spanning vast vertical gradients from fertile valley bottoms to frost-swept alpine ridges exceeding four thousand meters above sea level, the Andean landscape is characterized by thin, rocky soils, precipitous slopes often exceeding forty-five degrees, extreme diurnal temperature fluctuations, and severe seasonal precipitation deficits. Yet, between the early fifteenth and mid-sixteenth centuries, the Inca Empire (Tawantinsuyu) established a thriving, populous civilization across modern-day Peru, Bolivia, Ecuador, and northern Chile. The cornerstone of imperial Incan agronomic abundance was the construction of vast, hillside-transforming agricultural terracing complexes known locally as andenes.

B. Far from being simple steps carved into dirt hillsides, Incan andenes were sophisticated, multi-layered civil engineering structures engineered to optimize soil hydrology, thermal dynamics, and slope stability. The architectural construction of an andene began with the excavation of the natural hillside to establish a stable, level bedrock foundation. Behind a massive, slightly battered retaining wall—typically constructed from dressed granite, porphyry, or basalt fieldstones fitted together without mortar—Incan builders assembled a subterranean filtration and drainage bed consisting of three distinct stratigraphical layers: 1. Deep Sub-Base: A foundational layer of large, angular stone boulders and river gravel placed directly behind the retaining wall to provide structural stability and allow excess rainwater to drain away freely without building up hydrostatic pressure. 2. Intermediate Filter Bed: A middle layer of clean, coarse sand and medium-sized gravel that acted as a physical filter, preventing fine topsoil particles from washing down and clogging the lower drainage voids. 3. Enriched Topsoil Layer: An upper stratum (measuring between one and two meters in depth) composed of rich, fertile loam meticulously imported from fertile river floodplains, enriched with organic amendments such as llama manure, decomposed leaf litter, and nitrogen-rich sea bird guano transported hundreds of kilometers from the Pacific coast.

C. This multi-layered stratigraphic design resolved the primary structural hazard of steep mountain agriculture: catastrophic slope failure and soil erosion. During intense torrential downpours characteristic of the Andean rainy season, heavy surface water easily penetrated the permeable topsoil and percolated through the sand and gravel layers, escaping through weep holes engineered into the base of the stone retaining walls. By eliminating the accumulation of trapped water behind the stone facade, the andenes neutralized the hydrostatic forces that would otherwise trigger fatal landslides and destructive mudslides.

D. In addition to hydrological stabilization, andenes served as extraordinary passive thermal regulators that buffered crops against the mortal hazard of nocturnal high-altitude frost. In the thin atmosphere of the high Andes, solar radiation is intense during the day, but rapid radiational cooling causes ambient air temperatures to plunge below freezing at night. The massive stone retaining walls of the andenes, often measuring one to two meters in thickness and reaching heights of two to four meters, possessed immense thermal mass. Throughout the day, the dark stones absorbed and stored solar thermal energy; during the freezing night, the walls radiated this stored heat into the adjacent soil and air layers, creating a microclimatic buffer that elevated local temperatures by two to five degrees Celsius compared to surrounding slopes, successfully protecting delicate crops from frostbite.

E. Water distribution across these vertical staircases was regulated by monumental hydraulic canal networks. Sourcing water from glacial meltwater lakes, high-altitude springs, and canalized rivers, Incan hydrologists engineered stone-lined aqueducts that contoured along mountain ridges for tens of kilometers. Water flowed into the upper terrace tiers and descended through the agricultural complex via an intricate system of stone drop chutes, carved distribution sluices, and overflow channels. Individual terrace beds were flooded sequentially through adjustable stone gates, allowing farmers to deliver calibrated volumes of moisture directly to crop root zones without eroding the soil surface.

F. The deliberate exploitation of vertical ecological zones (vertical archipelago or ecological verticality) allowed Incan agronomists to cultivate an astonishing diversity of cultigens within a single river valley. At lower, warmer elevations (2,000 to 2,800 meters), andenes were dedicated to maize (Zea mays), the sacred imperial grain utilized for civic feasts and state-administered chicha beer brewing. At intermediate altitudes (2,800 to 3,500 meters), terraces produced high- protein grains like quinoa (Chenopodium quinoa) and amaranth, alongside legumes and squashes. At high alpine tiers (3,500 to 4,200 meters), specialized terraces supported thousands of cold-tolerant potato (Solanum tuberosum) varieties and indigenous Andean tubers such as oca, ulluco, and mashwa, which were subsequently freeze-dried into durable chuño rations for imperial state granaries (qollqas).

G. Following the Spanish conquest in the 1530s, the catastrophic demographic collapse of indigenous populations due to Old World epidemics, combined with forced labor relocations into colonial silver mines, caused the abandonment of up to 60 to 75 percent of the Incan terracing infrastructure. Without regular maintenance of stone retaining walls and drainage channels, unchecked rainwater breached terrace walls, triggering widespread erosion and soil loss across the Andes. In the 21st century, however, regional non-governmental organizations, indigenous agrarian cooperatives, and climate resilience initiatives are rehabilitating thousands of hectares of abandoned andenes. By combining ancient stone hydrology with modern agroecology, these restored terraces provide climate-resilient water security, reduce vulnerability to extreme weather, and restore food sovereignty across Andean communities.'
);

