-- Passage 3: Semantic Internalism versus Externalism in the Philosophy of Language
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '673bbfc8-55fd-5804-a0ad-f1f6608e44c2', '0ac18286-0afa-55aa-980c-850bbc194fb3', 3, 'Semantic Internalism versus Externalism in the Philosophy of Language',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the foundational philosophy of mind and language, one of the most vigorously contested battlegrounds concerns the fundamental nature of linguistic meaning and mental content. When an individual utters a meaningful word (such as "water," "gold," or "tiger") or holds an intentional mental thought (such as the belief that "water is wet"), where exactly does that semantic meaning reside? Is the meaning of a linguistic term completely determined by the internal cognitive and neurobiological states residing within the individual speaker’s skull? Or is meaning constitutively dependent upon the external physical environment and social linguistic community in which the speaker is situated? This profound ontological divide is formalized as the debate between Semantic Internalism and Semantic Externalism.

B. For centuries of Western philosophy, the internalist paradigm held almost undisputed dominance. Tracing its lineage through René Descartes, John Locke, Gottlob Frege, and early twentieth-century mentalists, Semantic Internalism (often associated with the doctrine of psychological individualism or Cartesian mentalism) rested upon the intuitive premise that psychological states supervene strictly on the local, internal physical properties of the individual. Under this classical internalist view, meanings are internal mental representations: if two human individuals are identical "atom-for-atom" (microscopic physical duplicates who share identical neurological brain states), their words and thoughts must possess identical semantic meanings and truth conditions, regardless of any differences in their external physical or social surroundings.

C. In 1975, however, American philosopher Hilary Putnam delivered a revolutionary blow to the internalist consensus in his landmark paper, The Meaning of ''Meaning''. Putnam advanced a radical thesis: linguistic meaning is not an internal psychological state. Putnam crystallized his argument through his famous and enduring thought experiment: the "Twin Earth" scenario. Putnam asked his readers to imagine a distant planet, Twin Earth, which is identical in almost every respect to Earth, containing exact physical and psychological duplicates of every human inhabitant on Earth.

D. On Earth, the clear, drinkable liquid that fills rivers and lakes and satisfies thirst is composed of the chemical compound $H_2O$. On Twin Earth, there is an identical-looking, identical- tasting, and identically behaving clear liquid that fills rivers and satisfies thirst, but which possesses a completely different, complex chemical composition, abbreviated symbolically as $XYZ$. Now consider Oscar on Earth and his exact physical duplicate, Twin Oscar, living on Twin Earth in the year 1750—before the historical emergence of modern chemical analysis and atomic theory. In 1750, neither Oscar nor Twin Oscar possesses any scientific knowledge of chemistry; their internal mental states, neurological firings, and subjective sensory impressions when interacting with their respective liquids are completely indistinguishable.

E. Yet, when Oscar on Earth utters the word "water," his word refers exclusively to the chemical substance $H_2O$; it does not refer to $XYZ$. Conversely, when Twin Oscar on Twin Earth utters the word "water," his word refers exclusively to $XYZ$; it does not refer to $H_2O$. If Oscar were magically transported to Twin Earth, he would be mistaken if he declared that the liquid in Twin Earth’s lakes was water. Because Oscar and Twin Oscar possess identical internal neurological and psychological states, yet their words and thoughts refer to two entirely different physical substances, Putnam delivered his celebrated philosophical dictum: "''Meanings'' just ain’t in the head!" Semantic reference is determined not by internal mental imagery, but by the causal relationships connecting the speaker to the physical nature of the external environment—a position known as Physical Externalism.

F. Four years later, in 1979, philosopher Tyler Burge expanded externalism from the physical environment to the social linguistic environment, formulating Social Externalism. Burge presented the thought experiment of an English speaker named Bert who visits his doctor complaining of pain in his thigh, stating, "I believe I have arthritis in my thigh." Bert holds a minor linguistic misconception: he does not realize that medical science defines "arthritis" strictly as an inflammation of the joints, not the muscles or thigh bones. The doctor corrects him, explaining that arthritis cannot occur in the thigh. Now imagine a counterfactual social scenario where Bert possesses the exact same internal neurological brain state, but lives in a linguistic community where the word "thritis" has been standardly defined by physicians to include rheumatoid muscle pains as well as joint inflammation. In this counterfactual society, Bert''s thought would be true. Because Bert''s internal brain state is held constant while his mental content changes based entirely on the social linguistic conventions of his community, Burge proved that social linguistic conventions directly constitute mental content.

G. In response to these externalist arguments, contemporary defenders of internalism, such as Jerry Fodor, Gabriel Segal, and David Chalmers, have developed sophisticated theoretical modifications, most notably Two-Dimensional Semantics. Internalists argue that externalism conflates two distinct dimensions of meaning: "wide content" and "narrow content." Wide content corresponds to externalist reference—the real-world extension ($H_2O$ or $XYZ$) determined by the physical world. Narrow content, by contrast, is the purely internal, cognitive aspect of meaning that is completely shared between Oscar and Twin Oscar—the functional mental concept that "the clear, drinkable liquid in rivers satisfies thirst." Internalists maintain that narrow content is what truly drives psychological behavior: both Oscar and Twin Oscar drink the liquid when thirsty because of their identical narrow mental states, regardless of what chemical formula is discovered centuries later by external scientists.

H. In contemporary cognitive philosophy, the debate between internalism and externalism has expanded beyond pure semantics into the "Extended Mind" thesis and cognitive neuro- technologies. Philosophers Andy Clark and David Chalmers argue that if external physical environments constitute semantic mental content, then external memory storage devices—such as notebooks, smartphones, and neural interfaces—should be viewed as physical extensions of the cognitive mind itself. Whether meaning is conceptualized as an internal biological computation or as an open, relational link to the surrounding physical and social cosmos, the debate over mental content illuminates the profound interfaces between human consciousness, language, and the world we inhabit.'
);

