-- Passage 1: The Discovery and Biological Role of the Coelacanth
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '715dbe98-ef64-5658-bbc9-d631feaa78ba', 'e352d3a1-4525-5ac2-82c6-feff0129ecb6', 1, 'The Discovery and Biological Role of the Coelacanth',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the annals of evolutionary biology and marine paleontology, few events have generated as profound a scientific shockwave as the unexpected re-emergence of the coelacanth (Latimeria chalumnae). Throughout the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, the global paleontological consensus held that coelacanths—an ancient order of lobe-finned sarcopterygian fish that first appeared in the fossil record during the early Devonian period, approximately 410 million years ago—had vanished entirely from the planet. Because exhaustive fossil surveys found no trace of coelacanth skeletal remains in geological strata younger than the late Cretaceous period, scientists concluded that the entire lineage had perished in the same end-Cretaceous mass extinction event that eliminated the non-avian dinosaurs sixty-six million years ago.

B. This absolute paleontological certainty was overturned on December 22, 1938, in the port city of East London, South Africa. Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer, the thirty-one-year-old curator of the modest East London Museum, received a routine telephone call from Captain Hendrik Goosen, master of the commercial trawler Nerine. Captain Goosen invited Courtenay-Latimer to inspect a pile of discarded sharks and marine bycatch hauled from a depth of approximately seventy meters near the mouth of the Chalumna River. Sifting through the marine debris on the vessel’s deck, Courtenay-Latimer uncovered an extraordinary creature measuring 1.5 meters in length and weighing over fifty kilograms: a heavy, iridescent steel-blue fish adorned with faint silver-white flecks, thick armor-like cosmoid scales, a distinct trilobed tail, and bizarre, fleshy, paddle-shaped paired fins resembling the limbs of a land animal.

C. Recognizing that the specimen matched no known living teleost fish, Courtenay-Latimer made detailed sketches and dispatched urgent telegrams to Dr. J.L.B. Smith, a prominent ichthyologist at Rhodes University in Grahamstown. Upon examining the preserved skin and mounted specimen several weeks later, Smith was thunderstruck, instantly identifying the creature as a living member of the Actinistia (coelacanths)—a discovery he famously described as akin to discovering a living dinosaur roaming a modern forest. Smith christened the species Latimeria chalumnae in honor of Courtenay-Latimer and the river of its capture, initiating an international scientific hunt that spanned fourteen years before a second living population was finally located in December 1952 around the volcanic Comoro Islands in the western Indian Ocean.

D. The anatomical architecture of Latimeria chalumnae provides a breathtaking window into the deep evolutionary transitions of vertebrate morphology. Unlike modern ray-finned bony fishes (Actinopterygii), whose fins are supported by delicate, fan-like bony spines, the paired pectoral and pelvic fins of the coelacanth are supported by internal bony structural elements and complex muscular systems that are anatomically homologous to the limb bones of early four- legged terrestrial tetrapods (the humerus, radius, and ulna of the forelimb, and the femur, tibia, and fibula of the hindlimb). When swimming, the coelacanth moves its fleshy paired fins in an alternating, diagonal sequence (left pectoral with right pelvic, followed by right pectoral with left pelvic)—a coordinated locomotor gait identical to the walking pattern of four-legged amphibians and reptiles.

E. Furthermore, the internal physiology of the coelacanth exhibits a suite of primitive and unique evolutionary specializations. Its central braincase features an intracranial joint—a hinged, flexible joint dividing the anterior and posterior sections of the cranium, allowing the fish to elevate its upper jaw and expand its mouth cavity to engulf large prey. Instead of a fully ossified vertebral column, the main axial structural support is provided by an oil-filled, unsegmented notochord measuring several centimeters in diameter. Located within the snout is an intricate, fluid-filled sensory organ known as the rostral organ, which functions as a sensitive electroreceptive array capable of detecting the faint micro-electrical fields generated by hidden benthic prey in absolute darkness. Internally, the fish possesses a fat-filled, vestigial lung—a degenerate organ inherited from ancient, shallow-water Devonian ancestors that utilized functional lungs to breathe atmospheric air during seasonal droughts.

F. Ecological and behavioral surveys conducted via specialized submersibles (such as the Jago) around the Comoros and South Africa''s Sodwana Bay have revealed that coelacanths inhabit a narrow, specialized marine niche. They dwell along steep, submarine volcanic cliffs and rocky drop-offs at depths ranging between 100 and 400 meters, where ambient water temperatures remain cold (between 15°C and 20°C). During daylight hours, groups of up to fourteen individuals congregate passively inside underwater lava caves and cavern overhangs to escape warm surface currents and conserve metabolic energy. At night, they disperse across the rocky substrate as solitary drift-feeders, performing passive head-down vertical drift postures while their rostral organs scan the seabed for nocturnal fish, squids, and octopuses.

G. In 1997, the coelacanth story yielded a second biological shock when marine biologists Mark and Arnaz Erdmann discovered a distinct sister species, Latimeria menadoensis (the Indonesian coelacanth), thriving nearly ten thousand kilometers to the east in the deep volcanic waters of North Sulawesi, Indonesia. Today, both species are classified as critically endangered by the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN). Their specialized cave habitats, low metabolic rates, extraordinary longevity (estimated to exceed one hundred years), and extremely slow reproductive biology—bearing live young after a gestation period lasting nearly five years, the longest known among vertebrates—render them acutely vulnerable to incidental bycatch in deep-set gillnets and commercial shark longlines. As iconic living fossils, coelacanths continue to inspire marine conservation efforts, serving as enduring testaments to the hidden evolutionary wonders of the deep ocean.'
);

