-- Passage 2: Thermal Energy Storage in Concentrated Solar Power (CSP) Systems
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '7c0f37a6-3195-51c9-8df0-60c399382594', 'e352d3a1-4525-5ac2-82c6-feff0129ecb6', 2, 'Thermal Energy Storage in Concentrated Solar Power (CSP) Systems',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. As global power systems accelerate the integration of clean electricity to meet international carbon reduction commitments, the inherent intermittency of renewable energy remains a primary engineering bottleneck. While solar photovoltaic (PV) panels and wind turbines have experienced steep capital cost declines, they generate electricity strictly when the sun shines or the wind blows. Because modern electrical grids demand constant, predictable power delivery to match consumer demand across evening peaks and cloudy days, utility operators have historically relied on fossil-fueled natural gas turbines to balance variable renewables. To provide true zero-emission, dispatchable baseload electricity around the clock, energy engineers are increasingly combining Concentrated Solar Power (CSP) with utility-scale Thermal Energy Storage (TES).

B. Concentrated Solar Power systems operate by capturing direct normal irradiance (DNI) from sunlight using vast optical fields of sun-tracking mirrors, known as heliostats or parabolic troughs. In a solar power tower configuration, thousands of dual-axis tracking heliostats focus solar thermal radiation onto a centralized thermal receiver mounted atop a towering central tower, concentrating solar flux by factors of up to one thousand. Inside the receiver, a circulating heat-transfer fluid absorbs the intense concentrated heat, reaching temperatures exceeding 560°C. While this thermal energy can be routed directly to a heat exchanger to produce high- pressure superheated steam that drives a conventional Rankine-cycle steam turbine generator, the true technological advantage of CSP lies in its ability to divert and store that thermal energy before electrical generation takes place.

C. The undisputed industry standard for utility-scale CSP storage is the two-tank indirect or direct molten-salt storage architecture. Molten salt is an exceptional thermal storage medium due to its high volumetric heat capacity, excellent thermal stability at elevated temperatures (operating safely up to 600°C without decomposing), non-flammability, low chemical toxicity, and low vapor pressure. The most common commercial formulation is "solar salt," a non-eutectic binary mixture consisting of 60 percent sodium nitrate ($NaNO_3$) and 40 percent potassium nitrate ($KNO_3$). Solar salt melts into a clear, water-like liquid at approximately 220°C to 240°C, remaining liquid throughout its entire operating thermal cycle.

D. The operational cycle of a two-tank molten-salt TES facility functions through continuous fluid transfer between two massive, heavily insulated storage tanks: 1. Charging Cycle (Daytime): Cold molten salt at approximately 290°C is pumped from the "cold tank" through the central solar receiver (or an intermediate heat exchanger), where concentrated sunlight heats the liquid to 565°C. The superheated salt is then transferred into the "hot tank," where it is stored with minimal thermal loss (typically losing less than 1°C per day through thick mineral wool and refractory ceramic insulation). 2. Discharging Cycle (Evening/Night): When electrical demand surges after sunset or during cloudy periods, the 565°C molten salt is drawn from the hot tank and pumped through a steam generator. The salt transfers its thermal energy to pressurized water, generating superheated steam at 540°C and 100 bar to drive the steam turbine and generate electricity. The cooled salt (now at 290°C) returns to the cold tank to repeat the cycle.

E. Despite its thermodynamic efficiency, operating molten-salt systems at extreme temperatures presents severe chemical and material challenges. The primary operational hazard is solidification (freezing). If the temperature of the salt anywhere within the piping, pumps, or valves drops below its 220°C freezing threshold, the salt solidifies into rock-hard mineral crystal blocks, causing catastrophic pipe blockages, mechanical warping, and pump failures. To prevent freezing during plant shutdowns or extended winter cloudy spells, all external pipelines, valves, and heat exchangers are fitted with active electrical impedance heating or mineral-insulated trace heating systems. Furthermore, at temperatures exceeding 550°C, molten nitrate salts become highly corrosive, requiring storage tanks and high-temperature pipework to be fabricated from expensive high-nickel austenitic stainless steel alloys (such as Inconel 625 or 316H stainless steel).

F. To reduce the high capital expenditure (CapEx) associated with constructing two separate massive steel storage vessels, chemical engineers are developing single-tank thermocline storage systems. In a thermocline tank, both hot and cold molten salt are stored within a single structural vessel, separated naturally by a dynamic thermal gradient layer (the thermocline) created by natural buoyancy differences: lower-density hot salt floats at the top of the tank, while higher- density cold salt remains pooled at the bottom. To further reduce costs, up to 70 to 80 percent of the expensive molten salt in the tank is replaced by an inexpensive solid packed bed of porous quartzite rock and silica sand filler materials, slashing overall storage capital costs by up to 35 percent.

G. As global clean energy grids demand multi-hour and multi-day energy storage capabilities that surpass the economic limits of lithium-ion batteries, Concentrated Solar Power with thermal storage is emerging as a cornerstone of clean grid stability. Modern commercial CSP facilities— such as the Noor Energy 1 complex in Dubai and the Cerro Dominador plant in Chile''s Atacama Desert—provide between twelve and fifteen hours of continuous, full-load thermal storage, delivering reliable, carbon-free electricity 24 hours a day. By converting volatile solar radiation into reliable, stored thermal energy, CSP systems prove that a 100 percent renewable power grid can achieve uncompromising baseload reliability.'
);

