-- Passage 1: The Construction and Operation of the Eddystone Lighthouses
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '258ad344-818b-5713-8bcc-47d7e6995874', 'c1df761c-c7b9-57a0-9a80-8fef96800d97', 1, 'The Construction and Operation of the Eddystone Lighthouses',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Situated approximately fourteen kilometers south-southwest of Rame Head off the rocky coast of Cornwall, England, the Eddystone Rocks have historically represented one of the most perilous maritime hazards in the English Channel. Composed of an isolated, jagged reef of submerged Precambrian gneiss, this treacherous formation lies directly in the primary navigation approach for merchant sailing vessels and naval warships entering the major naval anchorage of Plymouth Sound. For centuries, the combination of strong Atlantic tidal currents, prevailing southwesterly gales, and the fact that the rocks are almost completely submerged at high water resulted in hundreds of catastrophic shipwrecks and the loss of thousands of mariners'' lives. The urgent necessity to erect a permanent warning beacon on this wave-swept outcrop spurred four successive generations of innovative civil engineering.

B. The first attempt to conquer the reef was undertaken in 1696 by Henry Winstanley, an eccentric English merchant, showman, and inventor. Winstanley’s structure, completed in 1698, was the first open-ocean rock lighthouse ever constructed in the world. Fabricated primarily from an ornate timber framework anchored into the granite rock using forged iron stanchions, the tower was lavishly decorated with ornamental balconies, open galleries, exterior scrollwork, and gilded weather vanes. While it survived its first winter, proving that an artificial structure could withstand open-ocean swells, maritime critics warned that its elaborate exterior projections created immense aerodynamic drag and caught the full kinetic impact of crashing storm waves. Winstanley remained supreme in his confidence, famously declaring that he wished to be inside his lighthouse during the greatest storm that ever blew. In November 1703, during the Great Storm—the most violent tempest in recorded British history—the lighthouse was obliterated and washed cleanly into the sea, claiming the lives of all occupants, including Winstanley himself.

C. In 1706, a London silk merchant named John Lovett acquired the lease to the Eddystone Rocks and commissioned John Rudyard, a Cornish-born shipwright and merchant, to design a second tower. Recognizing that Winstanley’s ornate exterior had caught the destructive force of the sea, Rudyard designed a smooth, conical wooden tower modeled directly on the aerodynamic hull of a sailing ship. The structure consisted of a solid central core composed of alternating horizontal courses of Cornish granite blocks and heavy oak timbers, bolted to the bedrock with iron split-pins, and entirely sheathed in smooth, caulked vertical wooden planks. Rudyard’s lighthouse successfully withstood the violent fury of the Atlantic for nearly half a century. However, its organic material composition proved its ultimate downfall: in December 1755, the lantern caught fire from the heat of the tallow candles used for illumination, burning downward through the dry timber frame until the tower was completely destroyed.

D. The destruction of Rudyard''s beacon set the stage for the defining breakthrough in modern coastal engineering, executed by the Yorkshire civil engineer John Smeaton. Appointed by the Royal Society and Trinity House, Smeaton resolved that the third Eddystone lighthouse must be constructed entirely of solid stone masonry. Smeaton sought inspiration in the morphology of the natural world, explicitly modeling the tapering profile of his tower on the trunk of an English broad-leaved oak tree (Quercus robur), which spreads outward at its roots to transfer wind load smoothly into the earth.

E. Smeaton introduced two revolutionary technological innovations that permanently transformed structural engineering: 1. Dovetail Interlocking Masonry: Rather than relying on simple rectangular blocks that could be dislodged by the lateral shear forces of crashing waves, Smeaton designed individual granite blocks carved with intricate, interlocking dovetail joints. Each stone locked mechanically into its adjacent neighbors on all four horizontal and vertical faces, forming a monolithic, unbreakable stone matrix. 2. Hydraulic Lime Mortar: Standard lime mortar dissolved when exposed to seawater before setting. Smeaton conducted pioneering geochemical experiments, discovering that burning limestone containing a high proportion of reactive clay (argillaceous limestone from Aberthaw in Wales) produced a hydraulic lime that set and hardened rapidly underwater, resisting the chemical wash of the sea.

F. Completed in 1759, Smeaton''s granite tower stood as a triumph of Enlightenment engineering, guiding mariners safely for over a century. However, by the late 1870s, inspections revealed that while Smeaton’s masonry tower remained structurally sound, the continuous hydraulic pounding of Atlantic waves had undermined the very gneiss bedrock foundation beneath the lighthouse, causing the surrounding rock ledge to crack and vibrate alarmingly during heavy seas.

G. Consequently, Trinity House commissioned civil engineer Sir James Douglass to construct the fourth and present Eddystone lighthouse on a sturdier, adjacent reef platform 120 meters away. Douglass incorporated Smeaton''s dovetail interlocking masonry principles but added a massive cylindrical stone base to absorb wave energy before it reached the main tower, while utilizing modern Portland cement and powerful incandescent oil-gas burners. Smeaton’s historic upper tower was carefully dismantled stone by stone and re-erected on Plymouth Hoe as a permanent public monument to Smeaton’s genius. Automated in 1982 with a helipad installed atop its lantern, Douglass’s lighthouse continues to flash its warning across the English Channel today, concluding nearly four centuries of epic offshore engineering.'
);

