-- Passage 2: Indoor Air Quality in Mass Transit Subway Networks
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '8ca15b2d-308d-5a69-9b1d-2742f32cc0ac', 'c1df761c-c7b9-57a0-9a80-8fef96800d97', 2, 'Indoor Air Quality in Mass Transit Subway Networks',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across major metropolitan centers worldwide, subterranean rapid transit rail systems— commonly known as subways, metros, or undergrounds—serve as the vital circulatory systems of urban mass mobility. Transporting tens of millions of passengers daily through thousands of kilometers of underground tunnels, subways are widely championed as environmentally sustainable alternatives to private automotive transportation, reducing surface road congestion and curbing urban greenhouse gas emissions. However, beneath this green public image lies an overlooked occupational and environmental health concern: the degraded quality of indoor air within subterranean subway stations and transit tunnels.

B. Extensive aerosol sampling campaigns conducted across major subway networks—including London, Paris, New York, Seoul, and Beijing—have revealed that concentrations of airborne particulate matter ($PM_{2.5}$ and $PM_{10}$) in subterranean stations consistently exceed outdoor ambient air levels by factors of two to ten. While outdoor urban air pollution is dominated by combustion aerosols derived from vehicular tailpipes and fossil-fueled heating, subterranean transit air possesses a completely distinct, highly specific chemical fingerprint. Subterranean subway aerosols are predominantly mineral and metallic, generated almost exclusively through mechanical friction, abrasive wear, and electrical arcing occurring at the dynamic interfaces of railway operations.

C. The primary source of subway particulate matter is the continuous physical interaction between steel wheels and steel rails during high-speed rolling, braking, and cornering. Under immense mechanical friction and compressive shear stresses, microscopic metal fragments are sheared from the wheel treads and rail heads. This abrasive wear is heavily amplified by braking operations: conventional pneumatic friction brakes clamp composite or cast-iron brake pads against brake discs, generating dense plumes of microscopic friction dust. Furthermore, the sliding contact between electrical train collector shoes (pantographs) and overhead copper catenary wires or third-rail electrical supply lines generates intense electrical sparking (micro- arcing), which vaporizes and condenses volatile copper, carbon, and aluminum nanoparticles into the tunnel atmosphere.

D. Geochemical and toxicological analyses demonstrate that subway particulate matter is exceptionally rich in transition metals, with iron ($Fe$) accounting for upwards of 40 to 70 percent of total aerosol mass, typically present as iron oxides such as magnetite ($Fe_3O_4$), hematite ($\alpha\text{-}Fe_2O_3$), and wüstite ($FeO$). These iron-rich cores are heavily alloyed with toxic trace metals, including copper ($Cu$), manganese ($Mn$), chromium ($Cr$), nickel ($Ni$), and barium ($Ba$). In vitro toxicological assays reveal that due to their high transition metal content, subway dust particles exhibit potent bioreactivity: once inhaled into human lung alveoli, the transition metals catalyze Fenton chemical reactions, generating high levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS) and hydroxyl free radicals ($OH^\bullet$). This induces oxidative stress, cellular membrane lipid peroxidation, and pulmonary inflammatory cascades, posing serious long-term respiratory and cardiovascular health risks for daily commuters and transit employees.

E. The persistent accumulation and retention of high particulate concentrations underground are exacerbated by the severe physical aerodynamics of subway infrastructure. Deep underground subway tunnels act as semi-sealed subterranean enclosures with limited natural ventilation. As trains travel through narrow tunnel tubes, they act as mechanical pistons (the "piston effect"), pushing air columns ahead of them and sucking air in from behind. While the piston effect provides localized air movement, it also continuously resuspends settled metal dust from the trackbed ballast into the station platforms, preventing particles from settling and keeping them suspended in the passenger breathing zone.

F. To mitigate subway air pollution, transit authorities are deploying comprehensive structural and technological engineering interventions. The most effective structural barrier is the installation of full-height Platform Screen Doors (PSDs). By erecting continuous, airtight glass dividing walls along the entire platform edge, PSDs physically decouple the passenger waiting platforms from the active train tracks and tunnels. Comprehensive air quality monitoring in newly constructed Seoul and Barcelona metro stations indicates that full-height PSDs can reduce platform particulate matter concentrations by 50 to 80 percent, provided that trackbed air is evacuated through dedicated tunnel exhaust shafts.

G. Complementing physical barriers, environmental engineers are retrofitting subterranean ventilation systems with advanced magnetic filtration and electrostatic precipitators. Because subway aerosols are dominated by ferromagnetic iron oxide compounds, high-gradient magnetic filters installed within station air handling units and train undercarriages can capture metallic dust particles with exceptional filtration efficiency without creating the high airflow resistance associated with dense HEPA fiber filters. By synthesizing mechanical friction redesigns, platform isolation screens, and electromagnetic air filtration, modern subterranean rail networks can ensure that clean mass mobility does not compromise public health.'
);

