-- Passage 1: The Domestication and Silk Production of the Silkworm (Bombyx mori)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '4d510766-4d68-54fe-bfe3-f53b9db4ac2b', 'c9cb28a9-c1c6-5510-9207-1b5696914d61', 1, 'The Domestication and Silk Production of the Silkworm (Bombyx mori)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the history of global agriculture and textile manufacture, few biological relationships between humans and insects have been as commercially lucrative, culturally revered, and technologically transformative as sericulture—the cultivation of the domesticated silkworm (Bombyx mori) for the production of raw silk. Emerging during the Neolithic era in ancient China, the production of silk evolved from a closely guarded imperial secret into the primary luxury commodity that anchored the ancient Silk Road, bridging commercial and cultural exchange across Asia, the Middle East, and the Mediterranean basin. Today, Bombyx mori stands as a unique biological organism: a completely domesticated species that no longer exists in the wild, having undergone thousands of years of selective breeding that rendered it entirely dependent on human care for survival.

B. The wild progenitor of the domestic silkworm is Bombyx mandarina, a wild silk moth native to East Asia. Archaeological excavations at Neolithic sites along the Yangtze and Yellow River valleys—most notably the discovery of a carved ivory silkworm at Shuanghuai Shu in Henan and carbonized silk threads at Qianshanyang in Zhejiang—demonstrate that Chinese communities were actively harvesting and spinning silk fibers by the fourth and third millennia BCE. Over centuries of artificial selection, ancient breeders transformed the wild moth into the modern Bombyx mori. In doing so, they engineered profound morphological and behavioral modifications: the adult moths lost the physiological ability to fly, lost their functional mouthparts, developed completely docile temperaments, and lost their natural larval camouflage pigmentation, turning into pale, defenseless caterpillars.

C. The biological lifecycle of Bombyx mori is characterized by complete holometabolous metamorphosis, progressing through four distinct stages: egg, larva (caterpillar), pupa, and adult moth. The larval stage is exclusively monophagous, meaning the caterpillar feeds strictly on the fresh leaves of the white mulberry tree (Morus alba). Over a period of approximately twenty-five to thirty-two days, a silkworm larva feeds voraciously, increasing its initial hatching body weight by nearly ten thousand times while undergoing four successive molts (instars) to shed its rigid outer exoskeleton. Because the quantity, moisture content, and nutritional quality of the mulberry leaves directly dictate the volume and tensile strength of the silk produced, traditional sericulturists maintained meticulously pruned mulberry orchards, harvesting young, tender leaves free from dust or chemical impurities.

D. Upon reaching its fifth and final larval instar, the silkworm ceases feeding, turns translucent amber, and prepares to spin its protective pupal cocoon. The caterpillar anchors itself to a wooden rearing rack or straw frame and begins secreting liquid silk from a pair of specialized, modified salivary glands known as labial silk glands. These glands, which run the entire length of the caterpillar’s body, produce a complex biological composite consisting of two primary proteins: fibroin and sericin. The inner core of the silk filament is composed of fibroin, a tough, insoluble, crystalline structural protein characterized by antiparallel beta-pleated sheets. Surrounding this twin fibroin core is an outer coating of sericin, a sticky, water-soluble, amorphous gummy protein that binds the two fibroin filaments together into a single composite thread known as a bave.

E. Moving its head in a continuous, rhythmic figure-eight motion, the caterpillar extrudes the liquid silk through a microscopic nozzle on its mouthparts called the spinneret. Upon contact with atmospheric air, the liquid proteins instantly align, shear-crystallize, and solidify into a continuous, lustrous filament. Over a period of forty-eight to seventy-two hours of continuous spinning, a single silkworm constructs an uninterrupted silk thread measuring between 900 and 1,500 meters in length, encasing itself within a dense, protective oval cocoon.

F. In commercial sericulture, harvesting the silk demands precise thermodynamic timing before the pupa inside undergoes natural metamorphosis. If the pupa is allowed to mature into an adult moth, it will secrete an alkaline enzyme called cocoonase to dissolve an exit hole through the cocoon wall, severing the continuous silk filament into thousands of useless, broken short fibers. To preserve the unbroken integrity of the thread, sericulturists subject the intact cocoons to heat treatment—using hot air, steam, or boiling water (a process known as stifling)—to terminate the pupa inside. The dried cocoons are then submerged in hot water baths at approximately 60°C to 80°C. The hot water softens and partially dissolves the gummy sericin binder, allowing operators to locate the loose outer thread end.

G. Once the filament end is located, multiple threads from four to eight individual cocoons are gathered together and wound simultaneously onto a motorized reel, twisting the ultra-fine fibers into a single, cohesive strand of commercial "raw silk." The raw silk subsequently undergoes a chemical finishing process known as degumming, where hot alkaline solutions strip away the remaining sericin coating, revealing the lustrous, smooth, triangular cross-section of the pure fibroin filaments that gives silk its legendary prism-like sheen. Today, beyond high-fashion textiles, the biocompatibility and extreme tensile strength of pure silkworm fibroin have positioned silk as a cutting-edge biomaterial in modern medicine, utilized for surgical sutures, artificial ligament scaffolds, and implantable drug-delivery matrices.'
);

