-- Passage 3: The Frame Problem in Artificial Intelligence and Cognitive Architecture
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'fd530c69-6def-5d3d-aec9-ff3540b2a39e', 'c9cb28a9-c1c6-5510-9207-1b5696914d61', 3, 'The Frame Problem in Artificial Intelligence and Cognitive Architecture',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the early decades of artificial intelligence (AI) research, mainstream cognitive science and computer science operated under the confident paradigm of Classical Logic-Based AI (often termed Good Old-Fashioned AI, or GOFAI). Championed by pioneers such as John McCarthy, Marvin Minsky, and Herbert Simon, GOFAI conceptualized human intelligence as a formal deductive reasoning system. Under this logicist paradigm, an autonomous agent (whether a physical robot or a software program) understands its world by maintaining an extensive, internal symbolic database of logical propositions that explicitly describe facts, physical objects, spatial relationships, and causal rules governing the environment. Whenever the agent executes an action, it applies deductive rules of inference to update its knowledge base and calculate the next logical move.

B. In 1969, however, computer scientists John McCarthy and Patrick J. Hayes published a seminal paper that identified a profound, seemingly intractable mathematical and computational obstacle at the heart of logicist AI: the Frame Problem. In its original, narrow technical formulation, the Frame Problem concerns the challenge of formally representing what remains unchanged in an environment when an action is performed. In formal mathematical logic, an action axiom explicitly states what changes: for example, if a robot paints a wooden chair green, the color of the chair changes from brown to green ($Color(Chair, Green)$). But how does the computer know that the weight of the chair did not change, that the room’s temperature remained constant, that the walls did not turn into water, and that the President of the United States did not suddenly resign?

C. In the real physical universe, an infinite number of background facts remain completely unaffected by any given localized physical action. Yet, within a strict formal logical deduction system, a computer cannot simply assume that non-mentioned facts stay the same; it must possess formal axioms that explicitly state what does not change. These negative assertions are known in AI formalism as "frame axioms." For instance, an agent needs frame axioms stating: "Painting a chair green does not alter its weight," "Painting a chair green does not move the table," and "Painting a chair green does not alter the laws of gravity." As an AI agent''s world model expands to include thousands of objects and actions, the number of required frame axioms explodes exponentially into millions of non-change formulas, requiring the computer to execute millions of irrelevant deductive checks before it can calculate even the simplest real-world task.

D. Over the subsequent two decades, prominent cognitive philosophers—most notably Jerry Fodor and Daniel Dennett—demonstrated that the Frame Problem is not merely a narrow programming technicality in formal logic, but a profound, fundamental epistemological crisis concerning the nature of human rationality itself: the Problem of Holistic Relevance. When human beings make decisions in daily life, we do not spend hours verifying millions of irrelevant background facts; our cognitive architecture instantly, effortlessly, and non-deductively identifies which handful of facts are relevant to the current context, while completely ignoring the infinite universe of irrelevant information. How does the human mind instantly determine what is relevant without first computing and evaluating all the irrelevant data?

E. Dennett illustrated the terrifying comedy of the philosophical Frame Problem through his famous thought experiment featuring a sequence of autonomous robots designed to retrieve a spare battery from a locked room containing a ticking time bomb: 1. Robot R1: Programmed with basic logic, R1 finds the battery on a wagon and pulls the wagon out of the room. However, because it failed to calculate the non-intended implications of its action, it pulled the wagon containing the bomb out with the battery, and was promptly blown up. 2. Robot R1D1 (The Deductive Robot): Programmed to calculate all non-changes, R1D1 enters the room, locates the wagon, and begins calculating: "Pulling the wagon will not change the color of the walls; pulling the wagon will not move the ceiling..." While standing motionless calculating millions of non-implications, the bomb detonates. 3. Robot R2D1 (The Relevance Robot): Programmed to distinguish relevant implications from irrelevant ones, R2D1 enters the room. When an observer asks why it is standing still, the robot responds: "I am actively computing which of the millions of non- implications are irrelevant so I can ignore them!" While busy ignoring irrelevant facts, the bomb explodes.

F. The failure of GOFAI to solve the Frame Problem triggered a major epistemological paradigm shift in artificial intelligence and cognitive philosophy, leading to the rise of Connectionism (Artificial Neural Networks) and Embodied Cognition. Connectionist models abandoned explicit, rule-based symbolic propositions in favor of distributed sub-symbolic neural network weightings that process information through parallel statistical pattern recognition. Unlike classical deductive systems, neural networks naturally exhibit "graceful degradation" and context sensitivity, allowing them to balance multiple competing constraints simultaneously without evaluating explicit frame axioms.

G. Simultaneously, embodied roboticists such as Rodney Brooks pioneered "Nouvelle AI" and behavior-based robotics, famously asserting that "the world is its own best model." Brooks argued that the Frame Problem is an artificial artifact created by the mistaken assumption that an intelligent agent must maintain a massive, internal symbolic representation of the external world inside its CPU. By building simple, situated robots that couple sensor inputs directly to motor actions through low-level reactive behavior layers (subsumption architectures), Brooks proved that robots could navigate complex physical rooms in real time without calculating internal frame axioms or symbolic world models.

H. In contemporary cognitive science, the Frame Problem remains a vital benchmark for evaluating modern Large Language Models (LLMs) and artificial general intelligence (AGI). While modern deep neural networks successfully bypass formal frame axioms through statistical word associations and attention mechanisms, cognitive scientists emphasize that statistical models still struggle with genuine common-sense physical reasoning in novel edge cases. Understanding how biological brains effortlessly navigate the boundless sea of environmental information without drowning in computational paralysis remains the holy grail of cognitive philosophy, illuminating the remarkable, unmapped elegance of human common sense.'
);

