-- Passage 1: Traditional Indigo Dyeing and Vat Chemistry
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '1305a58a-4323-59e9-91fe-9e4d41103e9d', 'a46018ba-7868-5370-8cd5-17ac11e88db3', 1, 'Traditional Indigo Dyeing and Vat Chemistry',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of global textile production, few natural colorants have commanded as much economic power, cultural prestige, and chemical complexity as natural indigo. Known across antiquity as the "king of dyes," this deep, radiant blue pigment was prized by ancient civilizations spanning the Indus Valley, pharaonic Egypt, pre-Columbian Mesoamerica, and Han Dynasty China. Long before the 1897 commercial synthesis of artificial indigo by the German chemical firm BASF, natural indigo was extracted from a diverse array of botanical species, most notably Indigofera tinctoria (true indigo) native to tropical Asia, Isatis tinctoria (woad) in temperate Europe, and Polygonum tinctorium (Japanese indigo) in East Asia. However, unlike standard water-soluble vegetable dyes such as madder root or weld, which bind directly to textile fibers in an aqueous bath, indigo presents a fundamental biochemical paradox: in its vibrant, oxidized blue state, it is completely insoluble in water and cannot adhere to fabric.

B. The biological precursor of indigo within living plant tissues is indican, a colorless, water- soluble glucoside compound synthesized within the plant''s chloroplasts. Indican consists of an indoxyl molecule bonded to a glucose sugar unit. To harvest the pigment, freshly cut green leaves are steeped in large outdoor masonry fermentation vats filled with warm water. During this anaerobic soaking stage, naturally occurring plant enzymes known as beta-glucosidases hydrolyze the indican molecule, cleaving the glucose sugar away and liberating free, highly reactive indoxyl into the liquid. Once fermentation reaches an optimal stage, the amber liquid is drained into lower beating vats. Workers vigorously beat, paddle, or aerate the liquid using mechanical wheels or wooden oars, introducing atmospheric oxygen. Oxidation causes two indoxyl molecules to dimerize and bond together, precipitating out of solution as an insoluble, intense blue sediment: indigotin ($C_{16}H_{10}N_2O_2$).

C. Once dried into solid cakes or powder, this pure blue indigotin cannot simply be dissolved in water to dye cloth; submerging yarn in an indigo suspension merely coats the surface with loose, particulate dust that rubs off instantly upon drying. To enable the dye to penetrate and chemically bond with natural protein (wool, silk) or cellulosic (cotton, linen) fibers, the insoluble blue indigotin must undergo a chemical reduction back into its soluble, non-oxidized form, a pale yellow-green compound known as leuco-indigo (or "indigo white"). Because leuco-indigo is soluble only in a strongly alkaline environment (a pH between 9.0 and 11.5), traditional dyers had to engineer balanced, living biochemical fermentation ecosystems within large earthenware or wooden vats.

D. The establishment of a traditional fermentation vat required a delicate, empirical balance of three components: water, an alkaline agent, and an organic reducing agent. To achieve the requisite alkaline pH without modern synthetic caustic soda, pre-industrial dyers leached alkaline potash (potassium carbonate) and soda ash from the calcined ashes of hardwood trees, bracken ferns, or halophytic marine plants, or utilized aged stale urine, which naturally produces alkaline ammonia ($NH_3$) through urea hydrolysis. To drive the chemical reduction of indigotin to leuco-indigo, dyers introduced organic carbohydrates that served as fuel for anaerobic, alkali- tolerant bacteria (such as Clostridium and Bacillus species). Depending on local traditions, dyers added wheat bran, madder root, crushed dates, dried figs, honey, fermented rice wine, or iron vitriol (ferrous sulfate).

E. As the anaerobic bacteria metabolize the sugars within the warm vat (optimally maintained between 30°C and 35°C), they consume dissolved oxygen and secrete reducing chemical agents, gradually converting the blue indigotin into soluble, yellow-green leuco-indigo. A seasoned master dyer determined the health of a fermentation vat through keen sensory observation rather than chemical testing instruments: a healthy vat developed a thick, iridescent, coppery-blue scum on its surface (known as the "flower" or flos), smelled sweet, earthy, and mildly alcoholic, and possessed a clear, greenish-yellow liquor beneath the foam. If the vat turned blue or smelled sour, it meant oxygen had contaminated the liquid or acidity had climbed, requiring the immediate addition of more wood ash or fermentable sugars.

F. The physical act of dyeing with leuco-indigo is an optical and chemical spectacle. The pre- wetted yarn or woven textile is gently submerged into the yellow-green vat liquor, avoiding any splashing or turbulence that might introduce oxygen. Inside the bath, the soluble leuco-indigo molecules diffuse deep into the amorphous interior regions of the fiber polymers. After soaking for several minutes, the textile is slowly withdrawn from the liquid. At the exact moment of emergence, the fabric appears a striking, vivid chartreuse-yellow. However, as atmospheric oxygen contacts the saturated cloth, an instantaneous oxidation reaction occurs: the leuco-indigo molecules within the fiber walls oxidize, dimerize, and regenerate insoluble blue indigotin crystals trapped permanently inside the physical matrix of the fiber.

G. To achieve deep, saturated, dark shades—such as the famous midnight-blue "Japan blue" or rich West African royal cloths—dyers never leave fabric in a vat for hours at a time, as this can degrade the fiber and deplete the vat’s chemical balance. Instead, dyers practice repetitive "dips": repeatedly dipping, squeezing, aerating, drying, and washing the cloth across dozens of successive cycles over several weeks. Each immersion deposits a microscopic, microscopic layer of crystalline indigotin inside the fiber. Today, while petroleum-derived synthetic indigo dominates industrial denim manufacturing, a global resurgence of sustainable fashion and natural craft heritage has revitalized traditional fermentation vats, preserving the living biocultural chemistry that clothed human civilization for thousands of years.'
);

