-- Passage 1: Ancient Roman Glassblowing and Mold-Blown Vessels
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '53265dce-2d04-5643-af89-ad263e5748ee', '86e9534c-316b-5516-a8be-4f17eff3b2f6', 1, 'Ancient Roman Glassblowing and Mold-Blown Vessels',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the ancient Mediterranean world, glass had long been manufactured as an elite luxury material. For nearly two millennia across the Bronze and early Iron Ages, glassmakers in Mesopotamia and Pharaonic Egypt relied on core-forming and cast-molding techniques. In core- forming, an artisan sculpted a temporary clay-and-dung core around an iron rod, repeatedly dipped it into molten glass, and laboriously rolled the viscous mass against a flat stone to smooth the exterior. The core was subsequently scraped out once the vessel cooled. Because these manual fabrication methods were tedious and consumed substantial fuel, glass vessels— primarily minute cosmetic flasks, unguent jars, and perfumed oil bottles—remained rare, expensive prestige items reserved exclusively for royalty, temple treasuries, and wealthy aristocrats.

B. The definitive pyrotechnological revolution that dismantled this ancient luxury bottleneck occurred during the mid-first century BCE along the Syro-Palestinian coast of the eastern Mediterranean, within the territory of the Roman Levant. Anonymous craftsmen discovered that a gather of molten glass gathered on the tip of a hollow metal or clay tube could be inflated by human lung power into a hollow, translucent bubble. This discovery—free-blowing— transformed glassmaking from a slow, subtractive sculpting process into a rapid, expansive manufacturing art. An experienced glassblower could produce a finished, delicate glass vessel in minutes rather than days, drastically reducing labor costs and fuel consumption.

C. Following the Roman conquest of the Levant and the consolidation of the Pax Romana under Emperor Augustus, the technique of glassblowing spread rapidly across the Roman Empire. Italian and Levantine glassworkers established major workshops in Rome, the Campania region, the Rhine Valley, and Gaul. By the first century CE, artisans advanced free-blowing by inventing mold-blowing: inflating a gather of hot glass directly inside a carved, multi-part open-and-shut mold made of carved terracotta, stone, or cast bronze.

D. Mold-blowing unlocked two transformative commercial advantages: geometric standardization and mass-produced decorative relief. Unlike free-blown glass, which yielded organic, slightly asymmetrical shapes dependent entirely on the manual skill of the individual blower, mold-blown glass produced vessels with uniform volumetric capacities and standardized dimensions. This consistency allowed Roman merchants to package liquid commodities—such as fine wines, perfumed oils, garum (fermented fish sauce), and liquid medicines—into uniform glass containers suitable for maritime transport and retail trade. Furthermore, mold interiors could be deeply carved with intricate inscriptions, mythological scenes, civic emblems, and commercial branding.

E. A celebrated exemplar of early Roman commercial branding through mold-blowing is the workshop of Ennion, a master glassmaker operating in the city of Sidon (in modern Lebanon) during the early first century CE. Ennion carved his signature directly into his three-part and four-part bronze molds, producing delicate, thin-walled hexagonal and ribbed glass amphoriskoi and cups inscribed with Greek slogans such as "Ennion made me; let the buyer remember." Archaeological excavations have uncovered Ennion’s signed glassware across the Mediterranean basin—from Cyprus and Greece to Italy and the Spanish coast—proving the existence of an integrated, empire-wide luxury distribution network.

F. The democratization of glass manufacturing transformed everyday domestic life across Roman society. By the second century CE, glass had evolved from an elite royal treasure into an everyday consumer commodity. Inexpensive natural blue-green soda-lime glass cups, bowls, and storage jars became standard household items in modest urban homes, military forts along Hadrian''s Wall, and rural farming villas. Roman citizens preferred glass drinking vessels over traditional ceramic pottery and pewter because glass was completely non-porous, chemically inert, easy to clean, and did not impart unwanted mineral tastes to wine. Furthermore, Roman architects pioneered the use of cast and blown flat glass panes for window glazing in imperial bathhouses and private villas, maximizing solar heat gain through the greenhouse effect while keeping out cold winter drafts.

G. Beyond everyday domestic utility, the scale of Roman glass manufacture was sustained by an extensive international trade in raw glass ingots and an advanced recycling economy. Huge primary glass furnace installations in Roman Palestine and Egypt (such as Bet Eli''ezer and the Wadi Natrun) melted raw silica sand, natron flux, and shell lime in massive tank furnaces, producing monolithic slabs of raw uncolored glass weighing up to twenty metric tons. These slabs were broken into raw chunks and shipped aboard commercial freighters to secondary glass workshops across Western Europe, where local blowers remelted the raw chunks in small crucibles to craft finished vessels. Worn, broken, and discarded glass fragments (cullet) were collected systematically by municipal street traders and remelted, establishing a circular materials economy that allowed Roman glass technology to endure and flourish for centuries.'
);

