-- Passage 2: Additive Manufacturing in Aerospace Superalloy Components
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '37f22342-ea5a-5889-8175-37990cec6c37', '0e784133-9244-5ec3-a95b-ef7ddd8bf38d', 2, 'Additive Manufacturing in Aerospace Superalloy Components',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the demanding realm of modern aerospace and astronautical engineering, the relentless drive to improve propulsion efficiency, reduce payload launch costs, and minimize aviation greenhouse gas emissions depends fundamentally on the optimization of high-temperature turbine components. In the combustion chambers, turbine discs, and rocket engine exhaust nozzles of advanced jet engines and spacecraft launch vehicles, metallic materials are subjected to extraordinary mechanical and thermal extremes. Operating environments routinely combine mechanical stresses exceeding several hundred megapascals with gas temperatures surpassing 1,200°C to 1,400°C—temperatures close to or exceeding the melting points of conventional structural metals. To endure these punishing conditions without catastrophic failure, aerospace manufacturers rely on specialized high-temperature nickel-based superalloys, most notably Inconel 718 and Inconel 625.

B. Historically, fabricating flight-critical aerospace components from nickel-based superalloys relied on conventional subtractive manufacturing and formative metallurgy: casting, forging, and multi-axis CNC (Computer Numerical Control) precision machining. However, superalloys are notoriously difficult to machine. Their extreme mechanical toughness, high work-hardening rates, low thermal conductivity, and the presence of abrasive internal carbide precipitates cause rapid tool wear, excessive cutting vibrations, and frequent tool breakage. Subtractive machining of complex aerospace parts often results in exorbitant "buy-to-fly" ratios—a metric measuring the ratio of the mass of raw starting material purchased to the mass of the final finished component. For intricate aerospace parts, up to 80 to 90 percent of an expensive forged superalloy billet was routinely ground away into waste metal swarf.

C. To circumvent these metallurgical and economic constraints, aerospace engineering has rapidly embraced additive manufacturing (AM), commonly known as industrial 3D printing. Among the diverse AM modalities available for metal fabrication, Laser Powder Bed Fusion (LPBF)—also designated as Selective Laser Melting (SLM)—has emerged as the premier technology for fabricating flight-qualified superalloy components. In an LPBF system, a high- precision recoater blade spreads an ultra-thin layer of gas-atomized spherical superalloy powder (typically twenty to sixty micrometers in thickness) across an internal build platform enclosed within an inert gas chamber (flooded with high-purity argon to prevent molten oxidation). A high-power ytterbium fiber laser (typically 400 to 1,000 Watts) traces the exact cross-sectional geometry of the component, fully melting and fusing the metal powder particles along its path. The build platform then descends by one layer thickness, another powder layer is applied, and the process repeats sequentially across thousands of layers until a complete, fully dense three- dimensional component is created.

D. The primary engineering virtue of Laser Powder Bed Fusion is the liberation of geometric design constraints, unlocking advanced topology optimization and generative design. In conventional casting and machining, internal cooling galleries within rocket engine combustion chambers or turbine blades had to be straight or gently curved to allow drill access. With LPBF, engineers can fabricate complex, organic, conformal cooling channels that weave fluidly through the interior core of a turbine blade, positioning cooling airflows directly against the hottest thermal boundaries. Furthermore, LPBF allows multiple distinct sub-assemblies to be consolidated into a single monolithic part: rocket engine injectors that previously required assembling, brazing, and inspecting over two hundred individual machined pipes, valves, and fittings can now be 3D-printed as a single, seamless monolithic unit, slashing component mass, eliminating potential leak paths, and shortening manufacturing lead times from months to days.

E. Despite these remarkable geometric advantages, the extreme physics of laser powder melting introduces severe metallurgical defects that require meticulous post-processing management. During LPBF, the focused laser beam heats the localized melt pool above 1,700°C within microseconds, followed immediately by rapid thermal quenching as heat dissipates into the surrounding solid metal at rates exceeding one million degrees Celsius per second ($10^6 \text{ K/s}$). This extreme, non-equilibrium thermal cycle generates intense thermal gradients and severe residual tensile stresses within the printed part, which can cause cracking or structural warping if detached from the build plate. Furthermore, rapid directional solidification induces microstructural anisotropy—columnar grain growth aligned parallel to the vertical build direction—resulting in uneven mechanical strength along different axes.

F. To relieve residual stresses, eliminate internal micro-porosity, and homogenize anisotropic microstructures, as-printed superalloy components must undergo a mandatory sequence of high- temperature metallurgical post-treatments. Foremost among these is Hot Isostatic Pressing (HIP). During the HIP process, printed components are sealed inside a high-pressure furnace chamber, heated to temperatures between 1,120°C and 1,180°C, and subjected to an isostatic gas pressure of 100 to 150 megapascals using inert argon gas. Under this simultaneous application of extreme heat and multi-directional pressure, internal microscopic gas pores and un-fused voids collapse and diffusion-bond shut through plastic deformation, restoring the material’s fatigue life and tensile ductility to levels that meet or exceed traditional forged superalloys.

G. Following HIP consolidation, components undergo precision multi-stage solution annealing and precipitation age-hardening heat treatments to precipitate strengthening gamma-prime ($\gamma''$) and gamma-double-prime ($\gamma''''$) intermetallic phases within the nickel matrix. As non-destructive evaluation (NDE) techniques—such as high-resolution industrial micro-computed tomography ($\mu\text{-CT}$) scanning—continue to mature for structural quality assurance, additive manufacturing is solidifying its role as a cornerstone of next- generation aerospace propulsion. From commercial turbofan jet engines to reusable orbital rocket engines, 3D-printed superalloys are propelling human flight into a new era of unprecedented efficiency and performance.'
);

