-- Passage 3: The Neurophysics of Free Will and the Libet Dilemma
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '4bcebef8-fb38-5085-8996-878d08b925c5', '0e784133-9244-5ec3-a95b-ef7ddd8bf38d', 3, 'The Neurophysics of Free Will and the Libet Dilemma',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. For millennia of philosophical and theological contemplation, the concept of conscious free will—the subjective conviction that human agents possess autonomous control over their voluntary decisions and could have chosen otherwise—has stood as the bedrock of moral responsibility, legal culpability, and personal agency. In Western philosophical traditions, from Aristotle and Thomas Aquinas to René Descartes and Immanuel Kant, moral accountability rests upon the presumption that conscious intent precedes and directly initiates voluntary physical action. If a human being does not consciously author their choices, our entire framework of ethical praise, criminal punishment, and individual autonomy is plunged into foundational crisis.

B. In the early 1980s, this classical philosophical assumption was subjected to its first direct neurophysiological challenge by American neurophysiologist Benjamin Libet at the University of California, San Francisco. Building upon the 1965 discovery of the Bereitschaftspotential (readiness potential, or RP)—a gradual build-up of negative electrical potential recorded over the supplementary motor area of the cerebral cortex prior to voluntary movement, discovered by German neurologists Hans Helmut Kornhuber and Lüder Deecke—Libet designed an ingenious experimental protocol to measure the precise temporal relationship between the onset of unconscious brain activity, the subjective awareness of conscious intention, and the execution of a physical act.

C. Libet fitted human participants with high-resolution electroencephalography (EEG) scalp electrodes to record the electrical activity of their motor cortex, while electromyography (EMG) sensors placed on their wrists recorded the exact millisecond of physical muscle contraction. To measure the subjective timing of conscious intent, Libet placed participants in front of a specialized, high-speed cathode-ray oscilloscope clock (the "Libet clock"), around which a single dot of light rotated rapidly once every 2.56 seconds. Participants were instructed to perform a simple, spontaneous motor task—flicking their right wrist or pressing a button—at a moment of their own choosing whenever they felt the urge. Afterward, participants were asked to report the exact position of the rotating dot on the clock face at the precise moment they first became consciously aware of their decision or urge to act (a subjective temporal marker designated by Libet as the "W time," for will).

D. Libet’s empirical results sent shockwaves across neuroscience and the philosophy of mind. The EEG recordings revealed that the unconscious readiness potential (RP) began rising in the motor cortex approximately 550 milliseconds ($ms$) before the physical execution of the wrist movement (the muscle activation recorded by EMG). However, the participants’ subjective awareness of their conscious urge to move (the W time) occurred on average only 200 milliseconds before the physical action. In other words, the unconscious brain initiated the neurological preparation for the physical movement roughly 350 milliseconds before the conscious mind became aware that it had made any decision at all.

E. Libet’s findings appeared to deliver a devastating empirical blow to the classical doctrine of conscious free will. If the unconscious machinery of the brain has already committed to an action hundreds of milliseconds before the subjective feeling of conscious choice emerges, then conscious intention is not the true causal initiator of human action. Rather, conscious will appears to be an epiphenomenon—a post-hoc, retrospective illusion generated by the brain after the physical decision has already been executed unconsciously. Prominent materialist philosophers and neuroscientists, such as Daniel Wegner in The Illusion of Conscious Will (2002) and Sam Harris, declared that Libet’s experiments had decisively demonstrated that free will is an evolutionary fiction.

F. However, Libet himself rejected this fatalistic materialist conclusion, proposing an alternative neurophysiological compromise: the concept of the conscious "veto" (popularly dubbed "free won''t"). Libet pointed out that between the subjective awareness of the urge (the W time at $-200\text{ ms}$) and the final muscle contraction ($0\text{ ms}$), there exists a critical temporal window of approximately 100 to 150 milliseconds (excluding the final 50 milliseconds required for motor signals to travel down spinal nerves to the wrist). During this brief window, Libet argued, the conscious mind possesses the active neurological capacity to veto, abort, or suppress the unconsciously initiated motor program before it manifests physically. While consciousness does not originate the impulse to act, it retains the executive power to censor or allow the action, preserving a crucial foundation for moral responsibility and self-control.

G. Over the past two decades, contemporary cognitive neuroscientists, such as Aaron Schurger and John-Dylan Haynes, have fundamentally challenged the traditional interpretation of Libet''s readiness potential using advanced computational models and functional MRI. In a landmark 2012 study, Schurger demonstrated through a stochastic accumulator model that the classical readiness potential is not an unconscious, pre-determined "decision" to act. Rather, it reflects continuous, random sub-threshold fluctuations in background neural noise within the motor cortex. When a participant is instructed to perform an arbitrary movement at an unprompted moment, they act precisely when these spontaneous neural fluctuations randomly cross a threshold. Averaging hundreds of these fluctuating trials backward from the moment of movement creates the mathematical illusion of a smooth, directed ramp of electrical potential (the RP), when in physical reality no specific neural commitment occurred until milliseconds before the action.

H. In synthesizing the modern neurophysics of agency, contemporary cognitive science recognizes that reducing free will to a simplistic millisecond timing test fundamentally trivializes human choice. Genuine human agency is not manifested in arbitrary, meaningless button clicks performed in laboratory basements; it is expressed in complex, temporally extended deliberations—such as choosing a career, planning a scientific experiment, honoring an ethical commitment, or writing a book. These higher-order choices recruit distributed prefrontal executive networks, narrative identity, cultural values, and conscious prospective reflection over days, months, and years. Far from being an illusion, human agency is a dynamic, multi-tiered evolutionary capability, allowing conscious minds to actively steer their actions and co-create their destinies within a physical cosmos.'
);

