-- Passage 1: The History and Ecology of Traditional Saffron Cultivation
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'b0ea768d-6853-5512-b7fb-8e593c40e258', '369e308b-cc35-5207-8ade-cd3e06d97a79', 1, 'The History and Ecology of Traditional Saffron Cultivation',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of global agriculture and culinary commerce, few botanical commodities have commanded as astronomical a monetary value per unit weight as saffron. Renowned worldwide as "red gold," saffron consists exclusively of the dried, trifid stigmas harvested from the autumn-flowering crocus (Crocus sativus). For over three millennia— spanning Minoan Crete, Classical Greece, Pharaonic Egypt, Achaemenid Persia, and medieval Europe—this vibrant spice has been prized not only for its distinct bitter-honeyed flavor and intense golden dye, but also as an esteemed medicinal panacea and aristocratic perfume. Despite centuries of agricultural modernization and synthetic chemistry, authentic saffron remains one of the world''s few agricultural crops that resists mechanized harvesting, relying entirely on intensive manual labor.

B. The botanical identity of Crocus sativus presents a fascinating case of ancient human- directed artificial selection. Botanists and plant geneticists have established that Crocus sativus is a sterile, triploid cultigen possessing twenty-four chromosomes ($2n = 3x = 24$), derived through the hybridization of the wild diploid species Crocus cartwrightianus, native to the Aegean islands of Greece. Because of its triploid chromosomal structure, the saffron crocus undergoes abnormal meiosis and cannot produce viable, fertile seeds. Consequently, the plant is incapable of reproducing or surviving in the wild without human intervention. The species is maintained exclusively through clonal vegetative propagation: farmers manually excavate, divide, and replant the underground corms (bulb-like storage organs) that multiply around the parent plant during its annual dormancy.

C. Saffron cultivation demands a specific Mediterranean or continental arid climate characterized by hot, dry summers and cold, crisp winters. The corms are planted in late summer in well-drained, porous calcareous or silty-clay soils, as stagnant moisture and waterlogging cause fatal fungal corm rot (Fusarium oxysporum). Throughout early autumn, the dormant corms awaken, sending up narrow, grass-like green foliage followed by striking lilac or pale-purple flowers. The blossoming period is extraordinarily brief and intense: the entire annual crop blooms over a concentrated window of ten to fifteen days in October or November, requiring farmers to mobilize massive local labor forces to harvest the blossoms before they wilt under the autumn sun.

D. The physical harvesting of saffron is one of the most labor-intensive tasks in world agriculture. Flowers must be picked by hand during the crisp hours of dawn, just as the petals begin to unfurl, to prevent direct sunlight and wind from degrading the delicate chemical compounds within the exposed stigmas. Following picking, workers undergo the painstaking process of "stripping" or separating: carefully plucking the three vivid, crimson-red stigmas from the center of each blossom along with a minute section of the pale-yellow style. To produce a single kilogram of dried commercial saffron, harvesters must hand-pick and manually process between 150,000 and 200,000 individual flowers, representing roughly forty to fifty hours of intense physical labor.

E. Immediately after separation, the fresh, damp stigmas must undergo a rapid thermal dehydration and curing process to lock in their sensory qualities and prevent biological mold growth. In traditional Persian, Spanish, and Kashmiri methodologies, stigmas are spread across fine silk screens or mesh sieves suspended over gentle charcoal braziers, wood-fired hearths, or electric dehydrators at temperatures ranging between 50°C and 70°C for thirty to sixty minutes. During this thermal curing, three primary bioactive compounds undergo enzymatic and thermal synthesis: 1. Crocin: A water-soluble carotenoid pigment responsible for saffron’s brilliant golden- yellow coloring capacity. 2. Picrocrocin: A bitter monoterpene glucoside that imparts saffron''s characteristic earthy, bitter taste. 3. Safranal: A volatile, aromatic aldehyde synthesized via the thermal hydrolysis and degradation of picrocrocin, providing saffron’s powerful, sweet, hay-like aroma.

F. Throughout antiquity, saffron was an essential luxury commodity traded across maritime and terrestrial Silk Road networks. Frescoes preserved beneath volcanic ash at the Bronze Age settlement of Akrotiri on the Aegean island of Santorini (dating to circa 1600 BCE) depict young women harvesting saffron blossoms for religious offerings and herbal medicine. In ancient Persia, King Darius the Great and Cyrus the Great wore robes dyed with royal saffron, while saffron-infused oils were used to scent royal banquets and imperial baths. During the fourteenth century in Europe, the economic importance of the spice was highlighted by the "Saffron War" of 1374, triggered when a shipment of eight hundred pounds of saffron bound for Basel merchants was hijacked by a renegade nobleman, sparking a fourteen-week military standoff.

G. In the contemporary global market, Iran dominates international production, accounting for over 85 to 90 percent of global supply, predominantly concentrated in the arid eastern provinces of Razavi and South Khorasan. Other historic producers, including Spain (La Mancha), Greece (Kozani), and the Kashmir Valley in India, produce high-grade varieties protected by international Geographical Indications (GIs). However, because of saffron''s high market value, commercial fraud remains pervasive: dishonest merchants routinely adulterate powdered or whole saffron with dyed safflower (Carthamus tinctorius), marigold petals, corn silk, or synthetic coal-tar dyes like tartrazine. Modern food regulatory bodies deploy high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) and spectrophotometric assays under ISO 3632 standards to ensure that the ancient "red gold" retains its legendary chemical authenticity.'
);

