-- Passage 1: The Development and Construction of Medieval Trebuchets
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '60ff388b-1edc-5018-9cd5-173f118ea004', '8a6d8967-fdf8-5d3b-aa3e-75600dbf9f0c', 1, 'The Development and Construction of Medieval Trebuchets',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the battlefields of medieval Eurasia and the Mediterranean basin, the siege of fortified stone castles and walled cities was the defining operational challenge of military engineering. Throughout classical antiquity, Greek and Roman legions had relied primarily on torsion-powered artillery—such as the ballista and onager—which derived kinetic energy from the mechanical twisting of tightly wound skeins of animal sinew, horsehair, or hemp ropes. However, torsion artillery suffered from inherent physical limitations: organic skeins were exceptionally vulnerable to humidity, rotting in damp weather and losing elasticity, and could not be scaled up to launch projectiles heavy enough to breach thick, mortared stone curtain walls. The weapon that fundamentally revolutionized siege warfare was the gravity-powered counterweight trebuchet.

B. The historical evolution of the trebuchet progressed through two major technological stages: the traction trebuchet and the counterweight trebuchet. The traction trebuchet (known in Chinese as the pao or mangonel) originated in ancient China during the Warring States period (circa fourth century BCE) and was transmitted westward along the Silk Road and through the Islamic world by the sixth and seventh centuries CE. In a traction trebuchet, the short arm of a pivoting wooden lever beam was fitted with multiple pulling ropes hauled manually by a coordinated crew of several dozen to over a hundred men. While capable of firing rapidly—launching small stone shot weighing twenty to fifty kilograms up to several times a minute—the machine’s maximum throwing range and payload mass were strictly limited by the finite physical muscle power of its human pulling team.

C. The critical mechanical breakthrough occurred during the late twelfth century, developing simultaneously across the Byzantine Empire, the Islamic Levant, and Western Europe: the invention of the counterweight trebuchet (frequently termed the trebuchet, derived from the Old French trébucher, meaning to overthrow or stumble). In this definitive design, human muscle power was replaced by a colossal static counterweight box—a heavy timber chest filled with fifty to twenty metric tons of dense inert ballast, such as compacted soil, river gravel, sand, or shaped lead ingots—suspended from the short arm of an asymmetrical timber beam.

D. The mechanical operation of the counterweight trebuchet relies on the fundamental physics of a Class 1 lever paired with a whip-like hinged sling. The main pivoting throwing beam— typically constructed from massive, straight-grained trunks of resilient timber such as ash, elm, or oak—pivoted on a heavy forged iron axle resting atop a towering, cross-braced timber A- frame chassis. The beam was deliberately constructed with an asymmetrical length ratio, typically ranging between 1:4 and 1:6: the short arm holding the counterweight was short and thick to withstand immense compressive forces, while the long arm was elongated and tapered toward the tip to maximize tip velocity at release.

E. A crucial aerodynamic component that amplified projectile velocity was the flexible rope- and-leather sling attached to the end of the long arm. One end of the sling was anchored permanently to the beam tip, while the opposite end terminated in an iron ring that slipped loosely over a curved metal release prong (or guide hook). When the beam was winched down using heavy manual capstans or giant squirrel-cage treadwheels, the sling and its projectile lay along a smooth, grooved wooden trough on the ground beneath the chassis. Upon triggering the release trigger, the colossal counterweight dropped vertically, accelerating the beam through an upward arc. As the beam rotated past the vertical position, centrifugal force caused the iron ring to slide off the curved prong, opening the pouch and releasing the projectile with the amplified velocity of a mechanical whip.

F. Counterweight trebuchets possessed extraordinary destructive capabilities. Large machines could reliably hurl dressed limestone projectiles weighing between one hundred and three hundred kilograms across distances of two hundred to three hundred meters. The kinetic impact of a 200-kilogram stone traveling at high velocity generated massive crushing force, capable of shattering high stone battlements, toppling defensive towers, and creating breaches through multi-meter-thick stone curtain walls. Furthermore, trebuchets were deployed for psychological and biological warfare: besieging armies routinely used them to fling incendiary Greek fire ceramic pots, severed enemy heads, dead horses, and diseased animal carcasses over city walls to induce terror and spread lethal epidemics such as dysentery and bubonic plague among the garrison.

G. The supreme historical culmination of trebuchet engineering was the Warwolf (Loup de Guerre), constructed in 1304 by order of King Edward I of England during the siege of Stirling Castle in Scotland. Masterminded by royal master carpenter Master Reginald, the Warwolf was the largest siege engine ever built in medieval history. Constructed using the timber of dozens of giant oak trees and hauled to the siege lines on thirty wagons, the colossal machine required a crew of fifty carpenters working for three months to assemble. Its counterweight box held an estimated twenty metric tons of stone ballast, enabling it to fling 150-kilogram stone balls that demolished entire sections of Stirling Castle’s massive curtain walls, forcing the Scottish garrison to surrender unconditionally. By the late fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, the advent of gunpowder artillery and cast-iron bombards gradually made the counterweight trebuchet obsolete, ending two centuries of wooden mechanical dominance on European battlefields.'
);

