-- Passage 2: Carbon Nanotube Membranes for Desalination and Molecular Sieving
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '3f37e020-28e3-5df4-9846-3c015b5fb420', '8a6d8967-fdf8-5d3b-aa3e-75600dbf9f0c', 2, 'Carbon Nanotube Membranes for Desalination and Molecular Sieving',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. As global populations expand, industrial water demands escalate, and climate change- induced droughts intensify across arid and semi-arid regions, access to freshwater has emerged as one of the paramount humanitarian and ecological crises of the 21st century. Although seawater and brackish groundwater represent more than 97 percent of Earth''s total water reserves, converting saline water into potable drinking water remains an energy-intensive industrial process. Modern commercial seawater desalination relies predominantly on Seawater Reverse Osmosis (SWRO). In SWRO facilities, high-pressure pumps force saline feed water across synthetic polyamide thin-film composite (TFC) membranes at pressures exceeding 55 to 70 bar, filtering out dissolved sodium ($Na^+$) and chloride ($Cl^-$) ions while allowing freshwater molecules to pass through.

B. While commercial polyamide SWRO membranes have achieved widespread industrial adoption since the 1980s, they have reached fundamental thermodynamic and physical performance plateaus. Conventional polyamide active layers possess highly irregular, tortuous, and disordered nanoscale sub-structures. Because water molecules must navigate winding, narrow, and friction-laden pathways through the polymer matrix, polyamide membranes suffer from relatively low water permeability. To force high volumetric flux across the membrane, desalination plants consume vast quantities of electrical energy to drive high-pressure positive displacement pumps, resulting in high operational expenditures (OpEx) and substantial indirect carbon emissions. Furthermore, polyamide chemistry is exceptionally vulnerable to chemical degradation from chlorine disinfectants and prone to severe organic biofouling.

C. To overcome these structural boundaries, materials scientists and chemical engineers are developing next-generation biomimetic separation membranes centered on aligned Carbon Nanotubes (CNTs). A carbon nanotube is a cylindrical allotrope of carbon, conceptually formed by rolling a single-atom-thick sheet of graphene into a seamless, hollow cylinder. Depending on the rolling angle and diameter, CNTs can be synthesized as single-walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNTs) with internal pore diameters ranging from 0.8 to 2.0 nanometers. When aligned vertically and embedded within an impermeable polymer or ceramic barrier matrix, these nanoscopic hollow cylinders act as frictionless, molecular-scale water transport channels.

D. The fundamental physical phenomenon that makes carbon nanotube channels transformative for water purification is the "frictionless water slip" effect. In conventional macroscopic fluid mechanics, fluid flowing through a narrow pipe obeys the classical "no-slip" boundary condition, where viscous friction between the liquid molecules and the pipe wall slows fluid velocity to zero at the interface. Inside a smooth, atomically pristine carbon nanotube, however, the internal wall consists of an uninterrupted $sp^2$-hybridized carbon lattice. This non-polar, atomically smooth graphitic surface exhibits virtually zero frictional drag and extremely low adhesion toward polar water molecules.

E. Furthermore, when confined within sub-two-nanometer cylindrical channels, water molecules reorganize into single-file, hydrogen-bonded molecular chains (often termed "water wires"). Driven by rapid collective hydrogen-bond fluctuations, water molecules glide through the nanotube interior at velocities three to four orders of magnitude faster than predicted by classical continuum fluid dynamics (Hagen-Poiseuille law). This ultra-fast hydrodynamic slip enables CNT-based membranes to achieve water permeability rates upwards of ten to one hundred times higher than state-of-the-art commercial polyamide membranes, promising to drastically reduce the hydraulic pressure and electrical energy required for large-scale desalination.

F. In addition to ultra-fast water flux, sub-nanometer carbon nanotubes achieve precise molecular sieving and salt rejection through a combination of steric size-exclusion and electrostatic charge repulsion (Donnan exclusion). With internal pore diameters calibrated between 0.8 and 1.0 nanometers, the channel is physically narrower than the hydrated ionic diameters of common dissolved salts (a hydrated sodium ion has a diameter of roughly 0.72 nm, while a hydrated chloride ion measures roughly 0.66 nm). To enter the channel, an ion must shed its surrounding shell of coordinating water molecules—a process requiring high dehydration energy. Furthermore, by chemically functionalizing the entrance rims of the nanotubes with negatively charged carboxylate ($-COO^-$) or zwitterionic chemical groups, the membrane electrostatically repels negatively charged chloride ions, achieving salt rejection rates exceeding 99 percent.

G. Despite these revolutionary theoretical and laboratory capabilities, transitioning CNT membranes to commercial mass production confronts formidable chemical and manufacturing hurdles. Foremost among these is achieving high-density, defect-free vertical alignment of sub- nanometer SWCNTs across large surface areas. Synthesizing billions of nanotubes per square centimeter via chemical vapor deposition (CVD) and sealing the intervening interstitial gaps between nanotubes with impermeable polymers without blocking the microscopic channel openings requires atomic-scale precision. Furthermore, the high capital cost of raw CNT materials remains a major barrier. However, as scalable roll-to-roll CVD synthesis and block copolymer self-assembly methods mature, carbon nanotube membranes are poised to redefine global water purification, providing high-efficiency, low-energy desalination to quench the thirst of a changing planet.'
);

