-- Passage 3: The Hard Problem of Consciousness and the Explanatory Gap
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '318bf60b-5aee-5abc-a990-47b2354c2009', '8a6d8967-fdf8-5d3b-aa3e-75600dbf9f0c', 3, 'The Hard Problem of Consciousness and the Explanatory Gap',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In contemporary philosophy of mind, cognitive neuroscience, and analytical metaphysics, no inquiry is as profoundly perplexing as the nature of conscious subjective experience. In recent decades, cognitive science and neurobiology have achieved extraordinary breakthroughs in mapping the neurological underpinnings of human behavior. High-resolution functional neuroimaging, optogenetics, and electrophysiology have decoded how sensory receptors transduce environmental stimuli, how neural networks process visual information, and how motor circuits execute physical actions. Yet, despite these triumphs, a vast, seemingly unbridgeable conceptual divide persists: how and why do physical electrochemical processes in the biological brain give rise to subjective inner experience—the rich "what it is like" of consciousness?

B. In 1995, Australian philosopher and cognitive scientist David Chalmers crystallized this metaphysical challenge by formulating his famous distinction between the "Easy Problems" and the "Hard Problem" of consciousness in his seminal paper Facing Up to the Problem of Consciousness. The "Easy Problems" are those that concern the functional, computational, and behavioral mechanisms of the mind. These include explaining the neural mechanisms of sensory discrimination, the integration of information across brain regions, the focus of attention, the reportability of mental states, and the control of voluntary behavior. Chalmers labeled these problems "easy" not because their scientific solution is trivial, but because they are standard empirical questions: they can be completely addressed using conventional methods of cognitive neuroscience and computational modeling.

C. In stark contrast stands the "Hard Problem": the challenge of explaining phenomenal consciousness (qualia)—the felt, subjective qualities of conscious experience. Phenomenal consciousness encompasses the vivid redness of a ripe strawberry, the agonizing sharpness of a burn, the melancholic sweetness of a musical minor chord, or the subjective feeling of joy. Chalmers pointed out that even if neuroscience provides a complete, atom-by-atom physical description of all the neural firings, neurotransmitter releases, and synaptic oscillations that occur when an individual views a red object, an irreducible question remains: why should all this complex physical processing be accompanied by any subjective experience at all? Why couldn''t an organism process all that functional data in total subjective darkness?

D. The historical and metaphysical root of the Hard Problem is the "Explanatory Gap," a philosophical concept formulated in 1983 by philosopher Joseph Levine. Levine demonstrated that while scientific reductions in other domains explain how physical structures constitute macroscopic phenomena—such as explaining how the molecular kinetic motion of $H_2O$ molecules is temperature, or how electrical discharges are lightning—no such explanatory bridge exists between physical brain states and subjective feelings. We can fully understand why vibrating molecules cause heat, but there is no intelligible, deductive necessity linking the firing of C-fibers in the nervous system to the agonizing, subjective feeling of physical pain. The physical facts do not explain why pain feels like pain.

E. To demonstrate that phenomenal consciousness cannot be logically reduced to physical or functional computation, Chalmers revitalized the "Philosophical Zombie" (p-zombie) thought experiment. A philosophical zombie is a hypothetical entity that is physically and behaviorally identical to a normal human being in every microscopic detail—possessing the exact same brain architecture, neurotransmitter chemistry, and linguistic responses. If you pinch a zombie, its neural circuits fire identically to a human, and it yells "Ouch!" Yet, the zombie possesses zero inner life; it is entirely devoid of phenomenal consciousness, experiencing nothing behind its eyes. Chalmers argued that because philosophical zombies are logically conceivable (there is no internal logical contradiction in imagining a physical duplicate without conscious experience), physicalism—the metaphysical thesis that the universe is entirely physical—must be false.

F. Chalmers’ Hard Problem and the zombie argument provoked immediate, fierce counter- offensives from orthodox physicalist philosophers and computational neuroscientists, most notably Daniel Dennett and Patricia Churchland. Dennett famously argued that the Hard Problem is a philosophical illusion generated by faulty Cartesian intuitions. In Consciousness Explained (1991), Dennett asserted that once cognitive science has solved all the so-called "Easy Problems"—explaining all the functional mechanisms of attention, sensory processing, verbal report, and motor control—there is simply nothing left over to explain. In Dennett’s view, qualia are mythical, non-existent entities (analogous to the ancient concept of "vital life force" or "phlogiston"), and conscious experience is nothing more than the functional operation of multiple parallel computational drafts within the brain.

G. Caught between the apparent failure of reductive physicalism and the absurdity of denying conscious experience, contemporary philosophy of mind has explored radical alternative metaphysical paradigms. Foremost among these is Panpsychism, championed by philosophers like Galen Strawson and Philip Goff. Panpsychism proposes that consciousness is not an emergent property that magically appears when physical matter reaches high biological complexity; rather, proto-conscious experience is a fundamental, ubiquitous feature of the universe, present in elementary particles (such as quarks and electrons) alongside mass, electrical charge, and spin. In this view, the human mind is formed by the structured combination of these fundamental experiential constituents.

H. In synthesizing the modern debate, the Hard Problem of Consciousness remains one of the greatest intellectual frontiers of human thought. Whether the mystery is ultimately resolved through an expanded post-physicalist science, a breakthrough in integrated information theory, or a fundamental rethinking of how mind and matter interact, consciousness remains the primary lens through which we encounter reality. Without subjective conscious experience, the physical cosmos would exist in total silence and darkness; it is phenomenal consciousness that infuses the universe with meaning, value, and beauty.'
);

