-- Passage 1: The Domestication and Agricultural Role of the Reindeer
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'fc46ad28-4d3a-571c-8b73-a926ec7985cc', '4ab8cbf3-6be8-5c90-ab48-0f72331871c8', 1, 'The Domestication and Agricultural Role of the Reindeer',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the vast, circumpolar tundra and boreal taiga biomes of the Eurasian sub-Arctic— stretching from the Scandinavian mountains of northern Norway, Sweden, and Finland through the northern expanses of Siberia to the Chukchi Peninsula—few animal species have played as foundational a role in human cultural survival and high-latitude pastoralism as the reindeer (Rangifer tarandus). In an unforgiving polar climate where continuous permafrost and brief, frost-prone growing seasons make agrarian crop cultivation impossible, indigenous Arctic societies developed a complex, symbiotic relationship with this cold-adapted ungulate. Known as reindeer in Eurasia and caribou in North America, Rangifer tarandus provided northern pastoralists—including the Sámi of Fennoscandia, the Nenets of the Yamal Peninsula, the Evenki of central Siberia, and the Chukchi of the Far East—with an all-encompassing suite of vital resources: transport, nutrient-rich red meat, milk, hide clothing, sinew cordage, and bone tools.

B. The evolutionary success of the reindeer within the extreme conditions of the high Arctic is rooted in an extraordinary suite of morphological and physiological specializations. Unlike any other member of the deer family (Cervidae), both male and female reindeer grow large, branching antlers. Females retain their hard antlers throughout the harsh winter months, shedding them only after giving birth in the spring; this retention allows pregnant females to aggressively defend scarce feeding craters excavated in the snow from larger, antlerless males (who shed their antlers in early winter). To survive months of winter darkness, the reindeer’s eyes undergo a seasonal tapetal shift, changing from a golden hue in the sunny summer to a deep turquoise-blue in the dark winter. This physiological alteration increases optical scattering, boosting retinal sensitivity to detect ultraviolet (UV) wavelengths reflected across snowy landscapes.

C. Furthermore, reindeer possess highly specialized pelage and metabolic adaptations to combat sub-zero temperatures that regularly plunge below minus forty degrees Celsius. Their dense winter coat consists of two layers: a dense, woolly underfur beneath an outer layer of hollow, air- filled guard hairs that trap dead air to provide supreme thermal insulation while preventing body heat from escaping. Their hooves act as dynamic seasonal tools: in summer, the footpads become spongy to navigate soft, waterlogged peat bogs; in winter, the footpads shrink and tighten, exposing the sharp, crescent-shaped outer rim of the hoof. These hardened hoof rims allow the animal to gain traction on smooth glacial ice and "crater"—dig through meters of packed snow to reach their primary winter sustenance: fruticose ground lichens, commonly termed "reindeer moss" (Cladonia rangiferina).

D. The archaeological and ethnohistorical origins of reindeer domestication reflect a gradual transition from intensive hunting to managed pastoralism. Archaeological excavations of prehistoric hunting pit arrays and rock art across northern Scandinavia and Siberia demonstrate that early Holocene hunter-gatherers tracked wild reindeer herds along their natural seasonal migration routes. By the late Iron Age and early medieval period (circa 800 to 1500 CE), indigenous communities shifted from opportunistic hunting to decoy hunting using tame female animals, followed by the intensive corralling and selective breeding of semi-domesticated herds. Unlike fully domesticated farm animals such as cattle or sheep, reindeer were never subjected to strict phenotypic enclosure; rather, pastoralism evolved as a system of nomadic or semi-nomadic transhumance, where human families followed the seasonal migration of their herds between inland winter taiga forests and coastal or alpine summer pastures.

E. In traditional reindeer pastoralism, the herd served as the core economic unit and the primary source of technology. Reindeer were trained as draft and pack animals, harnessed to lightweight wooden sleds (pulks or narty) capable of gliding over deep snow and frozen rivers with remarkable efficiency. Strong male steers (castrated males or bullocks) were chosen for their stamina and calm temperament, allowing herders to travel hundreds of kilometers across trackless wilderness. Reindeer hides, stitched together with waterproof thread made from dried leg sinews, formed the insulating outer skin of portable conical tents (the Sámi lávvu and the Nenets chum), as well as multi-layered thermal boots (fur boots or nutuki) and parkas that protected families from lethal frostbite.

F. Reindeer pastoralism is characterized by diverse regional strategies across the Eurasian continent. In western Fennoscandia, the Sámi developed intensive dairy and meat pastoralism, utilizing specialized lasso techniques (suohpan) and trained herding dogs to manage small, highly managed herds. In contrast, across the vast Siberian tundra, the Nenets and Chukchi practiced extensive pastoralism, managing massive nomadic herds numbering several thousand animals that migrated over annual distances exceeding one thousand kilometers along the Ob and Yenisei river basins. In the mountainous taiga of southern Siberia and northern Mongolia, groups such as the Dukha (Tsaatan) and Evenki rode large, domestic pack reindeer directly like horses, relying on them for milking and seasonal hunting transport rather than large-scale meat slaughter.

G. In the 21st century, traditional reindeer pastoralism faces unprecedented socio-ecological pressures driven by accelerated Arctic climate change and expanding industrial extraction. Arctic surface temperatures are rising at nearly four times the global average, triggering frequent "rain- on-snow" events in mid-winter. When rain falls on existing snowpacks and subsequently freezes, it forms an impenetrable, rock-hard sheet of basal ground ice that reindeer cannot penetrate with their hooves, leading to catastrophic mass starvation events (icing events or krust). Furthermore, the encroachment of oil and natural gas pipelines, open-pit mineral mines, wind turbine arrays, and railway corridors across traditional migration routes fragments ancestral grazing pastures. In response, indigenous herders are integrating satellite GPS telemetry and drone tracking with ancestral ecological knowledge, fighting to preserve a timeless biocultural way of life that continues to define the human spirit of the circumpolar North.'
);

