-- Passage 2: Direct Air Carbon Capture (DACC) Solid-Sorbent Systems
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '33a8dac9-5964-5da7-b009-d91eccdd06c1', '4ab8cbf3-6be8-5c90-ab48-0f72331871c8', 2, 'Direct Air Carbon Capture (DACC) Solid-Sorbent Systems',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. As global atmospheric carbon dioxide ($CO_2$) concentrations surpass 420 parts per million (ppm)—a level unprecedented in human history—international climate frameworks emphasize that avoiding catastrophic planetary warming requires not only aggressive emission reductions, but also active carbon dioxide removal (CDR) from ambient air. While conventional point-source carbon capture isolates $CO_2$ directly from concentrated industrial flue gases (such as cement kilns and steel mills where $CO_2$ concentrations range from 10 to 30 percent), Direct Air Carbon Capture (DACC) targets ambient, unconstrained atmospheric air. Capturing $CO_2$ directly from ambient air represents an extraordinary chemical and thermodynamic challenge: because $CO_2$ is present at an ultra-dilute volumetric concentration of roughly 0.04 percent (420 ppm), a DACC facility must process vast air volumes—often millions of cubic meters of air per ton of captured $CO_2$—with minimal energy expenditure.

B. Commercial DACC engineering has crystallized around two competing chemical approaches: liquid solvent systems and solid-sorbent systems. Liquid solvent platforms (such as high-temperature aqueous potassium hydroxide loops) require massive chemical contactor towers and energy-intensive calcination furnaces operating above 900°C to release captured $CO_2$. In contrast, solid-sorbent DACC systems utilize porous solid materials chemically functionalized with basic chemical groups that selectively bind acidic $CO_2$ molecules at ambient temperatures. The primary thermodynamic advantage of solid-sorbent systems is their significantly lower regeneration temperature (typically between 80°C and 120°C). This low thermal footprint allows solid-sorbent facilities to be powered entirely by low-grade industrial waste heat, geothermal energy, or co-located solar thermal arrays.

C. The operational heart of a solid-sorbent DACC facility is the adsorption-desorption contactor unit. A contactor typically consists of a large, modular array of motorized fans that draw high volumes of ambient air across porous structured packing or honeycomb monolith matrices coated with solid sorbents. The most effective solid sorbents are amine-functionalized materials: high- surface-area porous substrates—such as mesoporous silica ($SiO_2$), metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), or cellulose nanofibers—chemically grafted or impregnated with organic polyamines, most commonly polyethylenimine (PEI) or amino-silanes. When ambient air passes through the matrix, basic amine groups ($-NH_2$) react chemically with acidic $CO_2$ gas molecules, forming stable chemical carbamate and bicarbonate species through chemisorption.

D. Once the solid sorbent reaches chemical saturation (the adsorption phase, which typically takes one to four hours), the contactor enters the regeneration (desorption) phase. The inlet and outlet air louvers are sealed to create an airtight vacuum chamber. Desorption is accomplished through a process known as Temperature-Vacuum Swing Adsorption (TVSA). An external heat source delivers thermal energy (steam or hot water at 95°C to 105°C) into the chamber while vacuum pumps lower the internal pressure to sub-atmospheric levels. The combination of elevated heat and reduced pressure destabilizes the chemical bonds between the amine groups and $CO_2$, releasing pure, concentrated $CO_2$ gas ($>99\%$ purity) into collection pipelines. Once desorption is complete, the chamber is cooled and re-opened to ambient air, restarting the continuous adsorption cycle.

E. Despite the energetic elegance of TVSA systems, solid-sorbent DACC confronts severe physical, chemical, and environmental degradation hurdles. The foremost operational challenge is competitive moisture co-adsorption. Ambient air naturally contains water vapor ($H_2O$) at concentrations that are often ten to fifty times higher than $CO_2$. Because basic amine sites also exhibit an affinity for water molecules, solid sorbents co-adsorb substantial quantities of ambient humidity during the adsorption phase. Desorbing this co-adsorbed water during the heating cycle consumes large amounts of latent heat of vaporization, substantially inflating the overall thermal energy demand of the capture process.

F. Furthermore, solid amine sorbents suffer from irreversible chemical degradation over prolonged operational cycles due to atmospheric contaminants. Continuous exposure to atmospheric oxygen ($O_2$) at elevated desorption temperatures induces oxidative degradation of the polyamine chains, forming volatile toxic degradation byproducts (such as aldehydes, formamides, and nitrosamines) that deactivate active amine binding sites. Additionally, acidic air pollutants present in ambient air—predominantly sulfur dioxide ($SO_2$) and nitrogen oxides ($NO_x$)—form irreversible, heat-stable salts with the amine groups, causing gradual, permanent loss of $CO_2$ capture capacity. To maintain high capture efficiency, facilities must implement upstream pollutant scrubbers and replace or chemically regenerate the solid sorbent every two to four years.

G. Once concentrated $CO_2$ is desorbed and collected, it is compressed to high pressures (typically exceeding 100 bar) for long-term geological sequestration or utilization. The leading storage methodology is subterranean mineralization, pioneered by commercial initiatives like the Orca and Mammoth facilities in Iceland. In this process, compressed $CO_2$ is dissolved in deep groundwater and injected into reactive subterranean basalt rock formations at depths exceeding one thousand meters. Within two years, the dissolved $CO_2$ reacts chemically with calcium, magnesium, and iron ions naturally present in the volcanic basalt, permanently precipitating as solid, immobile carbonate minerals (such as calcite, $CaCO_3$). By locking atmospheric carbon into solid stone, solid-sorbent DACC provides a secure, permanent, and verifiable pathway toward global negative emissions.'
);

