-- Passage 3: Bayesian Epistemology and the Problem of Old Evidence
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'f820d74b-bb50-5446-ad13-7bef6e3c1b7e', '4ab8cbf3-6be8-5c90-ab48-0f72331871c8', 3, 'Bayesian Epistemology and the Problem of Old Evidence',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the philosophy of science and formal epistemology, the central mathematical framework used to model rational belief revision and scientific confirmation is Bayesian Epistemology. Rooted in the eighteenth-century probability calculus of the English statistician and Presbyterian minister Thomas Bayes, Bayesianism conceptualizes an agent''s mental state as a probability distribution of subjective credences (degrees of belief) assigned to various hypotheses. Under this probabilistic paradigm, a rational scientist begins with a set of "prior probabilities" ($P(H)$), and upon acquiring new empirical evidence ($E$), updates their beliefs to a "posterior probability" ($P(H\vert{}E)$) through the strict mathematical formula known as Bayes’ Theorem: $$P(H\vert{}E) = \frac{P(E\vert{}H) \cdot P(H)}{P(E)}$$ In Bayesian confirmation theory, empirical evidence $E$ confirms hypothesis $H$ if and only if learning $E$ increases the probability of $H$—that is, if the posterior probability is strictly greater than the prior probability ($P(H\vert{}E) > P(H)$).

B. For decades, Bayesian confirmation theory enjoyed undisputed prominence in analytical philosophy, celebrated for its ability to provide a unified, mathematically rigorous foundation for scientific reasoning. Bayesian models successfully resolved several long-standing philosophical puzzles, such as Carl Hempel''s Paradox of the Ravens and Nelson Goodman''s New Riddle of Induction. By demonstrating how incremental empirical evidence gradually eliminates competing hypotheses and drives rational consensus across diverse scientific communities, Bayesianism appeared to offer a complete mathematical calculus of inductive scientific logic.

C. In 1980, however, American philosopher of science Clark Glymour exposed a profound, seemingly intractable mathematical vulnerability at the core of standard Bayesian confirmation theory in his landmark book Theory and Evidence: the celebrated "Problem of Old Evidence." Glymour pointed out that in the actual history of science, major scientific theories are frequently confirmed not by predicting novel, previously unknown experimental outcomes, but by successfully explaining old, well-established empirical anomalies that were already known to the scientific community decades or centuries before the theory was conceived.

D. The pre-eminent historical exemplar of this phenomenon is Albert Einstein’s General Theory of Relativity, formulated in 1915. For more than sixty years prior to Einstein''s breakthrough, astronomers had been baffled by a known anomaly in celestial mechanics: the anomalous perihelion precession of the planet Mercury. According to Newtonian gravitational mechanics, the elliptical orbit of Mercury slowly rotates around the sun due to the gravitational perturbations of other planets; however, precise astronomical observations revealed that Mercury’s orbit precessed by an extra forty-three arcseconds per century that Newtonian gravity could not account for. In November 1915, when Einstein discovered that his newly formulated field equations of General Relativity predicted an exact anomalous orbital advance of forty-three arcseconds per century without requiring any adjustable parameters, the scientific community instantly recognized this as stunning, definitive confirmation of the new theory.

E. Glymour demonstrated that standard Bayesian confirmation theory is mathematically incapable of accounting for this monumental historical confirmation. Consider the status of the evidence ($E$, the forty-three arcseconds anomaly) in November 1915. Because this astronomical fact had been universally known and accepted by the scientific community for more than half a century, the probability of the evidence was already certainty: $P(E) = 1$. Furthermore, because Einstein knew that the anomalous precession existed when constructing his calculations, the conditional probability of the evidence given the hypothesis was also unity: $P(E\vert{}H) = 1$. When these values are inserted into Bayes’ Theorem, a devastating mathematical paralysis occurs: $$P(H\vert{}E) = \frac{1 \cdot P(H)}{1} = P(H)$$ Because the posterior probability ($P(H\vert{}E)$) is strictly identical to the prior probability ($P(H)$), standard Bayesian confirmation theory mathematically dictates that the old evidence provided zero confirmation ($P(H\vert{}E) - P(H) = 0$) to General Relativity—a conclusion that directly contradicts one of the greatest triumphs in the history of science.

F. To rescue Bayesian epistemology from Glymour’s paradox, contemporary Bayesian philosophers have advanced several formal strategies, most notably the "Counterfactual Strategy" pioneered by Colin Howson and Peter Urbach. Proponents of the Counterfactual Strategy argue that when evaluating the evidential support of old evidence $E$ for hypothesis $H$, a Bayesian agent must not use their actual, historical background knowledge where $P(E) = 1$. Instead, the agent must perform a counterfactual thought experiment: subtract $E$ from their current background knowledge base, asking what their prior probability for $E$ would have been ($P^*(E)$) in an idealized historical state where the anomalous data was not yet known. By setting $P^*(E) < 1$, Bayes'' Theorem yields a posterior probability strictly greater than the prior, restoring confirmation for old evidence.

G. However, the Counterfactual Strategy has provoked fierce criticism from non-Bayesians, such as John Earman and Peter Achinstein. Critics point out that determining what an idealized historical scientist would have believed in the total absence of known evidence requires subjective, historically ungrounded guesswork. How can a modern epistemologist objectively reconstruct the precise prior probability that an astronomer in 1850 would have assigned to Mercury’s orbit without knowing astronomical observations? Furthermore, psychological studies show that human beings cannot completely erase known empirical facts from their cognitive models without distorting their entire structure of background beliefs.

H. In synthesizing the debate, modern philosophy of science recognizes that the Problem of Old Evidence exposes the limits of viewing scientific confirmation purely through subjective, temporal probability updates. Scientific confirmation is not merely a psychological tally of how an individual’s confidence shifts over time; it is an objective, structural relationship between a mathematical theory and the physical world. When a unified, non-ad-hoc theory naturally explains complex empirical anomalies without artificially engineering post-hoc parameters, that explanatory coherence provides powerful evidential support, regardless of the historical date on which the data was first recorded.'
);

