-- Passage 1: The Traditional Manufacture of Japanese Washi Paper
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'c8c74902-1ad3-525c-af55-511f7bbc7ff3', 'ce519c5b-dc34-58df-801a-3ddd2fb47d4d', 1, 'The Traditional Manufacture of Japanese Washi Paper',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For more than thirteen centuries across rural Japan, the craft of hand-making paper (washi) has represented a refined harmony of botanical knowledge, pyrotechnology, and seasonal hydrochemistry. Introduced to the Japanese archipelago from mainland Asia in the early seventh century CE—traditionally attributed by the ancient chronicle Nihon Shoki to the Korean Buddhist monk Damjing (Doncho) in 610 CE—papermaking quickly evolved beyond the rigid, thick hemp papers of ancient China. Japanese craftsmen adapted the technique to utilize the resilient bast (inner bark) fibers of indigenous mountain shrubs, developing a paper celebrated for its supple texture, lightweight strength, translucent luster, and extraordinary longevity. In 2014, UNESCO recognized this cultural heritage by inscribing traditional Japanese handmade papermaking (Tesuki Washi) onto the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.

B. The superior physical durability and tensile resilience of authentic washi are directly dictated by the botanical species selected for its raw fiber. Unlike modern industrial Western papers, which are manufactured from mechanically ground or chemically bleached wood pulp containing short, fragile cellulose fibers (typically measuring 1 to 3 millimeters in length) bound with acidic sizing agents, traditional washi relies exclusively on the long, flexible phloem fibers harvested from three primary deciduous shrubs: 1. Kozo (Paper Mulberry, Broussonetia papyrifera): The most widely utilized fiber, kozo produces exceptionally long, sinewy, and robust fibers measuring between 10 and 20 millimeters in length. Its high alpha-cellulose content and interlocking fiber geometry endow washi with exceptional tear strength, dimensional stability, and resistance to water degradation. 2. Mitsumata (Edgeworthia chrysantha): Introduced during the Edo period, mitsumata yields softer, finer, and shorter fibers that impart a delicate, warm-buff tone and an exceptionally smooth, glossy surface ideal for fine calligraphy and official government banknotes. 3. Gampi (Diplomorpha sikokiana): Harvested exclusively from wild mountain slopes because it defies agricultural cultivation, gampi produces lustrous, translucent, and non- porous paper that is naturally resistant to insect predation and moisture decay.

C. The fabrication of washi is an arduous, multi-stage winter craft traditionally conducted during the coldest months of the year (kanzuki, or cold-season making). The process begins in late autumn with the harvesting of one-to-two-year-old stems. The cut branches are bundled and placed inside a wooden steaming barrel suspended over an open cauldron of boiling water for several hours. Steaming softens the outer bark, allowing workers to peel the outer layer from the inner core in continuous strips. The peeled bark is then separated into three distinct layers: the rough, dark-brown outer bark (kurokawa), the intermediate green middle layer (nakakawa), and the pristine, ivory-white inner bast (shirokawa). Only the pure shirokawa is retained for the highest grades of fine paper.

D. The white inner bast fibers undergo a critical natural bleaching and washing process known as kawazarashi (river bleaching) or yukinzarashi (snow bleaching). In snowy mountain regions such as Echigo (modern Niigata Prefecture), the moist fibers are spread across deep snowbanks and exposed to intense winter sunlight for several days. As ultraviolet solar radiation strikes the snow, it generates reactive ozone ($O_3$) and hydrogen peroxide ($H_2O_2$) through photochemical reactions, naturally bleaching the fibers to a luminous white without degrading the cellulose structure. Following bleaching, the fibers are simmered for two to three hours in large iron or copper cauldrons with mild alkaline solutions leached from wood ash (potassium carbonate) or soda ash to dissolve non-cellulosic impurities, pectins, and lignins.

E. Once softened and rinsed in freezing river streams, the remaining fiber bundles undergo manual sorting (chiri-tori), where artisans sit beside cold water basins for hours, meticulously plucking out microscopic specks of residual bark, knots, and insect damage using fine bamboo tweezers. The cleaned fibers are then placed onto a heavy hardwood log or granite slab and subjected to vigorous mechanical beating (kouzo-uchi). Artisans strike the fiber mass rhythmically with heavy oak mallets for several hours, separating the tightly clustered fiber bundles into individual, free-floating micro-fibrils without fracturing the longitudinal length of the cellulose chains.

F. The defining technological leap that distinguishes traditional Japanese papermaking from all other global methods is the sheet-forming process known as nagashizuki (discharge papermaking), which utilizes a specialized mucilaginous formation aid called neri. Extracted from the crushed roots of the tororo-aoi plant (a species of wild hibiscus, Abelmoschus manihot), neri is a viscous, water-soluble polysaccharide that acts as a natural deflocculant. When added to the water vat (sukibune) containing suspended cellulose fibers, neri coats each individual fiber with a slippery, lubricating molecular sheath. This prevents the long fibers from tangling, clumping, or settling rapidly to the bottom of the vat, ensuring an even, homogeneous suspension.

G. To form a sheet, the papermaker dips a suketa—a two-part wooden frame clamping a flexible, finely woven bamboo screen (su)—into the vat. In a series of rapid, fluid, and rhythmic rocking motions, the artisan scoops the liquid slurry across the screen, sloshing it repeatedly along the longitudinal and transverse axes while discharging the excess water over the far edge of the frame. This dynamic rocking aligns the long cellulose fibers into an interlocking, multi- directional web. The wet sheets are sequentially couched onto a growing stack, pressed under heavy wooden levers to squeeze out residual water, and brushed individually onto heated ginkgo- wood or metal drying boards to dry in the sun. Due to the complete absence of acidic chemicals and the preservation of long, un-fractured cellulose chains, handmade washi exhibits a natural neutral pH, enabling preserved documents and artworks to endure for well over a millennium without yellowing or crumbling.'
);

