-- Passage 2: Vehicle-to-Grid (V2G) Systems and Battery Degradation Economics
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '3b299af5-1cd0-5f15-b307-958d610c8060', 'ce519c5b-dc34-58df-801a-3ddd2fb47d4d', 2, 'Vehicle-to-Grid (V2G) Systems and Battery Degradation Economics',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The global transition toward clean, electrified road mobility has driven millions of battery electric vehicles (EVs) onto public roads, with global EV fleets projected to surpass several hundred million units by 2040. While the mass adoption of electric vehicles slashes tailpipe greenhouse gas emissions and urban air pollution, it introduces immense operational challenges for municipal and national electrical power grids. Uncoordinated, simultaneous charging of millions of EVs during evening residential peak hours threatens to overload local distribution substations, trigger severe voltage instability, and force utilities to fire up carbon-intensive fossil peaker plants. However, transportation engineers and energy systems economists increasingly recognize that electric vehicles need not function merely as passive electricity consumers; when aggregated, their large onboard battery packs can serve as a massive, distributed energy storage reservoir through Vehicle-to-Grid (V2G) integration.

B. Vehicle-to-Grid is formally defined as a bidirectional electrical and digital communication system that enables electric vehicles to both draw electrical power from the grid during charging (Grid-to-Vehicle, or G2V) and inject stored electrical energy back into the power distribution network during periods of high grid demand (V2G). Under a V2G framework, when an EV is parked and plugged into a compatible bidirectional charger (equipped with DC-to-AC inverters conforming to protocols such as ISO 15118), it can supply high-value ancillary services to grid operators. These services include rapid frequency regulation (stabilizing grid frequency fluctuations within milliseconds), voltage support, spinning reserves, and peak demand shaving—discharging during evening spikes to displace expensive natural gas peaker generation, and recharging during periods of midday solar or nocturnal wind overgeneration.

C. Despite the immense macro-economic and grid-balancing potential of V2G, widespread commercial rollout confronts a primary technical and financial barrier: the accelerated electrochemical degradation of the vehicle’s lithium-ion battery pack. A traction battery is the single most expensive physical component of an electric vehicle, accounting for 25 to 35 percent of the total retail cost of the car. EV owners are naturally protective of battery health, fearing that participating in bidirectional grid discharging will prematurely wear out their battery cells, drastically reduce vehicle driving range, and void expensive manufacturer warranties.

D. The physical mechanisms governing lithium-ion battery degradation under V2G cycling are rooted in non-linear electrochemical wear at both the anode and cathode interfaces. Battery aging occurs through two concurrent pathways: 1. Calendar Aging: The continuous, passive loss of capacity over time that occurs regardless of whether the battery is being used, driven primarily by elevated ambient storage temperatures and high states of charge (SoC). 2. Cyclic Aging: The active mechanical and chemical degradation induced by charge and discharge cycles. During cycling, lithium ions shuttle between electrodes, causing the graphite anode particles to physically expand and contract. This cyclic mechanical strain cracks the protective Solid Electrolyte Interphase (SEI) passivation layer on the anode surface, exposing fresh graphite to the liquid electrolyte. The electrolyte continuously decomposes to repair the cracked SEI, permanently consuming active lithium inventory and increasing internal cell resistance (impedance).

E. Battery electrochemists and modeling engineers emphasize that not all V2G discharging protocols induce identical degradation. The severity of cyclic battery wear is heavily governed by three primary operational variables: depth of discharge ($\Delta\text{DoD}$), operating temperature, and the state-of-charge window. Extensive laboratory aging trials demonstrate that subjecting cells to full-depth cycles (e.g., cycling from 100% down to 0% SoC) causes severe mechanical lattice cracking and rapid capacity fade. However, when V2G participation is constrained to shallow, high-frequency micro-cycles within a moderate state-of-charge band (such as cycling between 40% and 70% SoC at low discharge rates), mechanical stress on the SEI layer is minimal. Under carefully managed smart-charging algorithms, the incremental degradation incurred by shallow V2G cycling is surprisingly low, often shortening the calendar lifespan of a battery pack by less than 2 to 4 percent over a ten-year vehicle lifespan.

F. To motivate consumer participation in V2G networks, energy economists are designing sophisticated dynamic financial compensation models. Because an individual EV owner will only allow their vehicle to discharge if the economic rewards exceed the monetized cost of battery capacity loss, utilities and third-party energy aggregators must provide clear price signals. Under dynamic time-of-use tariffs and automated aggregator contracts, EV owners are compensated through direct capacity availability payments, reduced retail charging tariffs, and revenue sharing from high-value grid ancillary services. Advanced cloud-based telematics platforms optimize charging schedules based on user-defined constraints: an owner can specify via a smartphone app that their vehicle must maintain at least an 80 percent charge by 7:00 AM for their morning commute, allowing the aggregator to trade only the surplus, flexible capacity overnight.

G. As solid-state battery chemistries and ultra-durable lithium iron phosphate (LFP) cells— capable of enduring over 4,000 to 6,000 full operational cycles with minimal capacity loss— continue to gain market share, the degradation penalty of V2G is diminishing rapidly. By transforming millions of idle private cars into a flexible, decentralized virtual power plant, Vehicle-to-Grid systems provide a vital, low-cost technological bridge toward a fully decarbonized, renewable-dominated global energy architecture.'
);

