-- Passage 1: The Evolution of Ancient Mediterranean Olive Oil Presses
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'b73ca382-6b77-5187-a047-65392ac9b32a', '24928d95-2b99-5d0b-ab38-59fe5b57d04f', 1, 'The Evolution of Ancient Mediterranean Olive Oil Presses',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of Mediterranean antiquity, the olive tree (Olea europaea) served as the botanical and economic lifeblood of classical civilization. Across the arid limestone hillsides of Greece, the Levant, Italy, the Aegean islands, and North Africa, olive oil was far more than an essential culinary staple; it was an indispensable multi-purpose commodity that underpinned daily domestic survival, religious ritual, athletic culture, personal hygiene, and long-distance maritime commerce. Olive oil provided the primary fuel for terracotta lighting lamps, formed the base for scented perfumes and therapeutic medical ointments, served as a cleansing agent in public baths, and lubricated wooden mechanical tools. The vast scale of Mediterranean demand drove a continuous, millennia-long evolution of mechanical extraction technologies, transforming domestic stone crushing basins into monumental, proto-industrial pressing factories.

B. The physical extraction of virgin olive oil requires two distinct, sequential mechanical operations: crushing (milling) and pressing (separation). The first phase—crushing—demands breaking the tough outer skin and fleshy pericarp (mesocarp) of harvested olives into an oily paste without smashing the hard internal stony endocarp (the pit or kernel). Smashing the hard pits releases unwanted bitter enzymes and astringent tannins that accelerate chemical oxidation, degrading the delicate flavor and culinary shelf-life of the oil. The second phase—pressing— involves subjecting the crushed olive pulp to controlled mechanical pressure to separate the liquid phase (an emulsion of oil and bitter vegetative water, known as amurca) from the solid vegetable pomace (sansa).

C. In the Bronze and early Iron Ages (circa 2500 to 800 BCE) across the Levant, Minoan Crete, and Mycenaean Greece, oil extraction relied on rudimentary gravity-and-lever systems. Harvesters crushed olives manually in hollowed stone mortars using handheld basalt pestles or rolled heavy cylindrical stone rollers across flat stone bed slabs. The resulting pulp was shoveled into porous woven bags (frails) fabricated from esparto grass, hemp, or rush fibers. These bags were stacked atop a circular stone press bed (ara) carved with a concentric drainage groove. Workers applied mechanical pressure using a massive timber lever beam: one end of the beam was anchored securely into a stone wall socket, while the opposite free end was weighed down by hanging heavy, perforated limestone boulders (lever-and-weights press).

D. The defining technological revolution in crushing mechanics occurred during the classical Hellenistic period (fourth to third centuries BCE) with the invention of the rotary olive mill, most famously formalized by Roman agricultural writers as the trapetum. Documented extensively by Cato the Elder in his agricultural treatise De Agri Cultura, the trapetum consisted of a massive, concave volcanic basalt basin (mortarium) featuring a central stone column (milliarium). Pivoting around this central column was a horizontal wooden axle supporting two hemispherical, convex stone grinding wheels (orbes). The genius of the trapetum lay in its precise geometric calibration: the curved orbes were suspended by wooden pivot pins precisely a few millimeters above the floor and sidewalls of the mortarium. As the stones rolled around the basin, they sheared and pulverized the oily olive flesh through gentle friction while allowing the hard pits to roll through the clearance gap completely unbroken.

E. By the late Roman Republic and early Empire (first century BCE to third century CE), Roman hydraulic and mechanical engineers revolutionized the pressing phase by replacing awkward hanging stone counterweights with mechanical screw presses (torcularia). Roman mechanics developed two primary variations of the screw press: 1. The Lever-and-Screw Press (Torcularium): The traditional long timber lever beam was retained, but rather than suspending loose boulders from its tip, the end of the beam was connected to a large vertical wooden screw threaded through an immense stone anchoring block embedded in the floor. By turning the screw using hand levers or windlass capstans, a small team of operators could pull down the great beam with unprecedented, uniform mechanical advantage. 2. The Direct Vertical Screw Press: Introduced in the first century CE (described by Pliny the Elder and Hero of Alexandria), this compact machine eliminated the bulky lever beam entirely. A massive vertical wooden screw was mounted within a rigid rectangular timber frame directly above the stack of frails, driving a heavy wooden pressure platen downward onto the olive paste.

F. The industrialization of olive oil production reached its historical zenith during the Roman High Empire (first to third centuries CE), particularly within the agrarian provinces of Hispania Baetica (modern Andalusia, Spain) and Roman North Africa (modern Tunisia and Libya). Sprawling rural processing complexes (villae rusticae) operated batteries of ten to twelve monumental lever-and-screw presses working simultaneously under unified masonry factory roofs. An industrial processing estate in Baetica could press tens of thousands of metric tons of olives each autumn harvest, filling millions of globular terracotta transport amphorae (most famously the standard Dressel 20 amphora, stamped with imperial administrative seals). Fleets of commercial freighters ferried these amphorae across the western Mediterranean to the port of Ostia, supplying the mandatory state food dole (annona) for the imperial capital of Rome.

G. Following the political collapse of the Western Roman Empire and the disruption of long- distance maritime trade routes, these monumental pressing factories fell into disuse. However, the foundational mechanical principles developed by ancient Mediterranean engineers—the rotary shearing mill and the mechanical screw press—persisted virtually unchanged across rural Mediterranean villages for nearly two millennia. It was not until the widespread commercial adoption of steam engines, hydraulic cylinder presses, and continuous centrifugal decanters in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries that the ancient stone and timber presses were finally retired, leaving their enduring stone basins and oil amphorae as monuments to classical engineering mastery.'
);

