-- Passage 2: Deep Geothermal Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'b06fc4d7-ce76-55fe-8b3b-76e200b452e4', '24928d95-2b99-5d0b-ab38-59fe5b57d04f', 2, 'Deep Geothermal Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS)',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. As international climate initiatives accelerate the transition toward zero-carbon energy grids, the quest for continuous, dispatchable renewable baseload electricity has intensified. While wind and solar power have achieved rapid commercial expansion, their weather-dependent intermittency requires massive overbuilding, long-duration battery storage, or fossil-fueled backup generation. Conventional hydrothermal geothermal power plants—which tap into naturally occurring, highly permeable subterranean reservoirs of steam and superheated water— provide steady, 24-hour clean electricity. However, conventional hydrothermal resources are geographically rare: they are restricted to active volcanic zones, tectonic plate boundaries, and geologically young magmatic regions (such as Iceland, New Zealand, and the western United States), representing less than two percent of global geothermal potential.

B. To unlock the colossal, untapped thermal energy stored within the Earth’s deep crust worldwide, geothermal energy engineers are developing Enhanced Geothermal Systems (EGS)—also designated as engineered or advanced geothermal systems. Beneath the surface of virtually every continent, at depths ranging from three to eight kilometers, lies hot dry rock (HDR)—dense crystalline basement formations, primarily granite, granodiorite, and metamorphic gneiss, heated by the Earth''s radioactive mantle decay to temperatures ranging from 150°C to over 300°C. However, because these deep crystalline rock formations possess near-zero natural porosity and microscopic permeability, water cannot naturally circulate through the rock to extract heat. EGS overcomes this barrier by artificially engineering an underground reservoir through hydraulic stimulation.

C. The operational architecture of an Enhanced Geothermal System begins with drilling an initial deep injection well directly into the hot crystalline bedrock. Once the target thermal depth is reached, high-pressure water (often mixed with trace friction-reducing additives and environmentally benign micro-proppants) is injected into the wellbore at pressures exceeding the in-situ minimum horizontal tectonic stress of the formation. Rather than fracturing pristine, unbroken rock (as in oil and gas hydraulic fracturing), EGS hydraulic stimulation operates primarily through "hydroshearing": the high-pressure fluid reduces effective normal stress along thousands of pre-existing, natural microscopic joints and geological fault planes, causing the rock faces to slip shear-wise by millimeters. Because natural crystalline fault faces are rough and irregular, this shear displacement prevents the fractures from closing back together once pressure drops, creating an extensive, permanent, self-propped subterranean network of interconnected fluid pathways.

D. Following the successful stimulation of this artificial reservoir, one or more production wells are drilled into the periphery of the stimulated fracture cloud to complete a closed-loop circulation doublet or triplet. In full commercial operation, cold working fluid (typically treated water or supercritical carbon dioxide, $sCO_2$) is pumped down the injection well. As the fluid circulates slowly through the vast network of hot, stimulated fractures across surface areas measuring several square kilometers, it absorbs heat from the surrounding rock matrix via conductive thermal transfer. The superheated fluid is then drawn up to the surface through the production wells at high temperatures (typically 175°C to 250°C) under high pressure.

E. At the surface power plant, the thermal energy is converted into electricity using advanced thermodynamic generation cycles. If the produced geothermal fluid exceeds 200°C, it can be flashed into high-pressure steam to directly drive a conventional steam turbine generator. For moderate-temperature EGS reservoirs (130°C to 190°C), power plants deploy Binary Organic Rankine Cycles (ORC). In an ORC plant, the hot geothermal brine passes through a closed heat exchanger, transferring its heat to a secondary organic working fluid (such as isobutane, isopentane, or hydrofluoroolefins) that possesses a significantly lower boiling point than water. The organic fluid vaporizes into high-pressure gas to drive a specialized expansion turbine, while the cooled geothermal fluid is continuously reinjected into the deep subsurface in a 100 percent closed-loop, emission-free cycle.

F. Despite its revolutionary potential, the engineering development of deep EGS confronts severe geophysical and technical risks. The foremost environmental hazard is induced seismicity (micro-earthquakes). As pressurized fluid lubricates deep fault planes during hydraulic hydroshearing, it can trigger seismic slip events: in 2006, an experimental EGS project in Basel, Switzerland, induced a magnitude 3.4 earthquake that caused minor structural damage to surface buildings and forced the permanent termination of the project. Modern EGS projects mitigate seismic risk through real-time downhole acoustic micro-seismic monitoring arrays and "traffic light" protocols that automatically throttle injection flow rates or bleed off wellbore pressure if seismic thresholds are approached.

G. In addition to seismic risks, deep EGS operations endure extreme mechanical and chemical downhole environments that push drilling technologies to their physical limits. Drilling through five kilometers of ultra-hard, abrasive granite at temperatures exceeding 250°C causes rapid wear of conventional tungsten carbide drill bits, thermal degradation of downhole electronic steering tools, and severe casing corrosion. However, the adaptation of advanced directional drilling innovations from the shale energy industry—including polycrystalline diamond compact (PDC) drill bits, high-temperature downhole telemetry tools, and millimeter-wave directed energy plasma drilling—is dramatically slashing drilling times and capital costs. As commercial EGS pilot facilities (such as Fervo Energy’s commercial projects in Nevada and Utah) achieve multi-megawatt power deliveries, deep enhanced geothermal systems are poised to unlock virtually limitless, 24-hour clean baseload power beneath the feet of humanity.'
);

