-- Passage 1: The Domestication and Ecological Role of the Honeybee (Apis mellifera)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '249b36a9-dbbf-568c-9d4d-7fb1b3a6d650', '33527f82-5fb8-5ea9-9659-5670647c5b61', 1, 'The Domestication and Ecological Role of the Honeybee (Apis mellifera)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the vast spectrum of human-insect interactions, few relationships have proven as economically indispensable, culturally pervasive, and ecologically central as apiculture—the management and husbandry of the Western honeybee (Apis mellifera). Long before the advent of industrial agriculture and modern commercial crop monocultures, ancient human communities recognized the dual value of honeybees: as producers of concentrated nutritional sweetness (honey) and versatile structural materials (beeswax), and as vital biological facilitators of terrestrial flowering plant reproduction. From ancient Paleolithic cave art depicted in the Cave of the Spider in Bicorp, Spain (dating to circa 8000 BCE), to the sophisticated clay-pipe apiaries of the Nile Delta, the historical trajectory of the honeybee reflects a gradual transition from dangerous opportunistic honey-hunting to systematic, scientific hive husbandry.

B. Originating in tropical Africa or the Middle East during the late Pleistocene epoch, Apis mellifera rapidly radiated across diverse geographical ecosystems, evolving into more than twenty-five distinct geographic subspecies (such as the Italian bee Apis mellifera ligustica, the European dark bee Apis mellifera mellifera, and the Carniolan bee Apis mellifera carnica). The biological engine that drives the evolutionary success of the honeybee is its advanced eusociality, characterized by overlapping adult generations, cooperative brood care, and an extreme reproductive division of labor. A mature colony functions as an integrated "superorganism" comprising three distinct biological castes: 1. The Queen: A single fertile female possessing fully developed ovaries, responsible for laying up to two thousand eggs per day and secreting a complex chemical cocktail of queen mandibular pheromones that maintains colonial cohesion and suppresses worker ovarian development. 2. Drones: Several hundred to a few thousand haploid males, produced via arrhenotokous parthenogenesis (from unfertilized eggs), whose exclusive biological function is mating with virgin queens during high-altitude nuptial flights. 3. Workers: Tens of thousands of non-reproductive diploid females that execute an age- dependent division of labor (temporal polyethism), transitioning sequentially from internal hive cleaning and nurse-feeding of larvae, to wax comb construction and guard defense, and finally to external foraging for floral nectar and protein-rich pollen.

C. The construction of the hexagonal honeycomb represents one of the supreme marvels of biological architecture. Secreted as liquid wax flakes from eight specialized sub-abdominal wax glands on worker bees, the wax is chewed with salivary enzymes and sculpted into vertical, double-sided arrays of interlocking hexagonal cells. The hexagonal geometry is mathematically optimal: known as the "honeycomb conjecture" (proven formally by mathematician Thomas Hales in 1999), a regular hexagonal tiling divides a planar surface into equal-area cells with the minimal total boundary perimeter, maximizing internal volumetric storage capacity while minimizing the costly metabolic expenditure of wax synthesis (a colony consumes roughly six to eight kilograms of honey to synthesize a single kilogram of beeswax). The comb cells are tilted upward at a subtle angle of two to four degrees to prevent un-ripened honey from spilling out before capping.

D. The coordination of floral foraging across several square kilometers of surrounding territory is mediated through an extraordinary symbolic communication system: the "waggle dance," deciphered by Austrian ethologist Karl von Frisch in the 1940s. When a successful foraging bee returns to the dark hive, it performs a figure-eight rhythmic dance on the vertical comb face. The angle of the central waggle run relative to vertical gravity directly translates the horizontal solar azimuth angle toward the floral food source, while the duration of the waggle run (roughly one second of buzzing per kilometer of distance) communicates the precise flight distance. By decoding these acoustic vibrations, body angles, and floral scents, recruit bees navigate directly to rich nectar blooms across flight paths exceeding five kilometers.

E. In human agricultural history, ancient Egyptian beekeepers pioneered the earliest mass-scale artificial hives during the Old and Middle Kingdoms (circa 2400 to 1500 BCE). Excavations at Tell Rehov in the Jordan Valley revealed thirty intact ceramic cylinder hives dating to the tenth century BCE, demonstrating that ancient Levantine apiaries maintained commercial rows of stacked unbaked clay and straw cylinders. In ancient Egyptian society, honey was not merely a culinary sweetener, but the primary base for pharmaceutical salves, embalming resins, and temple offerings, while beeswax was indispensable for lost-wax bronze metallurgy (cire perdue) and waterproof parchment coatings.

F. The transition to modern scientific beekeeping occurred in 1851 with the revolutionary invention of the movable-frame hive by American clergyman and apiarist Lorenzo Lorraine Langstroth. Langstroth discovered the principle of "bee space"—a precise physical gap measuring between 6.4 and 9.5 millimeters (roughly 1/4 to 3/8 of an inch). If a gap is narrower than 6.4 mm, bees cement it shut with sticky propolis (tree resin); if a gap is wider than 9.5 mm, bees build messy bridge combs of wax. By engineering wooden rectangular frames suspended inside a hive box with an exact perimeter of bee space, Langstroth allowed beekeepers to remove, inspect, harvest, and replace individual honeycombs without destroying the delicate wax architecture or killing the queen and brood inside.

G. In contemporary global food systems, the primary economic value of Apis mellifera has shifted decisively from honey production to commercial pollination services. More than one- third of all global food crops—including high-value horticultural commodities such as almonds, apples, blueberries, avocados, and melons—depend directly on insect cross-pollination to achieve optimal fruit set, seed development, and marketable size. In North America, the annual pollination of California''s central valley almond orchards mobilizes over 1.5 million honeybee colonies—representing nearly two-thirds of all commercial hives in the United States— transported across the continent on flatbed freight trucks in the largest migratory pollination event on Earth.

H. Despite their vital ecological and economic importance, honeybee populations face an unprecedented convergence of modern anthropogenic stresses. Commercial colonies suffer from elevated annual mortality rates driven by Colony Collapse Disorder (CCD), habitat fragmentation, monoculture nutritional stress, and chronic sublethal exposure to systemic neonicotinoid pesticides. Furthermore, the global transmission of the parasitic mite Varroa destructor—which feeds on the fat body tissues of adult bees and vectors lethal pathogens like Deformed Wing Virus (DWV)—threatens wild and managed colonies worldwide. Protecting honeybee health through ecological farming, breeding mite-resistant genetic lines, and reducing chemical agrochemical loads remains imperative to safeguard global food security and terrestrial biodiversity.'
);

