-- Passage 3: Mathematical Realism, Nominalism, and the Semantics of Fictional Entities
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '214174ed-d623-50f6-8f6c-246ba2a41670', '33527f82-5fb8-5ea9-9659-5670647c5b61', 3, 'Mathematical Realism, Nominalism, and the Semantics of Fictional Entities',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the foundational philosophy of language and mathematical ontology, one of the most profound and persistent metaphysical enigmas concerns the semantic truth conditions of discourse regarding non-physical, abstract entities. Consider two everyday assertions: Sentence 1: "Sherlock Holmes lives at 221B Baker Street." Sentence 2: "The number 7 is prime." Most competent language speakers immediately recognize a fundamental distinction between these two claims: the first is an informal statement within a fictional literary narrative, while the second is an objective, eternal mathematical truth. Yet, from a strict referential semantic perspective, both sentences present a glaring ontological problem: neither the fictional detective Sherlock Holmes nor the abstract prime number 7 possesses physical mass, spatial location, or temporal duration in the concrete universe. How can human language formulate objective, true propositions about entities that do not physically exist?

B. The classical metaphysical divide over this dilemma is formalized as the contest between Mathematical Realism (Platonism) and Mathematical Anti-Realism (Nominalism). Mathematical Realists, tracing their lineage from Plato and Gottlob Frege to contemporary platonists, assert that mathematical discourse must be interpreted literally: mathematical statements are true because mathematical entities (numbers, sets, functions) objectively exist as mind-independent, non-spatiotemporal abstract objects. Nominalists, by contrast, reject the existence of abstract objects altogether, arguing that the physical universe consists strictly of concrete particular entities located in space and time (particles, fields, physical objects).

C. For nominalists, explaining why mathematical statements like "$7 + 5 = 12$" are practically useful without committing to a mystical realm of abstract Platonist objects led to the development of Mathematical Fictionalism. Pioneered by philosophers such as Hartry Field and Gideon Rosen, fictionalism draws an explicit semantic parallel between mathematics and literary fiction. Fictionalists argue that mathematical sentences are literally false (or truth-valueless) in the strict ontological sense, because the mathematical entities they quantify over simply do not exist. However, just as the sentence "Sherlock Holmes is a detective" is true according to the fiction of Arthur Conan Doyle, the statement "$7 + 5 = 12$" is correct according to the standard fiction of arithmetic (such as the Peano-Dedekind axioms).

D. Despite the intuitive appeal of treating mathematics as a useful story, fictionalism encounters the formidable Quine-Putnam Indispensability Argument. Formulated by Willard Van Orman Quine and Hilary Putnam, the argument demonstrates that mathematics cannot be dismissed as a mere optional literary game because mathematical apparatus is deeply, indispensably woven into the foundational laws of empirical natural science. Physicists cannot formulate the fundamental differential equations of quantum mechanics or general relativity without quantifying over real numbers, Hilbert spaces, and Riemannian metric tensors. Under the doctrine of confirmational holism, when empirical observations confirm the predictive accuracy of a physical theory, that empirical support extends to all theoretical posits indispensable to the theory—including its mathematical entities.

E. To defend nominalism against the indispensability challenge, Hartry Field executed his celebrated "nominalization" project in his 1980 work Science Without Numbers. Field demonstrated that Newtonian gravitational physics can be reformulated using purely synthetic, coordinate-free geometric relations between physical space-time points (such as relations of "congruence" and "betweenness") without ever quantifying over real numbers or functions. Field proved that mathematics is "conservative" over nominalistic science: adding mathematical apparatus to a physical theory functions as an extraordinary computational shorthand that speeds up calculations, but it does not allow the deduction of any new physical consequences that could not, in principle, be proven without mathematics.

F. However, in the late 1990s, a new generation of Platonist philosophers, most notably Mark Colyvan and Alan Baker, advanced the "Enhanced Indispensability Argument." Baker and Colyvan pointed out that Field’s nominalization focused exclusively on the representational and computational role of mathematics in science. They argued that mathematics plays an indispensable explanatory role in natural science: in certain empirical phenomena, mathematical properties provide the core causal and structural explanation for why a physical event occurs.

G. A celebrated real-world exemplar of mathematical explanation in biology is the evolutionary life-cycle of periodical cicadas (Magicicada species) in North America. Periodical cicadas spend either exactly 13 or 17 years underground as nymphs before emerging simultaneously to mate. Why have these insects evolved life-cycle lengths that are specifically prime numbers? Evolutionary biologists demonstrate that prime-numbered periods minimize the mathematical intersection (lowest common multiple) with the life-cycle periods of local predators and competing cicada broods. For example, a 12-year cicada would intersect with 2-, 3-, 4-, and 6- year predators, whereas a 13-year or 17-year prime period minimizes overlapping encounters with predators of almost any periodic cycle. The prime number property is not merely a descriptive shorthand; it is the fundamental mathematical explanation for the biological adaptation.

H. In synthesizing the contemporary ontology of mathematics, philosophers recognize that the debate between realism and nominalism illuminates the profound boundaries between human thought, language, and reality. Whether mathematics is conceptualized as an objective, eternal landscape of abstract structures or as the most sophisticated, conservative fictional instrument ever constructed by human intellect, its uncanny ability to unlock the fundamental laws of the physical cosmos remains one of the greatest philosophical wonders of the universe.'
);

