-- Passage 1: The Ancient Water-Lifting Technologies of the Nile: The Shaduf, Saqiya, and Archimedes
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '370abcd9-3c67-5054-b473-3ce9baba78d4', 'fe17ca1a-04b0-5d41-91b0-be409d0e2772', 1, 'The Ancient Water-Lifting Technologies of the Nile: The Shaduf, Saqiya, and Archimedes',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the historical development of Egyptian civilization, agricultural survival and societal stability depended almost exclusively on the hydrological pulse of the Nile River. In an hyper-arid climate devoid of regular rainfall, the annual summer inundation (Akhet) inundated the low-lying floodplain, depositing nutrient-rich alluvial silt across agricultural fields. However, as the seasonal floodwaters receded in early autumn, Egyptian farmers confronted a persistent hydraulic dilemma: how to elevate water from the shrinking river channel, low-lying canals, and subterranean wells up onto elevated river terraces and secondary field plots during the prolonged dry growing seasons (Peret and Shemu). To sustain multi-cropping cycles and cultivate higher terraced orchards, ancient and medieval engineers developed three ingenious mechanical water- lifting devices: the shaduf, the saqiya, and the Archimedes screw (known locally as the tanbur).

B. The earliest and mechanically simplest water-lifting apparatus deployed along the Nile was the shaduf (counterpoise sweep), introduced into Egypt during the New Kingdom period (circa 1550–1077 BCE). The shaduf is an elegant application of a Class 1 lever. It consists of a long, tapered horizontal wooden beam pivoting upon a sturdy vertical timber upright or masonry pillar. Attached to the longer arm of the beam is a long vertical pole or rope supporting a leather bucket or woven wicker basket coated with pitch. Suspended from the opposite, shorter arm is a heavy counterweight, typically composed of a dense mass of dried river mud, clay, or a shaped limestone boulder.

C. To operate the shaduf, a single laborer pulls downward on the rope, submerging the bucket into the water source. Once the bucket is filled, the operator releases the downward pull; the heavy counterweight on the short arm naturally swings downward, effortlessly elevating the water-filled container to an upper discharge basin with minimal physical exertion. While a single shaduf had a limited vertical lift of one to two meters, Egyptian farmers overcame steep riverbank gradients by constructing stepped batteries of shadufs arranged in vertical tiers: the lowest shaduf lifted water into an intermediate masonry catch-basin, from which a second shaduf hoisted the water to a higher terrace, repeating the process across three or four vertical steps. An industrious operator could lift approximately 2,000 to 3,000 liters of water per day, transforming small-scale domestic horticulture and vegetable farming.

D. In the Hellenistic period (circa third century BCE) following the Ptolemaic conquest of Egypt, the introduction of classical Greek geometry and applied mechanics transformed Nilotic irrigation with the invention of the Archimedes screw (tanbur or cochlias). Traditionally attributed to the Syracusan polymath Archimedes during his studies in Alexandria, this device consists of a broad helical spiral blade (fashioned from flexible strips of willow, oak, or copper) wound tightly around a solid central wooden shaft. The entire rotating helix is encased within a hollow, watertight wooden cylinder sheathed in pitch-covered timber staves and iron hoops.

E. When the lower end of the cylinder is submerged in water at an inclined angle (typically 30 to 45 degrees relative to the horizontal) and the central shaft is rotated—either by an operator turning an external hand crank or treading directly upon external wooden cleats mounted on the cylinder''s outer circumference—a continuous stream of water is trapped within the spiraling pockets formed between the helix and the outer casing. As the screw revolves, these isolated pockets of water travel upward along the incline without backsliding, discharging a continuous fluid volume from the top of the cylinder into irrigation feeder canals. Operating with exceptional thermodynamic and mechanical efficiency, the Archimedes screw was particularly prized for its ability to pump sediment-laden, muddy water and slurry without clogging, lifting upwards of 15,000 to 20,000 liters per hour over modest vertical heads of 0.5 to 1.5 meters.

F. The ultimate triumph of ancient animal-powered water-lifting machinery arrived in the late Ptolemaic and early Roman periods with the development of the saqiya (or geared bucket- wheel). The saqiya is a complex, multi-geared mechanical apparatus that revolutionized agricultural productivity by utilizing draft animal power (such as oxen, donkeys, or camels) rather than human muscle. The mechanism consists of a large vertical wooden drive shaft fitted with an overhead horizontal wooden sweep bar to which a blindfolded draft animal is harnessed. As the animal walks in a continuous circular path around a central circular track, it rotates a large horizontal wooden spur wheel. The wooden teeth of the horizontal wheel mesh at a right angle with a vertical cogwheel mounted on an adjoining horizontal axle, transferring rotational energy through a 90-degree axis shift.

G. The horizontal axle drives a large vertical wheel positioned directly above a deep well or riverbank cut. Suspended over this wheel is a continuous, double-looped rope chain (pot-garland or sinia) carrying dozens of porous terracotta ceramic jars (qadus), each lashed to the ropes at regular intervals. As the wheel revolves, the empty jars plunge into the subterranean water source, invert, fill with water, and travel upward to the summit of the wheel. At the apex of the rotation, the jars tip downward, pouring their contents smoothly into a wooden or stone catchment trough connected to irrigation canals before descending empty to repeat the loop. By operating continuously, a single ox-driven saqiya could elevate water from depths exceeding ten to twenty meters, discharging 10,000 to 15,000 liters per hour. This massive hydraulic output enabled the year-round cultivation of sugar cane, cotton, citrus groves, and winter wheat across thousands of hectares of previously barren desert terraces, anchoring Egypt’s position as the agricultural breadbasket of the ancient Mediterranean.'
);

