-- Passage 3: The Eliminative Materialism Debate in the Philosophy of Mind
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '6ace75cd-66df-583a-a132-e448d8264a50', 'fe17ca1a-04b0-5d41-91b0-be409d0e2772', 3, 'The Eliminative Materialism Debate in the Philosophy of Mind',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In contemporary philosophy of mind, cognitive science, and theoretical neurobiology, the central ontological debate concerns the relationship between human subjective mental states and the physical neurobiology of the brain. When an individual explains human behavior using everyday mentalistic terms—such as stating that "John went to the kitchen because he believed there was water there and desired to quench his thirst"—they are utilizing an implicit, common- sense conceptual framework known to philosophers as "Folk Psychology." Folk psychology presumes that human conscious cognition is populated by discrete "propositional attitudes"— internal mental entities such as beliefs, desires, hopes, intentions, and fears that possess semantic content and causally direct physical human action.

B. For centuries, orthodox Western philosophy, functionalism, and classical cognitive science operated under the assumption that folk psychology is a fundamentally sound, roughly accurate descriptive model of the human mind. Classical computationalists (such as Jerry Fodor) argued that cognitive science’s primary duty is to discover the neurobiological and computational neural structures that directly correspond to folk psychological concepts: identifying the physical "belief boxes" and "desire circuits" inside the brain. In this realist view, mature neuroscience will ultimately vindicate and reduce folk psychological states into precise neurobiological mechanisms, just as classical thermodynamics was successfully reduced to the statistical mechanics of molecular motion.

C. In the early 1980s, however, Canadian-American philosophers Paul Churchland and Patricia Churchland fundamentally shattered this comfortable reductionist assumption by formulating the radical philosophical doctrine of Eliminative Materialism (often termed Eliminativism). The Churchlands advanced a shocking proposition: folk psychology is not a roughly correct theory that will be smoothly reduced to neuroscience; rather, folk psychology is a hopelessly flawed, radically false empirical theory that is destined for total scientific elimination. In the eliminativist view, propositional attitudes—such as beliefs, desires, and subjective intentions—do not actually exist in physical reality any more than ancient mythological concepts like demonic possession, caloric fluid, or the luminiferous aether existed.

D. The Churchlands grounded their eliminative thesis in three primary philosophical and historical arguments: 1. Explanatory Stagnation: Folk psychology has made zero theoretical progress for over two millennia. Sophisticated ancient Greek playwrights like Sophocles understood human behavior using the exact same concepts of belief and desire that modern laypeople use today. Furthermore, folk psychology is completely incapable of explaining massive domains of central cognitive phenomena: it offers no theoretical account of the mechanics of memory consolidation, visual processing, sleep and dreaming, the nature of learning, psychiatric illness, or motor coordination. 2. Historical Precedent of Radical Elimination: Throughout the history of science, when an older, primitive theoretical framework is superseded by a mature, rigorous science, the concepts of the older theory are rarely preserved or reduced; they are eliminated entirely. When modern combustion chemistry emerged (Antoine Lavoisier), scientists did not discover that "phlogiston" was actually oxygen; they proved that phlogiston does not exist. Similarly, when neurobiology matures, the concepts of belief and desire will simply vanish from scientific discourse. 3. Theoretical Incommensurability: Modern computational neuroscience reveals that the physical brain is a non-linear, massively parallel neural network operating through distributed activation vectors and synaptic weight configurations, containing no discrete, linguistically structured sentence-like representations corresponding to "beliefs."

E. In Neurophilosophy (1986) and A Neurocomputational Perspective (1989), Patricia and Paul Churchland argued that as cognitive neuroscience develops, humanity will abandon the clumsy, inaccurate vocabulary of folk psychology in favor of a direct, scientifically precise neurobiological language. Rather than saying, "I am feeling sudden anger and desire to yell," an individual might report, "My amygdala is undergoing high-frequency sympathetic firing and elevating norepinephrine concentrations in my prefrontal cortex." In this vision, human self- understanding will be permanently transformed by replacing mythological mentalistic language with the objective language of neural networks.

F. Despite its provocative elegance, Eliminative Materialism provoked fierce, sustained condemnation from mainstream philosophers of mind, cognitive scientists, and linguistic realists, most notably Jerry Fodor, Lynne Rudder Baker, and John Searle. The most famous philosophical objection is the "Self-Refutation Argument." Critics point out that in order to assert, argue for, or defend Eliminative Materialism, an eliminativist must believe that the theory is true, intend to convince others of its validity, and desire to advance scientific knowledge. But if beliefs, desires, and intentions do not exist, then the eliminativist''s own assertion is completely devoid of semantic meaning and cannot be rationally believed. Eliminativism appears to saw off the very rational branch upon which it sits.

G. Paul Churchland vigorously refuted the self-refutation charge by exposing it as an elementary question-begging fallacy, comparing it to an ancient vitalist defending the "vital force" (elan vital). Imagine an ancient vitalist asserting: "You anti-vitalists claim that vital living spirits do not exist. But if vital spirits do not exist, then your vocal cords are physically dead and you cannot speak! Therefore, your claim that vital spirits do not exist is self-refuting!" The vitalist argument fails because it presumes the very concept under debate. Similarly, asserting that an eliminativist must hold a "belief" to make an utterance merely assumes the validity of the folk psychological framework that neuroscience seeks to replace with neuro-computational vector states.

H. In contemporary 21st-century cognitive science, the debate has evolved beyond dogmatic extremes into nuanced models of pluralistic functionalism and predictive processing. While few contemporary neuroscientists advocate for the complete linguistic eradication of common-sense terms in daily social life, the eliminativist critique fundamentally dismantled naive mental realism. Modern cognitive science recognizes that our common-sense intuitions about our own minds are not infallible direct reflections of neural reality, but culturally constructed evolutionary models. By challenging humanity to confront the physical nature of the biological brain, Eliminative Materialism remains one of the most audacious, transformative philosophical inquiries in the history of thought.'
);

