-- Passage 1: The Traditional Craft and Chemistry of Japanese Urushi Lacquerware
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '7bcc30cd-3e4e-5288-b046-f5ff5d529566', 'd008fe55-107a-5f7e-9dce-1de8ffbdad57', 1, 'The Traditional Craft and Chemistry of Japanese Urushi Lacquerware',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For over nine millennia across the Japanese archipelago, the refined decorative craft of urushi (natural lacquerwork) has represented an extraordinary synthesis of botanical harvesting, organic polymer chemistry, and high-precision artisanal skill. Excavations at early Jōmon period archaeological sites—most notably the Kakinoshima site in Hokkaido, where red-lacquered ornaments and burial goods dating to circa 7000 BCE were unearthed—reveal that prehistoric Japanese communities were already exploiting the natural resin of the lacquer tree (Toxicodendron vernicifluum, historically Rhus verniciflua). Over millennia, urushi evolved from a functional waterproofing and bonding agent for stone projectile points and hunting tools into the supreme artistic medium of East Asian decorative arts, yielding lacquerware (shikki) celebrated for its deep, glass-like luster, chemical invulnerability, and remarkable durability.

B. The raw material of urushi is the sap of the lacquer tree, a deciduous tree indigenous to East Asia. The sap is harvested through an intensive, meticulous process known as urushi-kaki. Between June and November, licensed tappers (urushi-kaki-shokunin) use specialized hooked gouges to cut a sequence of shallow, horizontal incisions into the bark of mature trees (typically ten to fifteen years old). The tree secretes a grayish-white, milky emulsion to seal the wounds. Harvesters scrape this viscous exude into wooden or horn spatulas, collecting only about 150 to 200 milliliters of raw sap (ki-urushi) from a single mature tree over an entire harvest season before the tree is felled.

C. Raw urushi sap is a natural water-in-oil emulsion comprising four primary chemical constituents: 1. Urushiol (60%–70%): An oily organic mixture of catechols substituted with long, unsaturated hydrocarbon side chains containing 15 or 17 carbon atoms, acting as the primary monomer. 2. Water (20%–30%): The aqueous phase containing dissolved enzymes. 3. Plant Gums and Glycoproteins (5%–7%): Natural polysaccharides that act as natural emulsifiers. 4. Laccase (approx. 1%): A copper-containing oxidase enzyme that functions as the essential biological catalyst for polymerization.

D. The fundamental chemical paradox of urushi is that, unlike modern synthetic varnishes or oil-based paints that dry via the simple thermal evaporation of volatile solvents in warm, dry air, urushi does not "dry" at all; it hardens via a complex bio-catalytic oxidative polymerization that strictly requires high ambient humidity and moderate temperatures. The harvested raw sap is first refined through nayashi (stirring at low heat to homogenize the mixture) and kurome (controlled heating at 35°C to 45°C to evaporate excess moisture down to roughly 3%). When the refined lacquer is brushed onto an object, it must be placed inside a specialized, dark wooden curing chamber known as a furo or muro. The furo is maintained at a relative humidity of 70 to 80 percent and a temperature between 20°C and 25°C. In this moist atmosphere, the enzyme laccase utilizes ambient water molecules and atmospheric oxygen to catalyze the oxidation of urushiol into reactive radicals, which cross-link into an impenetrable, three-dimensional polymeric macromolecular network.

E. Once fully cured, the resulting cross-linked polymer film exhibits extraordinary chemical resilience. Cured urushi is completely insoluble in water, alcohol, and petroleum solvents, and resists degradation by boiling water, concentrated acids (including hydrochloric and nitric acids), and caustic alkalis. Furthermore, it possesses natural antibacterial, antiseptic, and antifungal properties, making urushi tableware, tea bowls, and bento boxes exceptionally hygienic and heat-insulating. The only natural physical vulnerability of cured lacquer is prolonged exposure to direct ultraviolet (UV) solar radiation, which causes microscopic photolytic surface erosion and chalking over time.

F. The physical fabrication of high-grade urushi vessels demands an arduous, multi-layered manufacturing cycle lasting months or years. The core base (kijit) is traditionally turned from seasoned, fine-grained hardwoods such as Japanese zelkova (keyaki), horse chestnut (tochi), or cypress (hinoki). To prevent the wood from warping or cracking across seasonal humidity shifts, artisans reinforce structural seams by pasting strips of unbleached hemp cloth (nuno-kise) onto the wooden core using a mixture of raw lacquer and rice paste (nori-urushi). Successive foundation layers (shitaji) composed of raw lacquer mixed with calcined diatomaceous earth (diatomite or jinoko) and fine clay are applied, cured in the furo, and laboriously ground flat using whetstones. On top of these foundation beds, multiple intermediate and top coats of refined, pigmented lacquer (such as black lacquer colored with iron particles or cinnabar-red lacquer colored with mercuric sulfide) are applied, with each individual layer cured, polished with charcoal (hoba-zumi), and buffed by hand with deer horn powder and rapeseed oil.

G. The decorative zenith of Japanese lacquer artistry is represented by the maki-e ("sprinkled picture") technique, developed during the Heian and Kamakura periods (eighth to fourteenth centuries CE). Using fine brushes made from the hair of rats or cats, master artisans paint delicate pictorial designs onto the polished lacquer surface using fresh, wet lacquer. Before the lacquer cures, the artist gently taps a bamboo quill (funzutsu) to sprinkle microscopic powders of pure gold, silver, or copper across the wet lines. Once the metal particles adhere, the surface is sealed with a protective coat of clear lacquer, cured in the furo, and polished down with charcoal to reveal radiant, shimmering metallic imagery embedded within the glossy black matrix. Today, while mass-produced petroleum polyurethane imitations flood the consumer market, master craftsmen (shokunin) in historic centers such as Wajima, Kanazawa, and Aizu-Wakamatsu continue to preserve this living biocultural chemistry, maintaining a sacred tradition that unites nature, art, and molecular science.'
);

