-- Passage 3: Structural Realism and Ontic Structuralism in the Philosophy of Physics
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'f4850ecf-7733-5552-97bf-dd553f811947', 'd008fe55-107a-5f7e-9dce-1de8ffbdad57', 3, 'Structural Realism and Ontic Structuralism in the Philosophy of Physics',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the philosophy of science and analytical metaphysics, the debate surrounding Scientific Realism represents one of the most foundational epistemological battlegrounds of the modern era. Scientific realists maintain that our best, mature scientific theories provide an approximately true description of an objective, mind-independent physical world, and that the unobservable entities postulated by these theories (such as electrons, quarks, gravitational fields, and curved spacetime) genuinely exist. Realists anchor their conviction in the celebrated "No-Miracles Argument" (formulated by Hilary Putnam), which asserts that scientific realism is the only philosophy that does not make the extraordinary predictive, technological, and instrumental success of modern science an inexplicable miracle.

B. Conversely, anti-realist philosophers challenge this confidence through the formidable "Pessimistic Meta-Induction" (PMI) on the history of science, crystallized by Larry Laudan. Laudan demonstrated that the history of science is a vast graveyard of mature, empirically successful theories whose central theoretical terms and ontological entities were subsequently discarded as completely non-existent. For example, eighteenth-century caloric thermodynamics made highly accurate empirical predictions about heat transfer, yet "caloric fluid" does not exist; nineteenth-century wave optics (formulated by Augustin-Jean Fresnel) predicted optical diffraction with mathematical perfection, yet the "luminiferous aether" through which light was supposed to propagate does not exist. If past empirically successful theories turned out to be completely false in their ontological posits, Laudan argued, inductive logic dictates that our current theories (like quantum mechanics and general relativity) will likely suffer the exact same fate.

C. In 1989, British philosopher of science John Worrall proposed a revolutionary metaphysical compromise designed to reconcile the No-Miracles Argument with the Pessimistic Meta- Induction: Structural Realism. Worrall re-examined the historical transition from Fresnel’s wave optics to James Clerk Maxwell’s electromagnetic theory of light. While Maxwell completely abandoned Fresnel’s ontological entity (the mechanical aether), the mathematical equations describing the structure of optical wave propagation (Fresnel''s equations) were preserved virtually intact within Maxwell’s framework. Worrall argued that what scientific theories get right across historical paradigm shifts is not the nature of unobservable "entities" or "objects," but the mathematical structure of physical relations that govern the universe.

D. In contemporary philosophy of physics, Structural Realism has bifurcated into two distinct, competing metaphysical frameworks: 1. Epistemic Structural Realism (ESR): Championed by Worrall, ESR is an epistemological position. It asserts that physical objects and entities do exist, but human observers can only ever know the relational and mathematical structures connecting them, never the intrinsic, non-relational natures of the objects themselves. 2. Ontic Structural Realism (OSR): Formulated by James Ladyman, Steven French, and Don Ross, OSR advances a radical ontological claim: there are no independent physical "objects" or "things" at all. Structure is all there is. In the famous slogan of French and Ladyman: "Every thing must go!"

E. Ontic Structural Realists argue that modern fundamental physics—specifically Quantum Mechanics and Quantum Field Theory (QFT)—demands the complete abandonment of classical object-oriented metaphysics. In classical Newtonian physics, the universe is conceptualized as a collection of individual, self-identical "billiard-ball" particles with distinct identities, occupying precise spatial coordinates. In quantum mechanics, however, identical subatomic particles (such as electrons or bosons) exhibit permutation symmetry and obey Fermi-Dirac or Bose-Einstein quantum statistics. Interchanging two identical electrons does not produce a new physical state of affairs; quantum particles lack individual identity and haecceity ("thisness").

F. Furthermore, quantum entanglement—formalized via the Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen (EPR) paradox and Bell’s Theorem—proves that two entangled particles possess holistic relational properties that cannot be decomposed into or explained by the intrinsic, local properties of the individual particles. The physical relation of entanglement exists without individual, localized relata. In Quantum Field Theory, this ontological shift is even more pronounced: elementary "particles" are not tiny physical spheres, but merely quantized, localized excitations of underlying relational operator fields characterized by abstract gauge group symmetries (such as $U(1) \times SU(2) \times SU(3)$ in the Standard Model).

G. In general relativity, OSR finds further support through the "Hole Argument," originally formulated by Albert Einstein and modernized by John Earman and John Norton. The Hole Argument demonstrates that if physical space-time points are treated as independent, substantial "entities" (spacetime substantivalism), general relativity’s fundamental property of diffeomorphism invariance (gauge freedom) leads to an unacceptable form of radical indeterminism. To preserve physical determinism, philosophers must reject the idea that space- time points possess primitive individual identities; spacetime is constituted entirely by the invariant geometric relations between gravitational fields.

H. Despite its growing popularity, Ontic Structural Realism faces intense philosophical pushback from metaphysical traditionalists, who raise the "No-Relations-Without-Relata" objection: how can a relational structure exist if there are no physical objects standing in those relations? OSR theorists respond by radically reconceptualizing relationality as ontologically fundamental: objects are not the mysterious, underlying "anchors" of physical relations, but are merely persistent nodes or structural intersections within an evolving mathematical universe. By steering a middle path between naive scientific realism and destructive skepticism, Structural Realism reveals that science progressively uncovers the deep, enduring mathematical tapestry that constitutes the cosmos.'
);

