-- Passage 1: The Architecture and Civil Engineering of Persian Qanats
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '72ebb931-ea89-5979-9ece-ead6e7a32a6c', '4eadefb2-9b19-5076-83b7-cedb7c83859f', 1, 'The Architecture and Civil Engineering of Persian Qanats',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the vast, arid plateau of ancient Persia (modern-day Iran), permanent human settlement and intensive agriculture have historically confronted severe climatic barriers. Flanked by rugged mountain ranges such as the Alborz and Zagros, the central Iranian plateau receives scant annual rainfall, often averaging less than 150 to 200 millimeters per year. Furthermore, intense summer heat and persistent arid winds cause rapid surface evaporation, making open-air canals, rivers, and surface reservoirs largely unviable for long-distance water transport. Yet, for more than three millennia, flourishing oasis cities, terraced agricultural orchards, and expansive desert civilizations thrived across this parched landscape. The technological cornerstone of this ancient civilization was the qanat—a monumental subterranean hydraulic engineering system that taps upland groundwater and transports it across tens of kilometers by gravity alone, completely shielded from solar evaporation.

B. The historical invention of the qanat traces back to the early first millennium BCE, during the transition between the late Bronze Age and the early Iron Age. Archaeological consensus suggests that the technique originated among ancient Persian miners in northwestern Iran who adapted subterranean mine dewatering techniques to harvest drinking and irrigation water. Following the rise of the Achaemenid Empire (circa 550–330 BCE), the construction of qanats was heavily incentivized by imperial decrees: royal rulers granted hereditary land-use rights and tax exemptions to families who financed the excavation of new subterranean channels across desert wastes. Consequently, qanat technology diffused across the ancient world, spreading westward throughout the Levant, North Africa (where it is known as a foggara), and the Iberian Peninsula, as well as eastward along the Silk Road into the Turpan Oasis of western China (known as a karez).

C. The civil engineering of a qanat begins with the meticulous location of a dependable subterranean water source. Specialized traditional engineers, known in Persian as muqannis, surveyed the piedmont alluvial fans where high-altitude mountain ranges met arid desert plains. By observing subtle surface indicators—such as the presence of deep-rooted, water-seeking phreatophyte vegetation (like wild licorice or tamarisk) and localized soil dampness—the muqanni determined the optimal location to sink an exploratory vertical shaft. This primary exploratory well, known as the "mother well" (madar-chah), was excavated deep into the alluvial aquifer, often reaching depths of fifty to one hundred meters, until it tapped the permanent, unconfined water table.

D. Once a dependable water supply was confirmed in the mother well, the muqannis began the extraordinary engineering task of excavating the main subterranean conduit (koreh). The defining mechanical requirement of a qanat is its slope gradient. If the downward slope is too steep, flowing water will gain excessive kinetic velocity, eroding the channel bed, undermining structural sidewalls, and causing catastrophic subterranean tunnel collapses. Conversely, if the slope is too flat or level, the water will stagnate, drop its suspended silt load, and rapidly clog the channel. Using simple traditional surveying instruments—such as plumb bobs, water-level spirit flasks, and graduated wooden sighting rods—the muqannis maintained a remarkably precise, gentle downward gradient, typically calculated between 1:1,000 and 1:1,500 (a vertical descent of just one meter for every kilometer of horizontal distance).

E. To facilitate subterranean excavation, remove excavated spoil, and provide vital life support for underground workers, the muqannis sank a regular series of vertical access shafts along the subterranean tunnel trajectory. Spaced at intervals of twenty to thirty meters, these vertical shafts served three indispensable civil engineering functions: 1. Spoil Removal: Workers hauled out thousands of tons of excavated stone, clay, and gravel using wooden winches (charkh) positioned above the shaft openings. 2. Ventilation: The shafts established natural convective airflow loops, delivering fresh air to subterranean diggers working in low-oxygen environments. 3. Maintenance Access: The shafts allowed inspection crews to descend and clear seasonal silt accumulation or repair tunnel cave-ins. At the surface, the excavated spoil was piled in circular mounds around each shaft mouth, creating distinctive "crater-like" ridges across the desert that prevented surface flash floods and blowing sand from washing into the subterranean openings.

F. To stabilize the tunnel through unstable sandy or gravelly strata, ancient Persian engineers developed modular ceramic reinforcing hoops known as navaldan or kaval. These oval-shaped, kiln-fired clay rings were lowered down the vertical shafts and stacked side-by-side along the tunnel interior, forming an impenetrable ceramic sleeve that prevented saturated sand from collapsing into the channel. Once the qanat reached the desert surface at the daylight point (mazhar), the water flowed into a network of open distribution channels (kariz) and communal underground water storage reservoirs (ab anbars). These domed subterranean cisterns were engineered with towering passive windcatchers (badgirs) that captured desert breezes to keep the stored water cool and fresh through natural evaporative cooling.

G. Beyond its civil engineering brilliance, the enduring success of the qanat system was anchored in traditional civic governance and equitable water allocation. Water rights were meticulously managed by an elected community water master, known as the mirab. The mirab calibrated water distribution among agricultural plots using an ancient water clock (tasht or fenjan), which measured the precise time required for a perforated copper bowl to sink in a water vessel. In 2016, UNESCO inscribed "The Persian Qanat" on the World Heritage List, celebrating eleven exemplary systems—including the Qanat of Zarch, which stretches over seventy-one kilometers with more than two thousand vertical shafts. As modern fossil-fuel-powered deep tube wells rapidly deplete non-renewable aquifers across the globe, the ancient Persian qanat endures as a timeless model of sustainable, gravity-driven water management.'
);

