-- Passage 2: Synthetic Biology and Metabolic Engineering for Bio-Based Chemical Synthesis
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '990942af-cc55-5ffe-9659-e810897335ba', '4eadefb2-9b19-5076-83b7-cedb7c83859f', 2, 'Synthetic Biology and Metabolic Engineering for Bio-Based Chemical Synthesis',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the twentieth century, the global industrial chemical manufacturing sector was constructed almost entirely around petrochemical refining. Derived from crude petroleum and fossil natural gas, petrochemical feedstocks serve as the primary raw precursors for an immense catalog of modern materials, spanning synthetic polymers, textile fibers, adhesives, solvents, pharmaceutical intermediates, and agricultural pesticides. However, the reliance on fossil resources entails severe ecological and geopolitical liabilities: fossil extraction generates massive greenhouse gas emissions, depletes finite geological reserves, and releases toxic chemical waste streams. In response to these sustainability crises, modern industrial biotechnology is advancing Synthetic Biology and Metabolic Engineering to transition chemical manufacturing from fossil refineries to bio-based microbial cell factories.

B. Metabolic Engineering is defined as the purposeful, directed modification of cellular metabolic networks and enzymatic pathways to optimize the production of specific target chemical compounds from renewable biological feedstocks (such as sugarcane sucrose, corn starch, cellulosic biomass, or industrial waste gases). Synthetic biology amplifies this capability by introducing standardized, artificial genetic parts (promoters, ribosome binding sites, and synthetic gene circuits) into industrial microbial host organisms (chassis strains). The goal is to re-wire microbial metabolism, turning common bacteria and yeasts into miniature, high- efficiency biochemical fermentation factories capable of synthesizing valuable drop-in chemicals that are structurally identical to petroleum-derived equivalents.

C. In industrial metabolic engineering, two domesticated microorganisms serve as the primary host chassis strains: the gram-negative bacterium Escherichia coli and the budding yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae. E. coli is favored for its rapid growth kinetics (doubling times under twenty minutes), well-characterized genetics, and ease of high-throughput recombinant DNA manipulation. In contrast, S. cerevisiae is the preferred eukaryotic platform for expressing complex multi-enzyme plant pathways, possessing natural tolerance to low pH environments and high concentrations of toxic organic acids and industrial alcohols.

D. The genetic restructuring of these microbial hosts has been revolutionized by precision genome-editing technologies, most notably the CRISPR-Cas9 (Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats) system. CRISPR-Cas9 allows metabolic engineers to introduce multiplex gene knockouts, precise point mutations, and targeted multi-gene insertions into host chromosomes with base-pair precision. By knocking out competing endogenous metabolic pathways that consume vital biochemical intermediates, engineers prevent the host cell from diverting carbon toward unwanted metabolic byproducts (such as lactic acid, acetate, or excess biomass). Simultaneously, engineers insert heterologous biosynthetic genes—often sourced from rare medicinal plants, marine organisms, or fungi—to establish novel, non-native enzymatic pathways.

E. To coordinate and balance complex metabolic networks containing dozens of sequential enzymatic reactions, computational bioengineers utilize Flux Balance Analysis (FBA) and constraint-based genome-scale metabolic models. In a living cell, every biochemical reaction is part of a complex, interconnected web governed by conservation of mass, reaction stoichiometry, and thermodynamic equilibrium. If one intermediate enzyme in a synthetic pathway operates too slowly, toxic metabolic intermediates accumulate, poisoning the cell; if an enzyme operates too quickly, vital metabolic cofactors (such as adenosine triphosphate, ATP, and nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate, NADPH) are rapidly exhausted, stalling cell growth. Flux Balance Analysis mathematically models the flow of metabolites through the entire cellular network, allowing researchers to optimize enzyme expression levels, balance redox cofactors, and eliminate kinetic bottlenecks in-silico before conducting laboratory experiments.

F. Despite remarkable laboratory successes in synthesizing high-value specialty molecules— such as the anti-malarial drug precursor artemisinic acid, bio-based vanilla (vanillin), and high- potency squalene—scaling microbial fermentation to industrial gigaliter production poses formidable bioprocess engineering hurdles. Inside commercial industrial bioreactors exceeding 50,000 to 100,000 liters in volume, physical mixing is non-uniform, creating spatial gradients in dissolved oxygen concentration, pH, nutrient availability, and hydrostatic shear stress. Microbial cells exposed to these fluctuating micro-environments undergo metabolic stress responses, down-regulating synthetic pathways and reducing overall product yield, titer, and productivity (metrics known as the YTP parameters). Furthermore, the accumulation of high concentrations of the target chemical product inside the fermentation broth often exhibits severe end-product toxicity, damaging microbial cell membranes and terminating cell viability prematurely.

G. To overcome product toxicity and improve bioreactor yields, synthetic biologists are engineering membrane-bound active efflux pumps that continuously export the target product out of the cytoplasm into the extracellular broth. Additionally, adaptive laboratory evolution (ALE) is utilized to select for robust, stress-tolerant microbial mutant strains capable of thriving under high product titers and elevated temperatures. By synthesizing computational pathway modeling, precision CRISPR editing, and advanced bioprocess engineering, bio-based chemical manufacturing is transitioning from experimental academic laboratories to commercial biorefineries, providing an essential foundation for a sustainable, circular bioeconomy.'
);

