-- Passage 3: The Extended Mind Thesis and Active Cognitive Externalism
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '8c14c9fe-2875-5099-9f42-13adb869b2e2', '4eadefb2-9b19-5076-83b7-cedb7c83859f', 3, 'The Extended Mind Thesis and Active Cognitive Externalism',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In traditional philosophy of mind, cognitive science, and Cartesian metaphysics, the boundaries of human cognition have long been treated as coextensive with the biological organism. Under this classical "internalist" and "intracranial" orthodoxy, the mind is presumed to reside strictly inside the physical boundaries of the skull and skin (intracranialism). Cognition is conceptualized as an internal biological computation executed by the neural circuitry of the central nervous system: sensory organs receive raw physical stimuli from the external environment, the brain processes this information internally through mental representations and computational transformations, and motor nerves execute physical actions in the outside world. The external physical environment is viewed merely as a passive source of input data and a stage for behavioral outputs, playing no constitutive role in the cognitive process itself.

B. In 1998, however, British philosopher Andy Clark and Australian philosopher David Chalmers fundamentally disrupted this internalist consensus by publishing their landmark paper The Extended Mind in the journal Analysis. Clark and Chalmers advanced a radical philosophical proposition: active cognition is not confined to the biological brain or the physical skull. Under certain conditions, non-biological physical artifacts located in the external environment—such as notebooks, pocket calculators, smartphones, computer screens, and interactive sketchpads—do not merely assist or augment internal thought; rather, they function as literal, constitutive physical components of the cognitive system itself. This philosophical framework is known as the Extended Mind Thesis (EMT), or Active Cognitive Externalism.

C. To establish their thesis, Clark and Chalmers articulated the famous "Parity Principle," a functionalist criterion designed to dismantle intracranial bias: "If, as we confront some task, a part of the world functions as a process which, were it done in the head, we would have no hesitation in recognizing as part of the cognitive process, then that part of the world is (so we claim) part of the cognitive process." The Parity Principle is strictly functionalist in its metaphysical logic: it asserts that cognitive status is determined by the functional, computational role that a physical structure performs in driving intelligent behavior, rather than by its material biological composition (whether it is made of biological carbon-based neurons or silicon-and-paper artifacts) or its spatial location (inside or outside the skull).

D. Clark and Chalmers illustrated the Parity Principle through their classic, enduring thought experiment comparing two individuals navigating New York City: Inga and Otto: 1. Inga (Internal Biological Memory): Inga hears about an art exhibition at the Museum of Modern Art (MoMA) on 53rd Street. She pauses, accesses her internal biological memory stored in her cerebral cortex, recalls that the museum is located on 53rd Street, and walks to the museum. Philosophers unanimously agree that Inga’s retrieval of her stored belief is a cognitive process, and that her stored memory represents a non- conscious, dispositional belief. 2. Otto (Extended Environmental Memory): Otto suffers from a severe form of Alzheimer’s disease and cannot retain new biological memories in his brain. To function in daily life, Otto carries a small physical notebook everywhere he goes. Whenever Otto learns new information, he immediately writes it down in his notebook; whenever he needs to recall a fact, he looks it up. Otto hears about the exhibition at MoMA, consults his notebook, retrieves the address "53rd Street," and walks to the museum.

E. Clark and Chalmers argue that Otto’s notebook plays the exact same functional and causal role for Otto that biological cortical memory plays for Inga. In both cases, the information is reliably available, readily accessible, automatically endorsed as true, and guides successful intentional behavior. Therefore, if Inga’s biological brain state constitutes a cognitive belief, consistency demands that Otto’s notebook must equally be recognized as a physical constituent of his extended cognitive memory. To deny cognitive status to Otto’s notebook simply because it is made of paper and resides outside the skin represents an unprincipled form of biological chauvinism.

F. The Extended Mind Thesis provoked immediate, intense resistance from orthodox internalist philosophers and cognitive scientists, most notably Fred Adams, Kenneth Aizawa, and Robert Rupert. Adams and Aizawa formulated the celebrated "Coupling-Constitution Fallacy" critique. They argued that Clark and Chalmers illegitimately conflate a causal connection (coupling) with an ontological constitution (constitution). While an external tool (like a notebook or calculator) is undeniably causally coupled to a human thinker, being causally linked to a cognitive system does not make the tool a constitutive part of the cognitive system itself, any more than a doctor’s stethoscope is a constitutive part of the human cardiovascular system.

G. Furthermore, Adams and Aizawa emphasized the criterion of "non-derived intentionality" and the "mark of the cognitive." They pointed out that internal biological brain states possess intrinsic, original intentionality—thoughts naturally mean what they mean without requiring external human interpretation. In contrast, marks on a piece of paper in Otto’s notebook possess only conventional, derived intentionality: the symbols mean nothing on their own until a conscious human eye reads, interprets, and assigns meaning to them. Because external artifacts lack intrinsic, non-derived intentional states and fast, low-level neural micro-dynamics, internalists argue that the true "mark of the cognitive" remains firmly confined within the biological skull.

H. In contemporary cognitive philosophy, the debate over the Extended Mind has evolved into the broader paradigm of "4E Cognition," which conceptualizes the human mind as Embodied, Embedded, Enactive, and Extended. In our modern digital era, where billions of human beings rely continuously on smartphones, cloud search engines, and artificial intelligence interfaces to store memories, navigate spatial landscapes, and execute complex reasoning, the philosophical boundaries between brain, body, and technology have become increasingly fluid. The Extended Mind Thesis reminds us that human intelligence is uniquely defined by our extraordinary evolutionary capacity to weave external tools directly into the fabric of our cognitive lives, transforming the human mind into an open, boundless partnership with the technological cosmos.'
);

