-- Passage 2: Phase-Change Materials (PCMs) in Passive Architectural Thermal Regulation
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'c9f0bc9e-aabf-567b-a188-e755c29b1758', 'f092e32e-7fe4-59d1-8d5c-917faa2bd76f', 2, 'Phase-Change Materials (PCMs) in Passive Architectural Thermal Regulation',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the global drive toward sustainable architecture and operational decarbonization, the thermal management of residential and commercial buildings represents a monumental engineering challenge. Building heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning (HVAC) systems account for roughly 40 percent of total building operational energy consumption and a substantial fraction of peak electrical grid demand during extreme summer heatwaves and winter cold snaps. Traditionally, architects integrated heavy, high-mass construction materials—such as solid stone, unreinforced brick masonry, and thick concrete slabs—to provide structural thermal mass. High thermal mass absorbs excess indoor heat during the day and releases it during cooler nighttime hours, smoothing indoor temperature swings. However, modern high-rise architectural construction relies predominantly on lightweight materials—such as steel frames, gypsum wallboards, and glass curtain walls—which possess negligible thermal mass, resulting in rapid indoor thermal fluctuations and inflated mechanical cooling loads.

B. To endow modern lightweight buildings with massive thermal buffering capacity without adding thousands of tons of structural deadweight, materials scientists and building physicists are integrating Phase-Change Materials (PCMs) into building envelopes. A phase-change material is a latent heat storage medium that absorbs, stores, and releases colossal quantities of thermal energy at an almost constant temperature during its physical phase transitions—typically between a solid and a liquid state. When ambient indoor air temperature rises above the PCM''s calibrated melting point, the material melts, absorbing large amounts of latent heat of fusion from the room and preventing the indoor space from overheating. Conversely, when the room cools at night below the solidification point, the PCM freezes, releasing its stored latent heat into the living area.

C. PCMs deployed in building applications are broadly categorized into three primary chemical families: organic, inorganic, and eutectic mixtures: 1. Organic PCMs (Paraffins and Bio-based Fatty Acids): Paraffin waxes (hydrocarbon chains, $C_n H_{2n+2}$) and bio-based fatty acids (derived from plant oils and tallow, such as lauric and palmitic acids) are the most widely deployed building PCMs. They exhibit exceptional chemical stability, complete non-corrosiveness toward metals, negligible supercooling, and no phase segregation over thousands of melting-freezing cycles. However, organic PCMs possess low intrinsic thermal conductivity ($\approx 0.15\text{ to }0.25 \text{ W/m}\cdot\text{K}$) and present a flammability hazard unless treated with flame-retardant additives. 2. Inorganic PCMs (Salt Hydrates): Inorganic salt hydrates (such as calcium chloride hexahydrate, $CaCl_2\cdot 6H_2O$, and sodium sulfate decahydrate, $Na_2SO_4\cdot 10H_2O$) offer higher volumetric latent heat storage capacities, higher thermal conductivity ($\approx 0.5\text{ to }1.0 \text{ W/m}\cdot\text{K}$), and are non- flammable and low-cost. However, salt hydrates suffer from phase segregation (incongruent melting where heavier anhydrous salt precipitates to the bottom of the container) and severe supercooling (where the liquid fails to solidify until cooled far below its nominal transition point). 3. Eutectic PCMs: Specific minimum-melting-point blends of organic-organic or inorganic- inorganic compounds that freeze and melt congruently without phase separation.

D. The practical integration of PCMs into building materials requires advanced microencapsulation technologies to prevent liquid leakage during melting cycles. Microencapsulated PCMs consist of microscopic polymer or silica shells (measuring between 1 and 30 micrometers in diameter) that permanently seal tiny cores of paraffin or bio-fatty acids. These microscopic capsules can be directly blended into standard building materials—such as wet gypsum plasterboards, acoustic ceiling tiles, interior wall paints, and concrete mortar— during manufacturing. A standard 1.5-centimeter-thick gypsum wallboard impregnated with 20 to 30 percent microencapsulated PCM provides the equivalent thermal heat-storage capacity of a solid 15-centimeter-thick concrete wall, reducing peak cooling loads by up to 25 to 35 percent.

E. Despite their high latent heat storage density, the widespread architectural deployment of PCMs is constrained by two critical thermal physics bottlenecks: low thermal conductivity and the necessity of complete nocturnal regeneration. Because organic PCMs transfer heat slowly, the outer layer of a PCM panel can melt while the interior core remains solid, limiting the effective depth of active thermal storage. To overcome low conductivity, materials engineers incorporate high-conductivity nanostructured additives—such as expanded exfoliated graphite, carbon nanotubes, copper foam matrices, or aluminum pin fins—which increase thermal conductivity by up to an order of magnitude.

F. An equally vital operational requirement is the diurnal regeneration cycle. For a PCM to function effectively day after day, it must completely re-solidify (freeze) every night to reset its latent heat storage capacity before the next daytime heating cycle begins. In climates where nocturnal summer temperatures remain warm and humid (such as tropical or sub-tropical urban areas), natural passive convective cooling through open windows is insufficient to freeze the PCM overnight. Under these conditions, the PCM remains permanently melted, losing its buffering capacity entirely. To guarantee complete nocturnal freezing, building engineers integrate PCMs with active night-flush mechanical ventilation, automated economizer dampers, or ground-source heat pump auxiliary loops.

G. As advanced computational energy modeling tools and mass-manufactured bio-based PCMs continue to mature, latent heat thermal storage is transitioning from experimental prototypes to standard architectural specifications. From net-zero energy retrofits in historic buildings to modern modular prefab construction, phase-change materials provide a passive, self-regulating thermal sponge that slashes energy costs, enhances occupant comfort, and supports resilient urban building design in an era of climate change.'
);

