-- Passage 3: Epistemic Virtue and the Value Problem in Epistemology
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '08930a75-0bde-5dea-aa8d-161729172ba3', 'f092e32e-7fe4-59d1-8d5c-917faa2bd76f', 3, 'Epistemic Virtue and the Value Problem in Epistemology',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In foundational epistemology and the philosophy of mind, the central intellectual inquiry has traditionally focused on defining the necessary and sufficient conditions of human knowledge. Since Plato’s classical dialogue Theaetetus, orthodox epistemology conceptualized knowledge as "Justified True Belief" (JTB). Under this classical tripartite model, an agent ($S$) knows a proposition ($p$) if and only if: 1. $p$ is true; 2. $S$ believes that $p$; and 3. $S$ is epistemically justified in believing that $p$. However, beyond the structural challenge of defining knowledge—famously disrupted in 1963 by Edmund Gettier’s counterexamples demonstrating that justified true beliefs could arise through sheer coincidence or epistemic luck—epistemologists have confronted an older, profound axiological puzzle: the Value Problem (also known as the Meno Problem).

B. First articulated by Socrates in Plato''s dialogue Meno, the Value Problem asks a fundamental question about the normativity of belief: why is genuine knowledge more valuable than mere true belief? To illustrate the paradox, Socrates presented the famous analogy of the road to Larissa. Imagine two guides offering to lead a group of travelers to the ancient Greek city of Larissa: Guide A possesses genuine knowledge of the route, having personally traveled the road many times and mapped its terrain. Guide B possesses merely a true belief about the route; by lucky coincidence or an ungrounded hunch, Guide B happens to believe that the city lies to the north, which happens to be factually correct. Socrates pointed out that both guides will successfully lead the travelers to Larissa. If true belief achieves practical success just as reliably as knowledge, why should rational societies place a higher intrinsic and instrumental value on knowledge?

C. In contemporary analytical epistemology, the Value Problem has been expanded into the "Swamping Problem," formalized by philosophers such as Jonathan Kvanvig and Wayne Riggs. The Swamping Problem asserts that if the primary value of an epistemic state is purely truth- conducive (veritistic instrumental value), then the value of the method used to obtain that truth is completely "swamped" by the value of the truth itself. Consider an analogy: a cup of high- quality, delicious espresso made by an expert barista has high value. If an identical, equally delicious cup of espresso is produced by a broken, unreliable coffee machine by pure fluke, the beverage is not less delicious because of its faulty origins. If truth is the sole epistemic good, then adding "justification" or "reliability" to a true belief adds zero additional value, rendering traditional epistemic theories axiologically bankrupt.

D. To rescue epistemology from the Swamping Problem and provide a comprehensive account of epistemic value, contemporary philosophers have developed Virtue Epistemology. Pioneered in the 1980s and 1990s by philosophers Ernest Sosa, Linda Zagzebski, and John Greco, Virtue Epistemology shifts the primary focus of epistemological evaluation away from isolated, static propositions and abstract belief structures toward the intellectual character, cognitive faculties, and epistemic virtues of the human agent.

E. In contemporary virtue epistemology, the field has bifurcated into two primary theoretical camps: Virtue Reliabilism and Virtue Responsibilism: 1. Virtue Reliabilism (Ernest Sosa): Sosa models epistemic competence on performance normativity, comparing knowledge to athletic virtuosity (such as an archer shooting an arrow at a target). Sosa formalizes epistemic evaluation through the "AAA Model": o Accuracy ($A_1$): Whether the shot hits the target (whether the belief is true). o Adroitness ($A_2$): Whether the shot was executed with skill and competence (whether the cognitive faculty is reliable). o Aptness ($A_3$): Whether the shot was accurate because it was adroit (whether the belief is true because of the agent’s intellectual competence, rather than lucky wind gusts). In Sosa’s reliabilism, genuine knowledge is apt belief. An apt belief has distinctive value because it represents an intellectual achievement creditable to the agent’s own cognitive ability. 2. Virtue Responsibilism (Linda Zagzebski): Zagzebski models epistemic virtues on Aristotelian moral virtues. Epistemic virtues are not mere subconscious biological faculties (like 20/20 visual acuity), but cultivated, acquired character traits and intellectual motivations. These include open-mindedness, intellectual courage, intellectual humility, intellectual tenacity, and epistemic fairness.

F. Virtue Epistemology provides an elegant resolution to the Swamping Problem by demonstrating that intellectual success achieved through genuine competence possesses an intrinsic, non-instrumental value that surpasses lucky success. Consider an athletic achievement: winning a marathon through years of rigorous training, pacing strategy, and athletic virtue is intrinsically more valuable and admirable than "winning" because all competing runners accidentally boarded the wrong bus. In the epistemic realm, reaching the truth through the exercise of cognitive virtues represents an authentic human achievement. The value of knowledge is not swamped by truth because the agent deserves epistemic credit for their cognitive success.

G. Furthermore, Virtue Responsibilism provides crucial tools for addressing modern social and political epistemic crises, such as epistemic polarization, dogmatism, and the spread of digital misinformation. Zagzebski and contemporary social epistemologists (such as Miranda Fricker and Jason Baehr) demonstrate that the primary vulnerabilities in modern informational ecosystems are not failures of raw computational processing or lack of data, but intellectual vices: confirmation bias, epistemic arrogance, close-mindedness, and intellectual cowardice. Fostering intellectual virtues in public education and civic discourse provides the essential ethical framework required for democratic societies to navigate complex technological futures.

H. In synthesizing the modern debate, Virtue Epistemology transforms the study of knowledge from a sterile, abstract logic of belief conditions into a rich, normative exploration of human intellectual character. Knowledge is not merely a passive state of holding accurate factual data; it is an active, living expression of human cognitive agency. By linking the pursuit of truth with the cultivation of intellectual virtue, epistemology reaffirms that the quest for knowledge is inseparable from the lifelong pursuit of wisdom, integrity, and human flourishing.'
);

