-- Passage 1: The Agricultural Systems and Terraced Agroforestry of the Ifugao Rice Terraces
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'd37de6a1-0216-5e14-a213-ef9322dc6128', '272ca782-8c9f-5430-a514-01cb7b3fbe28', 1, 'The Agricultural Systems and Terraced Agroforestry of the Ifugao Rice Terraces',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Perched precipitously across the rugged, mist-shrouded Cordillera Central mountain range on the northern island of Luzon in the Philippines, the Ifugao Rice Terraces represent one of the most extraordinary monuments of living indigenous agroecological engineering on Earth. Constructed across vertical mountain slopes often exceeding forty to fifty degrees, these stepped pond terraces (payyo) transform rugged, erosion-prone topography into highly productive, flooded agricultural amphitheatres. Carved and maintained for well over a millennium by the indigenous Ifugao people without the aid of draft animals, heavy machinery, or slave labor, the terraces demonstrate an intricate balance of hydraulic manipulation, forest conservation, and communal customary law. In 1995, UNESCO recognized the global significance of this living cultural landscape by inscribing the Rice Terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras onto the World Heritage List.

B. The enduring ecological sustainability of the Ifugao payyo is anchored in a complex, multi- tiered watershed management system known as the muyong or pinugo. The muyong is a privately owned or clan-managed natural forest lot situated directly on the mountain ridges and upper peaks above the stepped terraces. In Ifugao agroforestry, the muyong functions as a vital biological sponge and hydrologic buffer: the multi-layered rainforest canopy, dense root networks, and thick organic leaf litter absorb torrential monsoonal precipitation, minimize severe surface soil erosion, and regulate the continuous, slow subterranean percolation of water into natural mountain springs. The Ifugao actively manage these private forest plots through traditional silviculture, selectively harvesting mature timber while systematically planting indigenous hardwood trees, rattan vines, bamboo, and medicinal herbs.

C. Water exiting the high-altitude muyong forest springs is captured and channeled through an intricate, gravity-driven hydraulic network. Because the rocky mountain terrain is highly fragmented, Ifugao hydrologists engineered unlined earthen canals, stone-lined aqueducts, and hollowed-out wooden flumes (made from halved tree trunks) that contour across precipitous cliffs for kilometers. The water enters the uppermost terrace tier and cascades sequentially downward through the entire terraced complex via stone drainage chutes, carved spillways, and bamboo conduit pipes embedded in the terrace walls. Water levels in individual pond fields are calibrated with millimeter precision using adjustable wooden sluice gates, ensuring that the growing rice plants receive continuous, circulating water that regulates soil temperature and suppresses terrestrial weed germination.

D. The physical architecture of an individual payyo terrace represents a sophisticated triumph of geotechnical slope stabilization and soil layering. The foundation begins with a massive, slightly battered retaining wall (pingol) constructed from river stones or dressed mountain basalt boulders fitted tightly together without mortar, reaching heights of two to six meters. Behind this stone wall, the Ifugao construct a multi-layered, compacted soil bed: 1. Foundational Drainage Core: A lower bed of compacted gravel and large river stones that allows subsurface water to filter through without destabilizing the wall. 2. Puddled Clay Hardpan (Ga-ad): An impermeable middle layer of puddled, heavily trampled clay that seals the pond bed, preventing water from draining away through subterranean fissures. 3. Enriched Topsoil Bed (Lupah): An upper layer of rich, fertile muddy loam (measuring twenty to forty centimeters in depth), continuously enriched with decomposed rice straw, wild sunflower biomass (Tithonia diversifolia), azolla water ferns, and organic household compost.

E. Within this aquatic ecosystem, the Ifugao cultivate tinawon, a traditional, photoperiod- sensitive, high-altitude indigenous rice variety. Tinawon is prized for its cold tolerance, exceptional aroma, and nutritional quality, playing a central role in sacred religious rituals, harvest thanksgiving feasts, and the brewing of bayah (traditional rice wine). The pond fields function as polycultural aquatic ecosystems: alongside tinawon rice, farmers cultivate edible mudfish, native river snails (kollokol), freshwater crabs, and frogs. Furthermore, the wide, flat tops of the stone retaining walls (dike or banong) are utilized as raised dryland gardens to grow taro, ginger, sweet potatoes, and spring onions, maximizing caloric yield from every square meter of steep mountain real estate.

F. The operational management of this complex landscape is governed by chawa, a system of customary indigenous jurisprudence and communal labor exchange. Water rights are linked to ancestral inheritance, but customary law strictly prohibits any upstream terrace owner from monopolizing or cutting off water to downstream neighbors. During intensive agricultural phases—such as wall reconstruction after typhoon landslides, mud puddling, and the autumn harvest—villagers mobilize reciprocal labor groups (ubbu or baddang). Under the ubbu system, whole communities work collectively on individual family plots in rotation, ensuring that labor- intensive maintenance tasks are executed rapidly without monetary wages.

G. In the contemporary era, the Ifugao rice terraces face severe socio-economic, demographic, and environmental crises. Accelerated rural-to-urban migration and modern educational opportunities have led younger generations to seek wage employment in lowland cities, creating an acute shortage of the manual labor required to repair damaged stone retaining walls. Furthermore, invasive species—most notably the giant alien earthworm (Amynthas species)— burrow through the clay hardpan, causing severe water leakage and catastrophic wall collapses during heavy typhoons. Additionally, climate change-induced shifts in monsoonal precipitation patterns threaten high-altitude water security. In response, indigenous heritage councils, local agricultural cooperatives, and cultural preservation agencies are establishing heritage schools (Indigenous Knowledge Systems and Practices) and premium organic export markets for tinawon rice, striving to preserve the ancient terraced agroecosystem that has nourished the mountain peoples of Luzon for millennia.'
);

