-- Passage 2: Quantum Dot Luminescent Solar Concentrators (LSCs) in Building-Integrated Photovoltaics
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'c1da0d6e-86dd-5b6d-821f-ea793dc028db', '272ca782-8c9f-5430-a514-01cb7b3fbe28', 2, 'Quantum Dot Luminescent Solar Concentrators (LSCs) in Building-Integrated Photovoltaics',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In modern urban architecture, the rapid growth of high-density metropolitan skylines has transformed the energy footprint of modern cities. Commercial skyscrapers and high-rise office towers possess enormous vertical surface areas dominated by floor-to-ceiling glass facades and curtain walls, but have very limited rooftop surface space relative to their massive total floor area. While conventional opaque silicon solar panels are highly efficient when mounted on open rooftops or open fields, they cannot be deployed across transparent architectural windows without blocking daylight and destroying occupant visibility. To turn ordinary architectural glass facades into zero-emission renewable power generators without sacrificing optical transparency, materials scientists and optoelectronic engineers are developing Luminescent Solar Concentrators (LSCs) utilizing colloidal Quantum Dots (QDs).

B. A Luminescent Solar Concentrator is an optoelectronic device that captures incident sunlight over a large transparent aperture, shifts its wavelength, and concentrates the resulting optical energy onto small, high-efficiency photovoltaic (PV) cells mounted along the narrow perimeter edges of the glass pane. An LSC consists of a high-optical-quality transparent polymer or glass waveguide (such as poly(methyl methacrylate), PMMA, or low-iron architectural glass) embedded or coated with luminescent fluorophores (luminophores). When incoming solar radiation strikes the glass, the embedded luminophores absorb both direct and diffuse solar photons, re-emitting the energy as lower-energy, longer-wavelength photoluminescence (fluorescence).

C. Because the refractive index of the glass or polymer waveguide ($n \approx 1.5$) is significantly higher than that of the surrounding air ($n \approx 1.0$), a large fraction of the re- emitted photons (typically 75 to 80 percent) exceeds the critical angle for total internal reflection (TIR): $$\theta_c = \arcsin\left(\frac{1}{n}\right)$$ These trapped light waves bounce repeatedly between the polished front and back surfaces of the glass pane, guided laterally toward the narrow perimeter edges. At the edges, miniature, narrow strips of high-efficiency photovoltaic cells (such as crystalline silicon or gallium arsenide, GaAs) convert the concentrated optical flux into usable electricity. By collecting light across several square meters of window surface and focusing it onto tiny edge-mounted solar cells, LSCs drastically reduce the quantity and capital cost of expensive photovoltaic semiconductor materials required for solar power generation.

D. While early LSC prototypes developed in the late 1970s utilized organic dye molecules (such as rhodamines or coumarins), organic fluorophores suffered from two fatal physical limitations: severe reabsorption losses and rapid photolytic degradation under solar ultraviolet (UV) radiation. Organic dyes possess narrow Stokes shifts—the spectral difference between the light absorption band and the light emission band. Because the absorption and emission spectra heavily overlap, photons emitted by one dye molecule are rapidly reabsorbed by neighboring dye molecules as they travel along the waveguide, dissipating their energy as thermal heat before reaching the edge solar cells. Furthermore, organic dye chemical bonds break down rapidly after months of direct sun exposure, causing the window to lose its color and concentration efficiency.

E. To overcome these photophysical barriers, modern optoelectronics has pivoted toward colloidal semiconductor Quantum Dots (QDs). Quantum dots are nanometer-scale semiconductor nanocrystals (typically two to ten nanometers in diameter) whose electronic and optical properties are governed by quantum confinement effects. By tuning the physical size, shape, and chemical composition of the nanocrystal, engineers can precisely program the absorption and emission spectra across the entire ultraviolet, visible, and near-infrared (NIR) spectrum.

F. Foremost among quantum dot architectures for transparent windows are core-shell heterostructures, such as Cadmium Selenide/Cadmium Sulfide (CdSe/CdS) and heavy-metal-free Copper Indium Sulfide/Zinc Sulfide (CuInS$_2$/ZnS) nanocrystals. By engineering a giant inorganic shell around a small semiconductor core (the "giant quantum dot" architecture), researchers achieve an exceptionally large Stokes shift (often exceeding 400 to 500 millielectron- volts, meV). The thick outer shell absorbs high-energy photons over a wide spectral range, while the core emits photons at a distinctly lower near-infrared energy level where the shell is completely optically transparent. This spectral decoupling virtually eliminates reabsorption losses, allowing trapped light to travel several meters across large architectural window panes without attenuation.

G. Beyond eliminating reabsorption, quantum dots provide superior photostability and enable aesthetically neutral, transparent glazing. Unlike organic dyes, inorganic quantum dots can endure decades of continuous high-intensity solar UV exposure without chemical photodegradation. Furthermore, by designing quantum dots that absorb strictly invisible ultraviolet ($<400\text{ nm}$) and near-infrared ($>700\text{ nm}$) wavelengths while transmitting the entire visible spectrum ($400\text{ to }700\text{ nm}$), engineers can fabricate high-efficiency photovoltaic windows that appear completely colorless, transparent, and aesthetically neutral to building occupants. As scalable roll-to-roll liquid coating and laminated glass manufacturing techniques mature, quantum-dot luminescent solar concentrators are poised to transform metropolitan skyscrapers into vertical solar farms, unlocking an essential pathway toward carbon-neutral smart cities.'
);

