-- Passage 3: The Philosophy of Time: Presentism vs. Eternalism and the Block Universe
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'cf5720e3-82f4-56a8-97bb-421187e4c114', '272ca782-8c9f-5430-a514-01cb7b3fbe28', 3, 'The Philosophy of Time: Presentism vs. Eternalism and the Block Universe',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In analytical metaphysics, the philosophy of physics, and the ontology of time, few inquiries are as intuitively perplexing and mathematically contested as the objective reality of temporal passage. In everyday conscious human experience, time is perceived as a dynamic, directional river: the past is fixed, gone, and unalterable; the future is open, unrealized, and indeterminate; and our conscious reality exists strictly in the fleeting, razor-thin boundary of the "Now." Yet, when theoretical physicists formulate the foundational mathematical equations of modern physics—from Newtonian mechanics and Maxwellian electromagnetism to quantum mechanics and Einsteinian general relativity—the parameter of time ($t$) appears as a static, bidirectional coordinate. The fundamental laws of nature contain no privileged physical marker designating the "present," no mathematical indicator of an "objective flow," and no intrinsic distinction between past, present, and future.

B. The classical philosophical framework for analyzing temporal language and ontology was established in 1908 by Scottish idealist philosopher John McTaggart Ellis McTaggart in his landmark paper The Unreality of Time. McTaggart demonstrated that events in time can be ordered in two distinct ways: 1. The A-Series (Tensed Time): Events are characterized by dynamic, shifting temporal properties: being past, being present, or being future. In the A-series, an event (such as the landing on the Moon) was once in the distant future, moved into the present, and is now receding further into the past. 2. The B-Series (Tenseless Time): Events are ordered by static, unchanging two-place relational predicates: being earlier than, simultaneous with, or later than. In the B-series, the Moon landing is permanently "earlier than" the year 2026 and "later than" the construction of the Roman Colosseum; these temporal relations never change, regardless of what moment is experienced.

C. In contemporary analytical metaphysics, the debate over the ontological reality of the A- series and B-series has crystallized into two diametrically opposed metaphysical camps: Presentism and Eternalism. Presentism (an A-theory of time), defended by philosophers such as A.N. Prior, Ned Markosian, and William Lane Craig, asserts that only currently existing, present entities and events are real. In the presentist ontology, the past has completely ceased to exist (Julius Caesar is not an existing entity located somewhere else; he is literally nothing), and the future is purely non-existent (our descendants do not exist yet). The universe is an evolving three-dimensional manifold that undergoes continuous, objective ontological state transitions.

D. In stark opposition stands Eternalism (a B-theory of time), championed by metaphysical realists and modern philosophers of physics, including W.V.O. Quine, David Lewis, and J.J.C. Smart. Eternalism asserts that all points in time—past, present, and future—are equally real and ontologically co-existent. In the eternalist framework, temporal distance is ontologically analogous to spatial distance. Just as the city of Tokyo is fully real and physical despite not being located here where you are reading this text, the events of the Battle of Waterloo (1815) and the colonization of Mars (2150) are fully real and physically existing, despite not occurring now. The universe is conceptualized as a static, four-dimensional "Block Universe"—a four-dimensional spatiotemporal manifold containing all space and all time simultaneously.

E. In modern theoretical physics, the strongest empirical support for Eternalism and the Block Universe comes from Albert Einstein’s Special Theory of Relativity (1905). The fatal blow to Presentism delivered by Special Relativity is the Relativity of Simultaneity. Einstein proved that there is no absolute, universal time that ticks uniformly across the cosmos. Two physical events that appear simultaneous to an observer in one inertial reference frame will occur at different times (one in the past, one in the future) according to an observer moving at a different relative velocity.

F. The metaphysical consequence of the relativity of simultaneity was formalized by philosophers C.W. Rietdijk and Hilary Putnam in the celebrated "Rietdijk-Putnam Argument" (also known as the Andromeda Paradox). Imagine two people walking past each other on a street on Earth: Person A is walking toward the Andromeda Galaxy, while Person B is walking away from it. Because of their slight difference in relative velocity across an immense spatial distance (2.5 million light-years), their planes of simultaneity differ by several days. For Person A, an alien space fleet in the Andromeda Galaxy has already launched an invasion toward Earth; for Person B, the alien leaders have not even decided whether to launch the fleet yet! Because both observers are equally legitimate inertial observers in a relativistic spacetime, the launch event must already be real and physical. If the future is already real for one legitimate observer, the presentist claim that the future is non-existent becomes physically untenable.

G. To rescue temporal dynamism without violating Einsteinian physics, some metaphysicians advocate a hybrid model: the "Growing Block Universe" (originally proposed by C.D. Broad). In the Growing Block theory, the past and present are ontologically real, but the future is genuinely open and non-existent. Spacetime is conceptualized as an expanding block that grows continuously as new slices of the "present" freeze into objective reality. However, critics point out that the Growing Block theory creates an inescapable epistemological paradox: how do we know that we are currently located on the dynamic "cutting edge" of the growing block, rather than trapped as an observer in a past slice that froze millions of years ago?

H. In synthesizing the philosophy of time, modern cognitive science recognizes that our subjective feeling of temporal flow is an evolved psychological construct. Human consciousness integrates sensory inputs, short-term working memory, and prospective predictive planning, creating a powerful cognitive illusion of an moving "Now" to help biological organisms survive in a physical universe. While physical reality appears to be a magnificent, timeless four- dimensional block where all moments of history shine eternally, it is our human consciousness that moves through this landscape, experiencing the beauty, tragedy, and meaning of our passing lives.'
);

