-- Passage 1: The Domestication and Agricultural History of the Turkey (Meleagris gallopavo)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '4bdaf623-c631-5849-bfdd-b79c7cb6eb6d', '53a7251f-6aa6-500e-935c-36e05e931c68', 1, 'The Domestication and Agricultural History of the Turkey (Meleagris gallopavo)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the archaeozoological history of the Americas, the wild turkey (Meleagris gallopavo) stands as one of the few indigenous avian species successfully domesticated by Native American civilizations prior to European contact. While Eurasian agriculture domesticated diverse gallinaceous birds—most notably the red junglefowl (Gallus gallus, ancestor of the domestic chicken), greylag goose, and mallard duck—the Americas possessed a distinct suite of fauna. Across the arid scrublands of Mesoamerica and the canyonlands of the American Southwest, the wild turkey evolved into an indispensable economic, ritual, and nutritional resource for sedentary agricultural communities, anchoring complex systems of animal husbandry for well over two millennia.

B. For decades, the precise geographical and temporal origins of turkey domestication were subjects of intense archaeological and genetic debate. Early morphological models hypothesized a single, localized domestication event in central Mexico that subsequently diffused northward along ancient trade routes into the American Southwest. However, recent breakthroughs in ancient mitochondrial DNA (aDNA) sequencing and stable isotope analysis from archaeological bone assemblages have completely revised this model. Geneticists and zooarchaeologists have established that turkeys were domesticated through at least two independent, geographically distinct domestication trajectories: 1. The South Mexican Domestication Event: Occurring in central and southern Mesoamerica (spanning the Valley of Mexico, Oaxaca, and the Maya lowlands) around the late Formative or Preclassic period (circa 300 BCE to 100 CE), utilizing the local South Mexican subspecies (Meleagris gallopavo gallopavo). 2. The Southwestern Domestication Event: Occurring independently in the Four Corners region of the northern American Southwest (modern-day Arizona, New Mexico, Utah, and Colorado) under the Ancestral Puebloan (Anasazi) culture during the Basketmaker III and Pueblo I periods (circa 200 to 700 CE), utilizing the local Merriam''s or Rio Grande wild turkey lineages.

C. In the American Southwest, the initial motivation for turkey husbandry was not primary meat consumption, but the intensive harvesting of plumage for thermal textiles and ritual regalia. In the cold, high-elevation canyon environments of the Colorado Plateau, where winter temperatures plunge far below freezing and local cotton production was constrained by short growing seasons, turkey feathers provided critical insulation. Ancestral Puebloans meticulously wove turkey feather blankets (feather robes) by wrapping soft, downy turkey feathers around yucca-fiber cords and twining them into dense, warm textile blankets. These feather robes gradually replaced earlier, less durable rabbit-fur blankets as the primary winter garment.

D. Stable carbon ($\delta^{13}\text{C}$) and nitrogen ($\delta^{15}\text{N}$) isotopic analyses of archaeological turkey bones from Southwestern sites—such as Mesa Verde, Chaco Canyon, and Arroyo Hondo—reveal a dramatic dietary shift that mirrors the transition from wild hunting to intensive captive husbandry. While wild turkeys are omnivorous ground-foragers consuming acorns, juniper berries, insects, and wild grass seeds, archaeological domestic turkeys exhibit extraordinarily high $\delta^{13}\text{C}$ signatures. These elevated carbon values demonstrate that captive turkeys were fed an almost exclusive diet of cultivated maize ($C_4$ photosynthetic pathway grain), consuming corn kernels and stalks provided directly by human keepers. Excavations at sites like Sand Canyon Pueblo have revealed specialized turkey pens, formal roosting enclosures with thick accumulations of desiccated droppings, and intact eggshell fragments, confirming long-term captive breeding inside pueblo villages.

E. In Mesoamerican civilizations—including the Olmec, Maya, Zapotec, and Aztec (Mexica)— the domesticated turkey (huexolotl in Nahuatl) held immense cultural, religious, and culinary prestige. In Maya and Aztec cosmology, the turkey was revered as an earthly manifestation of the powerful, unpredictable deity Tezcatlipoca ("Smoking Mirror"), associated with nocturnal sorcery, royal authority, and divination. Turkeys were sacrificed in massive numbers during state religious ceremonies, solar equinoxes, and agricultural planting rituals, their blood and feathers dedicated to rain deities such as Tlaloc and Chaac. Economically, turkey meat formed a staple of aristocratic banquets, seasoned with chili peppers, wild tomatoes, and cacao beans in complex ceremonial stews (mulli), while the great daily marketplace of Tlatelolco in the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan sold thousands of live turkeys daily to urban residents.

F. Following the Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire in 1519–1521, the domesticated Mexican turkey was rapidly assimilated into European maritime commerce. Spanish conquistadors and mariners, astonished by the bird''s immense meat yield, hardiness, and rapid growth rates, transported live specimens aboard returning treasure galleons to Spain as early as 1520. Because the bird thrived on cheap grains, adapted readily to diverse European climates, and possessed a mild, succulent meat that appealed to European palates, it spread across the continent with astonishing speed. By the 1530s, turkeys were being commercially bred in Italy, France, and Germany, and by 1541, the bird was standard festive fare at English aristocratic banquets.

G. A curious historical irony of the turkey''s global dispersal is its common English name. In sixteenth-century London, English merchants of the Levant Company (popularly known as "Turkey merchants") imported foreign luxury fowl—predominantly the helmeted guineafowl (Numida meleagris)—from the eastern Mediterranean and the Ottoman Empire (Turkey). When the newly imported American bird arrived in English markets via Spanish and Levantine trade conduits, English consumers confused the exotic Mexican bird with the African guineafowl, dubbing it the "Turkey-cock" or "Turkey." Today, the descendants of the ancient Mexican domesticate—further crossed with wild North American subspecies to create commercial broad- breasted white breeds—constitute the multi-billion-dollar global poultry industry, continuing a culinary legacy that began in the ancient milpas of Mesoamerica millennia ago.'
);

