-- Passage 2: Engineered Cementitious Composites (ECC) and Bendable Concrete
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'd70643b2-500c-5785-954f-b57af0a7ca57', '53a7251f-6aa6-500e-935c-36e05e931c68', 2, 'Engineered Cementitious Composites (ECC) and Bendable Concrete',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of modern civil engineering and infrastructure development, conventional concrete has served as the foundational building material of global civilization. Measured by mass, concrete is the most consumed synthetic material on Earth, utilized in the construction of bridges, highway pavements, subterranean tunnels, high-rise buildings, marine seawalls, and water retention dams. However, conventional concrete possesses a fundamental mechanical flaw: it is inherently brittle and weak in tension. While standard concrete exhibits exceptional compressive strength (withstanding immense downward crushing forces), its tensile strain capacity is negligible—typically failing under tensile strains of less than 0.01 percent. When subjected to tensile, bending, or shear stresses, conventional concrete develops macro- cracks that allow water, de-icing salts, and airborne carbon dioxide to penetrate the structural matrix, rapidly corroding the internal steel rebar and causing catastrophic structural degradation.

B. To overcome the catastrophic brittleness of traditional masonry and concrete, civil engineer Victor Li and materials scientists at the University of Michigan developed Engineered Cementitious Composites (ECC)—popularly dubbed "bendable concrete"—in the early 1990s. ECC is an advanced class of fiber-reinforced cementitious composites (HPFRCC) engineered systematically through micromechanical modeling to exhibit high ductility and extreme tensile strain capacity. Unlike conventional concrete, which fractures catastrophically into open, unbridgeable cracks when bent, ECC exhibits tensile strain capacities exceeding 3 to 5 percent— a ductility three hundred to five hundred times greater than conventional concrete, behaving mechanically more like a ductile metal than a brittle ceramic.

C. The physical design of ECC relies on a fundamental departure from standard concrete mix formulation: the complete elimination of coarse aggregates (gravel and crushed stone). In conventional concrete, coarse stone aggregates act as large stress concentrators that promote rapid, uncontrolled macro-crack propagation through the brittle cement matrix. In contrast, the ECC mortar matrix consists strictly of fine silica sand (with particle sizes between 50 and 150 micrometers), Portland cement, water, industrial pozzolans (such as fly ash or ground granulated blast-furnace slag), and specialized polycarboxylate chemical superplasticizers. Dispersed randomly throughout this fine matrix is a low volume fraction (typically 1.5 to 2.0 percent by volume) of short, high-strength polymeric fibers, most commonly polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) or high-modulus polyethylene (PE) microfibers measuring 8 to 12 millimeters in length and roughly 40 micrometers in diameter.

D. The extraordinary ductile behavior of ECC is governed by the micromechanical phenomenon of fiber bridging and pseudo-strain-hardening. When an ECC structural element is subjected to increasing tensile or flexural loads, the cement matrix initiates a microscopic crack. However, because the PVA fibers possess calibrated interfacial friction and chemical bonding with the surrounding cement paste, the fibers span across the crack opening, acting as microscopic mechanical bridges that transfer tensile loads back into the un-cracked matrix.

E. Rather than allowing a single initial crack to widen into a destructive structural fissure, the bridged fibers force the matrix to generate thousands of adjacent, distributed micro-cracks throughout the material. Under extreme bending or tensile elongation, ECC develops a dense, steady-state multiple-cracking pattern, where each individual micro-crack remains strictly bounded to a hair-thin crack width of less than 60 micrometers (thinner than a human hair). This continuous micro-cracking process absorbs immense fracture energy without losing load-bearing capacity, allowing an ECC structural slab to deform, flex, and bend into deep curves without structural fracture.

F. A transformative physical capability of ECC micro-crack mechanics is robust autogenous self-healing. In conventional concrete, macro-cracks measuring several hundred micrometers cannot heal naturally, requiring costly manual epoxy injection or structural replacement. In ECC, because the maximum crack width is held permanently below 60 micrometers by the bridging fibers, the material heals itself automatically upon exposure to natural environmental moisture and atmospheric carbon dioxide ($CO_2$). Rainwater entering the narrow micro-cracks dissolves unhydrated calcium oxide ($CaO$) and calcium silicate phases within the cement matrix, reacting with dissolved carbon dioxide to precipitate insoluble calcium carbonate ($CaCO_3$, calcite) crystals that completely seal the micro-crack voids within several weeks, fully restoring the material’s original mechanical stiffness and waterproof impermeability.

G. In full-scale civil infrastructure applications, ECC has demonstrated transformative performance in seismic resilience and infrastructure longevity. In earthquake-prone regions such as Japan, ECC is utilized in the structural coupling beams of high-rise skyscrapers (such as the Namba Tower in Osaka) and the expansion joints of highway viaducts (such as the Mihara Bridge in Hokkaido). During major seismic events, ECC coupling beams absorb severe lateral shear deformations without brittle spalling or crushing, eliminating the need for post-earthquake structural repairs. Furthermore, ECC bridge decks resist freeze-thaw damage and de-icing salt corrosion, extending infrastructure maintenance lifespans from decades to over a century. By synthesizing micromechanical fiber design, extreme ductility, and autonomous self-healing, Engineered Cementitious Composites are transforming civil engineering from a paradigm of brittle vulnerability into an era of adaptive, resilient infrastructure.'
);

