-- Passage 3: Constructive Empiricism and the Scientific Realism Debate
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '1ed77722-d734-59b9-b99f-d6a8e61ad2c9', '53a7251f-6aa6-500e-935c-36e05e931c68', 3, 'Constructive Empiricism and the Scientific Realism Debate',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the philosophy of science, theoretical physics, and analytical epistemology, no dispute is as fiercely contested as the debate over Scientific Realism. Scientific Realism is the philosophical thesis that our best, mature scientific theories provide a literally true, objective description of both the observable world and the unobservable entities, structures, and processes that govern physical reality. When modern physics asserts that matter is composed of subatomic quarks and gluons, that space-time is curved by gravitational fields, or that genetic information is encoded in double-helical DNA molecules, realists argue that a rational person is epistemically obligated to believe that these unobservable entities genuinely exist in the physical cosmos.

B. For decades, scientific realists supported their position using the celebrated "No-Miracles Argument" (formulated by Hilary Putnam and Richard Boyd). The No-Miracles Argument asserts that scientific realism is the only philosophy that does not make the extraordinary predictive, technological, and instrumental success of modern science an inexplicable miracle. If unobservable entities like electrons, viruses, and electromagnetic waves were mere theoretical fictions, why should theories that postulate them make novel, stunningly accurate empirical predictions down to twelve decimal places? Realists maintain that empirical success is an infallible indicator of theoretical truth.

C. In 1980, however, Dutch-American philosopher Bas van Fraassen fundamentally overturned this realist consensus by publishing his landmark treatise The Scientific Image. Van Fraassen formulated a revolutionary anti-realist paradigm known as Constructive Empiricism. Constructive Empiricism challenges scientific realism not by denying that scientific language is meaningful, but by radically redefining the fundamental epistemic aim of scientific inquiry.

D. According to Constructive Empiricism, the aim of science is not to discover literal "truth" regarding unobservable entities; rather, the aim of science is to produce theories that are empirically adequate. Van Fraassen defined "empirical adequacy" with mathematical and philosophical precision: a theory is empirically adequate if and only if what it says about the observable phenomena in the world is completely true—past, present, and future. Under Constructive Empiricism, an agent accepts a scientific theory not as an article of metaphysical faith, but as a reliable, empirically adequate instrument. Acceptance of a theory involves only two commitments: 1. A Cognitive Belief: The belief that the theory is empirically adequate (that it saves the observable phenomena). 2. A Pragmatic Commitment: A practical commitment to use the theory''s mathematical framework, vocabulary, and models to design experiments, solve engineering problems, and guide future research.

E. A foundational pillar of Constructive Empiricism is Van Fraassen’s strict distinction between the "observable" and the "unobservable." Van Fraassen argued that what counts as "observable" is an objective, species-specific biological fact about the sensory apparatus of the human organism ($X$ is observable if there are circumstances which are such that, if $X$ is present to a human being under those circumstances, the human will observe $X$ with their unaided senses). Thus, distant moons of Jupiter are observable because a human astronaut traveling in a spaceship could observe them with the naked eye. In contrast, an electron, a neutrino, or a magnetic field is unobservable because no human being, under any spatiotemporal conditions, could ever perceive them directly with unaided biological senses.

F. Realists immediately attacked this distinction, with philosophers like Paul Churchland and Ian Hacking raising the "Continuum Objection." Churchland pointed out that there is a continuous, unbroken spectrum between unaided human vision, looking through ordinary eyeglasses, looking through a magnifying glass, looking through a light microscope, and looking through an electron microscope. Churchland argued that drawing an arbitrary metaphysical line along this continuous optical spectrum is scientifically unprincipled: if looking through eyeglasses allows us to observe objects, why shouldn''t looking through an electron microscope count as observing a virus or a crystal lattice?

G. Van Fraassen countered the Continuum Objection by insisting that there is a profound physical and epistemic difference between a transparent optical medium and an electronic detection instrument. When an astronomer looks through a standard glass window or optical telescope, they are looking through a passive, transparent physical conduit. In contrast, when a scientist uses an electron microscope, the computer detects electrical currents, applies complex algorithmic data transformations, and displays a synthetic image on a digital monitor. In Van Fraassen’s view, the digital image is an observable physical phenomenon on a screen, but inferring that it represents a literal, unobservable entity in the physical world requires a leap of metaphysical faith that goes beyond empirical adequacy.

H. In contemporary 21st-century philosophy of science, Constructive Empiricism remains one of the most powerful challenges to metaphysical dogmatism. It demonstrates that science can be fully rational, progressive, and technologically triumphant without requiring scientists to make speculative metaphysical claims about the invisible fabric of the universe. By anchoring scientific rationality strictly in empirical adequacy and human observation, Constructive Empiricism preserves the true spirit of scientific humility, reminding us that science is an empirical quest to understand the observable world we inhabit.'
);

