-- Passage 1: The Engineering and Operation of 19th-Century Atlantic Telegraph Cables
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '06396786-7f4a-5f3a-8009-0619a706297c', '7581a4aa-9658-55a2-b2c7-addf005869ce', 1, 'The Engineering and Operation of 19th-Century Atlantic Telegraph Cables',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the early nineteenth century, the rapid commercial development of terrestrial electric telegraph networks—pioneered by Samuel Morse in the United States and William Fothergill Cooke and Charles Wheatstone in Great Britain—fundamentally transformed continental communications. By transmitting encoded electrical pulses over overland copper wires suspended from timber poles, information that previously required days or weeks to travel via horseback courier or steam locomotive could be delivered across landmasses in mere seconds. However, when these terrestrial networks reached oceanic coastlines, global communication encountered an impassable barrier. Intercontinental diplomacy, financial markets, and news remained tethered to the physical speed of coal-fired steamships, requiring ten to twelve days to cross the tempestuous North Atlantic Ocean between Europe and North America.

B. The primary technological breakthrough that made submarine telecommunications feasible was the discovery of gutta-percha. Harvested as a natural latex sap from the Palaquium gutta tree indigenous to the tropical rainforests of the Malay Peninsula, gutta-percha is a natural thermoplastic polymer (trans-1,4-polyisoprene). Unlike natural rubber, which degrades and becomes brittle under seawater exposure, gutta-percha exhibits extraordinary chemical stability and water impermeability. When heated in warm water, it becomes soft and malleable, allowing it to be extruded seamlessly around metallic copper conductor wires; upon cooling, it solidifies into an elastic, mechanically robust, and electrically non-conductive insulating sheath that preserves its insulating integrity under immense hydrostatic pressures at the bottom of the ocean.

C. In 1854, American industrialist Cyrus West Field formed the New York, Newfoundland and London Telegraph Company, partnering with British telegraph engineer Charles Tilston Bright and Canadian inventor Frederic Newton Gisborne. Field envisioned a daring submarine cable stretching over three thousand kilometers across the North Atlantic seabed, directly linking Valentia Island off the southwest coast of Ireland to Heart''s Content in Trinity Bay, Newfoundland. In 1857 and 1858, early cable-laying expeditions were mounted using two converted naval warships: the British screw-propelled battleship HMS Agamemnon and the American steam frigate USS Niagara. After several catastrophic cable snaps in deep ocean trenches, a continuous cable was finally brought ashore on August 5, 1858. On August 16, 1858, Queen Victoria transmitted a ninety-eight-word congratulatory message to US President James Buchanan, initiating the era of transatlantic electronic communication.

D. However, the 1858 cable was plagued by severe electrical signal degradation and operating failures. Over oceanic distances exceeding 3,000 kilometers, a submarine cable acts as a colossal electrical capacitor: the central copper core acts as the inner conductor, the gutta-percha serves as the dielectric medium, and the surrounding conductive seawater acts as the outer ground plate. When an electrical pulse was injected at one end, the cable naturally charged up, causing the sharp rectangular Morse code signal to disperse, smear, and retard into a faint, sluggish electrical wave (signal retardation). To read these faint, smeared signals, the chief electrician, Edward Orange Wildman Whitehouse, mistakenly believed that overcoming cable resistance required blasting the line with massive induction coils generating upwards of two thousand volts. Within three weeks of operation, Whitehouse’s destructive high voltages breached the delicate gutta- percha insulation, causing an irreversible electrical short-circuit that killed the 1858 cable permanently.

E. Following this public disaster, British mathematical physicist William Thomson (later Lord Kelvin) took over the scientific direction of transatlantic telegraphy. Thomson proved mathematically through his "Law of Squares" that signal retardation time ($\tau$) increases with the square of the cable length ($L^2$), resistance ($R$), and capacitance ($C$): $$\tau \propto R C L^2$$ Thomson demonstrated that transmitting signals across ocean basins demanded not high voltages, but ultra-sensitive, low-voltage reception instruments capable of detecting infinitesimal current fluctuations. To achieve this, Thomson invented the Mirror Galvanometer. The instrument consisted of a tiny, feather-weight mirror fitted with microscopic magnets, suspended by a single strand of raw cocoon silk inside a magnetic coil. A beam of light reflected off the mirror projected an amplified optical pointer onto a scale several feet away. The slightest electrical pulse deflected the mirror, allowing telegraphers to read high-speed Morse code while operating the cable with a single low-voltage battery.

F. In 1865, Cyrus Field secured the deployment of the SS Great Eastern, a mammoth iron steamship designed by legendary civil engineer Isambard Kingdom Brunel. At 211 meters in length and over 22,000 metric tons of displacement, the Great Eastern was five times larger than any other vessel afloat and was the only ship on Earth capable of holding the entire 4,200-metric- ton coiled mass of a heavily armored transatlantic cable within three massive iron cargo tanks flooded with seawater. The 1865 cable was engineered to exacting standards: a seven-strand copper conductor core covered with four concentric layers of gutta-percha, wrapped in tarred hemp, and armored with ten high-tensile steel wires encased in preservative-treated Manilla hemp yarns.

G. On July 27, 1866, the Great Eastern successfully landed the cable at Heart’s Content, establishing the first permanent, uninterrupted transatlantic telegraph connection. Demonstrating maritime and engineering virtuosity, the crew then steamed back out into the open Atlantic, located the snapped 1865 cable in four thousand meters of water, hauled it to the surface using specialized grapnel hooks, spliced it with fresh cable, and brought a second line ashore. The successful operation of the Atlantic cables instantly unified the stock exchanges of London and New York, compressed the diplomatic transmission of geopolitical events from weeks into minutes, and established the foundational global network that laid the groundwork for modern international telecommunications.'
);

