-- Passage 3: The Modularity of Mind: Fodorian Modules vs. Massive Modularity
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '42926a15-5e99-5301-8a6b-acef31bcfcd3', '7581a4aa-9658-55a2-b2c7-addf005869ce', 3, 'The Modularity of Mind: Fodorian Modules vs. Massive Modularity',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In cognitive science, evolutionary psychology, and the philosophy of mind, the central structural inquiry concerns the fundamental computational architecture of the human brain. Is the human mind a unified, general-purpose computational engine that applies general rules of logic, learning, and induction across all intellectual domains? Or is the mind a heterogeneous collection of specialized, domain-specific computational subsystems—modules—that evolved to solve distinct evolutionary tasks? This fundamental theoretical divide is formalized as the debate over the Modularity of Mind.

B. The classical foundations of cognitive modularity were established in 1983 by American philosopher and cognitive scientist Jerry Fodor in his seminal book The Modularity of Mind. Fodor proposed that human cognitive architecture is bifurcated into two distinct structural tiers: low-level, modular input systems (responsible for sensory perception and language processing) and non-modular, domain-general central systems (responsible for high-level reasoning, decision-making, belief fixation, and scientific thought).

C. In Fodor’s taxonomy, an input system qualifies as a genuine cognitive "module" if and only if it satisfies a specific cluster of functional, biological, and computational criteria: 1. Domain Specificity: A module processes only a dedicated, restricted class of sensory inputs (e.g., the visual edge-detection module processes light contrast but is completely deaf to acoustic tones). 2. Mandatory Operation: Modules operate automatically and reflexively in response to appropriate stimuli, entirely outside conscious voluntary control (e.g., you cannot choose to hear spoken words in your native language as meaningless acoustic noise; comprehension is mandatory). 3. Fast Processing: Because modular operations are hardwired and unencumbered by central deliberation, they execute within milliseconds. 4. Informational Encapsulation: The defining and non-negotiable hallmark of Fodorian modularity. A module executes its internal computations in complete isolation from the beliefs, desires, and knowledge stored in the central cognitive system. 5. Localized Neural Architecture: Modules correspond to dedicated, innately specified neural circuits in the brain that exhibit specific breakdown patterns (cognitive deficits) when damaged.

D. Fodor illustrated informational encapsulation through classical optical illusions, most famously the Müller-Lyer illusion. In the Müller-Lyer illusion, two horizontal lines of identical physical length are presented with inward-pointing or outward-pointing arrowheads at their ends. Even after an observer uses a physical ruler to measure the lines and forms the conscious, fully justified belief that both lines are mathematically identical in length, the visual perception system continues to perceive one line as distinctly longer than the other. The low-level visual processing module is informationally encapsulated: its internal computational algorithms cannot be accessed or overridden by high-level central knowledge.

E. However, Fodor insisted that high-level central cognitive processes—such as scientific reasoning, analogical thought, and belief fixation—are strictly non-modular. Fodor argued that central reasoning is "isotropic" (any piece of background knowledge can, in principle, be relevant to evaluating a belief) and "Quinean" (the degree of confirmation assigned to a hypothesis depends on the epistemic coherence of the entire belief network). Because isotropic and Quinean processes require global access to the entire web of human knowledge, Fodor famously declared in his "First Law of the Non-Existence of Cognitive Science" that the more global a mental process is, the less anyone understands it, concluding that high-level human rationality defies modular explanation.

F. In the 1990s, evolutionary psychologists—most prominently John Tooby, Leda Cosmides, and Steven Pinker, alongside philosopher Peter Carruthers—radically challenged Fodor’s limited modularity by formulating the Massive Modularity Hypothesis (MMH). Evolutionary psychologists argued that the human mind does not possess a mysterious, non-modular central reasoning core; rather, the mind is modular "all the way down." In the famous metaphor coined by Cosmides and Tooby, the human mind is a cognitive "Swiss Army knife"—an integrated collection of hundreds or thousands of specialized, genetically evolved computational modules designed by natural selection to solve specific ancestral adaptive problems (such as cheater detection, mate selection, predator avoidance, spatial navigation, tool mechanics, and kin recognition).

G. Proponents of massive modularity defended their thesis through two primary theoretical arguments: the "Argument from Design" and the "Computational Tractability Argument." Natural selection builds complex biological adaptations out of specialized functional components; just as the human body evolved distinct organs (the heart, lungs, and liver) to execute distinct physiological functions, the mind must have evolved distinct computational modules to execute distinct adaptive tasks. Furthermore, evolutionary psychologists pointed out that a purely domain-general computational system would succumb to the computational Frame Problem: a domain-general engine attempting to evaluate all possible consequences of an action across an infinite sea of background knowledge would suffer from immediate computational paralysis. To act rapidly and successfully, cognition must rely on domain-specific heuristic modules with pre-programmed assumptions.

H. In contemporary cognitive neuroscience, both Fodorian modularity and the massive modularity hypothesis have encountered significant theoretical revision through discoveries in neuroplasticity and connectomics. Modern neuroimaging demonstrates that the human brain exhibits extraordinary developmental plasticity: cortical regions are not rigid, pre-wired genetic chips, but dynamic, highly connected neural networks that differentiate into specialized functional circuits through learning, cultural transmission, and environmental interaction. While the debate over modularity remains open, it has dismantled the naive view of the mind as a passive blank slate, revealing that human intelligence is an exquisite biocultural tapestry woven from evolutionary adaptations and dynamic, lifelong neural learning.'
);

