-- Passage 2: Autonomous Underwater Vehicles (AUVs) in Deep-Sea Oceanographic Mapping
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '6c68a927-4411-54fd-9d7a-58c4eca781fe', 'd497aad9-0517-589f-b92a-96364844ea48', 2, 'Autonomous Underwater Vehicles (AUVs) in Deep-Sea Oceanographic Mapping',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. The global ocean covers more than seventy percent of Earth''s surface, playing a fundamental role in regulating global climate, storing oceanic carbon, and sustaining planetary biodiversity. Yet, despite centuries of maritime navigation, the deep ocean seabed remains one of the least explored environments on the planet: less than twenty-five percent of the global seafloor has been mapped using high-resolution bathymetric soundings. Historically, oceanographic bathymetry relied almost exclusively on hull-mounted multibeam echosounders operated from surface research vessels. However, as surface sound waves travel down through thousands of meters of the water column, the acoustic beam expands geometrically, resulting in poor lateral resolution (typically fifty to one hundred meters per pixel at abyssal depths of four to six kilometers). To achieve sub-meter resolution of deep abyssal features, oceanographers and marine roboticists are deploying Autonomous Underwater Vehicles (AUVs).

B. An Autonomous Underwater Vehicle is an untethered, self-propelled robotic submersible capable of executing pre-programmed scientific survey missions without real-time human intervention or physical umbilical cables connecting it to a surface ship. Powered by high- energy-density rechargeable lithium-ion or lithium-polymer battery packs, modern deep-rated AUVs (such as the Autosub6000, Sentry, or Remus 6000) are engineered with streamlined hydrodynamic torpedo-shaped or hover-capable fuselages fabricated from high-strength syntactic foam (a matrix of microscopic hollow glass microspheres embedded in epoxy resin) and titanium pressure housings rated to withstand crushing hydrostatic pressures exceeding 60 megapascals (MPa) at depths of six thousand meters.

C. Operating an untethered robot thousands of meters beneath the ocean surface introduces a formidable engineering obstacle: the complete opacity of seawater to electromagnetic radiation. Radio waves, GPS signals, and high-bandwidth wireless telecommunications penetrate seawater for only a few centimeters or meters before being entirely absorbed and attenuated. Consequently, an AUV cannot rely on satellite GPS to navigate while submerged, nor can it receive live operator instructions. To navigate through total darkness across complex undersea topography, AUVs utilize dead-reckoning navigation systems centered on Inertial Navigation Systems (INS) tightly integrated with acoustic sensors.

D. The core navigation payload combines high-precision optical ring-laser gyroscopes (RLGs) or fiber-optic gyroscopes (FOGs), sensitive internal accelerometers, and a downward-looking Doppler Velocity Log (DVL). The DVL is an acoustic instrument that emits high-frequency acoustic pings (typically 300 to 1,200 kilohertz) toward the seabed. By measuring the Doppler frequency shift of the sound waves bouncing back from the seafloor, the DVL calculates the vehicle’s exact three-dimensional velocity over ground with millimeter-per-second precision. By combining DVL velocity data with inertial gyro measurements, the AUV’s onboard Kalman filtering algorithms maintain highly accurate positioning estimates, limiting navigational drift to less than 0.1 percent of the total distance traveled.

E. For high-resolution benthic mapping, modern AUVs carry an advanced suite of acoustic and optical survey payloads: 1. Synthetic Aperture Sonar (SAS): Unlike standard side-scan sonar whose spatial resolution degrades with range, Synthetic Aperture Sonar mathematically combines multiple sequential acoustic pings received as the vehicle moves along its track, synthesizing a virtual array that is ten to twenty times longer than the physical transducer. SAS achieves an astonishing, range-independent spatial resolution of one to three centimeters across swath widths exceeding several hundred meters. 2. Multibeam Swath Bathymetry: Downward-looking swath sounders map fine-scale micro- bathymetric contours, identifying faults, submarine landslides, and seamount structures. 3. High-Resolution 3D Optical Photogrammetry: When flying close to the seabed (typically two to four meters altitude), high-intensity LED strobe arrays illuminate the dark benthic floor, allowing digital stereo cameras to capture thousands of overlapping high-resolution photographs to construct millimeter-scale 3D digital elevation models of delicate hydrothermal vent chimneys and cold-water coral reefs.

F. Beyond geophysical bathymetric surveying, modern AUVs function as sophisticated chemical and biological sampling platforms. Equipped with in-situ mass spectrometers, laser fluorometers, oxidation-reduction potential (ORP) sensors, and miniature chemical sniffers, autonomous vehicles can detect and track chemical anomalies. In deep-sea exploration, AUVs are deployed to locate elusive, ultra-deep hydrothermal vents. The vehicle navigates through the water column detecting faint anomalies in turbidity, dissolved methane ($CH_4$), dissolved manganese, and temperature. Upon detecting a hydrothermal plume, the vehicle’s adaptive autonomy software autonomously switches from grid mapping to plume-tracing behaviors, following chemical concentration gradients upstream until it pinpoints the exact coordinates of active "black smoker" chimneys on the volcanic seabed.

G. As artificial intelligence, long-duration fuel cell systems, and swarm robotics continue to mature, the operational capabilities of AUVs are expanding from short, twenty-four-hour survey sorties to multi-month trans-oceanic missions. Coordinated fleets of cooperating micro-AUVs communicate via low-bandwidth acoustic modems, sharing mapping workloads and dynamically adapting their search patterns in real time. By bypassing the physical boundaries of surface ships and human diving limits, autonomous underwater vehicles are bringing the alien, hidden geography of our deep ocean planet into sharp, radiant focus.'
);

