-- Passage 1: The Construction and Hydrology of Roman Hypocaust Heating Systems
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'b4eb12d5-0795-5746-954c-400264222e6c', '49b5baca-c584-5d82-be49-2a13b4cb2cac', 1, 'The Construction and Hydrology of Roman Hypocaust Heating Systems',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the vast territories of the Roman Empire, monumental public baths (thermae) and elite aristocratic countryside villas (villae) stood as enduring architectural symbols of Roman civic engineering and imperial luxury. In the chilly climates of the northern and western provinces—spanning Roman Britain (Britannia), Gaul (Gallia), and the Rhine and Danube frontiers—sustaining comfortable indoor living environments and maintaining warm public bathing chambers through freezing winters required sophisticated thermal engineering. To achieve uniform, radiant indoor warmth without filling interior living quarters with toxic smoke, particulate soot, or noxious combustion gases, Roman civil architects perfected the subterranean radiant heating system known as the hypocaust (derived from the Greek hypo, meaning under, and kaiein, meaning to burn).

B. The historical invention of the hypocaust is traditionally credited by the Roman architectural writer Vitruvius in De Architectura to the Campanian engineer and entrepreneur Sergius Orata during the early first century BCE. Orata originally devised underfloor heating to warm shallow seawater ponds for commercial oyster breeding near Baiae in the volcanic Gulf of Naples, soon adapting the system to heat luxurious private plunge baths. Following the consolidation of imperial engineering standards under Augustus and Agrippa, hypocaust heating was integrated into virtually every public civic bath complex across the Mediterranean basin and European provinces.

C. The physical architecture of a hypocaust functioned through the coordinated interplay of three distinct structural components: the external furnace (praefurnium), the subterranean underfloor plenum (suspensura), and the vertical wall flues (tubuli or tegulae mammatae). The heating process originated in the praefurnium, a semi-subterranean masonry furnace chamber excavated outside the exterior walls of the heated room. Operating continuously throughout the day, the furnace was fueled by dry hardwood timber, seasoned logs, or high-density charcoal stoked by enslaved stokers (fornacarii).

D. Hot air, smoke, and combustible combustion gases generated in the furnace were drawn naturally into the suspensura—the hollow underfloor chamber. The suspended floor was supported by hundreds of regularly spaced, vertical pillars (pilae) constructed from square or circular kiln-fired ceramic bricks (lateres) bonded with refractory lime mortar. These pillars typically measured sixty to ninety centimeters in height and were spaced roughly forty to sixty centimeters apart. Resting atop these brick stacks was a thick, multi-layered suspended floor slab: 1. Bipedales Layer: Large, flat ceramic floor tiles (measuring two Roman feet, or roughly sixty centimeters square) bridging the tops of the pillars. 2. Rubble-Mortar Core (Rudus): A dense, heavy layer of volcanic pozzolana cement, lime, and crushed terracotta brick fragments measuring fifteen to thirty centimeters in thickness. 3. Decorative Paving: An upper walking surface paved with decorative mosaic tesserae or polished marble slabs (opus sectile).

E. This multi-layered composite floor possessed immense thermal mass. As hot furnace gases circulated between the brick pillars beneath the floor slab, the thick ceramic and concrete layers absorbed thermal energy. The stored heat was conducted upward through the floor, radiating a gentle, consistent heat into the room above, maintaining comfortable ambient air temperatures between 25°C and 35°C. To prevent smoke and toxic carbon monoxide from collecting in the underfloor cavity, Roman builders engineered vertical chimney flues inside the masonry walls. Hollow rectangular terracotta box tiles (tubuli) or flanged wall tiles (tegulae mammatae) were stacked vertically along the interior walls and connected to open exhaust vents beneath the roofline.

F. The vertical tubuli generated a continuous chimney draft: rising hot air within the wall flues created a natural convective pressure differential, drawing hot combustion gases out of the furnace, through the underfloor chamber, and upward through the entire wall envelope before venting safely out into the atmosphere. This ingenious convective design transformed the walls into massive, radiant heating panels, enveloping bathers in 360-degree radiant warmth while completely isolating indoor occupants from toxic furnace fumes.

G. In public thermae, the hypocaust system was intricately graded to heat a sequence of interconnected bathing chambers with progressive temperatures: the warm transition room (tepidarium), the hot sweat room (caldarium), and the dry sauna (laconicum). The caldarium, situated closest to the praefurnium, featured vaulted ceilings designed with condensation channels that prevented dripping water, as well as a large heated immersion pool (alveus). Water inside the alveus was heated directly via a hollow bronze boiler (testudo alvei) built directly over the furnace mouth, utilizing natural thermosiphon water circulation. Following the decline of the Western Roman Empire and the collapse of municipal infrastructure, the resource-intensive hypocaust system largely disappeared from Western Europe, surviving primarily in the traditional Turkish baths (hammams) of the Islamic world, leaving behind magnificent archaeological tile hypocausts as testaments to the brilliance of Roman pyrotechnological design.'
);

