-- Passage 2: Gallium Nitride (GaN) Power Electronics in Clean Energy Grids
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'd6522163-75db-5030-b5b4-d5705c0ecc2f', '91d75e66-0f90-542c-a1a0-54d1ca077303', 2, 'Gallium Nitride (GaN) Power Electronics in Clean Energy Grids',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For more than six decades, the modern electrical power infrastructure—spanning electricity generation, high-voltage transmission, industrial motor drives, and consumer power supplies— has relied almost exclusively on silicon-based semiconductor power devices. Conventional silicon ($Si$) metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistors (MOSFETs) and insulated-gate bipolar transistors (IGBTs) served as the workhorses of power conversion. However, as global energy architectures accelerate the integration of renewable power sources, electric vehicles (EVs), and hyper-scale data centers, conventional silicon is reaching its physical and thermodynamic limits. Silicon’s narrow bandgap (1.12 electron-volts, eV) imposes fundamental limits on breakdown voltage, operating temperature, and switching frequency, resulting in substantial energy losses dissipated as waste heat. To build the high-efficiency power grids of the future, semiconductor physicists and electrical engineers are transitioning to wide-bandgap (WBG) semiconductors, most notably Gallium Nitride (GaN).

B. Gallium Nitride is a binary III/V direct wide-bandgap semiconductor possessing exceptional electronic and physical properties. With a wide bandgap of 3.4 eV—roughly three times wider than that of silicon—GaN exhibits a critical breakdown electric field strength of 3.3 megavolts per centimeter ($MV/cm$), which is more than ten times higher than that of silicon ($0.3\text{ MV/cm}$). This high breakdown field allows GaN power devices to withstand immense operational voltages across extremely thin semiconductor drift regions, drastically slashing internal device resistance (on-state resistance, $R_{\text{DS(on)}}$) and reducing electrical conduction losses by up to eighty percent compared to equivalent silicon components.

C. The core technological architecture that enables GaN to deliver superior power conversion performance is the High-Electron-Mobility Transistor (HEMT). In a GaN HEMT, power does not flow through a conventional doped semiconductor channel; rather, it flows through a naturally occurring, ultra-conductive quantum layer known as a Two-Dimensional Electron Gas (2DEG). A 2DEG is formed at the heterojunction interface between a thin layer of Aluminum Gallium Nitride ($AlGaN$) grown epitaxially atop a Gallium Nitride ($GaN$) layer. Due to the spontaneous and piezoelectric polarization mismatch between the two crystalline lattices, an intense sheet of mobile electrons accumulates at the interface without requiring any intentional chemical dopant impurities.

D. Because the 2DEG layer is free from ionized dopant impurities that normally scatter moving charge carriers, electrons within the channel achieve extraordinary electron mobility (typically 1,500 to 2,000 $\text{cm}^2/\text{V}\cdot\text{s}$) and very high electron saturation velocities. This high mobility enables GaN HEMTs to switch on and off at frequencies exceeding tens of megahertz (MHz)—hundreds of times faster than traditional silicon IGBTs. Operating at ultra- high switching frequencies allows electrical engineers to downsize bulky passive inductive coils, transformers, and filtering capacitors by 70 to 90 percent. Consequently, GaN-based power converters achieve unprecedented power densities, allowing power supplies and EV onboard chargers to shrink to a fraction of their traditional physical size and weight while achieving electrical conversion efficiencies exceeding 98 to 99 percent.

E. In clean energy distribution systems, GaN power transistors play a transformative role in solar photovoltaic (PV) micro-inverters and electric vehicle drivetrains. In solar installations, compact GaN inverters convert direct current (DC) generated by rooftop solar panels into alternating current (AC) for the grid with minimal thermal loss, operating efficiently without heavy, noisy cooling fans. In electric vehicles, GaN-based DC-DC bidirectional converters and traction inverters reduce energy losses, extending vehicle driving range on a single battery charge while accelerating high-voltage fast-charging rates. Furthermore, in commercial telecommunications and cloud computing data centers, GaN power supplies slash electrical conversion waste, saving billions of kilowatt-hours of grid electricity annually.

F. Despite its revolutionary performance, the widespread commercial adoption of GaN power electronics faces critical material, thermal, and manufacturing hurdles. A primary engineering bottleneck is native substrate availability: growing large, defect-free single-crystal GaN boules is economically prohibitive. Therefore, commercial manufacturers grow thin GaN crystalline films heteroepitaxially onto foreign substrates, most commonly silicon ($GaN\text{-on-}Si$) or silicon carbide ($GaN\text{-on-}SiC$). However, large mismatches in lattice constants and thermal expansion coefficients between GaN and silicon generate high mechanical stress and crystalline threading dislocations, which can cause current collapse and premature electrical breakdown if not carefully managed with complex graded buffer layers.

G. Furthermore, GaN power devices generate intense localized heat within their sub-micron 2DEG channels during high-power switching operations. Because silicon has a relatively modest thermal conductivity, dissipating localized heat fluxes from $GaN\text{-on-}Si$ wafers requires advanced thermal packaging architectures, such as direct diamond-substrate bonding or micro- channel liquid cold plates. As scalable 200-millimeter (8-inch) and 300-millimeter silicon wafer manufacturing processes continue to mature, GaN power semiconductor devices are achieving cost parity with legacy silicon. By unlocking ultra-high efficiency, miniaturized footprints, and superior high-temperature operation, Gallium Nitride is driving the global decarbonization of electrical power infrastructure.'
);

