-- Passage 3: The Nature of Epistemic Injustice: Testimonial and Hermeneutical Oppression
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '7788636b-4bd3-5f97-919a-cb77dd5cf49b', '91d75e66-0f90-542c-a1a0-54d1ca077303', 3, 'The Nature of Epistemic Injustice: Testimonial and Hermeneutical Oppression',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In traditional epistemology and analytical philosophy, the evaluation of human knowledge was long conducted through an idealized, individualistic framework. Epistemologists conceptualized knowers as abstract, interchangeable rational agents who exchange propositions, evaluate empirical evidence, and form justified beliefs in a social vacuum. Questions of political power, social identity, gender, race, and structural oppression were dismissed as irrelevant sociological noise that had no bearing on the pure, normative mechanics of epistemic justification. In 2007, however, British philosopher Miranda Fricker fundamentally transformed contemporary epistemology by publishing her ground-breaking treatise Epistemic Injustice: Power and the Ethics of Knowing. Fricker advanced a powerful thesis: social power relations and identity prejudices directly distort our epistemic practices, inflicting distinct, non-accidental harms on individuals specifically in their capacity as knowers.

B. Fricker defined "epistemic injustice" as a distinctive kind of wrong done to someone specifically in their capacity as an epistemic subject—a giver of knowledge, a teller of truth, or an understander of social experience. Fricker demonstrated that epistemic injustice is not merely an incidental byproduct of broader political or economic oppression; rather, it is a primary, irreducible form of injustice that damages the epistemic agency and human dignity of marginalized subjects. Fricker divided epistemic injustice into two foundational, structurally distinct categories: Testimonial Injustice and Hermeneutical Injustice.

C. Testimonial Injustice occurs when a speaker receives a deflated level of credibility from a hearer due to an identity prejudice operating in the hearer’s mind. When a speaker conveys testimony (asserting a factual claim, reporting an observation, or offering expert advice), the hearer must make an implicit judgment regarding the speaker''s credibility. If the hearer harbors a negative identity prejudice—an irrational, negative stereotype regarding the speaker’s social group (such as gender, race, social class, disability, or sexual orientation)—the hearer assigns an unwarranted "credibility deficit" to the speaker''s words.

D. Fricker illustrated testimonial injustice through literary and historical exemplars, most notably the courtroom trial in Harper Lee’s novel To Kill a Mockingbird. In the segregated American South of the 1930s, Tom Robinson, a Black man falsely accused of raping a white woman, gives clear, calm, and logically consistent testimony proving his physical innocence. However, the all-white jury operates under a pervasive, racialized identity prejudice: the deeply ingrained stereotype that Black men are inherently deceitful and hyper-sexualized. Consequently, the jurors assign a catastrophic credibility deficit to Robinson''s testimony, entirely discounting his truthful account and convicting an innocent man. The injustice is twofold: a primary ethical harm (wronging Robinson in his fundamental capacity as a truth-teller) and a catastrophic secondary practical harm (unjust execution).

E. In contrast to the interpersonal dynamics of testimonial injustice, Hermeneutical Injustice is a structural, collective phenomenon. Hermeneutical injustice occurs at an earlier stage of epistemic life: when a marginalized group suffers a significant disadvantage in making sense of their own social experiences because of a gap in collective interpretive resources. This collective epistemic gap is caused by "hermeneutical marginalization"—the systematic exclusion of subordinate social groups from participating equally in the cultural, academic, legal, and media institutions that forge shared language and conceptual frameworks.

F. Fricker illustrated hermeneutical injustice through the historical emergence of the concept of "sexual harassment" in the United States during the late 1960s and 1970s. Prior to the coining of this explicit linguistic phrase by women’s consciousness-raising groups in New York (most notably surrounding the case of Carmita Wood at Cornell University in 1975), women in workplaces routinely endured unwanted sexual advances, physical intimidation, and coercive threats from male superiors. However, because the collective vocabulary possessed no conceptual category for "sexual harassment," victims were unable to articulate or understand their own suffering as a structural, systemic wrong; society dismissed their distress as petty personal grievances, flirtatious banter, or emotional instability. The lack of a shared conceptual resource prevented both the victim from understanding her experience and the broader society from recognizing the injustice.

G. Since Fricker’s foundational work, contemporary social epistemologists—such as Kristie Dotson, José Medina, and Gaile Pohlhaus Jr.—have substantially expanded the taxonomy of epistemic oppression. Kristie Dotson identified two critical communicative mechanisms of testimonial suppression: 1. Testimonial Quieting: When an audience fails to identify a speaker as a legitimate knower, effectively silencing their testimony before it can be registered. 2. Testimonial Smothering: When a speaker voluntarily self-censors, truncates, or dumbs down their own testimony because they anticipate that an unsympathetic or prejudiced audience will fail to comprehend or fairly evaluate it. Furthermore, José Medina has highlighted "epistemic vices of the privileged"—such as epistemic arrogance, closed-mindedness, and epistemic laziness—demonstrating that dominant social groups often cultivate an active "willful ignorance" to preserve their power.

H. In synthesizing the ethics of knowing, modern epistemology recognizes that overcoming epistemic injustice requires cultivating collective and individual virtues. Fricker advocates for the virtue of "Testimonial Justice"—a reflexive critical awareness where hearers actively compensate for potential implicit biases by adjusting their credibility assessments upward when listening to marginalized speakers. Furthermore, building inclusive hermeneutical institutions ensures that all social groups have the voice and conceptual tools needed to name and interpret their lived realities. By placing justice at the heart of epistemology, philosophy transforms the pursuit of truth into an empowering, collective project of human liberation.'
);

