-- Passage 1: Ancient Roman Pozzolanic Concrete and Marine Engineering
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '257749d9-5504-5d46-b94b-221140612b2a', 'c0c3662b-d2c4-5d7b-b1b2-24394564583a', 1, 'Ancient Roman Pozzolanic Concrete and Marine Engineering',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of classical civil engineering, the architectural monuments of the Roman Empire—spanning monumental domes like the Pantheon, colossal amphitheatres like the Colosseum, and massive maritime breakwaters across Mediterranean harbors—stand as enduring testaments to the longevity of Roman materials science. While modern Portland-cement concrete structures exposed to aggressive marine environments typically suffer from structural degradation and internal steel rebar corrosion within fifty to one hundred years, ancient Roman maritime structures have endured for over two millennia immersed in turbulent seawater. The material foundation of this unprecedented physical durability was opus caementicium (Roman concrete), specifically maritime concrete formulated with reactive volcanic ash.

B. The architectural secrets of Roman hydraulic concrete were documented extensively by ancient writers, most notably the architect Vitruvius in his ten-volume treatise De Architectura (circa 30–20 BCE) and the naturalist Pliny the Elder in Naturalis Historia. Vitruvius described a miraculous volcanic powder found in the hills surrounding the Bay of Naples, particularly near the coastal town of Puteoli (modern Pozzuoli). Termed pulvis puteolanus (pozzolanic ash), this fine, glassy volcanic ejecta possessed the unique ability to react chemically with lime and water to form an impenetrable, stone-like composite that hardened even when completely submerged under open seawater.

C. The physical fabrication of Roman maritime concrete involved an intimate, multi-stage thermochemical process. Roman builders first calcined limestone (calcium carbonate, $\text{CaCO}_3$) in high-temperature kilns to produce quicklime (calcium oxide, $\text{CaO}$). Rather than completely hydrating the quicklime in large water troughs (as in traditional cold slaking), Roman engineers often used a technique termed "hot mixing": blending dry quicklime directly with coarse volcanic ash (pozzolana) and volcanic tuff rock fragments (caementa) before adding water. The intense exothermic hydration reaction generated temperatures exceeding 80°C to 100°C, producing a thick, rapidly setting mortar.

D. For harbor installations—such as breakwaters, piers, and quays at major naval ports like Portus, Cosa, and Caesarea Maritima—Roman engineers deployed specialized wooden formwork caissons (pilae). Heavy timber frameworks fabricated from oak or pine planks were constructed on land or lowered directly into the sea. Laborers filled these submerged wooden caissons with the hot volcanic mortar mix along with fist-sized chunks of volcanic tuff and pumice aggregate. As the composite set underwater, it formed massive monolithic stone blocks capable of absorbing the continuous pounding of ocean waves without fracturing.

E. In recent years, advanced geoarchaeological and synchrotron micro-diffraction studies spearheaded by geochemical mineralogist Marie Jackson have deciphered the precise chemical mechanism governing the multi-millennial longevity of Roman marine concrete. In modern Portland cement, seawater percolation causes deleterious chemical attacks, dissolving calcium hydroxide and precipitating expansive ettringite that cracks the mortar. In Roman marine concrete, however, seawater acts not as a destructive agent, but as an active, beneficial chemical catalyst.

F. When seawater slowly percolates through the porous matrix of Roman concrete, it dissolves residual alkaline volcanic glass and lime clasts (small, unslaked remnants of quicklime embedded in the mortar). This fluid-rock interaction triggers a secondary, post-curing mineralization process: 1. Aluminum-Tobermorite Formation: Dissolved silica, alumina, and calcium react chemically with seawater to precipitate platy crystals of a rare, high-durability calcium- silicate-hydrate mineral known as aluminum-tobermorite ($\text{Al-tobermorite}$, $[\text{Ca}_4(\text{Si}_5.5\text{Al}_{0.5})\text{O}_{16}(\text{OH})_2]\cdot\text{Ca }_{0.5}\cdot 4\text{H}_2\text{O}$). These interlocking, layered crystals grow progressively within microscopic void spaces and interfacial boundary zones. 2. Phillipsite Growth: Simultaneously, interlocking crystals of the zeolite mineral phillipsite crystallize along micro-cracks. Rather than expanding and fracturing the composite, these interlocking crystalline networks reinforce the matrix, increasing compressive strength and fracture toughness over centuries.

G. Furthermore, researchers have discovered that the embedded millimeter-scale white lime clasts act as a natural, autonomous self-healing reservoir. When structural stress or thermal contraction generates microscopic fissures in the concrete, water ingress dissolves calcium from the adjacent lime clasts. The calcium-saturated pore fluid reacts with dissolved atmospheric carbon dioxide and carbonate ions in the seawater, precipitating fresh calcite ($\text{CaCO}_3$) crystals that automatically seal the micro-cracks before they can widen into catastrophic structural fractures. Today, materials scientists and environmental engineers are adapting ancient pozzolanic chemistry to formulate next-generation, low-carbon geopolymer cements for modern seawalls and coastal flood defenses, unlocking an ancient recipe to protect modern coastlines against rising sea levels.'
);

