-- Passage 2: Atmospheric Water Harvesting via Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'caa4142d-4286-591a-9fdb-49b22a95a742', 'c0c3662b-d2c4-5d7b-b1b2-24394564583a', 2, 'Atmospheric Water Harvesting via Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs)',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Access to clean, potable freshwater represents one of the most critical humanitarian, agricultural, and geopolitical challenges of the twenty-first century. According to United Nations global environmental projections, more than two billion people reside in regions experiencing severe, chronic water scarcity. While seawater desalination and groundwater extraction provide vital freshwater supplies along coastal shorelines and over regional aquifers, inland hyper-arid desert regions (such as the Sahel, the Arabian Desert, and the interior Australian outback) possess neither marine access nor renewable aquifers. However, Earth''s atmosphere contains a colossal, continually replenished freshwater reservoir: an estimated 13,000 cubic kilometers of atmospheric moisture in the form of water vapor and droplets. To extract potable water from hyper-arid air without consuming large amounts of electrical energy, chemical engineers and materials scientists are developing Atmospheric Water Harvesting (AWH) systems utilizing Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs).

B. Conventional atmospheric water harvesting technologies—such as active mechanical condensation chillers and passive fog collection nets—suffer from severe physical and operational boundaries. Fog harvesting requires ambient relative humidity (RH) levels approaching 100 percent and dense physical fog banks, restricting its utility to narrow coastal mountain valleys (such as the Atacama Desert in Chile). Conversely, conventional refrigeration- based de-humidifiers must chill large volumes of incoming air below its dew point temperature to induce condensation. In arid desert climates where relative humidity drops below 20 to 30 percent, the dew point drops below freezing (sub-zero temperatures), requiring massive electrical refrigeration power that makes standard condensation systems energetically unfeasible.

C. To overcome these thermodynamic constraints, sorption-based atmospheric water harvesting deploys highly porous solid materials that selectively capture water molecules at low ambient humidity through physical adsorption. Among various porous materials (such as zeolites, silica gels, and hygroscopic salts), Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as the most promising sorbent class. First synthesized in the late 1990s by chemist Omar Yaghi, MOFs are crystalline, highly ordered hybrid materials composed of positively charged metal ions or inorganic clusters (metal nodes, such as zirconium, aluminum, or iron) interconnected by polydentate organic molecules (organic linkers).

D. The defining physical attribute of MOFs is their extraordinary internal porosity and tailored chemical functionality. A single gram of high-surface-area MOF can possess an internal surface area exceeding 3,000 to 7,000 square meters—equivalent to the area of an entire soccer field folded into a microscopic sugar cube. Unlike traditional adsorbents whose water sorption isotherms show gradual, linear uptake, MOFs engineered for water harvesting exhibit a distinctive, sharp "stepped" Type-IV or Type-V water sorption isotherm. By chemically tuning the pore size (typically between 0.5 and 1.5 nanometers) and functionalizing the organic linkers with specific hydrophilic chemical groups (such as amino, carboxyl, or hydroxyl groups), researchers can precisely position the steep adsorption step to occur at low desert humidity thresholds—typically between 10% and 20% RH.

E. The operational cycle of a solar-powered MOF water harvesting device operates through two distinct thermodynamic phases: 1. Nocturnal Adsorption Phase: During the cool desert night, when ambient relative humidity is relatively higher and temperatures are lower, ambient air is drawn across an open MOF sorbent bed. Water vapor molecules diffuse into the ordered nanoscale cavities, binding to specific hydrophilic coordination sites through hydrogen bonding and filling the pores via capillary condensation. 2. Diurnal Desorption and Condensation Phase: At dawn, the sorbent chamber is sealed beneath a transparent glass enclosure. Sunlight strikes the device, heating the MOF bed to moderate temperatures (typically 65°C to 85°C). This low-grade solar thermal energy destabilizes the weak hydrogen bonds, releasing the trapped water as concentrated, high- purity water vapor. The evaporated steam migrates to an adjoining ambient air-cooled condenser, where it condenses into pure, mineral-free liquid drinking water, which is collected in storage vessels.

F. The pre-eminent benchmark material in atmospheric water harvesting is MOF-801, a zirconium-based framework ($\text{Zr}_6\text{O}_4(\text{OH})_4(\text{fumarate})_6$), and its next-generation analog, MOF-303, an aluminum-based framework ($\text{Al(OH)(pyrazole- dicarboxylate)}$). In arid field trials conducted in the Mojave Desert in California and the Sonoran Desert in Arizona under extreme conditions ($<10\%\text{ RH}$), MOF-303 devices successfully delivered over 0.7 to 1.3 liters of pure potable water per kilogram of MOF per daily solar cycle, operating entirely passively without any external electrical power or moving parts.

G. Despite these technological breakthroughs, commercial scaling of MOF harvesters confronts material synthesis costs and long-term hydrothermal stability hurdles. Synthesizing zirconium- and aluminum-based MOFs currently relies on organic solvents like dimethylformamide (DMF) and costly organic linkers. Furthermore, repeated operational cycles of rapid thermal heating and water vapor condensation can induce structural framework degradation and hydrolytic linker cleavage over multi-year deployments. However, the development of green, water-based synthesis routes, continuous roll-to-roll sorbent manufacturing, and multi-cycle active desorption fans is rapidly lowering production costs, positioning MOF-based atmospheric water harvesters as a vital decentralized water lifeline for arid communities worldwide.'
);

