-- Passage 1: The Traditional Craft and Material Science of Japanese Tatami Mats
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '490f55eb-e877-5d35-8b3c-a59039fff9b2', 'f68b4d5d-d4a2-5b88-b6bd-783dde5705e5', 1, 'The Traditional Craft and Material Science of Japanese Tatami Mats',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the architectural and cultural history of traditional Japan, few interior structural elements have defined the aesthetic atmosphere, sensory experience, and spatial geometry of domestic spaces as comprehensively as the tatami mat. For over a millennium—evolving from thin, foldable sleeping mats utilized exclusively by the imperial nobility in the Nara and Heian periods (eighth to twelfth centuries CE) into the universal flooring of Edo-period urban houses and modern dwellings—tatami has anchored the bioclimatic design of the Japanese home (washitsu). More than a decorative floor covering, the tatami mat represents a refined harmony of agricultural cultivation, botanical processing, and passive hygrothermal material engineering, expertly tailored to moderate Japan’s humid subtropical climate.

B. The standardized physical architecture of a traditional tatami mat is constructed from three distinct, layered components: 1. The Core (Tatami-doko): The structural foundation, measuring 5.0 to 5.5 centimeters in thickness, traditionally composed of densely compressed, dried organic rice straw (wara). To achieve high structural rigidity, un-rotted rice straw is laid out in multiple perpendicular, criss-crossed layers (typically containing up to forty to fifty kilograms of straw per mat) and stitched under heavy mechanical pressure using durable hemp cord. 2. The Surface Cover (Tatami-omote): A supple, finely woven exterior textile face woven from the culms (stems) of the soft rush plant (igusa, Juncus effusus var. decipiens). 3. The Border Fabric (Tatami-beri): Decorative woven fabric ribbons (made from cotton, linen, or silk) stitched along the two longitudinal edges of the mat to bind the surface to the core and conceal the exposed fiber ends.

C. The botanical foundation of the tatami-omote is the igusa rush, cultivated predominantly in the alluvial river plains of Kumamoto, Fukuoka, and Hiroshima prefectures. Harvested in midsummer when the slender stems reach lengths of 100 to 150 centimeters, the raw green rush stems undergo a specialized, ancient preservation treatment known as sendo-zome (mud-dyeing). Immediately after harvesting, the damp rush stems are submerged in a liquid slurry of fine, mineral-rich natural clay (sendo or tsuchi) before being transferred into low-temperature drying kilns. The fine clay particles coat each individual stem, protecting natural chlorophyll from photolytic oxidation, locking in an elastic green hue, and imparting the characteristic sweet, grassy fragrance of fresh tatami.

D. The internal micro-structure of the dried igusa stem reveals an extraordinary natural porous cellular geometry. The interior core of the rush stem is populated by a honeycomb-like network of stellate (star-shaped) aerenchyma parenchyma cells. This sponge-like cross-section contains thousands of microscopic air voids that endow the tatami surface with superior hygroscopic (moisture-regulating) and thermal insulation properties. In the sweltering, moisture-laden months of the Japanese summer, the porous igusa fibers and dense straw core actively absorb excess indoor ambient humidity; during the cold, dry winter, the fibers desorb their stored moisture back into the indoor living space, functioning as a natural, non-mechanical humidity buffer. A single tatami mat can absorb and release up to 500 milliliters of water vapor during seasonal humidity swings.

E. In addition to passive moisture regulation, tatami surfaces function as natural bio-chemical air filters and acoustic dampers. Gas-sorption studies demonstrate that the natural cellular pores and clay coating of tatami-omote actively adsorb and break down common indoor volatile pollutants, capturing nitrogen dioxide ($\text{NO}_2$), formaldehyde ($\text{HCHO}$), and sulfur dioxide ($\text{SO}_2$) through chemical chemisorption. Furthermore, the fibrous elasticity of the combined rush-and-straw matrix absorbs high-frequency acoustic vibrations, creating a serene, hushed acoustic environment that underpins traditional cultural practices such as the Japanese tea ceremony (chanoyu) and Zen meditation (zazen).

F. Beyond its material properties, tatami served as the fundamental geometric module that governed traditional Japanese architectural design (kiwari). Rather than planning rooms around arbitrary external dimensions, traditional architects designed floorplans based on standard multiples of the tatami mat ($1 \times 0.5$ ken, roughly 182 by 91 centimeters in the Tokyo Edo-ma standard, or 191 by 55 centimeters in the Kyoto Kyo-ma standard). Traditional room dimensions—such as the classic 4.5-mat (yojohan), 6-mat (rokujohan), or 8-mat (hachijohan) configurations—dictated the placement of structural wooden pillars, sliding paper doors (fusuma and shoji), and ceiling heights.

G. In the contemporary era, the traditional tatami industry is undergoing technological transformation to adapt to modern construction standards and lifestyle shifts. To reduce weight and eliminate susceptibility to mold growth and mite infestation in modern airtight apartments, manufacturers frequently replace the heavy straw core with lightweight extruded polystyrene foam (EPS) and compressed wood-fiber boards (kenaf boards). Furthermore, synthetic tatami surfaces woven from polypropylene polymers or resin-coated Japanese washi paper fibers have been developed to resist water staining and ultraviolet fading. Nevertheless, traditional master artisans (tatami-shokunin) continue to hand-craft authentic straw-and-rush mats for historic temples, cultural tea rooms, and modern homes, preserving a living bioclimatic heritage that unites Japanese agricultural rhythms with the timeless beauty of natural interior design.'
);

