-- Passage 1: The History and Metallurgy of Traditional Bell Founding
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '03447cf8-a6e8-5213-926d-52b3f3bee864', 'ef6f6828-7604-5b3e-8430-e5b23a80ed26', 1, 'The History and Metallurgy of Traditional Bell Founding',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For over four millennia across Eurasia, the casting of monumental acoustic bells has represented a supreme triumph of pyrotechnology, metallurgy, and acoustic physics. From the bronze musical chime bells (bianzhong) of Shang and Zhou Dynasty China to the colossal swinging bells of medieval European cathedrals and the stationary sacred temple bells (bonshō) of feudal Japan, cast bronze bells have served as essential instruments of state ritual, religious liturgy, municipal timekeeping, and civic alarm. Unlike common utilitarian bronze tools or structural statuary, an acoustic bell is subjected to severe, repeated mechanical shock: it must withstand the violent, localized impact of a heavy iron clapper or wooden beam without fracturing, while simultaneously vibrating to produce a rich, resonant, and sustained musical timbre.

B. The acoustic brilliance and structural durability of traditional bells are dictated by the precise chemical composition of the casting alloy: bell metal. Bell metal is a specialized, high-tin binary bronze alloy formulated strictly from copper ($\text{Cu}$) and tin ($\text{Sn}$), typically in a precise mass ratio of approximately 78 to 80 percent copper to 20 to 22 percent tin (often cited as the classic $4:1$ ratio). While modern structural bronzes contain significant additions of zinc, lead, or aluminum to improve machinability and ductility, traditional bell founders strictly excluded lead (typically limiting lead impurities to less than 0.5 percent). Because lead does not dissolve in solid copper-tin solutions, it segregates into soft, microscopic elemental globules that absorb vibrational energy, acting as internal acoustic dampeners that deaden the bell''s musical resonance.

C. In physical metallurgy, the high-tin formulation of bell metal creates an exceptionally dense, rigid intermetallic crystal structure. When the liquid alloy cools and solidifies within the mold, it undergoes complex phase transformations, forming a hard, brittle matrix dominated by the delta ($\delta$) phase intermetallic compound ($\text{Cu}_{31}\text{Sn}_8$). This rigid crystal lattice exhibits negligible internal friction (internal damping), allowing acoustic shock waves generated by the clapper strike to propagate through the bronze shell for dozens of seconds with minimal attenuation, producing the shimmering sustain characteristic of high-grade bells.

D. The physical molding of a monumental church bell is an arduous, multi-stage artisanal process centered on loam molding using a pivoting sweep board (strickle). The fabrication process progresses through three concentric structural layers: 1. The Core: A stationary inner dome constructed from refractory red clay bricks built upon an iron baseplate. The brickwork is plastered with a loam mixture composed of fine silica clay, horse manure, hair, and straw. A wooden sweep board rotating around a central vertical iron spindle sweeps the wet loam into the exact interior contour of the bell, which is then dried with an internal charcoal fire. 2. The False Bell: A sacrificial intermediate layer of clay, wax, and grease applied over the core. The outer sweep board shapes this layer to the exact thickness, profile, and external dimensions of the finished bronze bell. Inscriptions, religious iconography, and heraldic crests molded from beeswax are affixed to the exterior surface. 3. The Cope (Mantle): A heavy outer iron flask or masonry mantle lined with multiple layers of fine refractory loam built over the false bell. Once the cope is fully cured, it is lifted vertically; the sacrificial false bell is carefully chipped away and destroyed, leaving an empty, pristine hollow cavity between the core and the cope that precisely matches the intended geometry of the bell.

E. For casting, the assembled mold is lowered into a deep casting pit excavated in the foundry floor and packed tightly with damp sand to withstand the immense hydrostatic pressure of the molten bronze. Bell metal ingots are melted in large reverberatory furnaces fueled by dry hardwood or coal, reaching temperatures of 1,100°C to 1,200°C. Once molten, the furnace tap hole is breached, allowing the liquid bronze to flow through stone-lined troughs directly into the mold’s pouring basin. The pouring must be executed in a single, uninterrupted stream lasting mere minutes; any pause or turbulence introduces air bubbles, slag inclusions, or thermal cold shuts that cause fatal acoustic flaws or structural cracking.

F. After the casting has cooled slowly over several days or weeks, the bell is excavated from the pit, cleaned of refractory clay, and subjected to acoustic harmonic tuning. A high-quality musical bell is a complex acoustic instrument that vibrates in multiple modal patterns simultaneously, producing five primary partial frequencies: The Hum Tone: The lowest fundamental frequency, generated by the vibration of the upper waist. The Prime (Strike Tone): An octave above the hum tone, defining the apparent pitch of the bell. The Tierce: A minor third above the prime, imparting the bell''s characteristic melancholic overtone. The Quint: A perfect fifth above the prime. The Nominal: An octave above the prime (two octaves above the hum tone).

G. In historical foundries, master tuners tuned bells by hand using curved cold chisels; modern foundries mount the inverted bell on a massive vertical boring lathe. Using fine cutting tools, the tuner shaves microscopic shavings of metal from specific internal concentric bands of the bronze interior: removing metal from the soundbow lowers the nominal, while thinning the waist lowers the hum and tierce tones. By harmonizing all five partials into perfect consonances, bell founders transform heavy metallurgical bronze into living musical instruments whose voices echo across centuries of human history.'
);

