-- Passage 1: The Domestication and Dispersal of the Yak (Bos grunniens)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '39e90f37-1dfb-5927-a32e-cef919121a53', '2cc161d0-c75f-58e1-9385-d71efcda8f30', 1, 'The Domestication and Dispersal of the Yak (Bos grunniens)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the colossal, wind-scoured expanses of the Qinghai-Tibetan Plateau and adjoining high-altitude Himalayan mountain systems—spanning modern-day Tibet, Nepal, Bhutan, northern India, Kyrgyzstan, and Mongolia—the domestic yak (Bos grunniens) represents the biological keystone of human existence. In a severe, hypobaric alpine environment situated at altitudes ranging from 3,000 to 5,500 meters above sea level, where ambient temperatures frequently plunge below minus forty degrees Celsius, intense solar ultraviolet radiation bathes the landscape, and atmospheric oxygen concentrations are less than half of sea-level values, ordinary agricultural crop production is virtually impossible. For over four millennia, indigenous high-altitude pastoralist communities (most notably the Drokpa nomads of Tibet) have relied almost entirely on this cold-adapted bovid to supply an integrated spectrum of life-sustaining resources: dense dairy milk, nutrient-rich red meat, long guard hair and soft down fiber for clothing and tents, fuel in the form of dried dung, and draft transport power.

B. The evolutionary origins and precise archaeological chronology of yak domestication have been illuminated through modern paleogenomic sequencing and archaeological excavations across the Tibetan interior. The domestic yak was bred from its ancestral wild progenitor, the wild yak (Bos mutus), a massive, aggressive ungulate whose adult bulls can weigh over one thousand kilograms. Mitochondrial DNA and whole-genome coalescence analyses indicate that the initial split between wild and domestic lineages occurred during the late Neolithic period (circa 2500 to 1500 BCE), closely coinciding with the arrival of sedentary barley-farming and pastoralist cultures on the northeastern margin of the plateau (such as the Qijia and Zongri cultures). Selective breeding aimed at producing smaller, more manageable body sizes, varied coat colors (such as white and piebald variations), and docility transformed the formidable wild beast into a compliant herd animal.

C. The extraordinary capacity of Bos grunniens to thrive under chronic, high-altitude environmental stress is supported by an intricate suite of genetic, anatomical, and physiological adaptations. Unlike lowland cattle (Bos taurus), which suffer from fatal pulmonary hypertension, right ventricular heart failure, and edema when transported to high elevations (a condition known as "brisket disease"), yaks possess specialized cardiopulmonary architecture. A yak possesses an exceptionally large, non-muscular pulmonary trunk, thin-walled pulmonary blood vessels that do not constrict under low oxygen, and an enlarged heart and lung volume relative to its total body mass. Furthermore, its blood contains small, numerous red blood cells (erythrocytes) packed with a unique fetal-like hemoglobin variant possessing an extraordinarily high chemical affinity for binding and transporting oxygen under low atmospheric partial pressures.

D. In addition to hypoxia resilience, the yak possesses supreme anatomical insulation against extreme sub-zero cold. The yak’s skin is thick and devoid of functional sweat glands across most of its surface area, minimizing evaporative heat loss. The animal is enveloped in a dense, double- layered pelage: a long, coarse outer skirt of water-resistant guard hairs that reaches almost to the ground, shielding the underbelly from snow and glacial ice, and an ultra-dense, microscopic inner undercoat of fine, crimped down fibers (khullu) that traps air to provide supreme thermal resistance. Furthermore, the yak''s large, non-functional subcutaneous fat layer stores lipid energy reserves, while its specialized digestive physiology—featuring an enlarged rumen capable of slowly fermenting tough, high-fiber alpine sedges (Kobresia species) and mountain grasses— generates continuous internal metabolic fermentation heat.

E. In traditional Tibetan nomad pastoralism, the domestic yak serves as the primary engine of mobile transhumance. The long, coarse outer skirt hair is hand-spun and woven on traditional backstrap looms into thick, waterproof panels to construct the classic black nomad tent (ba). These tents are ingeniously adapted to alpine microclimates: during dry, cold weather, the coarse weave allows sunlight and air to circulate, while the fibers swell in rain or snow, sealing the fabric completely against moisture. The soft, cashmere-like inner down (khullu) is harvested during the annual spring molt to craft warm garments, blankets, and footwear. Furthermore, in a tree-less landscape completely devoid of timber, dried yak dung (jiba) is the universal, indispensable domestic fuel used for daily cooking and indoor space heating.

F. The yak is equally celebrated as an exceptional dairy producer in high-latitude pastoral economies. While total volumetric lactation yield is modest compared to commercial lowland dairy cows, yak milk is exceptionally concentrated and nutrient-dense, containing five to seven percent butterfat and high concentrations of total solids, calcium, and vitamin A. Tibetan pastoralists process this rich milk into shelf-stable dairy commodities, most notably yellow yak butter and dried rock-hard curd cheese (chhurpi or qula). Yak butter is an essential cultural staple: it is blended with hot black tea and roasted barley flour (tsampa) to make traditional salted butter tea, burned in brass liturgical butter lamps in Buddhist monasteries, and traded along historical caravan routes (such as the ancient Tea Horse Road, or Chamadao).

G. In the contemporary era, yak pastoralism is confronting modern environmental, demographic, and market disruptions. Accelerated global climate change is causing rapid permafrost degradation, glacial retreat, and desertification of alpine grasslands across the Tibetan Plateau, reducing pasture carrying capacities and shifting traditional grazing ranges. Concurrently, national settlement policies and modern urban employment are leading younger generations to abandon the arduous lifestyle of nomadic pastoralism. In response, regional agricultural cooperatives are developing sustainable genetic crossbreeding programs—crossing yaks with lowland cattle to produce fertile female hybrids (dzo-mo) with higher milk yields—and establishing international commercial markets for high-value organic yak cheese and luxury khullu textiles, striving to sustain a timeless highland culture that has endured on the roof of the world for millennia.'
);

