-- Passage 2: High-Entropy Alloys (HEAs) in Extreme Cryogenic Environments
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '759ade2a-30a3-532d-aaff-be931d45c474', '2cc161d0-c75f-58e1-9385-d71efcda8f30', 2, 'High-Entropy Alloys (HEAs) in Extreme Cryogenic Environments',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of materials science and physical metallurgy, the development of structural metallic alloys has followed a traditional, single-principal-element paradigm. Conventional engineering alloys—such as structural steels, aluminum alloys, titanium airframes, and nickel-based superalloys—are formulated with one dominant base metal (typically comprising eighty to ninety-five atomic percent of the total mixture), to which minor quantities of secondary alloying elements are added to optimize specific mechanical properties like hardness or corrosion resistance. However, conventional engineering alloys encounter severe physical failure when subjected to extreme cryogenic temperatures (temperatures below minus one hundred and fifty degrees Celsius, $-150^\circ\text{C}$, down to liquid nitrogen, $- 196^\circ\text{C}$, and liquid helium, $-269^\circ\text{C}$). At these extreme temperatures, virtually all standard metals undergo a catastrophic ductile-to-brittle transition (DBT), losing their impact toughness and shattering under minor mechanical strain.

B. In 2004, materials scientists J.W. Yeh and Brian Cantor shattered this ancient metallurgical dogma by independently formulating High-Entropy Alloys (HEAs)—also designated as Multi- Principal Element Alloys (MPEAs) or chemically complex alloys. Rather than relying on a single dominant host element, High-Entropy Alloys are synthesized by blending five or more principal metallic elements in near-equiatomic proportions (typically between five and thirty-five atomic percent each).

C. The foundational thermodynamic principle enabling HEAs to form stable, single-phase solid solutions rather than complex, brittle intermetallic compounds is the maximization of configurational entropy of mixing ($\Delta S_{\text{conf}}$). According to Gibbs free energy thermodynamics: $$\Delta G_{\text{mix}} = \Delta H_{\text{mix}} - T \Delta S_{\text{mix}}$$ For an ideal multi-element solid solution containing $n$ equiatomic components, the configurational entropy per mole is given by Boltzmann''s statistical equation: $$\Delta S_{\text{conf}} = R \ln(n)$$ where $R$ is the universal gas constant. When five or more elements are blended in equal proportions, $\Delta S_{\text{conf}}$ surpasses $1.61 R$ (high-entropy regime). At elevated liquidus and solidification temperatures ($T$), the colossal negative $T \Delta S_{\text{mix}}$ entropic term outweighs the enthalpy of mixing ($\Delta H_{\text{mix}}$), suppressing the formation of multiphase intermetallics and driving the molten alloy to crystallize into simple, disordered single-phase crystal lattices: most commonly Face-Centered Cubic (FCC), Body- Centered Cubic (BCC), or Hexagonal Close-Packed (HCP).

D. The undisputed archetypal model material in high-entropy metallurgy is the equiatomic Cantor alloy, composed of five transition metals: $\text{FeCoNiCrMn}$ (iron, cobalt, nickel, chromium, and manganese in equal twenty atomic percent ratios). The single-phase FCC Cantor alloy exhibits an extraordinary mechanical anomaly that defies classical metallurgy: its strength, ductility, and fracture toughness all increase simultaneously as the temperature decreases from ambient room temperature ($+20^\circ\text{C}$) down to cryogenic liquid nitrogen ($77\text{ K}$) and liquid helium ($4.2\text{ K}$) temperatures. While standard titanium and steel alloys become glassy and brittle, the fracture toughness ($K_{\text{Jc}}$) of the Cantor alloy at cryogenic temperatures surpasses an astonishing two hundred megapascals square-root meter ($>200\text{ MPa}\cdot\text{m}^{1/2}$), rendering it one of the toughest metallic materials known to physical science.

E. High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) and in-situ synchrotron X-ray diffraction have decoded the multi-stage micromechanical deformation mechanisms governing this cryogenic toughness. The superior performance is driven by a sequential, temperature- dependent activation of multiple plastic deformation pathways: 1. Dislocation Glide: At room temperature, plastic deformation occurs predominantly through classical planar planar dislocation slip across close-packed $\{111\}$ crystal planes. 2. Stacking Fault Formation: As the temperature plunges toward cryogenic levels, the material’s intrinsic stacking fault energy (SFE) decreases sharply, promoting the dissociation of full dislocations into widely separated partial dislocations and creating dense networks of planar stacking faults. 3. Deformation Nano-Twinning (TWIP Effect): Under extreme cryogenic strain, the alloy activates mechanical deformation twinning. Microscopic nano-twin boundaries form throughout the crystal grains, dividing larger grains into sub-micron crystalline domains. These dynamic twin boundaries act as impenetrable barriers to mobile dislocations (a dynamic Hall-Petch grain refinement effect), simultaneously increasing mechanical yield strength while maintaining vast reservoirs of plastic ductility.

F. Beyond the classic five-element Cantor alloy, materials engineers are developing non- equiatomic medium-entropy alloys (MEAs), such as the ternary $\text{CoCrNi}$ and quaternary $\text{FeCoCrNi}$ systems. Ternary $\text{CoCrNi}$ exhibits an even higher cryogenic crack- initiation fracture toughness exceeding $260\text{ MPa}\cdot\text{m}^{1/2}$ at liquid nitrogen temperatures. The extreme resistance to fracture in $\text{CoCrNi}$ is further amplified by a stress-induced, phase-transformation-induced plasticity (TRIP) effect, where the FCC crystal lattice undergoes localized, progressive martensitic transformations into a hexagonal close- packed (HCP) crystal phase at the propagating crack tip, absorbing immense fracture energy and actively blunting the advancing crack.

G. The extraordinary cryogenic properties of high-entropy alloys make them transformative structural materials for next-generation aerospace, deep-space exploration, and cryogenic clean energy infrastructure. In space launch vehicles and orbital satellites, HEAs provide ultra- resilient, lightweight containment vessels and structural brackets capable of enduring the thermal shock of liquid hydrogen ($-253^\circ\text{C}$) and liquid oxygen propellants without risk of catastrophic brittle rupture. Furthermore, in terrestrial clean energy systems, HEAs are ideal materials for piping, valves, and containment tanks in superconducting magnetic energy storage (SMES), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) magnets, and maritime liquefied natural gas (LNG) carrier transport. As vacuum induction melting and additive manufacturing (laser powder bed fusion 3D printing) of HEA powders continue to mature, high-entropy alloys are poised to unlock the frozen frontiers of science and engineering.'
);

