-- Passage 3: The Problem of Induction: Goodman’s "Grue" Paradox and Epistemic Entrenchment
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '95f11afc-cace-59ba-89ed-893840123533', '2cc161d0-c75f-58e1-9385-d71efcda8f30', 3, 'The Problem of Induction: Goodman’s "Grue" Paradox and Epistemic Entrenchment',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the philosophy of science, formal epistemology, and inductive logic, no inquiry is as foundational as the problem of inductive confirmation: how do empirical observations of past events justify our rational predictions regarding unobserved future events? In the eighteenth century, Scottish Enlightenment philosopher David Hume famously formulated the classical Problem of Induction in his Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding. Hume demonstrated that all inductive inferences—such as concluding that "the sun will rise tomorrow" based on the fact that it has risen every morning in recorded history—rely on an unprovable metaphysical assumption: the Principle of the Uniformity of Nature (that the unobserved future will resemble the observed past). Hume showed that any attempt to justify this uniformity principle using past inductive success is circular, leaving inductive reasoning without a deductive foundation.

B. For centuries, logicians and empiricist philosophers (most notably Rudolf Carnap and Carl Hempel) sought to resolve Hume’s skepticism by constructing a formal, purely syntactical inductive logic. Logical positivists believed that just as deductive validity depends strictly on the grammatical and logical form of an argument (e.g., modus ponens is valid regardless of what specific words replace $P$ and $Q$), inductive confirmation could be formalized through purely syntactic rules connecting observation sentences to general universal hypotheses.

C. In 1954, however, American philosopher Nelson Goodman fundamentally undermined this syntactic dream by publishing Fact, Fiction, and Forecast, formulating what is now universally celebrated as the "New Riddle of Induction" (or Goodman’s "Grue" Paradox). Goodman demonstrated that inductive confirmation cannot be defined by syntactic form alone, because purely formal rules validate absurd, contradictory hypotheses just as easily as sound scientific laws.

D. To construct the paradox, Goodman devised an artificial, time-dependent predicate: "grue." The predicate "grue" applies to all things examined before a specific future time $t$ (for instance, midnight on December 31, 2030) if and only if they are green, and applies to other things if and only if they are blue. Formalized logically: $$x \text{ is grue} \iff (x \text{ is examined before time } t \land x \text{ is green}) \lor (x \text{ is not examined before time } t \land x \text{ is blue})$$ Now, consider a geologist examining millions of emeralds mined across the globe over centuries prior to time $t$. Every single observed emerald has been verified to be green.

E. Goodman pointed out that this vast observational evidence provides identical syntactic confirmation for two completely incompatible hypotheses: Hypothesis 1: "All emeralds are green." Hypothesis 2: "All emeralds are grue." Because every observed emerald was examined before time $t$ and was green, every observed emerald was, by definition, also grue. Under standard syntactical confirmation logic, both universal hypotheses share the exact same logical form: $\forall x (Ex \to Gx)$. Therefore, according to formal inductive logic, our past empirical observations confirm Hypothesis 2 just as strongly as Hypothesis 1!

F. The devastating epistemic consequence of the Grue Paradox manifests at time $t$. Hypothesis 1 predicts that the next emerald uncovered after time $t$ will be green. Hypothesis 2 predicts that the next emerald uncovered after time $t$ will be blue (because an unexamined emerald after time $t$ must be blue to be grue). Pure syntactical confirmation theory provides zero logical grounds for choosing the sane prediction (green) over the insane prediction (blue). The paradox reveals that while some universal statements (like "all emeralds are green") represent genuine, law-like empirical generalizations, other statements with identical syntactic structures (like "all emeralds are grue") are illegitimate, non-projectible pseudo-hypotheses.

G. To resolve the New Riddle of Induction and establish a principled boundary between projectible and non-projectible predicates, Goodman formulated the pragmatic doctrine of Epistemic Entrenchment. Goodman argued that the difference between "green" and "grue" is not a formal syntactical property, but a historical and linguistic one. The predicate "green" is deeply "entrenched" in human linguistic and scientific practice: it has a long, successful history of past projections in hypotheses that were tested, confirmed, and adopted across generations. In contrast, "grue" is an un-entrenched, artificial predicate with zero historical projection pedigree. A hypothesis containing well-entrenched predicates takes precedence over competing hypotheses containing poorly entrenched predicates.

H. While Goodman’s entrenchment theory provided a pragmatic linguistic resolution, it provoked sharp philosophical criticism from realists and metaphysicians, such as David Armstrong and Willard Van Orman Quine. Critics argued that grounding scientific projection purely in historical linguistic habits reduces natural science to linguistic relativism. Quine famously argued that projectible predicates must correspond to "natural kinds"—objective, real- world physical properties carved at the joints of nature—rather than arbitrary linguistic conventions. [I] In synthesizing modern philosophy of science, the New Riddle of Induction remains an indispensable touchstone for artificial intelligence, machine learning, and epistemology. In modern machine learning, Goodman’s paradox is manifested as the "Overfitting Problem" and the "Inductive Bias" challenge: how can a neural network distinguish genuine underlying statistical patterns from artificial, grue-like noise in training data? By forcing science to acknowledge that inductive logic requires background assumptions, conceptual frameworks, and natural kinds, Goodman’s paradox illuminates the intricate, indispensable partnership between human language, cognitive models, and the physical cosmos.'
);

