-- Passage 1: The Traditional Manufacture of French Limoges Enamel
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '0ffd5589-7ffd-5683-a0dd-927601451869', 'efb3cfb3-f9f1-5d5d-b808-6aa55f8d94b7', 1, 'The Traditional Manufacture of French Limoges Enamel',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. For over four centuries across medieval and Renaissance Europe, the city of Limoges, situated in the historic Limousin province of central-southwestern France, served as the pre- eminent capital of vitreous enamel artistry. From the late twelfth century through the sixteenth century, the master enamellers of Limoges pioneered successive pyrotechnological and artistic revolutions, transforming utilitarian copper vessels and ecclesiastical liturgical furnishings into radiant masterpieces of international luxury. Celebrated for their luminous color palettes, complex mythological and biblical iconography, and extraordinary durability, Limoges enamels (émaux de Limoges) were eagerly commissioned by popes, European monarchs, aristocratic dynasties, and major monastic orders from England and Spain to Italy and the Holy Roman Empire.

B. The foundational material of vitreous enamel is powdered glass (a silica-based flux composed of pulverized quartz or flint sand mixed with potassium or sodium carbonates to lower the melting point) chemically colored with microscopic transition metal oxides. When fused at high temperatures inside a ceramic kiln, this granular glass frit transforms into a hard, non- porous, and brilliant glassy coating bonded molecularly to a metallic substrate. In Limoges, the universal substrate of choice was unalloyed, high-purity hammered copper sheet. Unlike silver or gold—which were economically prohibitive for mass production and prone to deformation during firing—or bronze, whose tin content caused violent blistering during thermal glass fusion, pure copper possessed an ideal coefficient of thermal expansion, matched the melting characteristics of lead-silica glass, and was easily hammered into complex curved forms.

C. In the medieval era (spanning the late twelfth through the fourteenth centuries), Limoges attained global fame through the mastery of the champlevé technique. In the champlevé method (literally "raised field"), master metalsmiths began with a heavy plate of hammered copper, typically three to five millimeters in thickness. Using fine hardened-steel chisels and gouges, the artisan carved out recessed troughs, shallow cells, and depressions corresponding to the intended artistic composition, leaving narrow raised ridges of un-carved copper to delineate the contours and outlines of the figures.

D. Once the recesses were excavated and thoroughly degreased with acidic wine lees or vinegar, the enameller packed the wet, pulverized colored glass paste into the hollowed cavities using fine bronze spatulas. The palette of medieval Limoges was defined by specific metal oxide formulations: 1. Deep Cobalt Blue: Synthesized from roasted cobalt oxide minerals, producing the legendary, intense "Limoges blue." 2. Turquoise and Emerald Green: Formulated from copper carbonate (malachite) or cupric oxide. 3. Opaque Brick Red: Derived from reduced cuprous oxide ($\text{Cu}_2\text{O}$). 4. Opaque White and Pale Yellow: Formulated from tin oxide and antimony compounds. The prepared plaque was placed atop a clay tile (marle) inside a wood-fired kiln and heated to temperatures between 800°C and 900°C. At this temperature, the glass powder melted, fused, and flowed into a smooth liquid pool, completely filling the excavated copper cells.

E. After slow cooling, the enamel surface was rough, uneven, and elevated above the copper boundaries. The artisan embarked on an arduous polishing process: rubbing the surface with abrasive sandstones, pumice blocks, and fine emery powders lubricated with water until the enamel fields and the raised copper ridges formed a single, perfectly flush, mirror-like plane. Finally, the exposed copper ridges and relief figures were coated with an amalgam of gold and mercury (fire-gilding) and heated gently to vaporize the toxic mercury, leaving a glittering, corrosion-proof layer of pure gold that accentuated the jewel-like enamel colors.

F. In the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, Limoges witnessed a profound stylistic and technological paradigm shift: the emergence of "Painted Enamel" (émail peint), spearheaded by legendary master dynasties such as the Pénicaud, Limosin (notably Léonard Limosin), Reymond, and Court families. Painted enamel abandoned the time-consuming chiseled cells of champlevé. Instead, artisans used copper plates as blank canvases. The copper sheet was first coated on both sides—the reverse received a protective layer of sacrificial glass (contre-émail) to balance thermal contraction stresses and prevent the copper from warping during firing—and fired with an opaque, dark background layer (usually black or deep midnight blue).

G. On top of this dark, glossy foundation, Renaissance artists painted elaborate figurative scenes using fine sable-hair brushes and specialized white enamel paste formulated from finely ground calcined tin oxide mixed with lavender oil or water. This monochromatic technique, known as grisaille (from the French gris, meaning gray), utilized multiple translucent layers: thick applications of white yielded brilliant, opaque highlights, while thin, semi-transparent washes allowed the dark underlayer to show through, creating subtle mid-tones, delicate anatomical modeling, and atmospheric chiaroscuro. Finished plaques, decorative ewers, salt cellars, and portrait medallions were subjected to multiple sequential kiln firings at carefully graded temperatures, with delicate gilded line details (dorure) applied in the final low- temperature firing. Today, preserved in leading global museums, Limoges enamels endure as brilliant testaments to the mastery of Renaissance pyrochemistry and decorative metalwork.'
);

