-- Passage 2: Triboelectric Nanogenerators (TENGs) for Mechanical Energy Harvesting
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '7c32eb01-f4ea-53ed-a7b9-5f2e879a2f8b', 'efb3cfb3-f9f1-5d5d-b808-6aa55f8d94b7', 2, 'Triboelectric Nanogenerators (TENGs) for Mechanical Energy Harvesting',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the rapidly expanding era of the Internet of Things (IoT), wearable health monitors, wireless sensor networks, and ubiquitous smart infrastructure, the demand for decentralized, self- sustaining electrical energy sources has escalated dramatically. Trillions of distributed micro- electronic sensor nodes deployed across civil structures, environmental habitats, and the human body require continuous electrical power. However, conventional chemical batteries suffer from severe operational limitations: they possess finite lifespans, require costly periodic replacement or recharging, and present environmental disposal hazards due to toxic heavy metals and flammable organic electrolytes. To overcome the battery maintenance bottleneck, materials scientists and applied physicists are developing energy-harvesting technologies capable of scavenging ambient mechanical energy directly from the environment, most prominently Triboelectric Nanogenerators (TENGs).

B. Invented in 2012 by materials scientist Zhong Lin Wang and his research team at the Georgia Institute of Technology, the Triboelectric Nanogenerator is an energy-transducing device that converts ambient, low-frequency mechanical motion—such as human footsteps, body joint movements, acoustic vibrations, wind turbulence, ocean water waves, and vehicle vibrations— into usable electrical electricity. Unlike conventional electromagnetic generators that rely on heavy permanent magnets and copper coils operating at high rotational speeds, TENGs are lightweight, flexible, cost-effective, and exceptionally efficient at converting random, low- frequency mechanical vibrations (below 5 to 10 Hz) into electrical power.

C. The physical operation of a TENG is rooted in the synergistic coupling of two fundamental physical principles: Contact Electrification (the triboelectric effect) and Electrostatic Induction. Contact electrification is an ancient, ubiquitous quantum mechanical surface phenomenon: when two dissimilar materials with different electron affinities are brought into physical mechanical contact, electronic charge transfer occurs across the interface. One material, possessing a higher work function, attracts and captures electrons, becoming negatively charged, while the opposite material donates electrons, becoming positively charged.

D. When the two charged surfaces are subsequently separated by an external mechanical force, a spatial gap is established between the opposing static surface charges. This physical separation creates a time-dependent electrostatic potential difference across the device. To balance this potential gradient, free electrons are driven to flow through an external electrical load connected between conductive back electrodes attached to the materials. When the mechanical force pushes the two surfaces back into contact, the electrostatic potential difference collapses, driving an equal and opposite reverse flow of electrons through the external circuit. Continuous mechanical pressing and releasing thus generates an alternating current (AC) electrical output.

E. Depending on the electrode configuration and mechanical motion trajectory, TENGs operate in four fundamental physical operational modes: 1. Vertical Contact-Separation Mode: Two dielectric films move perpendicularly into and out of direct contact. 2. Lateral Sliding Mode: Two planar surfaces slide laterally against each other, generating charges through continuous frictional shear. 3. Single-Electrode Mode: A moving charged object (such as a human finger or clothing fabric) approaches and departs from a single stationary grounded electrode, ideal for touchscreens and biometric tracking interfaces. 4. Freestanding Triboelectric-Layer Mode: A freely moving dielectric object shuttles symmetrically between a pair of stationary electrodes without requiring attached wiring, offering high durability for rotary and wind harvesters.

F. The electrical power conversion efficiency of a TENG is directly determined by the surface charge density ($\sigma$) accumulated on the triboelectric layers. Materials are categorized along the "Triboelectric Series"—a calibrated empirical hierarchy ranking materials by their tendency to gain or lose electrons. To maximize output voltage and power density, engineers pair highly tribo-positive materials (such as nylon, human skin, or layered double hydroxides) with highly tribo-negative materials possessing high dielectric constants and strong electronegative fluorine atoms, most notably polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE, Teflon), fluorinated ethylene propylene (FEP), and polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS). Furthermore, materials scientists deploy nano-patterning techniques (such as photolithography, plasma etching, and chemical self- assembly) to fabricate microscopic arrays of nano-pyramids, nano-pillars, or porous sponge morphologies across the material surfaces, expanding the effective contact surface area by orders of magnitude and multiplying output current.

G. Despite achieving open-circuit voltages exceeding thousands of volts and instantaneous power densities surpassing several hundred watts per square meter, the commercial implementation of TENG systems faces critical electrical and mechanical hurdles. A primary electrical challenge is impedance mismatch: TENGs inherently generate ultra-high voltages combined with very low output currents and high internal capacitive impedance (mega-ohms), while standard electronic microchips require low-voltage (1.8 to 3.3 V), high-current direct current (DC). To resolve this mismatch, power electronics engineers design specialized power management circuits (PMCs) utilizing inductive buck-boost converters and electronic switches to achieve energy transfer efficiencies exceeding 85 to 90 percent. Furthermore, long-term mechanical wear and friction-induced material abrasion present longevity challenges that are being addressed through self-lubricating liquid-solid TENGs and non-contact electrostatic designs. As self-powered micro-systems mature, Triboelectric Nanogenerators are charting a definitive path toward sustainable, self-sufficient electronics and macro-scale ocean "blue energy" harvesting.'
);

