-- Passage 1: The Ecology and Traditional Husbandry of the European Honey Mushroom (Armillaria)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'ad2167ed-44c6-5ec5-a44a-e69ca0ebb37d', '0665cbb1-a3ef-5b2e-b859-c527ba6c6c6d', 1, 'The Ecology and Traditional Husbandry of the European Honey Mushroom (Armillaria)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the temperate and boreal mixed-hardwood forests of central and northern Europe, the genus Armillaria—popularly known as the honey mushroom or honey fungus (Hallimasch in German)—occupies a dual, highly paradoxical ecological niche. To forest pathologists and silviculturists, Armillaria species (most notably Armillaria mellea and Armillaria ostoyae) are notorious, highly aggressive parasitic pathogens responsible for white root rot disease, causing widespread mortality in commercially managed coniferous timber plantations and broadleaf orchards. Yet, to woodland mycologists, ethnomycologists, and traditional foragers across the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Poland, Austria, and Bavaria, Armillaria represents an indispensable ecological recycler, a vital biological indicator of forest succession, and a cherished autumnal culinary harvest that has sustained rural woodland cultures for centuries.

B. The extraordinary vegetative success and longevity of Armillaria are driven by its specialized subterranean foraging architecture: rhizomorphs. Often termed "bootlaces" or "shoestrings," rhizomorphs are specialized, root-like subterranean cables composed of hundreds of thousands of tightly packed, parallel fungal hyphae enveloped within a melanized, water- impermeable black cortical sheath. Unlike standard fungal mycelia that grow diffusely through localized organic matter, rhizomorphs act as pressurized, long-distance anatomical conduits capable of foraging through meters of un-colonized, nutrient-poor soil. Fueled by metabolic energy exported from a central food base (such as an infected tree stump), rhizomorphs actively seek out and penetrate the root bark of healthy neighboring trees, allowing a single genetic clonal individual (genet) to expand across square kilometers of forest floor over thousands of years.

C. In biochemical pathology, Armillaria functions as a potent white rot fungus. The fungus secretes a formidable battery of oxidative and hydrolytic extracellular enzymes, including lignin peroxidases, laccases, and cellulases. These enzymes depolymerize the complex, aromatic polymer matrix of lignin—the rigid chemical component that provides structural rigidity to woody plant cell walls—while concurrently consuming cellulose and hemicellulose. As lignin is metabolized, the dense brown heartwood of infected trees softens, turning into a stringy, spongy, and pale-white fibrous mass. This structural decay destabilizes the tree''s root anchor system, causing infected canopy giants to topple during heavy winter windstorms, creating vital natural canopy gaps that permit sunlight to reach the understory, thereby driving cyclic forest renewal.

D. A celebrated bio-luminescent phenomenon historically associated with decaying Armillaria wood is "foxfire" or "fairy fire." During active fungal respiration in damp autumn weather, decomposing logs colonized by Armillaria mycelia emit an eerie, continuous greenish-blue glow in total nocturnal darkness. This cold bioluminescence is catalyzed by the luciferase enzyme system: the oxidation of the fungal luciferin precursor hispidin by molecular oxygen generates an electronically excited intermediate, which releases visible photons at a wavelength of approximately 525 to 530 nanometers upon returning to ground state. Historical folklore across medieval Europe linked this ghostly light to forest spirits and nocturnal fairies, while early woodland laborers used luminescent rotting wood slivers as natural, spark-free markers to navigate dark forest trails.

E. In the seasonal foraging traditions of Central and Eastern Europe, the autumnal fruiting of Armillaria—typically triggered by heavy autumn rainfall and sharp temperature drops in late September through November—mobilizes massive foraging expeditions. Known as václavky in the Czech Republic (because they traditionally emerge around the feast day of Saint Wenceslas on September 28), honey mushrooms fruit in colossal, dense caespitose clusters containing dozens of overlapping caps emerging from decaying tree bases and stumps. The fruiting bodies feature distinct honey-yellow to golden-brown caps sprinkled with dark hairy scales, pale gills, and a distinct membranous ring (annulus) encircling the fibrous stem.

F. Despite their popularity as an edible delicacy, raw or improperly prepared Armillaria mushrooms contain thermolabile gastrointestinal toxins (including complex sesquiterpenes and toxic proteins) that induce acute nausea, cramping, and severe vomiting. Traditional culinary lore mandates rigorous preparation: the tough, fibrous stems are discarded, and the fresh caps must be parboiled in salted boiling water for fifteen to twenty minutes, with the bitter, foamy cooking water completely discarded before the mushrooms are sautéed, stewed, or preserved in vinegar- and-spice marinades. Once properly heat-treated, the mushrooms yield a firm, slippery texture and an earthy, nutty, and slightly peppery flavor profile that defines traditional seasonal dishes such as Czech guláš and Polish pickled woodland preserves (grzyby marynowane).

G. In contemporary forest management, modern silviculture is transitioning from aggressive chemical eradication of Armillaria toward integrated ecological management. Complete chemical extirpation of subterranean rhizomorph networks from forest soils is practically impossible and ecologically destructive. Instead, foresters utilize silvicultural thinning, encourage natural mixed-species stands with broadleaf trees possessing higher systemic resistance (such as birch and alder), and deploy biological control agents—such as antagonistic saprotrophic fungi (Trichoderma species) that colonize fresh stumps and physically exclude Armillaria hyphae. By recognizing the delicate balance between forest pathology and essential ecological decomposition, sustainable forestry preserves the vital woodland tapestry in which the honey mushroom has flourished for millennia.'
);

