-- Passage 2: Electrokinetic Remediation (EKR) of Heavy-Metal-Contaminated Soils
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'e594d19e-583e-58b8-9671-3cf2f29917a4', '0665cbb1-a3ef-5b2e-b859-c527ba6c6c6d', 2, 'Electrokinetic Remediation (EKR) of Heavy-Metal-Contaminated Soils',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout modern industrialized societies, historical manufacturing operations, non-ferrous metal smelting, chemical manufacturing, industrial electroplating, and military weapons testing have left an alarming global legacy of heavy-metal-contaminated land. Toxic heavy metals— predominantly lead ($\text{Pb}$), cadmium ($\text{Cd}$), chromium ($\text{Cr}$), arsenic ($\text{As}$), and mercury ($\text{Hg}$)—pose severe, non-biodegradable hazards to human health, agricultural food security, and groundwater aquifers. While conventional civil remediation methods—such as excavation followed by off-site landfill disposal ("dig and dump") or ex-situ soil washing—are feasible for loose, highly permeable sandy soils, they become technically ineffective and economically prohibitive when applied to fine-grained, low- permeability clay soils and deep saturated silts.

B. Fine-grained clay soils possess extremely small pore diameters (often less than one to five micrometers) and microscopic hydraulic conductivities ($K < 10^{-7}\text{ cm/s}$), which prevent the physical circulation of washing fluids through the soil matrix under hydraulic pressure gradients. Furthermore, clay minerals (such as montmorillonite, kaolinite, and illite) possess high specific surface areas and net negative surface electrical charges (high cation exchange capacity, CEC), which strongly bind and immobilize positively charged heavy-metal cations onto mineral surfaces through electrostatic adsorption. To decontaminate these low- permeability soils in situ without destructive physical excavation, environmental engineers are advancing Electrokinetic Remediation (EKR).

C. Electrokinetic Remediation is an in-situ physicochemical soil decontamination technology that utilizes low-voltage direct electric current (DC) applied across an array of inert electrodes inserted directly into saturated contaminated ground. A typical EKR field installation consists of an array of anode (positive) and cathode (negative) electrodes fabricated from chemically inert, corrosion-resistant conductive materials (such as graphite, mixed-metal oxide-coated titanium, or conductive carbon fibers) placed into vertical electrode wells drilled into the contaminated soil zone. When a low-voltage direct current electric field is applied (typically 1 to 2 Volts per centimeter, with current densities of 5 to 20 Amperes per square meter), it mobilizes contaminants via three primary transport mechanisms.

D. The physical transport of water and chemical contaminants during EKR is governed by the coordinated coupling of three electrokinetic phenomena: 1. Electromigration (Ionic Migration): The directional movement of electrically charged ionic species dissolved in the soil pore water toward the oppositely charged electrode. Positively charged metal cations (such as $\text{Pb}^{2+}$, $\text{Cd}^{2+}$, $\text{Cu}^{2+}$, and $\text{Zn}^{2+}$) migrate electromigratively toward the negative cathode, while toxic oxyanions (such as hexavalent chromate, $\text{CrO}_4^{2-}$, and arsenate, $\text{AsO}_4^{3-}$) migrate toward the positive anode. 2. Electro-osmosis (Electro-osmotic Flow, EOF): The bulk convective movement of the soil pore water itself relative to the stationary, negatively charged clay mineral surface. Because the excess counter-ions in the electrical double layer (EDL) along clay surfaces are predominantly positive cations, the application of an electric field pulls the hydrated cations toward the cathode, dragging the surrounding water molecules along via viscous momentum transfer, thereby generating a continuous pore-water flush through low- permeability clay toward the cathode. 3. Electrophoresis: The minor movement of charged colloidal particles and organic soil micelles suspended in the pore fluid toward the opposing electrode.

E. A critical electrochemical dynamic that profoundly dictates EKR efficiency is water electrolysis occurring at the electrode surfaces. When direct electric current passes through the water-saturated soil, oxidation and reduction reactions split water molecules at the electrode interfaces: At the Anode (Oxidation): $$2\text{H}_2\text{O} \to \text{O}_2 \uparrow + 4\text{H}^+ + 4e^-$$ This reaction generates an acidic environment ($\text{pH} < 2$) by producing hydronium ions ($\text{H}^+$). At the Cathode (Reduction): $$2\text{H}_2\text{O} + 2e^- \to \text{H}_2 \uparrow + 2\text{OH}^-$$ This reaction generates an alkaline environment ($\text{pH} > 11$) by producing hydroxide ions ($\text{OH}^-$).

F. The generation of these opposing chemical species creates two advancing fronts that migrate through the soil toward each other: an acidic front moving from the anode to the cathode, and a basic front moving from the cathode to the anode. Because hydronium ions ($\text{H}^+$) possess an ionic mobility nearly two times higher than hydroxide ions ($\text{OH}^-$), the acidic front naturally dominates and sweeps across the majority of the soil bed. This acidic front is highly beneficial: low pH protonates clay mineral surfaces, desorbing bound heavy-metal cations and dissolving insoluble metal salts into free, mobile ions. However, when the migrating metal cations encounter the alkaline basic front advancing from the cathode, the high local pH triggers immediate metal precipitation as insoluble metal hydroxides (such as $\text{Pb(OH)}_2$ or $\text{Cd(OH)}_2$). This premature precipitation forms an impenetrable mineral "precipitation barrier" near the cathode, clogging soil pore spaces and terminating remediation.

G. To prevent premature hydroxide precipitation and maximize metal extraction, environmental engineers utilize "Enhanced Electrokinetic Remediation" (EEKR) through cathode electrolyte conditioning and chemical complexing agents. By circulating weak organic acids (such as acetic acid or citric acid) or synthetic chelating agents (such as ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid, EDTA) through the cathode well, engineers neutralize the alkaline front, keeping the pore water acidic ($\text{pH } 3\text{ to }5$) throughout the entire treatment zone. Furthermore, complexing agents transform heavy metals into stable, soluble anionic complexes (such as $\text{[Pb- EDTA]}^{2-}$), which reverse migration direction toward the anode for collection in specialized electrolyte treatment systems. As coupling with phytoremediation and solar-powered photovoltaic arrays matures, Electrokinetic Remediation provides a low-carbon, minimally invasive technology to clean up industrial brownfields and protect planetary soil ecosystems.'
);

