-- Passage 3: Scientific Perspectivism, Pluralism, and the Quest for Objectivity
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '00010eaf-4b58-55da-80c0-a4568d088a64', '0665cbb1-a3ef-5b2e-b859-c527ba6c6c6d', 3, 'Scientific Perspectivism, Pluralism, and the Quest for Objectivity',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the philosophy of science, theoretical physics, and analytical epistemology, the foundational quest has long focused on characterizing the relationship between human scientific theories and objective physical reality. In traditional Scientific Realism (championed by philosophers such as Richard Boyd and early Hilary Putnam), science is conceptualized as an objective, cumulative enterprise aimed at producing a single, uniquely true, and complete description of the cosmos: a "God’s-Eye View" of reality. Realists claim that our best mathematical models depict the universe as it exists mind-independently and unmediated by human observers. Conversely, Social Constructivism and Radical Relativism (tracing their lineage from Thomas Kuhn’s incommensurable paradigm shifts and Paul Feyerabend’s methodological anarchism) argue that scientific theories are merely subjective, culturally contingent social constructs that reflect historical power dynamics rather than objective truths about nature.

B. In the early 2000s, American philosopher of science Ronald Giere and contemporary Italian- British philosopher Michela Massimi pioneered an influential philosophical middle ground designed to transcend this sterile realism-relativism dichotomy: Scientific Perspectivism (also termed Perspectival Realism). Perspectivists advance a nuanced epistemological thesis: human scientific knowledge is inherently perspectival—inextricably shaped by our historical, technological, theoretical, and biological vantage points—yet it is genuinely objective and capable of achieving authentic truth about the physical world.

C. To illuminate the logic of scientific perspectivism, Ronald Giere, in his 2006 masterwork Scientific Perspectivism, presented an intuitive physical analogy: human color vision. Human color vision is trichromatic, relying on three distinct types of cone photoreceptors in the retina sensitive to short, medium, and long wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation. Other animal species perceive the exact same physical environment through radically different optical perspectives: honeybees possess ultraviolet photoreceptors, while birds possess tetrachromatic vision containing a fourth cone sensitive to near-ultraviolet light.

D. Giere pointed out that the existence of multiple optical perspectives does not mean that color perception is an arbitrary, subjective illusion: 1. Color vision is not a pure social construct; it is grounded in real, objective physical interactions between ambient electromagnetic reflectance spectra and biological sensory architecture. 2. No single biological perspective represents the uniquely privileged "true" optical reality; human trichromacy, avian tetrachromacy, and bee ultraviolet vision are all legitimate, objectively grounded perspectives on the same physical reality. Perspectivists argue that scientific instruments (such as optical telescopes, radio interferometers, mass spectrometers, and scanning tunneling microscopes) and mathematical theoretical models function in precisely the same way: as specialized scientific perspectives that access specific aspects of physical reality while remaining bounded by their operational limits.

E. In contemporary philosophy of physics, Michela Massimi has expanded perspectival realism in her 2022 treatise Perspectival Realism, addressing the widespread presence of "Incompatible Models" across mature sciences. In theoretical nuclear physics, for example, physicists describe the atomic nucleus using multiple radically incompatible mathematical models: The Liquid Drop Model: Treats the atomic nucleus as an incompressible, continuous fluid droplet governed by surface tension and electrostatic repulsion, perfectly predicting nuclear fission dynamics. The Nuclear Shell Model: Treats the exact same nucleus as an array of discrete, non- interacting quantum nucleons occupying quantized quantum energy levels, perfectly predicting nuclear spin and magnetic moments. Under naive scientific realism, because these two models contradict each other (a fluid cannot be a collection of independent particles in quantized orbits), at least one model must be strictly false. Under Perspectival Realism, however, both models are objectively true within their respective scientific perspectives, providing complementary, context- dependent insights into the multifaceted quantum architecture of the nucleus.

F. A crucial epistemological challenge confronting perspectivism is avoiding collapsing into vulgar relativism. If scientific claims are true only relative to a specific perspective, what prevents perspectivism from endorsing the claim that flat-Earth theories or astrological systems are equally "true from their own perspectives"? Massimi resolves this challenge by formulating the criterion of "Cross-Perspectival Assessment" and "Robustness."

G. Massimi demonstrates that authentic scientific perspectives are not self-contained, isolated bubbles; rather, they are historically open and subject to cross-perspectival evaluation across independent scientific communities and technological domains. A scientific claim qualifies as objectively true if and only if it exhibits cross-perspectival robustness—that is, if its empirical validity is retained and confirmed across multiple independent theoretical models, experimental measurement techniques, and observational platforms. Furthermore, a new scientific perspective must retain epistemic continuity with the established empirical successes of prior perspectives: Einsteinian General Relativity does not simply discard Newtonian mechanics as an arbitrary fiction; rather, it retains and explains Newtonian gravitational equations as a precise low-velocity limit within its expanded geometric framework.

H. In synthesizing modern philosophy of science, Scientific Perspectivism transforms our understanding of scientific progress. Objectivity is not achieved by attempting to step outside our human skins to view the cosmos from an impossible, detached "nowhere"; rather, objectivity is an active, ongoing social and collective achievement forged through the continuous dialogue, cross-checking, and integration of diverse scientific perspectives. By embracing both the situated nature of human reason and the objective richness of the physical universe, Perspectival Realism celebrates science as humanity’s magnificent, multi-perspectival window onto the boundless depths of nature.'
);

