-- Passage 1: The Architectural Mechanics and Engineering of Medieval Windmills
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'a3863dc7-7411-5605-880c-a5b51c609f38', '820ea1f4-fad4-5c31-a6af-da30f26bfbea', 1, 'The Architectural Mechanics and Engineering of Medieval Windmills',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the low-lying coastal plains and open landscapes of medieval northwestern Europe— particularly in eastern England, Flanders, the Netherlands, and northern France—the harnessing of kinetic wind energy represented a foundational revolution in mechanical engineering. While watermills had provided mechanical power across classical antiquity and the early Middle Ages, water-powered milling was strictly constrained by geography: mills required continuous, fast- flowing streams or reliable tidal estuaries. In flat, water-scarce arable plains or frozen winter landscapes, watermills were either unviable or subject to seasonal freezing. By the late twelfth century (circa 1180–1190 CE), medieval engineers developed the horizontal-axis post mill, creating the first mechanical power source capable of operating independently of flowing water networks.

B. The definitive operational challenge in windmill design is the unpredictable, shifting nature of wind direction (wind yaw). Unlike waterwheels, which receive fluid kinetic energy from a fixed, permanent directional stream, a windmill must continuously align its rotating sails perpendicular to shifting wind vectors to capture maximum aerodynamic power. To achieve this alignment, medieval carpenters developed the "Post Mill." In a post mill, the entire wooden millhouse—a multi-story timber box containing the rotating sails, drive shafts, heavy millstones, and grain hoppers—is balanced and suspended upon a single colossal vertical oak post (the main post or trestle post).

C. The structural base supporting this towering main post was an open timber framework known as the trestle or substructure. The main post rested upon two massive, crossed horizontal oak beams (cross-trees) laid out on low masonry foundations. Four diagonal timber struts (quarter-bars) angled upward from the ends of the cross-trees to brace the vertical post, forming a rigid pyramidal support frame. At the top of the main post, an iron pivot pin fitted into a horizontal beam called the crown tree, which formed the structural spine of the revolving millhouse. Extending outward from the rear of the millhouse was a long, heavy wooden lever: the tailpole. When the wind direction shifted, the miller walked along a circular dirt path around the mill, manually pushing the tailpole (often assisted by a portable wooden winch or a draft pony) to rotate the entire multi-ton millhouse around the central post into the eye of the wind.

D. The internal drivetrain of a medieval windmill was a triumph of heavy wooden mechanical engineering. Wind kinetic energy was captured by four large sails (typically measuring six to nine meters in length) constructed from lightweight wooden lattice frameworks covered with heavy linen canvas sailcloth. The sails rotated an immense, slightly inclined horizontal timber axle known as the windshaft, which was turned on a hard stone or bronze bearing. Mounted securely on the windshaft inside the upper story of the mill was the brake wheel—a massive wooden gear wheel measuring two to three meters in diameter, lined with dozens of hard cormier, hornbeam, or applewood teeth along its rim.

E. The brake wheel performed a dual mechanical function: 1. Braking Mechanism: The outer rim of the brake wheel was surrounded by a flexible band of curved elm or iron friction blocks. When the miller pulled an external brake rope, the friction band tightened around the wheel, bringing the multi-ton revolving sails to a controlled stop during dangerous high-wind gales. 2. Gear Transmission: The wooden teeth of the brake wheel meshed with a smaller vertical wooden pinion gear known as the wallower or lantern pinion. This right-angle gear transmission shifted the rotation from the horizontal windshaft to a vertical spindle, stepping up rotational speed. The vertical spindle directly drove the upper rotating millstone (runner stone) against the stationary lower millstone (bedstone), grinding grain kernels into fine flour at speeds exceeding sixty to eighty revolutions per minute.

F. In the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, European carpenters introduced the "Tower Mill" (and its timber variant, the "Smock Mill"), which fundamentally improved structural durability. In a tower mill, the main body of the mill was constructed as a permanent, stationary masonry tower built from stone or brick. Rather than revolving the entire multi-ton building to face shifting winds, only a small, lightweight wooden roof dome (the cap) was rotated. The rotating cap was mounted on a circular iron or bronze curb track fitted with grease-lubricated wooden rollers or iron wheels. This architectural innovation allowed mills to be built significantly taller and wider, housing multiple pairs of grinding stones, grain cleaning sieves, and flour dressers under a single roof.

G. The ultimate technological refinement of the traditional windmill occurred in 1745 with the invention of the automated fantail by English blacksmith and inventor Edmund Lee. The fantail was a small, multi-bladed secondary wooden wind rotor mounted on the rear of the rotating cap at a right angle to the main sails. When the main sails were facing directly into the wind, the fantail remained stationary. However, as soon as the wind shifted, the wind struck the blades of the fantail, causing it to spin rapidly. Through a reduction worm gear train connected to the circular curb track, the spinning fantail automatically geared the entire cap back into the wind without requiring any manual intervention by the miller. By combining aerodynamic canvas sails, resilient wooden gear trains, and self-regulating control systems, traditional windmills dominated European agricultural industry for six centuries, laying the foundational mechanical blueprint for modern industrial automation.'
);

