-- Passage 2: Superhydrophobic and Slippery Liquid-Infused Porous Surfaces (SLIPS)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'e4fd8ee3-6029-553a-a5a1-7c2b1e5acb68', '820ea1f4-fad4-5c31-a6af-da30f26bfbea', 2, 'Superhydrophobic and Slippery Liquid-Infused Porous Surfaces (SLIPS)',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout modern surface chemistry, materials science, and fluid engineering, the control of liquid wettability on solid surfaces represents a foundational physical domain. In industrial and commercial applications—spanning aerospace anti-icing, marine anti-fouling coatings, high- efficiency steam condensers, biomedical catheters, and oil transport pipelines—preventing liquids, ice, biological biofilms, and sticky crude oils from adhering to solid surfaces is critical. For decades, materials engineers sought to achieve ultimate non-wetting properties by mimicking the natural "Lotus effect"—the remarkable self-cleaning, water-repellent behavior exhibited by the leaves of the sacred lotus plant (Nelumbo nucifera).

B. The physics of lotus-leaf-inspired Superhydrophobic Surfaces (SHSs) relies on the synergistic coupling of low-surface-energy chemical coatings with micro- and nanoscale surface roughness. When a liquid droplet rests on a rough solid surface, it can adopt one of two distinct thermodynamic states: 1. The Wenzel State: The liquid droplet completely penetrates the microscopic grooves and asperities, wetting the entire textured surface area (homogeneous wetting). This state produces high contact angle hysteresis and strong liquid pinning. 2. The Cassie-Baxter State: The liquid droplet cannot penetrate the narrow microscopic crevices due to surface tension. Instead, the droplet rests atop a composite interface of solid peaks and trapped pockets of air (a microscopic air cushion or plastron). On a superhydrophobic surface in the Cassie-Baxter state, a water droplet forms an almost perfect sphere with an apparent contact angle ($\theta$) exceeding 150 degrees and a tiny roll-off sliding angle of less than 5 to 10 degrees. Water droplets roll off the surface effortlessly, carrying away dirt particles in a self-cleaning cascade.

C. However, despite their impressive laboratory performance, traditional superhydrophobic surfaces suffer from fundamental physical and thermodynamic vulnerabilities that severely constrain their real-world commercial viability. The primary failure mode of superhydrophobic surfaces is the catastrophic breakdown of the fragile air plastron: Pressure-Induced Plastron Collapse: When exposed to elevated hydraulic pressure (such as deep water immersion, high-speed rain impact, or turbulent pipe flow), the trapped air cushion is physically compressed and expelled, causing an irreversible transition from the slippery Cassie-Baxter state into the sticky, high-drag Wenzel state. Condensation and Frosting Failure: In humid environments or freezing temperatures, water vapor condenses directly inside the microscopic cavities beneath the droplet. When the condensed micro-droplets freeze, the ice interlocks mechanically with the rough surface textures, resulting in severe ice pinning and high ice-adhesion strength. Low Surface Tension Liquids: Superhydrophobic surfaces cannot repel low-surface- energy organic liquids (such as oils, alcohols, and liquid fuels); these fluids instantly wet and spread across the textured surface.

D. In 2011, materials scientist Joanna Aizenberg and her research team at Harvard University fundamentally revolutionized interface engineering by inventing Slippery Liquid-Infused Porous Surfaces (SLIPS). Rather than attempting to trap fragile pockets of air beneath a liquid droplet, SLIPS takes inspiration from an entirely different biological model: the carnivorous pitcher plant (Nepenthes). The slippery peristome collar of the Nepenthes pitcher plant traps a stable, microscopic lubricating film of water, creating an ultra-slippery liquid interface that causes foraging insects to lose traction and slide into the digestive pitcher below.

E. SLIPS mimics this biological mechanism by infusing a chemically inert, low-surface-energy lubricating liquid into a micro- or nano-porous solid substrate. A functional SLIPS architecture requires three fundamental thermodynamic criteria: 1. Substrate Retention: The lubricating liquid must completely wet, wick into, and adhere stably within the microscopic porous matrix (the solid must exhibit a higher affinity for the lubricant than for the ambient fluid). 2. Immiscibility: The lubricating liquid must be completely immiscible with (incapable of dissolving into) the target repelled liquids (such as water, crude oil, or blood). 3. Stable Overlayer Formation: The lubricant must form a smooth, continuous, and defect- free liquid overlayer that covers the rough solid asperities entirely. In typical synthetic SLIPS systems, the porous substrate (such as porous polytetrafluoroethylene, etched aluminum, or nano-structured silica) is infused with high- density, ultra-low-volatility perfluorinated polyether (PFPE) lubricants (e.g., Krytox oils).

F. Because the outer interface of a SLIPS coating is an atomically smooth, defect-free liquid lubricant rather than a rough solid with air pockets, it exhibits extraordinary non-wetting and self-healing properties that surpass traditional superhydrophobic materials. Because there is no air plastron to collapse, SLIPS maintains its liquid-repellent performance under extreme hydrostatic pressures exceeding several hundred atmospheres (equivalent to the deep ocean). Furthermore, SLIPS repels virtually any liquid—including low-surface-tension crude oils, organic solvents, biological fluids, and complex slurries—with sliding angles below 2 to 5 degrees. In anti-icing applications, ice formed atop a SLIPS surface has virtually zero mechanical interlocking, sliding off under gentle gravity or light breezes.

G. In biomedical, marine, and energy applications, SLIPS has demonstrated transformative potential. In medical devices, SLIPS coatings on surgical catheters and arterial blood lines prevent biological blood clotting (thrombosis) and completely suppress bacterial biofilm colonization (Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Staphylococcus aureus) without requiring antibiotic drugs. In marine transport, SLIPS bio-coatings prevent the attachment of barnacles and algae on ship hulls, reducing hydrodynamic skin-friction drag and cutting marine fuel consumption by up to twenty percent. As eco-friendly, non-toxic bio-lubricants and robust sprayable coatings mature, slippery liquid-infused surfaces are redefining materials science, providing a permanent, self-healing shield across global industrial infrastructures.'
);

