-- Passage 3: The Metaphysics of Free Choice: Agent Causation vs. Event-Causal Libertarianism
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '1e9bff7d-2ea3-5148-b534-ba10761bfd23', '820ea1f4-fad4-5c31-a6af-da30f26bfbea', 3, 'The Metaphysics of Free Choice: Agent Causation vs. Event-Causal Libertarianism',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In analytical metaphysics, action theory, and moral philosophy, no inquiry is as central to human agency and moral responsibility as the problem of Free Will. For centuries, the debate has been defined by the tension between Causal Determinism and Libertarian Free Will. Causal determinism is the metaphysical thesis that every physical event—including every human thought, intention, choice, and physical action—is completely necessitated by prior historical events combined with the immutable laws of nature. In a deterministic universe, given the exact state of the cosmos at the Big Bang, only one unique future is physically possible. In response, Libertarians reject determinism, asserting that human beings possess genuine free will and moral responsibility, and that free choice strictly requires the existence of alternative possibilities (the "Principle of Alternative Possibilities," PAP).

B. However, simply introducing physical indeterminism—such as the objective, probabilistic wave-function collapses of quantum mechanics—does not automatically rescue free will. Metaphysicians across the philosophical spectrum point out the formidable "Luck Objection" (or the Mind Argument): if an agent’s choice between Option A (acting bravely) and Option B (acting cowardly) is not causally determined by their prior mental states, but occurs due to an uncaused, indeterministic quantum fluctuation in their brain, then the outcome is a matter of sheer, un-governed random chance. If a choice occurs by random fluke, the agent has no control over it, and cannot be held morally praiseworthy or blameworthy for the outcome. Indeterminism without agency merely substitutes random chaos for causal necessity.

C. To resolve the Luck Objection and provide an authentic account of autonomous human freedom under indeterminism, libertarian philosophers have bifurcated into two primary metaphysical traditions: Event-Causal Libertarianism and Agent-Causal Libertarianism.

D. Event-Causal Libertarianism—most famously formulated and defended by American philosopher Robert Kane in his landmark 1996 treatise The Significance of Free Will—attempts to explain free agency strictly within the naturalistic ontological framework of events, physical processes, and neural states. Kane argues that genuine human freedom does not require every daily action to be indeterministic; rather, it is anchored in specific, pivotal moments of deep moral or prudential conflict that Kane terms "Self-Forming Actions" (SFAs).

E. A Self-Forming Action occurs when an agent experiences a profound inner conflict between competing motives: for instance, a businesswoman deciding between stopping to aid an assault victim or rushing to an important career-defining corporate meeting. In Kane’s model, this intense psychological conflict generates cognitive dissonance, which stirs up chaotic, non-linear thermodynamic and quantum neural fluctuations in the brain. The agent simultaneously exerts conscious effort in two competing directions (striving to be moral while striving to pursue self- interest).

F. When one effort succeeds and the choice is made (e.g., choosing to help the victim), the outcome is indeterministic (it was not causally guaranteed in advance), yet the agent exercises "dual rational control": whichever choice occurs, it was an outcome the agent was actively trying to achieve, endorsed by their reasons, and for which they take full ownership. Through hundreds of SFAs over a lifetime, an agent constructs their own enduring moral character (volitional character), so that later actions that flow deterministically from that established character remain fully autonomous.

G. In stark opposition to event-causal models stands Agent-Causal Libertarianism, championed by metaphysicians such as Timothy O''Connor, Randolph Clarke, and Roderick Chisholm. Agent-causalists argue that event-causal models fail to solve the Luck Objection: in Kane’s model, at the exact moment of choice, nothing about the agent causally determines whether Effort A or Effort B succeeds; the final resolution remains a matter of luck. Agent-causalists assert that to achieve genuine ultimate control, we must recognize a fundamentally distinct, irreducible category of causation: Substance Causation (or Agent Causation).

H. In standard physical science, all causation is "event causation": an event (a change in state at time $t_1$) causes another event (a change in state at time $t_2$). In Agent Causation, by contrast, the causal initiator is not a prior event, neural state, or desire; the cause is the agent itself—an enduring, irreducible substance possessing primitive, active causal powers. An agent can initiate a new causal chain in the physical universe ex nihilo without being causally determined by prior mental events. While reasons and desires provide the evaluative context or motivation for action, the agent possesses the primitive power to determine their own choice among competing alternatives. [I] In synthesizing the contemporary metaphysics of human freedom, both Event-Causal and Agent-Causal models illuminate the profound complexity of conscious human agency. While event-causal libertarianism preserves naturalistic continuity with physical cognitive neuroscience at the risk of luck vulnerabilities, agent-causal models preserve our deepest introspective convictions of personal authorship at the cost of postulating non-standard metaphysical causal categories. Whether human freedom is ultimately deciphered as an emergent property of non- linear neuro-computational dynamics or as a fundamental feature of conscious substantial agents, the philosophical quest to understand free choice reaffirms that human beings are not passive mechanical automatons, but active, responsible authors of their own lives and destinies.'
);

