-- Passage 1: The Domestication and Dispersal of the Domestic Donkey (Equus asinus)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'c0dd7b46-4130-5236-ba21-59011ed27eeb', '16de1650-1b35-5a4f-91e8-8c8fbe525c1a', 1, 'The Domestication and Dispersal of the Domestic Donkey (Equus asinus)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the arid and semi-arid landscapes of Africa, the Near East, and the Mediterranean basin, few domestic quadrupeds have exerted as transformative an influence on human trade, pastoral mobility, and agrarian infrastructure as the domestic donkey (Equus asinus). Historically overshadowed in romantic literature by the horse (Equus caballus), the donkey was in fact domesticated millennia before equids were ridden into cavalry warfare. As an extraordinarily robust, sure-footed, and fuel-efficient beast of burden, the donkey made the trans-desert transportation of bulk agricultural commodities, mineral ores, and water across hyper-arid terrains economically viable for the first time in human history. Today, global donkey populations exceed fifty million head, serving as an indispensable working lifeline for rural smallholder communities in developing nations.

B. The evolutionary ancestry, geographic cradle, and archaeological chronology of donkey domestication have been resolved through comprehensive ancient mitochondrial DNA (aDNA) sequencing and archaeological bone assemblages. Geneticists and zooarchaeologists have established that the domestic donkey descends directly from the African wild ass (Equus africanus), a critically endangered desert equid comprising two distinct historical subspecies: 1. The Nubian Wild Ass (Equus africanus africanus): Historically native to the arid desert plateaus of northeastern Sudan, southern Egypt, and the Red Sea hills. 2. The Somali Wild Ass (Equus africanus somaliensis): Characterized by distinctive horizontal black leg stripes, native to the volcanic deserts of the Horn of Africa (Eritrea, Ethiopia, and Somalia). Genomic coalescence analyses show that donkey domestication occurred approximately 5000 to 4500 BCE (the late fifth to early fourth millennium BCE) in Northeast Africa, coinciding with the rapid desertification of the Sahara, which forced nomadic pastoralist communities to adapt to increasing aridity.

C. The physical capacity of Equus asinus to survive in extreme desert environments is anchored in an extraordinary suite of physiological and behavioral adaptations. Originating in the hyper- arid deserts of Northeast Africa, the donkey possesses a digestive physiology based on hindgut fermentation. The donkey’s large cecum and colon ferment fibrous, low-protein woody shrubs and desert grasses with exceptional efficiency. Unlike horses or cattle, a donkey can survive on coarse, high-cellulose forage that would cause severe impaction colic or starvation in other ungulates. Furthermore, donkeys exhibit remarkable water-conservation mechanics: a donkey can tolerate dehydration losses of up to thirty percent of its total body weight without circulatory collapse, and can rehydrate completely within minutes by drinking up to thirty liters of water without suffering osmotic hemolysis.

D. In addition to digestive and hydrologic efficiency, the donkey possesses distinct anatomical adaptations for heat dissipation and rough terrain navigation. The donkey’s large, vascularized ears (pinnae) act as highly effective thermal radiators: warm blood circulating through dense networks of superficial capillary blood vessels is cooled by convection, dissipating core metabolic body heat without wasting precious water through sweating. Furthermore, unlike the wide, cupped hooves of horses adapted to soft grasslands, donkey hooves are upright, deep- cupped, and highly elastic. This upright hoof geometry provides supreme grip and mechanical traction across loose gravel, jagged scree slopes, and narrow mountain paths, making the animal virtually immune to slipping.

E. In the ancient civilizations of the Nile Valley, the Levant, and Mesopotamia, the domesticated donkey transformed regional economies during the fourth and third millennia BCE. During the Early Dynastic Period in Egypt (circa 3100–2686 BCE) and the Early Bronze Age in the Levant, donkeys were the premier pack animals of long-distance trade caravans. Caravans consisting of hundreds of pack donkeys transported Egyptian gold, Nubian ebony, Sinai turquoise, and Levantine wine and olive oil across hundreds of kilometers of waterless desert tracks. In elite royal contexts, donkeys were celebrated with high ritual prestige: excavations at the Royal Cemetery of Abydos in Egypt uncovered ten intact donkey skeletons ceremonially interred in brick-lined mortuary tombs adjacent to the burial complex of an early pharaoh, with osteological analyses revealing healed vertebral lesions consistent with long careers carrying heavy pack loads.

F. Following its establishment in the Near East, the domestic donkey dispersed westward across the Mediterranean basin and eastward along the overland Silk Road into Central Asia and China. During the Roman Empire, the donkey was vital to agricultural milling and municipal logistics. Roman agronomists crossbred large female donkeys (jennies) with male horses (stallions) to produce hinnies, and bred male donkeys (jacks) with female horses (mares) to produce mules. Mules combined the heavy muscular power and size of the horse with the physiological endurance, heat tolerance, and sure-footedness of the donkey, becoming the premier draft and pack animal of the Roman military legions and transport infrastructure.

G. In the contemporary era, the donkey remains a cornerstone of rural agrarian economies across the Global South, while facing modern market and welfare pressures. In sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia, and Latin America, millions of smallholder farming families rely on donkeys for domestic water hauling, firewood transport, and plowing marginal soils. However, the global donkey population is currently threatened by an unprecedented commercial crisis: the industrial harvesting of donkey hides to fuel the international trade in ejiao, a traditional gelatin product used in Chinese cosmetics and pharmaceuticals. In response, international animal welfare agencies and African governments are enacting export bans and establishing breeding conservation protocols, striving to safeguard a resilient working animal that has anchored human civilization across six millennia.'
);

