-- Passage 2: Solid-State Sodium-Ion Batteries (SSIBs) for Grid-Scale Energy Storage
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '7b339880-e957-5f9b-a4c1-b9ccd33fec6e', '16de1650-1b35-5a4f-91e8-8c8fbe525c1a', 2, 'Solid-State Sodium-Ion Batteries (SSIBs) for Grid-Scale Energy Storage',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. As the global transition toward renewable energy accelerates, the massive expansion of intermittent solar photovoltaic arrays and wind turbines has created an urgent demand for utility- scale stationary energy storage. To balance grid frequencies, store surplus midday generation, and supply electrical power during nocturnal peak hours, electrical utilities require battery systems that deliver high round-trip energetic efficiency, multi-thousand-cycle durability, and supreme operational safety. For over three decades, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) utilizing liquid organic carbonate electrolytes have dominated consumer electronics and electric mobility. However, scaling conventional liquid-electrolyte LIBs to terawatt-hour grid installations confronts formidable geopolitical, economic, and safety barriers: global lithium, cobalt, and nickel reserves are geographically concentrated in politically volatile regions, raw material prices fluctuate wildly, and flammable liquid electrolytes are vulnerable to catastrophic thermal runaway fires and explosions.

B. To bypass lithium supply chain bottlenecks while eliminating fire risks entirely, electrochemists and materials scientists are developing Solid-State Sodium-Ion Batteries (SSIBs). Sodium ($\text{Na}$) is one of the most abundant elements in the Earth''s crust (constituting roughly 2.6 to 2.8 percent of crustal mass), available globally at negligible cost from terrestrial rock salts and ocean seawater. Furthermore, replacing flammable liquid electrolytes with non-flammable inorganic solid electrolytes completely eliminates the risk of catastrophic fires, while allowing batteries to operate across wider temperature ranges without auxiliary thermal cooling systems.

C. The foundational technological component governing SSIB performance is the solid electrolyte (SE). A functional solid electrolyte must possess high room-temperature ionic conductivity ($\sigma_{\text{Na}^+} > 10^{-4}\text{ to }10^{-3}\text{ S/cm}$), negligible electronic conductivity (preventing internal short-circuits), and a wide electrochemical stability window. Solid sodium electrolytes are broadly categorized into three primary materials families: 1. NASICON-Type Ceramic Electrolytes: Sodium Super Ionic Conductors ($\text{Na}_{1+x}\text{Zr}_2\text{Si}_x\text{P}_{3-x}\text{O}_{12}$), pioneered by Goodenough and Hong in the late 1970s. NASICON features a rigid 3D framework of corner-sharing $\text{ZrO}_6$ octahedra and $\text{(P/Si)O}_4$ tetrahedra containing interconnected 3D diffusion pathways that allow $\text{Na}^+$ ions to hop rapidly through the crystal lattice. 2. Beta-Alumina Ceramics ($\beta''''\text{-alumina}$): Layered sodium-aluminate ceramics ($\text{Na}_{1+x}\text{Al}_{11}\text{O}_{17+x/2}$) featuring high-conductivity 2D conduction planes, widely used in high-temperature sodium-sulfur grid systems. 3. Sulfide-Based Solid Electrolytes: Compounds such as $\text{Na}_3\text{PS}_4$ and $\text{Na}_{11}\text{Sn}_2\text{PS}_{12}$, which exhibit high ionic conductivities due to the high polarizability of large sulfur anions, although they are sensitive to moisture.

D. The primary electrochemical challenge in designing all-solid-state sodium batteries occurs at the solid-solid interfacial boundaries: the physical interface between the solid electrolyte and the solid electrodes. Unlike liquid electrolytes that naturally wet every microscopic crevice of a porous electrode, rigid solid-solid contact generates high interfacial impedance (contact resistance). During repetitive charge and discharge cycles, electrode materials undergo substantial volumetric expansion and contraction (breathing). Over hundreds of cycles, this cyclic mechanical strain causes microscopic delamination and void formation at the interface, disrupting $\text{Na}^+$ ion transport pathways and accelerating capacity decay.

E. Furthermore, when using metallic sodium anodes to maximize energy density, solid-state batteries are vulnerable to localized sodium dendrite penetration. While early theorists assumed that rigid ceramic solid electrolytes would mechanically block dendrite growth (according to the Monroe-Newman criterion, which states that an electrolyte with a shear modulus twice that of the metal will prevent dendrites), experiments revealed that sodium dendrites can propagate through microscopic grain boundaries and surface flaws in ceramic pellets. Localized electric field concentrations at grain boundary voids drive electrodeposition of metallic sodium filaments inside the solid electrolyte, eventually short-circuiting the cell.

F. To eliminate interfacial resistance and suppress dendrite propagation, materials engineers deploy advanced interfacial engineering strategies. Applying ultra-thin functional interlayers— such as atomic layer deposition (ALD) coatings of aluminum oxide ($\text{Al}_2\text{O}_3$), tin ($\text{Sn}$), or fluorinated polymer buffers—promotes uniform interfacial wetting and forms a robust, self-healing solid electrolyte interphase (SEI). Concurrently, cathode researchers are formulating earth-abundant, cobalt-free cathode active materials: Prussian Blue Analogs (PBAs): Open-framework coordination polymers ($\text{Na}_{2- x}\text{Fe}[\text{Fe(CN)}_6]$) that accommodate large $\text{Na}^+$ ions without significant lattice distortion. Layered Transition Metal Oxides: Earth-abundant $\text{O3}$- and $\text{P2}$-type layered oxides (such as $\text{Na}_x\text{Fe}_{0.5}\text{Mn}_{0.5}\text{O}_2$) utilizing low-cost iron and manganese redox couples.

G. In grid-scale economics, all-solid-state sodium-ion batteries provide compelling advantages in levelized cost of storage (LCOS) and lifecycle sustainability. While the gravimetric energy density of SSIBs (typically 150 to 200 Watt-hours per kilogram, $\text{Wh/kg}$) is lower than premium nickel-rich lithium batteries, energy density is not the primary metric for stationary utility storage, where physical footprint and mass are secondary to capital expenditure per kilowatt-hour, cycle life, and thermal safety. As automated roll-to-roll dry-film manufacturing and high-temperature sintering processes continue to scale, solid-state sodium-ion batteries are poised to replace fossil-fuel peaker plants, establishing a safe, sustainable foundation for the global clean energy grid.'
);

