-- Passage 1: The Ancient Minoan Water Management and Drainage Engineering of Knossos
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '1d615ee1-cf52-5d9e-9e48-bc21908278af', 'defb3db9-4652-57c2-9643-10cccb5aaa88', 1, 'The Ancient Minoan Water Management and Drainage Engineering of Knossos',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Situated on the fertile Kephala hill near the northern coast of the Mediterranean island of Crete, the monumental Bronze Age Palace of Knossos stands as an extraordinary pinnacle of ancient architectural sophistication and civil engineering. Constructed by the Minoan civilization during the Protopalatial and Neopalatial periods (circa 1900 to 1450 BCE), this sprawling multi- story architectural complex encompassed over twenty thousand square meters, housing ceremonial halls, royal residential apartments, administrative offices, workshops, and communal plazas. Beyond its architectural grandeur and vibrant frescoes, the defining technological triumph of Knossos was its integrated, multi-tiered hydraulic and drainage infrastructure—a network of pressurized terracotta water-supply pipes, stone-paved storm-water drainage channels, and sanitary flush latrines that operated millennia before comparable systems appeared in classical Greece or Rome.

B. The hydrogeological sustainability of Knossos was anchored in a sophisticated municipal water-supply system designed to provide continuous potable water to an estimated palace and urban populace exceeding twenty to thirty thousand individuals. While the palace had access to localized limestone aquifers tapped by shallow wells, Minoan engineers sought a cleaner, pressurized water source. They constructed gravity-fed aqueducts conveying pristine mountain spring water from high-elevation natural karstic springs at Mavrokolybos and Mount Juktas, located several kilometers to the south.

C. To transport this water across undulating terrain without contamination, Minoan civil engineers developed advanced, standardized terracotta water pipes (tubuli or terracotta conduits). Measuring fifty to seventy-five centimeters in length and eight to twelve centimeters in internal diameter, these pipes were shaped into a distinct parabolic profile: each individual pipe tapered from a wide mouth down to a narrow spigot nozzle. When joined together, the spigot of one pipe seated snugly into the socket mouth of the next, sealed with a waterproof limestone-and-clay mortar. The tapered, parabolic geometry was an extraordinary hydraulic design: 1. Kinetic Acceleration: As water flowed from the wide pipe body into the narrow constriction nozzle, the fluid accelerated (the Venturi effect), increasing flow velocity and preventing the sedimentation of suspended mineral particulates. 2. Vortex Scouring: The high-velocity jet induced self-cleaning turbulence that prevented interior biofouling and calcium scale accumulation. 3. Hydrostatic Integrity: The tapered design maximized joint strength, allowing the closed pipeline to withstand considerable positive hydrostatic bursting pressures as it crossed terrain depressions under inverted siphon action.

D. In addition to pressurized potable water delivery, the monumental architecture of Knossos required vast flood-mitigation and storm-water management engineering. In the subtropical Mediterranean climate of Crete, intense winter downpours and monsoonal deluges routinely dump colossal volumes of rainfall onto the landscape. Because the Palace of Knossos was built on a terraced hillside with extensive flat roofs, paved open-air lightwells, and expansive central courtyards, uncontrolled runoff would have rapidly saturated the unreinforced rubble-and-mortar masonry, triggering catastrophic structural collapses and foundation erosion.

E. To mitigate flood hazards, Minoan architects integrated an expansive subterranean drainage network constructed from dressed limestone masonry blocks lined with smooth, water- impermeable hydraulic plaster. Vertical stone-lined rainwater chutes embedded within the palace walls collected roof runoff and channeled it down to ground-level drainage gutters. These open gutters featured ingenious parabolic stepped sediment traps: series of small, curved stone baffles and settling basins designed to decelerate rushing runoff, dissipate destructive kinetic energy, and trap suspended grit and debris before the water entered the main subterranean drains.

F. The main subterranean sewer conduit (the Great Cloaca) was a monumental masonry- vaulted tunnel measuring nearly one meter in width and up to two meters in height, allowing maintenance laborers to walk through the subterranean passage to clear blockages. This central artery ran diagonally beneath the entire palace footprint, draining runoff safely eastward into the adjacent Vlychia stream and Kairatos River. Furthermore, the continuous flow of captured rainwater served a vital sanitary function: it flushed the palace’s sophisticated waste disposal infrastructure.

G. Foremost among these sanitary facilities was the royal residential flush latrine located in the Queen’s Megaron (the Domestic Quarter). The latrine featured a raised wooden or plaster seating bench constructed directly over a stone-lined drainage channel connected to an exterior vertical rainwater reservoir. After use, occupants poured water from terracotta jugs into the basin, or opened a stone sluice gate to release collected roof runoff, flushing human waste instantly through a sloping siphon trap into the main subterranean sewer. Following the fiery destruction of Knossos in the mid-fifteenth century BCE and the subsequent collapse of Minoan civilization, this extraordinary level of sanitary hydro-technology vanished from European architecture for over three millennia, surviving as an archaeological monument to the technical brilliance of Europe’s first advanced civilization.'
);

