-- Passage 1: The Traditional Craft and Metallurgy of Japanese Mokume-Gane
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '388a2f60-c164-5211-9507-e4aa096726e0', '8b2cfb44-2b97-53ce-87da-e78335983d61', 1, 'The Traditional Craft and Metallurgy of Japanese Mokume-Gane',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the rich history of Japanese decorative metalworking (kinkō), few pyrotechnological techniques have achieved the aesthetic subtlety, metallurgical complexity, and visual elegance of mokume-gane (literally "wood-grain metal"). Originating in early seventeenth-century feudal Japan during the transition into the peaceful Edo period (1603–1867), mokume-gane was invented by master metalsmith Denbei Shoami (1651–1728) in Akita Prefecture. Developed initially to adorn the opulent sword fittings—most notably the handguards (tsuba), pommels (kashira), and scabbard fittings (kozuka)—of samurai katana swords, mokume-gane transforms rigid non-ferrous metals into fluid, organic patterns resembling natural wood grain, swirling water ripples, or topography lines.

B. The foundational metallurgical triumph of mokume-gane is the process of solid-state diffusion bonding. Unlike conventional jewelry fabrication and brazing techniques, which rely on low-melting-temperature metallic solders (such as silver solder) to glue metal sheets together, authentic mokume-gane completely excludes solder. The inclusion of solder creates hard, brittle seams that crack during subsequent heavy forging, while solder lines resist chemical patination, creating unsightly bright streaks across the finished artwork. Instead, traditional masters stack dozens of alternating, microscopically cleaned sheets of distinct non-ferrous metals and alloys— typically numbering between ten and fifty layers—clamped securely between heavy forged iron pressure plates.

C. To create striking visual contrast, traditional Japanese smiths utilized an exquisite palette of pure metals and specialized native non-ferrous alloys: 1. Pure Copper (Akagane): Provides deep reddish-brown tones. 2. Pure Silver (Shirogane): Imparts brilliant, reflective white highlights. 3. Shakudō: A prized, proprietary Japanese alloy composed of ninety-six to ninety-nine percent copper and one to four percent pure gold. Upon final chemical patination, shakudō transforms from a dull bronze color into a lustrous, velvety raven-black or deep purplish-black sheen. 4. Shibuichi: An alloy composed of copper and silver, formulated in varying ratios (traditionally one part silver to three or four parts copper). When patinated, shibuichi yields an array of subtle, muted silver-gray, greenish-gray, and olive-brown hues. 5. Kuromido: An alloy of copper and arsenic that produces deep charcoal-gray tones.

D. The assembled, clamped billet is placed into a charcoal-fueled kiln or furnace and heated with extreme thermal precision. The heating must bring the metal stack to a narrow, critical temperature window: roughly fifty to one hundred degrees Celsius below the solidus melting point of the lowest-melting alloy in the stack (typically between 750°C and 850°C). At this elevated temperature, atomic thermal vibrations increase, and metallic atoms begin to migrate and inter-diffuse across the microscopic interfacial boundaries between adjacent sheets. The application of high mechanical clamping pressure forces the metallic lattices into intimate atomic contact, causing the crystalline grain structures of the dissimilar metals to interlock and merge, creating a solid, homogeneous laminated ingot (billet) without a single drop of liquid melt.

E. Once the laminated billet has successfully fused and cooled, the artisan transforms the flat, parallel laminate layers into organic three-dimensional surface patterns through alternating cycles of plastic deformation and material removal. The smith uses hardened steel chisels, gouges, drills, and punch dies to carve deep conical cavities, cross-hatched troughs, or embossed bumps through the top layers, exposing the contrasting colored alloys beneath. The excavated billet is then repeatedly annealed (heated and quenched to relieve work hardening) and hot- forged flat using heavy hand hammers on an anvil. As the carved cavities are compressed flat, the horizontal metal strata deform and emerge on the surface as undulating, concentric wood- grain contours.

F. The final, transformative phase of mokume-gane manufacturing is chemical patination using niage—a traditional Japanese chemical boiling bath. Before patination, the metal surface is meticulously polished with charcoal blocks, wiped with baking soda, and abraded with pulverized daikon radish paste to remove all traces of surface grease and oxidation. The polished object is then submerged into an active boiling aqueous solution of rokushō (a traditional green mineral patinating compound composed of basic copper carbonate, copper sulfate, and potassium alum) mixed with pure mountain spring water and plum vinegar.

G. Inside the boiling rokushō bath, complex, controlled electrochemical oxidation reactions occur across the dissimilar metals. Because each metal in the composite possesses a different electrochemical oxidation potential, the acidic mineral salts selectively oxidize the surface atoms of the copper-rich alloys, growing micro-thin, chemically stable interference oxide films on each distinct layer: shakudō turns velvety jet-black, shibuichi shifts to misty greenish-gray, pure copper darkens to deep chestnut-red, while pure silver remains completely unaffected and shines as luminous white bands. Following the Meiji Restoration (1868) and the abolition of the samurai class (Haitōrei edict banning sword carrying), master metalworkers adapted mokume- gane to luxury art vessels, ceremonial jewelry, and wedding rings. Today, master artisans preserve this ancient metallurgical synthesis of fire, pressure, and chemistry, producing heirlooms of living Japanese heritage.'
);

