-- Passage 2: Thermoelectric Nanomaterials for Waste Heat Energy Harvesting
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'ae65e8c4-d1df-5671-a55b-6376d1e01aba', '8b2cfb44-2b97-53ce-87da-e78335983d61', 2, 'Thermoelectric Nanomaterials for Waste Heat Energy Harvesting',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout modern global industrial, transportation, and power generation systems, massive quantities of primary energy are lost as uncaptured waste heat. More than sixty percent of all fossil energy combusted worldwide—in internal combustion vehicle exhaust pipes, industrial manufacturing kilns, thermal power plants, and computer server racks—is dissipated directly into the atmosphere as low-grade and mid-grade thermal waste. If even a minor fraction of this colossal thermal waste could be converted directly into usable electrical electricity, global carbon emissions would decline significantly while overall energy efficiency soared. To achieve direct, solid-state thermal-to-electrical energy conversion without moving mechanical parts or greenhouse gas emissions, applied physicists and materials scientists are developing high- efficiency Thermoelectric Nanomaterials.

B. Thermoelectric energy conversion is governed by the Seebeck Effect, discovered in 1821 by German-Estonian physicist Thomas Johann Seebeck. When a temperature gradient ($\Delta T = T_{\text{hot}} - T_{\text{cold}}$) is applied across a solid conducting or semiconducting material, mobile charge carriers (electrons in n-type semiconductors, or positively charged holes in p-type semiconductors) at the hot junction gain kinetic energy and diffuse toward the cold junction. This spatial thermal diffusion of charge carriers establishes an electrostatic potential difference ($\Delta V$), generating an electrical voltage directly proportional to the temperature differential: $$\Delta V = -S \cdot \Delta T$$ where $S$ is the material''s Seebeck coefficient (or thermopower, measured in microvolts per Kelvin, $\mu\text{V/K}$).

C. The physical efficiency of a thermoelectric material is evaluated by the dimensionless Thermoelectric Figure of Merit ($ZT$): $$ZT = \frac{S^2 \sigma T}{\kappa}$$ where $S$ is the Seebeck coefficient, $\sigma$ is electrical conductivity, $T$ is absolute temperature, and $\kappa$ is total thermal conductivity. Total thermal conductivity is the sum of electronic thermal conductivity ($\kappa_e$) and lattice phonon thermal conductivity ($\kappa_l$): $$\kappa = \kappa_e + \kappa_l$$ To achieve a high $ZT$ ($ZT > 2.0\text{ to }3.0$), an ideal thermoelectric material must simultaneously possess a high Seebeck coefficient ($S$), high electrical conductivity ($\sigma$), and ultralow thermal conductivity ($\kappa$)—a challenging physical paradox famously formalized by physicist Glen Slack as the "Phonon-Glass Electron-Crystal" (PGEC) paradigm. The material must conduct electrical charge carriers effortlessly like a pristine single crystal, while scattering heat-carrying lattice sound waves (phonons) like a disordered glass.

D. For over half a century, achieving high $ZT$ in bulk materials was constrained by the strict, adverse quantum mechanical and thermodynamic trade-offs between $S$, $\sigma$, and $\kappa_e$. According to the Wiedemann-Franz Law, increasing electrical conductivity ($\sigma$) inherently increases electronic thermal conductivity ($\kappa_e$), causing heat to flow rapidly across the device and collapsing the temperature gradient. Furthermore, according to the Pisarenko relation, increasing charge carrier density ($n$) boosts electrical conductivity but causes a sharp decline in the Seebeck coefficient ($S$). Consequently, bulk commercial thermoelectric alloys—such as bulk bismuth telluride ($\text{Bi}_2\text{Te}_3$) and silicon- germanium ($\text{SiGe}$)—remained permanently trapped at modest $ZT$ values around $0.8\text{ to }1.0$, limiting thermal conversion efficiencies to a modest five to seven percent.

E. In the early 1990s, theoretical physicists Mildred Dresselhaus and Lyndon Hicks fundamentally broke this bulk efficiency bottleneck by demonstrating that low-dimensional nanostructuring can decouple these interrelated transport properties. Nanostructuring enhances thermoelectric performance through two distinct quantum mechanical mechanisms: 1. Quantum Confinement Effects: In low-dimensional nanomaterials—such as two- dimensional (2D) quantum wells, one-dimensional (1D) nanowires, and zero-dimensional (0D) quantum dots—the electronic density of states (DOS) develops sharp, step-like peaks near the Fermi energy level. This sharp electronic asymmetry dramatically enhances the Seebeck coefficient ($S$) without requiring a destructive reduction in carrier concentration. 2. Preferential Phonon Scattering: In nanostructured materials containing high densities of grain boundaries, nanoscale precipitate inclusions, and superlattice interfaces, heat- carrying phonons are scattered aggressively. Because the mean free path of heat-carrying phonons (typically 10 to 100 nm) is significantly longer than the mean free path of electrical electrons (typically 1 to 5 nm), nanoscale interfaces scatter phonons intensely without disrupting the passage of electrical charge carriers, selectively suppressing lattice thermal conductivity ($\kappa_l$) by up to eighty percent.

F. Modern high-performance nanostructured thermoelectric materials achieve record-breaking $ZT$ values exceeding $2.2\text{ to }2.6$ across various operating temperatures: Low-Temperature Regimes ($<200^\circ\text{C}$): Nanostructured bismuth antimony telluride alloys ($(\text{Bi, Sb})_2\text{Te}_3$) synthesized via mechanical ball-milling and spark plasma sintering (SPS) exhibit $ZT \approx 1.4\text{ to }1.5$, ideal for body- heat-powered medical sensors. Mid-Temperature Regimes ($300^\circ\text{C to }600^\circ\text{C}$): Nanocrystalline lead telluride ($\text{PbTe}$) doped with sodium and engineered with endotaxial strontium telluride ($\text{SrTe}$) nanoscale precipitates achieves $ZT > 2.2$ at 650°C, recovering exhaust heat from heavy-duty diesel engines and industrial furnaces. High-Temperature Regimes ($>700^\circ\text{C}$): Silicon-germanium ($\text{SiGe}$) nanomeshes and half-Heusler alloys provide robust waste-heat harvesting in aerospace systems and radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTGs) aboard deep-space exploration probes like NASA''s Voyager and Perseverance rover.

G. Despite these remarkable scientific achievements, commercial deployment of thermoelectric nanomaterials faces critical manufacturing and integration challenges. Sintering large-scale nanostructured pellets without inducing high-temperature grain growth remains technically demanding. Furthermore, developing stable, low-resistance metallic diffusion barrier brazes that withstand continuous high-temperature cyclic thermal expansion without cracking is critical. As automated spark plasma sintering, conductive polymer composites, and additive manufacturing (3D printing) of thermoelectric inks mature, solid-state thermoelectric energy harvesting is poised to capture the vast rivers of global waste heat, playing a vital role in the clean energy transition.'
);

