-- Passage 3: The Self-Locating Belief Problem: Sleeping Beauty and Anthropics
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'c07e84ef-8716-5da3-9f00-d07bac16895f', '8b2cfb44-2b97-53ce-87da-e78335983d61', 3, 'The Self-Locating Belief Problem: Sleeping Beauty and Anthropics',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In formal epistemology, Bayesian confirmation theory, decision theory, and the philosophy of probability, few conceptual puzzles have generated as intense and polarized an intellectual debate as the "Sleeping Beauty Problem." First articulated in modern decision theory by British logician Adam Elga in his landmark 2000 paper Self-Locating Belief and the Sleeping Beauty Problem (adapting an earlier concept by Arnold Zuboff), the puzzle exposes a profound, unresolved crisis in probability theory: how should rational agents update their degrees of belief (credences) upon receiving centered, "self-locating" evidence regarding their own temporal or spatial location in the cosmos?

B. The experimental protocol of the Sleeping Beauty problem is structured with rigorous simplicity: 1. On Sunday evening, Sleeping Beauty is informed of the complete experimental design and put to sleep with a potent drug. 2. The experimenters toss a fair, unbiased coin: Heads ($H$) or Tails ($T$). 3. If the coin lands Heads: Beauty is briefly awakened on Monday morning, interviewed, and put back to sleep with a memory-erasing amnesic drug that completely erases all memories of her Monday awakening. Beauty then sleeps through until the experiment ends on Wednesday. 4. If the coin lands Tails: Beauty is awakened and interviewed on Monday morning, put back to sleep with the amnesic drug, and then awakened and interviewed a second time on Tuesday morning, before sleeping through until Wednesday. 5. In every awakening interview, Beauty is asked a single, precise question: "What is your rational degree of belief (credence) right now that the coin landed Heads, $P(H)$?"

C. Crucially, whenever Beauty awakens in her windowless laboratory room, she possesses zero external clues regarding whether it is Monday or Tuesday, and no memory of whether she has been awakened before. The central philosophical question is: what number should a rational agent assign to $P(H)$ upon awakening?

D. The debate among analytical philosophers and decision theorists has crystallized into two diametrically opposed, irreconcilable theoretical camps: the "Halfers" and the "Thirders."

E. The "Halfers" (defended by philosophers such as David Lewis, Peter van Inwagen, and Richard Swinburne) argue that upon awakening, Beauty’s credence in Heads should remain strictly one-half: $$P(H) = \frac{1}{2}$$ Lewis’s argument rests on the Principle of Conditionalization. On Sunday evening, Beauty knew with absolute mathematical certainty that the coin was fair ($P(H) = 1/2$). When Beauty awakens on Monday morning, she learns no new uncentered, objective factual information about the world: she already knew with certainty on Sunday that she would be awakened at least once during the experiment, regardless of whether the coin landed Heads or Tails. Because Beauty has received zero new relevant empirical information that distinguishes Heads from Tails, standard Bayesian rationality dictates that her prior probability of $1/2$ must remain unchanged.

F. The "Thirders" (defended by Adam Elga, Nick Bostrom, Frank Arntzenius, and the majority of contemporary formal epistemologists) argue that upon awakening, Beauty’s rational credence in Heads must drop to one-third: $$P(H) = \frac{1}{3}$$ Elga’s thirder argument analyzes the three distinct, mutually exclusive subjective awakening states that Beauty could be experiencing: $H_1$: The coin landed Heads, and it is Monday. $T_1$: The coin landed Tails, and it is Monday. $T_2$: The coin landed Tails, and it is Tuesday. Because Beauty’s subjective conscious state is completely indistinguishable across all three awakenings, Elga argues via the Principle of Indifference that given Tails, Beauty has no reason to think it is Monday rather than Tuesday: $P(T_1 \mid T) = P(T_2 \mid T)$, meaning $P(T_1) = P(T_2)$. Furthermore, if Beauty were informed upon awakening that it is Monday, the symmetry between Heads and Tails ensures $P(H_1) = P(T_1)$. Consequently: $$P(H_1) = P(T_1) = P(T_2) = \frac{1}{3}$$ Because $H$ corresponds exclusively to $H_1$, her overall credence in Heads upon awakening is $1/3$.

G. Thirders powerfully reinforce their position using the "Betting Argument" (Dutch Book and long-run frequency arguments). Imagine the experiment is repeated across 1,000 independent weeks, and in every single awakening interview, Beauty is offered a fair bet with even odds that the coin landed Heads. If Beauty adopts the Halfer strategy ($P(H) = 1/2$), she will accept even bets and suffer catastrophic, guaranteed financial ruin over the long run, winning 500 bets (on Monday of Heads weeks) but losing 1,000 bets (on Monday and Tuesday of Tails weeks). Only by assigning $P(H) = 1/3$ will her betting behavior align with the true statistical frequency of her awakenings.

H. Beyond an isolated puzzle of amnesic awakenings, the Sleeping Beauty problem carries profound, existential implications for modern cosmological physics and anthropic reasoning: The Many-Worlds Interpretation of Quantum Mechanics: In quantum cosmology, branching universe models predict that quantum measurements create multiple duplicate copies of observers across branching parallel worlds, mirroring the duplicated awakenings in the Tails scenario. The Doomsday Argument: Formulated by astrophysicist Brandon Carter and philosopher John Leslie, the Doomsday Argument uses anthropic self-locating probability to predict that humanity will face premature extinction, arguing that our current temporal position among all humans who will ever live suggests that the total human population will not expand to astronomical cosmic numbers. Whether a theorist adopts Halfer or Thirder principles directly determines how they evaluate the probability of parallel quantum multiverses and human cosmic extinction. [I] In synthesizing modern formal epistemology, the Sleeping Beauty problem reveals that our classical theories of probability were designed exclusively for impersonal, "uncentered" propositions. By demonstrating that conscious observers must continuously navigate "centered," self-locating beliefs regarding their identity and temporal position, Sleeping Beauty forces philosophy to rewrite the fundamental rules of Bayesian rationality, illuminating the intricate connection between conscious subjective experience, probability, and our place in the cosmos.'
);

