-- Passage 3: Perception and Phenomenal Content: Representationalism vs. Naive Realism
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '39c8521a-6fb6-5f1e-956f-b487b5fc0130', '3834510b-8a49-57e5-8241-7f0083e83792', 3, 'Perception and Phenomenal Content: Representationalism vs. Naive Realism',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In the philosophy of mind, cognitive epistemology, and sensory perception theory, the central structural inquiry concerns the fundamental nature of conscious perceptual experience: when an individual opens their eyes and looks at a ripe red apple resting on a table, what is the metaphysical nature of their conscious perceptual state? For centuries, classical philosophy approached perception through the lens of indirect realism or sense-datum theory (formulated by John Locke, Bertrand Russell, and G.E. Moore), claiming that humans never perceive mind- independent physical objects directly; rather, we perceive private, subjective mental images (sense-data) that represent the external physical world. In contemporary analytical philosophy, this classical debate has evolved into a fierce, sophisticated battle between two foundational paradigms: Representationalism (Intentionalism) and Naive Realism (Relationalism).

B. Representationalism—championed by prominent philosophers of mind including Fred Dretske, Michael Tye, and Alex Byrne—is currently the dominant orthodox view in cognitive science and philosophy. Representationalists argue that conscious perceptual experiences are fundamentally representational mental states. When you perceive a red apple, your visual perceptual system constructs an internal mental representation whose semantic content specifies that there is a spherical, red, solid physical object located at a specific spatial coordinate in front of you.

C. The core metaphysical claim of strong Representationalism is the "Identity Thesis" (or Intentionalism): the subjective, qualitative character of a conscious perceptual experience (its phenomenology, or "what it is like" for the perceiver) is completely identical to, or supervenes entirely upon, its representational content. There can be no difference in phenomenal experience without a corresponding difference in representational content. Representationalists explain illusions and hallucinations through representational accuracy conditions: Veridical Perception: Occurs when the external physical environment matches the representational content constructed by the brain (the apple really is red, spherical, and present). Illusion: Occurs when an object is present, but the brain misrepresents its objective physical properties (e.g., misrepresenting a white wall as yellow under sodium streetlights). Hallucination: Occurs when the brain generates a representational content without any physical object being present in the external world.

D. In stark opposition to representationalism stands Naive Realism (championed by philosophers such as John McDowell, M.G.F. Martin, and Bill Brewer). Naive realists reject the claim that perception is a representational or computational intermediary constructed by the brain. Instead, Naive Realism asserts that veridical sensory perception is an unmediated, direct relation of conscious acquaintance between the perceiving subject and the mind-independent physical object itself.

E. In the naive realist framework, the physical objects and their real properties (the actual skin of the apple, its physical reflectance, its spatial boundaries) are literal, constitutive physical components of the conscious experience itself. If you remove the physical apple from the room, you do not simply alter the accuracy of an internal mental representation; you destroy the constitutive relationship that defines the perceptual state. Naive realists argue that representationalism commits a form of cognitive alienation, placing an internal representational "veil of ideas" between the human mind and the physical universe.

F. The primary philosophical battlefield where these two paradigms collide is the "Argument from Hallucination." Consider a seamless, subjectively indistinguishable total hallucination: an individual suffering from a neurochemical hallucination experiences a vivid sensory event that is subjectively indistinguishable from seeing a real red apple on a table. Representationalists argue that because the conscious experience feels identical in both cases, both the veridical perception and the hallucination must share the exact same mental nature—namely, the exact same internal representational content (the Common Kind Assumption).

G. To defend Naive Realism against the Argument from Hallucination, naive realists formulate Disjunctivism. Disjunctivism (pioneered by J.M. Hinton and M.G.F. Martin) rejects the Common Kind Assumption. Disjunctivists assert that a veridical perception and a hallucination do not share a common mental nature. Rather, the perceptual state must be analyzed as an exclusive disjunction: $$\text{Perceptual Experience} = \text{Either } (\text{Direct Acquaintance with an External Object}) \text{ OR } (\text{A Mere Hallucination})$$ In a veridical perception, the mental state is an authentic, direct relation to the physical object. In a hallucination, the mental state is a distinct, non-relational cognitive event that merely possesses the negative epistemic property of being indistinguishable from a veridical perception to the subject. Disjunctivists insist that the mere fact that two mental states cannot be distinguished from the inside by introspection does not prove that they are metaphysically identical, just as a real diamond and an identical-looking fake cubic zirconia do not share the same physical nature.

H. In synthesizing the modern debate, the contest between Representationalism and Naive Realism touches the core of human epistemology and scientific ontology. While representationalism aligns neatly with computational cognitive neuroscience and brain-imaging models of sensory processing, naive realism preserves our foundational common-sense intuition that we inhabit a shared, unmediated physical cosmos. By forcing philosophy to investigate whether the mind is a painter of internal representations or an open window onto the world, the metaphysics of perception illuminates the profound, living bridge between conscious subjectivity and physical reality.'
);

