-- Passage 1: The History and Metallurgy of Traditional Indonesian Kris Daggers
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'c611ec9e-4745-57b2-9ade-7ce46c5d5d44', 'f755a05d-0720-5bed-8d88-53e731c00a44', 1, 'The History and Metallurgy of Traditional Indonesian Kris Daggers',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the maritime expanse of the Indonesian archipelago and the broader Malay world— spanning Java, Bali, Sumatra, Madura, the southern Philippines, and the Malay Peninsula—few cultural and technological artifacts possess as revered a status as the kris (or keris). More than a functional thrusting dagger, the kris is an extraordinary synthesis of complex pyro-metallurgy, sculptural artistry, ancestral talismanic spirituality, and social prestige. Originating in tenth- century central and eastern Java during the transition between the classical Mataram and Majapahit empires, the kris evolved into a universal symbol of personal honor, spiritual protection, and aristocratic heritage. In 2005, UNESCO recognized the extraordinary significance of this living tradition by proclaiming the Indonesian Kris a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity.

B. The defining visual and structural hallmark of an authentic kris is its asymmetrical blade (bilah), which widens dramatically near the hilt into a broad, integral handguard collar known as the ganja. Below the hilt, the blade can be forged in one of two fundamental physical profiles (dapur): 1. Kris Lurus (Straight Blade): A straight, razor-sharp tapering blade symbolizing deep spiritual contemplation, emotional tranquility, and unwavering moral focus. 2. Kris Berkelok (Wavy Blade): An undulating, serpentine blade featuring an odd number of rhythmic curves or waves (luk), typically numbering between three and thirteen. In classical Javanese and Balinese cosmology, the undulating waves represent the primordial serpent deity (Naga), symbolizing vital cosmic energy, aquatic fertility, and spiritual defense.

C. In traditional metallurgical science, the manufacturing of a kris blade represents an advanced form of pattern-welded crucible metallurgy. Traditional master bladesmiths, known in Javanese as empu, were revered not merely as skilled blacksmiths, but as high-caste spiritual adepts who performed fasting, meditation, and sacred offerings (sesajen) before lighting the forge. The metallurgical core of the kris is constructed by forge-welding alternating layers of two distinct ferrous materials: Base Carbon Iron: Low-to-medium-carbon bloomery iron or sponge iron, providing high fracture toughness and core structural ductility. Contrasting High-Nickel Metal: Sourced historically from nickel-bearing iron ores or rare, nickel-rich iron meteorites.

D. The pre-eminent historical source of astronomical metal in Javanese swordsmithing was the legendary Prambanan meteorite, which fell on the central Javanese plains near Surakarta in 1749. Possessing an exceptionally high nickel concentration (roughly four to nine percent nickel by weight), fragments of this celestial meteorite were distributed exclusively to royal court empus by the Susuhunan of Surakarta. The incorporation of celestial meteoric iron (pamor meteor) was believed to endow the finished blade with potent spiritual energy, providing invulnerability and supreme authority to royal rulers.

E. The physical fabrication of the patterned steel (pamor) required extraordinary patience and thermomechanical forging skill. The empu stacked alternating sheets of iron and nickel-iron ore into a compact billet, bound it with wire, and heated it in a charcoal forge blown by double- cylinder bamboo piston bellows (ububan). Heated to yellow-hot welding temperatures (1,100°C to 1,200°C), the billet was forge-welded using heavy hammers. The smith repeatedly folded, cut, twisted, fullered, and re-welded the billet—often executing sixteen to thirty-two folding cycles to create hundreds or thousands of microscopic, alternating layers. The empu intentionally manipulated the metal through surface gouging, twisting (pamor puntir), or filing to reveal specific, pre-determined sacred motifs (pamor beras wutah resembling scattered rice grains, or pamor blarak ngirid resembling woven coconut leaves).

F. The final, transformative phase of kris manufacture was chemical etching and patination (warangi or marangi). After the forged blade was shaped, ground, and polished with abrasive stones, the bare metal appeared as an undifferentiated, uniform metallic gray. To bring out the hidden layered pattern, the empu bathed the degreased blade in an acidic chemical bath formulated from fermented lime juice (Citrus aurantiifolia) mixed with pulverized arsenic trioxide mineral powder (warangan).

G. Inside the acidic bath, a selective electrochemical oxidation reaction took place. The acidic citric acid dissolved and oxidized the iron atoms on the surface, turning the carbon-iron layers into a dark, velvety charcoal-black tone. In contrast, the nickel atoms present in the meteoric layers resisted acid attack, remaining completely bright, lustrous, and silvery. The result was a dramatic, contrasting surface topography of shimmering silver ribbons woven through a dark metallic matrix. Finally, the etched blade was consecrated with scented floral oils (such as jasmine and sandalwood oils) to protect the metal from tropical humidity and activate its spiritual guardianship. Today, contemporary master empus in Yogyakarta and Surakarta continue to forge patterned kris daggers, preserving a millennia-old metallurgical art that binds ancient science to living Indonesian heritage.'
);

