-- Passage 2: Silicon Heterojunction (SHJ) Interdigitated Back-Contact (IBC) Solar Cells
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'c7d13e7b-ffcb-5240-a320-bcee0edf0038', 'f755a05d-0720-5bed-8d88-53e731c00a44', 2, 'Silicon Heterojunction (SHJ) Interdigitated Back-Contact (IBC) Solar Cells',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the global transition toward decarbonized power generation, commercial photovoltaic (PV) solar technologies have been dominated by crystalline silicon ($c\text{-Si}$) solar cells, capturing more than ninety-five percent of the global solar panel market. For over two decades, standard front-contacted Passivated Emitter and Rear Cell (PERC) and Tunnel Oxide Passivated Contact (TOPCon) architectures have driven steady gains in power conversion efficiency. However, conventional front-contacted silicon solar cells are rapidly approaching their practical efficiency limits. Standard solar cells possess opaque metallic grid lines (silver busbars and fingers) screen-printed directly across their front light-receiving surface, which physically blocks four to six percent of incoming solar illumination (optical shading losses). Furthermore, direct physical contact between metallic contacts and the silicon wafer induces high carrier recombination, limiting open-circuit voltage ($V_{\text{oc}}$).

B. To eliminate front-surface shading losses while drastically slashing electronic recombination, photovoltaic semiconductor engineers are developing Silicon Heterojunction Interdigitated Back- Contact (SHJ-IBC or HBC) solar cells. By combining the supreme surface passivation of amorphous silicon heterojunctions with the complete elimination of front metal grids via interdigitated back-contact patterning, HBC solar cells represent the absolute pinnacle of single- junction silicon photovoltaic performance, achieving laboratory power conversion efficiencies exceeding 27.0 to 27.5 percent—very close to the theoretical Auger efficiency ceiling of crystalline silicon (29.4 percent).

C. The core physical design that distinguishes an interdigitated back-contact cell from conventional solar cells is the complete relocation of all positive and negative electrical contacts to the rear, un-illuminated side of the silicon wafer: 1. The Front Illuminated Surface: The front surface consists of a textured crystalline silicon wafer (featuring chemical potassium-hydroxide-etched micro-pyramids that trap incoming light) covered by an ultra-thin, highly transparent surface passivation layer and an anti-reflective coating (ARC, such as silicon nitride, $\text{SiN}_x$, or transparent conductive oxides). Because no metal grids or wires exist on the front, one hundred percent of the front aperture area collects sunlight without any optical shading. 2. The Interdigitated Rear Surface: On the rear side, alternating, interdigitated finger-like stripes of p-type (hole-collecting) and n-type (electron-collecting) semiconductor regions are patterned in close parallel alignment, contacted by corresponding interdigitated positive and negative metallic electrodes.

D. The integration of Silicon Heterojunction (SHJ) technology into the back-contact architecture provides unprecedented electronic surface passivation. In standard diffused silicon p-n junctions, heavy chemical doping creates high densities of crystalline defect states and Auger recombination centers. In an SHJ architecture, by contrast, the crystalline silicon wafer ($c\text{- Si}$) is coated with an ultra-thin layer of intrinsic (undoped) hydrogenated amorphous silicon ($i\text{-a-Si:H}$, measuring a mere three to five nanometers in thickness). The hydrogen atoms passivate dangling silicon bonds on the wafer surface, reducing the surface defect state density to record lows and enabling minority carrier lifetimes exceeding ten milliseconds.

E. Atop this intrinsic passivating layer on the rear side, alternating stripes of heavily doped p- type amorphous silicon ($p\text{-a-Si:H}$) and n-type amorphous silicon ($n\text{-a-Si:H}$) are deposited to form selective carrier-collecting heterojunctions: Electron Collection: The $n\text{-a-Si:H}$ regions create an energy band alignment that permits free electrons to cross easily while blocking holes. Hole Collection: The $p\text{-a-Si:H}$ regions create a valence band alignment that extracts holes while repelling electrons. This extraordinary level of surface passivation enables SHJ-IBC cells to achieve exceptionally high open-circuit voltages ($V_{\text{oc}}$) exceeding 750 to 760 millivolts ($\text{mV}$), compared to 680 to 710 $\text{mV}$ in conventional industrial PERC cells.

F. However, the physical separation of the front light-absorbing surface from the rear carrier- collecting junctions introduces a critical transport requirement: bulk carrier diffusion length. In an HBC cell, photogenerated charge carriers created near the front surface must travel through the entire thickness of the silicon wafer (typically 120 to 160 micrometers) to reach the collecting junctions on the rear. If the crystalline silicon wafer contains internal metallic impurities or structural crystal defects, carriers will recombine before reaching the back contacts, causing severe loss of short-circuit current density ($J_{\text{sc}}$). Therefore, HBC cells require ultra-high-purity n-type float-zone (FZ) or magnetic Czochralski (MCz) single-crystal silicon wafers with carrier diffusion lengths several times greater than the wafer thickness.

G. Despite their unmatched electrical conversion efficiencies, the widespread commercial mass production of SHJ-IBC solar cells has historically been constrained by complex, costly fabrication processes. Patterning microscopic, interdigitated alternating p- and n-type stripes on the rear surface has traditionally required multi-step photolithography, precision laser ablation, or multiple shadow-mask plasma-enhanced chemical vapor deposition (PECVD) cycles. Furthermore, copper or silver metallization patterning requires strict alignment tolerances to prevent electrical short-circuits between adjacent positive and negative fingers. However, recent advances in direct high-speed laser patterning, screen-printable polymer resist masks, and electroplated copper metallization are drastically slashing manufacturing costs, positioning SHJ- IBC technology as the definitive high-efficiency solar engine for residential rooftops, vehicle- integrated photovoltaics (VIPV), and premium clean energy power grids.'
);

