-- Passage 1: The Construction and Pyrotechnology of Traditional Japanese Tatara Iron Smelting
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '8e422c15-9726-5a3b-aa22-a37010b1811e', 'd5d780b1-1d3e-5d82-ad1b-2d20a42325b2', 1, 'The Construction and Pyrotechnology of Traditional Japanese Tatara Iron Smelting',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Across the mountainous terrain of western Japan—most notably within the Chūgoku region of Shimane and Hiroshima prefectures—the production of high-purity iron and high-carbon steel (tamahagane) has followed an ancient, non-continuous pyrotechnological tradition known as the tatara method. Developed during the late Kofun and Asuka periods and perfected over the Edo period (1603–1867), the tatara furnace represents a unique metallurgical system. Unlike Western continuous-blast furnaces that rely on liquid-phase slag-metal separation and high-carbon cast iron, the traditional Japanese tatara operated as a direct reduction, batch-smelting bloomery. Over a single continuous three-day operating campaign, this clay furnace converted iron-rich alluvial black sand into a massive, semi-solid spongy metallic bloom (kera), yielding the coveted tamahagane ("jewel steel") essential for forging Japanese samurai katana swords.

B. The geological foundation of traditional Japanese ironmaking is satetsu (iron-bearing black sand), weathered from volcanic granitic mountains. Ancient prospectors divided satetsu into two primary mineralogical classes: 1. Masa-satetsu (True Black Sand): Weathered from acidic granites, dominated by titanomagnetite with exceptionally low concentrations of deleterious chemical impurities (less than 0.02% phosphorus and sulfur, and low titanium dioxide, $\text{TiO}_2$). Masa-satetsu was the mandatory raw material for producing premium tamahagane blade steel. 2. Akome-satetsu: Derived from basic diorites and volcanic basalt, containing higher titanium concentrations, used primarily to smelt ductile wrought iron (hōchōtetsu) for agrarian tools. To harvest and concentrate millions of metric tons of low-density iron sand without mechanical excavators, ancient miners developed the hydraulic kanna-nagashi (sand- washing) method. Entire hillsides of weathered granite were excavated and washed down sloping hillside flumes; the dense iron sand settled into stepped settling canals, while the lighter silica gravel washed away into rivers.

C. The physical architecture of a tatara furnace was an intricate, temporary clay structure designed to operate for a single three-day cycle before being demolished. Built inside a protective timber pavilion (takadono), the furnace rested upon a monumental subterranean moisture-barrier foundation (hondoko) engineered to eliminate underground humidity. Excavated several meters deep into the earth, the foundation comprised layered beds of dry charcoal, impermeable clay, and subterranean ventilation flumes (kobune). Because any subterranean steam rising into the furnace would absorb thermal energy, crack the clay walls, and re-oxidize metallic iron, this multi-layered dry hearth maintained bone-dry thermal conditions.

D. Atop this subterranean foundation, the master smelter (murage) built the open-topped clay furnace trough (tatara). Measuring roughly three meters in length, one meter in width, and 1.2 to 1.5 meters in height, the furnace walls were molded from refractory red clay mixed with river sand. The sidewalls tapered inward toward the base, pierced along both longitudinal sides by two rows of twenty to forty ceramic tuyeres (hondoko-buki) connected to double-acting wooden foot- powered rocking bellows (tenbin-fuigo).

E. A standard tatara smelting campaign, known as ichidai (one generation), proceeded continuously without interruption for three days and three nights (roughly seventy to seventy-two hours): Ignition & Preheating: The furnace was packed with dry pine charcoal (sumi) and heated slowly for twenty-four hours to dry and vitrify the inner clay walls. Continuous Feeding: The murage and his skilled team began feeding precise, alternating charges of masa-satetsu iron sand and crushed pine charcoal into the open furnace top every ten to fifteen minutes. Over the three-day campaign, the furnace consumed approximately ten to twelve metric tons of iron sand and twelve to thirteen tons of high- carbon charcoal. Air Blast Management: Teams of treaders (banko) operated the rocking tenbin-fuigo bellows continuously, maintaining a precise air blast velocity that held furnace core temperatures between 1,200°C and 1,400°C.

F. Inside the furnace, the solid iron sand particles trickled down through the descending column of incandescent charcoal. The high-temperature combustion of charcoal generated carbon monoxide ($\text{CO}$), which chemically reduced iron oxide particles into pure solid iron: $$\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4 + 4\text{CO} \to 3\text{Fe} + 4\text{CO}_2 \uparrow$$ Crucially, the operating temperature remained below the full melting point of pure iron (1,538°C). The reduced iron particles sintered together in the semi-solid state at the base of the furnace, absorbing carbon through solid-state carburization to form a massive, multi-ton consolidated metallic bloom (kera) weighing two to three metric tons. Excess molten fayalite slag ($\text{Fe}_2\text{SiO}_4$) was periodically tapped out through bottom drainage holes (noro-ana).

G. On the fourth morning, the temporary clay furnace walls were broken apart, and the glowing red-hot kera bloom was hauled out using iron levers and pulleys. After quenching in water, the bloom was shattered with heavy drop-hammers into fist-sized chunks and sorted visually by the murage. The core of the bloom yielded roughly one ton of prime tamahagane—high-carbon hypereutectoid steel containing 1.0 to 1.5 percent carbon, virtually free of phosphorus and sulfur—distinguished by its brilliant silvery, crystalline fracture surface. Today, the Society for the Preservation of Japanese Art Swords (NBTHK) operates an authentic historic tatara in Okuizumo, Shimane Prefecture, firing the ancient clay hearth annually to supply raw tamahagane to master swordsmiths, preserving a three-thousand-year-old metallurgical lineage.'
);

