-- Passage 2: Zinc-Air Flow Batteries for Multi-Day Grid Energy Storage
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '22910b67-8458-5aaf-98ff-ab574adf472e', 'd5d780b1-1d3e-5d82-ad1b-2d20a42325b2', 2, 'Zinc-Air Flow Batteries for Multi-Day Grid Energy Storage',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. As global utility power grids integrate high penetrations of intermittent renewable generation, electrical systems confront an acute operational challenge: multi-day energy storage. While lithium-ion batteries dominate short-duration (two to four-hour) grid balancing, their high capital cost per kilowatt-hour ($\text{CapEx/kWh}$) makes them economically unviable for long-duration energy storage (LDES, spanning ten to one hundred hours of continuous discharge). Furthermore, long-duration grid facilities require non-flammable, earth-abundant chemistries that can be scaled independently in power rating and energy capacity. To meet the demands of multi-day grid resilience, applied electrochemists and clean-energy engineers are advancing aqueous Zinc-Air Flow Batteries (ZAFBs).

B. A Zinc-Air Flow Battery is an open-system hybrid electrochemical energy storage device that pairs an alkaline zinc metal negative electrode (anode) with an open-air positive gas- diffusion electrode (cathode). Zinc ($\text{Zn}$) is an ideal electrochemical fuel: it possesses a high theoretical specific energy density of 1,086 Watt-hours per kilogram ($\text{Wh/kg}$), a high volumetric energy capacity ($5,855\text{ Ah/L}$, roughly five times higher than metallic lithium), low toxicity, and natural abundance in global mineral deposits.

C. The basic electrochemical architecture of a Zinc-Air Flow Battery consists of a flowing alkaline electrolyte (typically 6 to 9 molar potassium hydroxide, $\text{KOH}$, enriched with zincate ions, $[\text{Zn(OH)}_4]^{2-}$) circulated between separate storage tanks and a power reactor stack: 1. Discharge Reactions: o At the Zinc Anode: Metallic zinc is electro-oxidized into soluble zincate ions, releasing electrons: $$\text{Zn} + 4\text{OH}^- \to [\text{Zn(OH)}_4]^{2-} + 2e^- \quad (E^\circ = - 1.25\text{ V vs. SHE})$$ When zincate reaches its chemical saturation limit in the electrolyte, it decomposes into solid zinc oxide ($\text{ZnO}$) precipitate: $$[\text{Zn(OH)}_4]^{2-} \to \text{ZnO} \downarrow + \text{H}_2\text{O} + 2\text{OH}^-$$ o At the Air Cathode: Oxygen ($\text{O}_2$) absorbed from ambient air is reduced to hydroxide ions via the Oxygen Reduction Reaction (ORR): $$\text{O}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} + 4e^- \to 4\text{OH}^- \quad (E^\circ = +0.40\text{ V vs. SHE})$$ Combining these half-reactions yields a standard theoretical cell voltage of 1.65 Volts.

D. The defining architectural advantage of a flow configuration over static closed-cell zinc batteries is the decoupling of power and energy. In a zinc-air flow battery, the power output (in megawatts, MW) is determined strictly by the physical surface area of the reactor cell stack, whereas the total energy storage capacity (in megawatt-hours, MWh) is determined independently by the volume of zinc slurry or zinc oxide pellets stored in external plastic electrolyte tanks. To double the storage duration from ten hours to twenty hours, a utility operator simply adds larger storage tanks and more low-cost zinc powder, without needing to construct expensive additional reactor stacks.

E. Despite these compelling structural and economic virtues, commercializing rechargeable zinc-air systems has historically been constrained by severe electrochemical degradation at both electrodes during the reverse (charging) cycle: Anodic Zinc Dendrite Formation & Shape Change: During recharging, zincate ions in the flowing electrolyte are electro-reduced back into metallic zinc at the anode. At high current densities, non-uniform electrical field gradients cause localized electrodeposition, growing needle-like metallic zinc dendrites that pierce the separator membrane and short- circuit the cell. Furthermore, non-uniform zinc dissolution causes structural "shape change," stripping active material from current collectors. Cathodic Catalyst Degradation: During the charging Oxygen Evolution Reaction (OER), the air electrode is subjected to high positive anodic potentials, causing severe corrosion of carbon catalyst supports and irreversible oxidation of transition metal catalysts. Electrolyte Carbonation: Ambient air contains carbon dioxide ($\text{CO}_2$). When $\text{CO}_2$ enters the open-air cathode, it reacts with the alkaline potassium hydroxide electrolyte: $$\text{CO}_2 + 2\text{KOH} \to \text{K}_2\text{CO}_3 \downarrow + \text{H}_2\text{O}$$ This reaction consumes active $\text{OH}^-$ ions and precipitates solid potassium carbonate ($\text{K}_2\text{CO}_3$) crystals that clog the microscopic gas diffusion pores of the cathode, choking off airflow.

F. To resolve these degradation bottlenecks, materials engineers are deploying advanced fluidic and catalytic countermeasures: 1. Electrochemical Flow Hydrodynamics: Continuous laminar circulation of the zinc slurry electrolyte prevents localized concentration polarization, smoothing electrodeposition and completely suppressing dendrite initiation. 2. Bifunctional Non-Precious Catalysts: Air electrodes are engineered with transition-metal spinel oxides (such as $\text{Co}_3\text{O}_4$ and $\text{NiFe}$-layered double hydroxides) supported on corrosion-resistant carbon nanotubes, achieving high catalytic activity for both ORR discharge and OER charging over thousands of cycles. 3. Membrane $\text{CO}_2$ Scrubbing: Upstream gas purification units or selective hydrophobic gas-permeable membranes filter out atmospheric $\text{CO}_2$ before incoming air reaches the alkaline electrolyte.

G. In utility-scale economics, aqueous zinc-air flow batteries offer levelized capital costs below fifty to eighty dollars per kilowatt-hour ($\$50\text{--}\$80\text{/kWh}$) for twelve to twenty- four-hour durations—less than one-third the projected cost of equivalent lithium-ion installations. With non-toxic, non-flammable water-based chemistry, zinc-air flow batteries provide a safe, scalable, and earth-abundant energy storage solution, enabling clean energy grids to bridge multi-day wind and solar lulls in a fully decarbonized energy future.'
);

