-- Passage 1: The Hydraulic Engineering of Roman Watermills at Barbegal
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '1a03721b-9b42-5ff1-8e58-9808bdebd5f2', '045a36b1-44c5-537d-83b1-b332f91e8217', 1, 'The Hydraulic Engineering of Roman Watermills at Barbegal',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout the history of classical antiquity and Roman industrial archaeology, no site demonstrates the scale, organizational complexity, and mechanization of ancient mass production as dramatically as the hydraulic milling complex of Barbegal. Situated near the historic town of Fontvieille, approximately twelve kilometers northeast of the Roman provincial administrative center of Arelate (modern Arles in southern France), Barbegal was constructed during the late first to early second century CE (spanning the reigns of Trajan and Hadrian). Often designated by historians as the greatest known concentration of mechanical power in the ancient world, the Barbegal complex was not an isolated rural mill; it was a centralized, state- engineered grain-milling factory designed to convert massive bulk harvests of wheat into flour to feed the urban populace and military garrisons of Roman Gaul.

B. The geological and civil engineering brilliance of Barbegal was anchored in its utilization of steep topographic relief and municipal aqueduct infrastructure. Water feeding the mill complex was diverted from the northern branch of the Arles Aqueduct, which channeled pristine spring water from the nearby Alpilles mountains. As the aqueduct approached the crest of a steep, rocky limestone ridge known as the Vallon des Arcs, Roman hydraulic engineers excavated a deep, vertical rock-cut trench through the solid limestone ridge to deliver a high-velocity, controlled water flow to the southern downhill slope. The southern slope presented a dramatic natural escarpment dropping nineteen vertical meters over a horizontal distance of merely sixty-five meters—an exceptionally steep gradient of nearly thirty percent.

C. To harness the gravitational energy of this nineteen-meter descent, Roman engineers constructed two parallel, symmetrical cascading flumes running side-by-side down the steep hillside. Each flume powered a vertical series of eight overshot waterwheels arranged in a stepped cascade: 1. The Parallel Cascades: The sixteen total waterwheels were grouped into eight descending tiers, with two identical wheels operating in parallel at each elevation. 2. Sequential Overshot Flow: Water exiting the rock-cut intake basin was split equally between the two parallel channels. The water entered the uppermost wheel via an elevated timber flume, struck the wheel buckets from above (overshot configuration), drove the wheel through gravitational potential and kinetic force, and was discharged into a lower stone tailrace that immediately fed the next overshot waterwheel directly below. This process repeated sequentially through all eight tiers before draining into the low- lying marshlands at the base of the hill.

D. The selection of overshot vertical waterwheels—measuring roughly 2.1 to 2.2 meters in diameter and 0.7 meters in width—reflected an advanced understanding of mechanical power efficiency. While undershot waterwheels (where water flows beneath the wheel) rely solely on the kinetic velocity of flowing water and achieve modest energy conversion efficiencies of fifteen to twenty percent, overshot waterwheels utilize the full physical weight of water filling individual wooden buckets, achieving mechanical conversion efficiencies exceeding sixty to seventy percent. Each rotation of an overshot wheel was transmitted through a horizontal iron- tipped drive axle through the millhouse wall into a wooden right-angle gear transmission.

E. Inside the sixteen distinct milling chambers flanking the central staircase, the drivetrain utilized a large vertical wooden drive gear (toothed wheel) meshing with a smaller horizontal wooden lantern pinion. This right-angle gearing stepped up the rotational speed from the slow- turning waterwheel (roughly six to ten revolutions per minute) to an optimal operating speed of forty to sixty revolutions per minute at the vertical drive spindle. The spindle rotated a heavy upper millstone (runner stone) carved from high-grade porous basalt or silicified freshwater limestone (quernstone) imported from the Massif Central, grinding wheat grains against a stationary lower stone (bedstone).

F. Quantitative industrial assessments by archaeologists (such as Philippe Leveau and C.W. Passchier) show that the sixteen mills of Barbegal operated simultaneously as a high-capacity industrial assembly line. Operating for ten hours a day, each pair of mills could grind roughly 500 to 600 kilograms of flour per hour, yielding a collective daily output of four to five metric tons of refined flour. This staggering volume was sufficient to supply the daily caloric grain requirements of over twelve to fifteen thousand citizens of Arles.

G. Following the political fragmentation of the Western Roman Empire in the late third and fourth centuries CE, municipal infrastructure declined, and the Barbegal complex was gradually abandoned. Over the subsequent centuries, carbonate-rich karstic water flowing through the abandoned flumes deposited thick layers of calcium carbonate (calcareous tufa or travertine) across the masonry walls and wooden structural timbers. In recent decades, geoarchaeologists analyzing synchrotron micro-CT scans of these fossilized travertine encrustations have recovered the precise negative imprints of the vanished wooden waterwheels, drive gears, and mill flumes. These geochemical fossil records prove that Barbegal was a pinnacle of classical mass manufacturing, refuting the outdated historical assumption that ancient Mediterranean societies lacked the mechanical capability for industrial automation.'
);

