-- Passage 2: Acoustic Metamaterials for Sub-Wavelength Sound Absorption and Cloaking
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '3beb210a-ed14-5db9-a9f7-bf51661c4c6f', '045a36b1-44c5-537d-83b1-b332f91e8217', 2, 'Acoustic Metamaterials for Sub-Wavelength Sound Absorption and Cloaking',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout modern architectural acoustics, industrial noise mitigation, aerospace propulsion, and submarine defense, the control and suppression of acoustic sound waves represent a foundational engineering challenge. In classical wave physics, the manipulation of acoustic waves—whether airborne sound, structural vibration, or underwater sonar—is governed by the fundamental diffraction limit and the mass law. According to the mass law, the acoustic transmission loss through a barrier is directly proportional to its physical mass density and the frequency of the sound wave. While high-frequency sound waves (short wavelengths) are easily blocked by thin panels, absorbing or isolating low-frequency acoustic noise (below 100 to 500 Hertz, where acoustic wavelengths span several meters) has traditionally required massive, impractically thick concrete walls, heavy lead sheeting, or bulky porous fiberglass foam baffles.

B. To transcend the classical mass law and control sound at scales hundreds of times smaller than the acoustic wavelength, materials physicists and applied acousticians are developing Acoustic Metamaterials (AMMs). Acoustic metamaterials are artificially engineered composite structures whose macroscopic acoustic properties (such as effective mass density, $\rho_{\text{eff}}$, and effective bulk modulus, $K_{\text{eff}}$) are determined not by the intrinsic chemical composition of their base materials, but by the precise geometry, spatial symmetry, and resonant topology of their sub-wavelength structural unit cells.

C. In classical fluid and solid acoustics, naturally occurring materials possess strictly positive mass density ($\rho > 0$) and positive bulk modulus ($K > 0$). In acoustic metamaterials, however, locally resonant structural elements can be engineered to exhibit exotic, non-natural effective parameters near their resonant frequencies: 1. Negative Effective Mass Density ($\rho_{\text{eff}} < 0$): Achieved using membrane- type or locally resonant mass-spring unit cells (such as elastic membranes loaded with central metallic proof masses). Near the structural anti-resonance frequency, the internal mass accelerates out-of-phase with the incoming acoustic pressure wave (dynamic negative inertia), reflecting the acoustic wave completely. 2. Negative Effective Bulk Modulus ($K_{\text{eff}} < 0$): Achieved using sub-wavelength Helmholtz resonator arrays or side-branch resonant cavities. Near resonance, the fluid inside the neck compresses and expands out-of-phase with the applied pressure wave, storing acoustic energy and creating negative compressibility. 3. Double-Negative (DNG) Acoustic Metamaterials: Combining negative mass density and negative bulk modulus simultaneously ($\rho_{\text{eff}} < 0$ and $K_{\text{eff}} < 0$), producing a negative acoustic refractive index ($n_{\text{eff}} < 0$) where phase velocity and group velocity propagate in opposite directions.

D. For ultra-thin, low-frequency sound absorption, acousticians deploy "Space-Coiling" (or labyrinthine) acoustic metamaterials. In a space-coiling unit cell, acoustic sound waves entering a tiny aperture are forced to travel through an intricate, zigzagging internal labyrinthine channel. By folding an elongated acoustic path into a flat, sub-wavelength disk (measuring a few millimeters in thickness), the effective acoustic refractive index is increased dramatically. The acoustic wave is slowed down and trapped within the labyrinth, dissipating its sound energy through viscous boundary-layer friction and thermal relaxation at the channel walls. A space- coiling acoustic panel measuring only two centimeters in thickness can achieve near-perfect (ninety-nine percent) sound absorption for low-frequency sound waves whose physical wavelength exceeds two meters, slashing material thickness by more than ninety percent.

E. A transformative application of acoustic metamaterials is transformative Transformation Acoustics and Acoustic Cloaking. First formalized mathematically in 2006 by physicists John Pendry, Steven Cummer, and David Schurig, transformation acoustics applies coordinate transformations to the acoustic wave equation. Just as optical metamaterials bend light around an object, an acoustic cloak coordinates the spatial distribution of mass density and bulk modulus to guide incoming sound waves smoothly around a hidden central region, returning the waves to their original trajectories on the other side without scattering or casting an acoustic shadow.

F. In underwater maritime engineering, acoustic cloaking shells fabricated from radially layered fluid-solid metamaterials (such as concentric rings of brass and silicone rubber) manipulate underwater acoustic sonar waves. When active sonar pings strike the metamaterial cloak, the sound waves are channeled harmlessly around the submerged hull, making the submarine completely undetectable to active sonar hydrophone arrays. Furthermore, seismic metamaterials—large-scale periodic arrays of subterranean concrete-and-rubber columns excavated around nuclear power plants or urban skyscrapers—scatter and redirect destructive low-frequency seismic Rayleigh waves around the foundation during major earthquakes.

G. Despite these revolutionary capabilities, translating laboratory acoustic metamaterials to mass industrial deployment faces critical physical and manufacturing hurdles. A primary limitation is operational bandwidth: most resonant metamaterials operate within narrow frequency bands around their local resonance peaks, rendering them ineffective against wideband white noise. To overcome this limitation, engineers are designing active acoustic metamaterials integrating piezoelectric membrane actuators, micro-electro-mechanical systems (MEMS), and tunable digital feedback controllers capable of dynamically shifting resonant frequencies in real time. As automated 3D printing and roll-to-roll nano-imprint lithography continue to advance, acoustic metamaterials are poised to revolutionize architectural acoustics, silent aircraft propulsion, and defense stealth technologies.'
);

