-- Passage 1: The Ecology and Traditional Cultivation of Truffles (Tuber Species)
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    'd1448e30-b3f4-50f7-80e9-c88dd7358490', 'eace61a3-f87b-5c20-bd6e-cc89e02479c2', 1, 'The Ecology and Traditional Cultivation of Truffles (Tuber Species)',
    'Questions 1-7: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 8-13: Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Throughout European culinary history and forest ecology, few wild biological organisms command the gastronomic prestige, economic value, and ecological intrigue of the truffle. Belonging to the genus Tuber within the fungal phylum Ascomycota, truffles are subterranean, hypogeous fruiting bodies (ascomata) of mycorrhizal fungi. Unlike epigeous forest mushrooms that emerge above ground and broadcast their reproductive spores via atmospheric wind currents, truffles develop their entire life cycle completely buried five to thirty centimeters beneath the soil surface. Celebrated across French, Italian, and Spanish haute cuisine for their pungent, complex aromas and savory earthy flavors, culinary truffles—most notably the Périgord black truffle (Tuber melanosporum) and the prized Italian Piedmont white truffle (Tuber magnatum)—are among the most valuable natural agricultural commodities on Earth, commanding wholesale prices exceeding several thousand dollars per kilogram.

B. The ecological survival and subterranean fruiting of Tuber species depend entirely on an obligate mutualistic symbiosis: ectomycorrhizae. In temperate forest soils, truffle mycelia cannot synthesize carbohydrates through photosynthesis, nor can they survive purely as saprotrophic decomposers of dead organic wood. Instead, the fungal hyphae establish an intimate structural and physiological connection with the living root systems of specific deciduous and evergreen host trees, primarily oaks (such as the holm oak, Quercus ilex, and downy oak, Quercus pubescens), hazels (Corylus avellana), and hornbeams (Carpinus betulus).

C. In this ectomycorrhizal partnership, the fungal hyphae form a dense mantle (sheath) enveloping the exterior of the tree''s finest root tips, extending an intricate microscopic network of intercellular hyphae (the Hartig net) between the root cortical cells: 1. Nutrient Transfer: The vast fungal mycelial network acts as an immense root-extension system, mining the surrounding soil for scarce mineral nutrients (primarily bio-available phosphorus and nitrogen) and water, which it transports directly to the host tree. 2. Carbohydrate Exchange: In reciprocal exchange, the host tree allocates up to twenty to thirty percent of its net photosynthetic sugars (sucrose and glucose) to nourish the fungal symbiont. This symbiotic zone, termed the mycorrhizosphere, requires well-drained, alkaline calcareous soils (typically limestone-derived soils with a soil pH between 7.5 and 8.5) with high calcium carbonate content.

D. A distinctive ecological hallmark of active Tuber melanosporum colonization in Mediterranean orchards is the formation of a brûlé (literally "burnt area"). Around the base of a colonized host tree, the lush grassy undergrowth and herbaceous wild weeds begin to wither, die, and vanish, leaving a stark, circular barren ring of bare earth that resembles scorched ground. Phytochemical and soil microbiological analyses have revealed that the brûlé is created by active fungal allelopathy: the expanding Tuber mycelium synthesizes and secretes volatile organic compounds (VOCs) and phytotoxic secondary metabolites that selectively inhibit the seed germination and root growth of competing herbaceous weeds. By clearing away competing vegetation, the fungus eliminates competing plant root systems that would otherwise deplete vital soil moisture and solar thermal warming from the ground.

E. Because truffles fruit entirely underground, they cannot rely on wind dispersion to broadcast their reproductive ascospores. To ensure spore dispersal, truffles evolved an ingenious evolutionary adaptation based on mammalian zoochory and volatile chemical mimicry. As the subterranean fruiting body matures in late autumn and winter, it releases a rich, pungent bouquet of volatile aromatic compounds that permeate upward through the soil cap into the atmosphere. Gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC-MS) has identified over fifty volatile molecules contributing to this signature aroma, most notably dimethyl sulfide, 2-methylbutanal, and the steroid molecule $5\alpha$-androst-16-en-3$\alpha$-ol (androstenol)—a chemical pheromone synthesized naturally in the saliva of male pigs (boars).

F. Historically, European foragers exploited this olfactory mimicry by using female domestic pigs to hunt wild truffles: female sows, instinctively drawn to the pheromonal scent of androstenol, locate truffles buried beneath leaf litter with extraordinary accuracy. However, because pigs frequently damage delicate tree rootlets with their heavy snouts and tend to devour the valuable truffles upon excavation, modern truffle hunters (trufficulteurs in France and trifolao in Italy) have replaced pigs almost exclusively with trained truffle dogs (cani da tartufo, such as the Italian Lagotto Romagnolo). Dogs are trained to recognize the sulfurous aroma of ripe truffles, indicating the precise underground location with a gentle scratch without damaging the delicate subterranean mycorrhizae or attempting to eat the harvest.

G. In the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries, traditional wild truffle foraging in Mediterranean oak woodlands has been augmented by modern agricultural cultivation (truffières). In controlled nurseries, oak and hazel seedlings are inoculated with certified Tuber spore suspensions in sterile greenhouses to produce myco-certified saplings. When planted in prepared calcareous fields with controlled micro-irrigation systems, cultivated truffle orchards begin producing commercial yields within six to ten years. While the Italian white truffle (Tuber magnatum) remains stubbornly resistant to commercial cultivation and must still be harvested exclusively from wild riparian woodlands, cultivated Périgord black truffle orchards across southern France, Spain, Australia, and New Zealand have revitalized global production, bridging ancient forest mycology with modern sustainable agroforestry.'
);

