-- Passage 2: Photonic Integrated Circuits (PICs) in Optical Quantum Computing
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '1ddbcdae-8677-5893-95a9-45974899bc1f', 'eace61a3-f87b-5c20-bd6e-cc89e02479c2', 2, 'Photonic Integrated Circuits (PICs) in Optical Quantum Computing',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. Over the past two decades, the global race to construct scalable, fault-tolerant quantum computers has explored diverse physical hardware architectures, spanning superconducting transmon qubits, trapped atomic ions, neutral atom arrays, and semiconductor quantum dots. However, conventional solid-state and atomic quantum platforms confront formidable physical and environmental bottlenecks: superconducting circuits and spin qubits require colossal, energy-intensive dilution refrigerators operating at cryogenic temperatures near absolute zero (ten to twenty millikelvin, mK) to suppress thermal noise. Furthermore, scaling these systems to millions of interconnected physical qubits requires complex, high-loss microwave wiring interfaces that suffer from severe cross-talk. To achieve room-temperature quantum computation and universal fault-tolerant processing, quantum physicists and optoelectronic engineers are advancing Photonic Integrated Circuits (PICs).

B. Linear Optical Quantum Computing (LOQC) utilizes single photons (discrete quanta of electromagnetic radiation) as quantum information carriers (qubits). Photons possess exceptional quantum properties: they exhibit virtually zero interaction with the external environment, preserving their delicate quantum coherence states across macroscopic distances and operating robustly at ambient room temperatures without requiring cryogenic cooling. In a photonic quantum processor, quantum information is encoded within the fundamental quantum degrees of freedom of individual photons: Dual-Rail Spatial Encoding: A qubit state ($\alpha \vert{}0\rangle + \beta \vert{}1\rangle$) is encoded in the spatial superposition of a single photon occupying one of two parallel optical waveguides. Polarization Encoding: Qubits are encoded in orthogonal polarization states (horizontal, $\vert{}H\rangle$, or vertical, $\vert{}V\rangle$). Time-Bin Encoding: Qubits are encoded in the relative temporal arrival times of a photon traveling through short or long optical paths.

C. The physical platform that enables large-scale optical quantum processing is the Photonic Integrated Circuit. Similar to how electronic integrated circuits integrate millions of transistors onto a single silicon chip, a quantum PIC integrates thousands of microscopic optical components—including single-photon sources, reconfigurable beam splitters, phase modulators, optical delay lines, and single-photon detectors—onto a monolithic semiconductor or dielectric chip measuring a few square millimeters.

D. In a quantum PIC, on-chip single-photon generation is achieved through non-linear optical processes: 1. Spontaneous Four-Wave Mixing (SFWM): In silicon ($\text{Si}$) or silicon nitride ($\text{Si}_3\text{N}_4$) nanophotonic waveguides, two high-intensity pump laser photons interact with the non-linear third-order susceptibility ($\chi^{(3)}$) of the material, annihilating to generate a correlated pair of entangled "signal" and "idler" photons. 2. Spontaneous Parametric Down-Conversion (SPDC): In second-order non-linear materials ($\chi^{(2)}$), such as lithium niobate on insulator (LNOI), a single high- energy pump photon splits into two lower-energy entangled photons. By detecting the idler photon with an on-chip herald detector, the system confirms the simultaneous presence of the signal photon (heralded single-photon generation), providing a deterministic quantum light source.

E. Once generated, single-photon qubits are routed through reconfigurable universal quantum photonic mesh networks built from integrated Mach-Zehnder Interferometers (MZIs). An integrated MZI consists of two 50:50 directional waveguide couplers linked by two parallel waveguide arms. By applying localized thermal heating (thermo-optic effect) or micro-volt electrical fields (electro-optic Pockels effect in lithium niobate) to one arm, engineers modulate the optical phase shift ($\Delta \phi$). This phase control dynamically tunes the quantum interference of the single-photon wavefunctions, executing single-qubit rotations and multi-qubit quantum logic gates (such as the Controlled-NOT, CNOT gate) via quantum Hong-Ou-Mandel (HOM) interference.

F. The final stage of photonic quantum processing is quantum state measurement using Superconducting Nanowire Single-Photon Detectors (SNSPDs). An SNSPD consists of an ultra- narrow, serpentine nanowire of superconducting niobium nitride ($\text{NbN}$) or tungsten silicide ($\text{WSi}$) cooled just below its critical superconducting transition temperature ($T_c \approx 2\text{ to }4\text{ K}$). When a single photon strikes the nanowire, the absorbed energy breaks superconducting Cooper pairs, creating a localized resistive "hotspot." This hotspot diverts electrical current into an external readout circuit, generating a sharp voltage pulse that registers the single photon with over ninety-eight percent quantum detection efficiency and picosecond timing jitter.

G. Despite these revolutionary capabilities, scaling photonic quantum computing to millions of qubits faces critical optical loss and probabilistic gate bottlenecks. Because photons do not naturally interact with each other in linear optical media, two-qubit entangling gates are inherently probabilistic (relying on measurement-induced non-linearities with success probabilities typically around twenty-five percent). To overcome this limitation, quantum computer scientists utilize Fusion-Based Quantum Computing (FBQC) and Measurement-Based Quantum Computing (MBQC): generating large, highly entangled multi-photon cluster states (resource states) that enable fault-tolerant quantum error correction. As low-loss silicon nitride waveguides (losses $<0.1\text{ dB/m}$) and monolithic hybrid integration mature, Photonic Integrated Circuits are charting a definitive course toward room-temperature, utility-scale quantum supremacy.'
);

