-- Passage 3: The Normativity of Instrumental Reason and Means-End Rationality
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '607cce20-6257-5328-8d68-0e3e21b1a49b', 'eace61a3-f87b-5c20-bd6e-cc89e02479c2', 3, 'The Normativity of Instrumental Reason and Means-End Rationality',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In meta-ethics, philosophical logic, and the theory of practical rationality, the central inquiry concerns the fundamental authority of reasons for action: what makes a rational agent obligated to take the necessary means to achieve their intended goals? In everyday human deliberation, few principles seem as obvious, uncontroversial, and intuitively self-evident as Instrumental Rationality (Means-End Rationality). If an agent intends to bake a loaf of bread, and buying flour is a strictly necessary means to bake the bread, standard rationality dictates that the agent ought to buy flour. Since Aristotle’s practical syllogism and Immanuel Kant’s formulation of the "Hypothetical Imperative" ("Whoever wills the end also wills the indispensable necessary means"), instrumental rationality has served as the bedrock of rational action theory.

B. Despite its intuitive obviousness, contemporary meta-ethicists and action theorists—most prominently John Broome, Niko Kolodny, Christine Korsgaard, and R. Jay Wallace—have uncovered a deep, stubborn philosophical puzzle: what grounds the normativity of instrumental reason? What is the precise nature and logical structure of the rational requirement that binds our means to our ends?

C. In contemporary analytical philosophy, the debate over the logical structure of instrumental rationality has crystallized into a foundational contest between two opposing frameworks: Narrow-Scope Requirements and Wide-Scope Requirements.

D. The "Narrow-Scope" model formalizes the instrumental requirement as a conditional detachment rule: $$\text{If an agent intends to achieve end } E, \text{ then the agent ought to take necessary means } M.$$ $$\text{Intend}(E) \to \mathbf{O}(\text{Intend}(M))$$ In the narrow-scope view, the normative operator "ought" ($\mathbf{O}$) governs strictly the consequent: having an intention gives you a direct, detached practical reason to take the means.

E. However, philosophers like John Broome and Niko Kolodny demonstrated that the Narrow- Scope model leads to fatal philosophical paradoxes, most famously the problem of "Bootstrapping." Imagine an agent who forms an utterly evil, irrational, or foolish intention: for instance, an assassin who intends to murder an innocent neighbor, where buying poison is the necessary means. Under a narrow-scope interpretation, because the assassin intends the murder, it follows logically that the assassin ought to buy poison! Narrow-scope rationality magically "bootstraps" a genuine normative reason into existence out of sheer arbitrary intention. The mere fact that an agent adopts an evil end cannot create a normative obligation to take the vile means.

F. To eliminate the bootstrapping fallacy, John Broome formulated the "Wide-Scope" model of instrumental rationality. Under the wide-scope framework, the normative operator "ought" does not govern the means in isolation; rather, it governs the entire conditional relationship between the end and the means: $$\mathbf{O}(\text{Intend}(E) \to \text{Intend}(M))$$ In English, the wide-scope requirement states: "Rationality requires of you that you do not simultaneously [intend end $E$ and fail to intend necessary means $M$]."

G. The wide-scope model resolves the bootstrapping paradox by demonstrating that an agent facing an instrumental inconsistency has two equally legitimate, rational ways to satisfy the requirement: 1. Take the Means: Form the intention to take necessary means $M$ (e.g., buying flour). 2. Abandon the End: Give up the intention to achieve end $E$ (e.g., giving up the murder plot). The wide-scope requirement demands only internal psychological consistency between mental states, without generating ungrounded normative reasons out of thin air.

H. However, Niko Kolodny raised the celebrated "Myth of Instrumental Rationality" challenge against the wide-scope view. Kolodny argued that while wide-scope formulas capture logical consistency, they fail to explain the first-person phenomenology of practical deliberation. When you realize that buying flour is necessary to bake bread, you do not experience the requirement as a symmetrical choice between buying flour or abandoning baking; you experience a directed, forward-looking pressure to buy flour! Kolodny argued that instrumental rationality is a cognitive illusion: we have reasons to take the means only if we have independent, substantive normative reasons to pursue the end in the first place. [I] In synthesizing modern theories of practical reason, philosophers increasingly ground instrumental normativity in the constitutive nature of human agency. Theorists like Christine Korsgaard argue that instrumental rationality is not an external rule imposed from outside; rather, it is a constitutive principle of rational agency itself. To be an active, self-determining human agent—rather than a passive bundle of biological instincts—just is to organize one’s actions through means-end coherence. By exploring the foundations of instrumental reason, philosophy illuminates the profound bridge between logic and action, reminding us that to choose an end is to take responsibility for weaving that choice into the physical fabric of reality.'
);

