-- Passage 2: Dual-Ion Batteries (DIBs) for Sustainable Stationary Energy Storage
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '27196e93-7774-5fd6-aeb0-8478f1945c19', 'aa6ec67d-bf3c-51e1-aa43-9bce9714113a', 2, 'Dual-Ion Batteries (DIBs) for Sustainable Stationary Energy Storage',
    'Questions 14-19: Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings.

Questions 20-23: Which paragraph contains the following information?

Questions 24-26: Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.',
    'A. In the worldwide quest for grid-scale renewable energy storage and sustainable electric mobility, traditional lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) represent the dominant commercial standard. However, the immense global expansion of LIBs is constrained by severe resource limitations and environmental liabilities: standard LIBs rely heavily on expensive, scarce, and toxic transition metals—most notably cobalt ($\text{Co}$) and nickel ($\text{Ni}$)—whose mining is associated with intense ecological degradation and human rights crises in supply-chain hubs. Furthermore, conventional LIBs operate via a single-ion "rocking-chair" mechanism, where lithium cations ($\text{Li}^+$) shuttle back and forth between intercalation hosts, which imposes fundamental limits on cell voltage and materials cost. To construct low-cost, environmentally benign, and high-voltage energy storage systems for stationary electric grids, battery chemists and materials scientists are developing Dual-Ion Batteries (DIBs).

B. A Dual-Ion Battery is an electrochemical storage device that utilizes both the cations and the anions of the electrolyte salt simultaneously during the electrochemical charge and discharge process. Unlike rocking-chair batteries, where only cations migrate between electrodes while the electrolyte serves merely as a passive ion-conducting medium, in a DIB, the liquid electrolyte acts as an active chemical reservoir of charge carriers: 1. Charging Process: When an external electrical voltage is applied, cations (such as $\text{Li}^+$, $\text{Na}^+$, $\text{K}^+$, or organic cations) migrate to and intercalate into the negative electrode (anode), while anions (such as hexafluorophosphate, $\text{PF}_6^-$, bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide, $\text{TFSI}^-$, or perchlorate, $\text{ClO}_4^-$) migrate concurrently to and intercalate into the positive electrode (cathode). 2. Discharge Process: During discharge, both cations and anions de-intercalate simultaneously from their respective host electrodes, migrating back into the bulk electrolyte solution.

C. The pre-eminent technological advantage of Dual-Ion Batteries is the utilization of low-cost, earth-abundant carbon materials for both electrodes. In a standard Dual-Carbon Battery (DCB, the most common subclass of DIBs), both the cathode and the anode are fabricated from graphitic carbon (such as natural graphite, expanded graphite, or hard carbons derived from agricultural biomass waste). This complete elimination of scarce transition metals (like cobalt, nickel, and manganese) slashes raw material costs by up to sixty to seventy percent compared to conventional lithium nickel-manganese-cobalt (NMC) cathode materials.

D. In addition to low material costs, DIBs deliver exceptionally high working cell operating voltages. Because the electrochemical intercalation of large anions ($\text{PF}_6^-$) into the layered graphene sheets of a graphite cathode occurs at ultra-high positive redox potentials (typically 4.5 to 5.0 Volts vs. $\text{Li/Li}^+$), a dual-graphite battery operates at high average discharge voltages between 4.2 and 4.8 Volts—significantly higher than standard lithium-ion batteries (typically 3.6 to 3.8 Volts) and standard sodium-ion batteries (typically 2.8 to 3.2 Volts). This high operational voltage endows DIBs with high theoretical specific energy densities despite the relatively modest charge-storage capacities of graphite intercalation compounds.

E. However, operating an electrochemical device at voltages approaching 5.0 Volts introduces severe thermodynamic and material degradation bottlenecks: Electrolyte Oxidative Decomposition: Standard organic carbonate solvents (such as ethylene carbonate, $\text{EC}$, and dimethyl carbonate, $\text{DMC}$) undergo rapid, irreversible electrochemical oxidation at potentials above 4.3 to 4.5 Volts. This continuous oxidation consumes active electrolyte, releases flammable hydrocarbon gases, and causes severe cell swelling. Solvent Co-Intercalation & Graphite Exfoliation: Because anions are surrounded by a bulky shell of coordinating solvent molecules, solvent molecules can enter the graphite interlayer spaces alongside the anions (solvent co-intercalation). The physical expansion of these co-intercalated solvent molecules tears apart the crystalline graphene sheets, causing severe structural "exfoliation" and rapid capacity fade over a few dozen cycles. Electrolyte Depletion: Because the electrolyte salt is consumed directly during charging (acting as active mass), DIBs require high electrolyte volumes, which can reduce the cell- level gravimetric energy density if not carefully optimized.

F. To suppress solvent co-intercalation and stabilize high-voltage operation, electrochemists are developing advanced electrolyte formulations, most prominently "Water-in-Salt" (WiSE) or "Super-Concentrated" non-aqueous electrolytes (solvent-in-salt configurations). In highly concentrated electrolytes (typically containing salt concentrations exceeding 3 to 5 molar, or mole ratios of solvent to salt below 2:1), virtually all solvent molecules are bound tightly into the primary coordination shells of cations and anions, leaving zero "free" uncoordinated solvent molecules. This complete coordination widens the electrochemical stability window of the electrolyte beyond 5.0 Volts, completely suppresses solvent co-intercalation, and prevents graphite exfoliation.

G. Furthermore, researchers are deploying non-flammable ionic liquids and solid polymer electrolytes to eliminate fire hazards entirely. In stationary grid storage economics, dual-ion batteries provide compelling advantages: non-toxic, cobalt-free carbon active materials, ultra- high operating voltages, and high safety profiles. As continuous reel-to-reel graphite electrode manufacturing and high-concentration salt recycling processes continue to mature, Dual-Ion Batteries are emerging as a transformative, low-cost battery architecture for long-duration grid storage, load leveling, and the decarbonization of stationary electrical infrastructure.'
);

