-- Passage 3: The Skeptical Challenge of Evolutionary Anti-Realism about Reasons
INSERT INTO test_sections (id, test_id, position, title, instructions, passage_text)
VALUES (
    '3c2b9166-1dcf-5f1f-acb0-b62fa2955ceb', 'aa6ec67d-bf3c-51e1-aa43-9bce9714113a', 3, 'The Skeptical Challenge of Evolutionary Anti-Realism about Reasons',
    'Questions 27-31: Choose the correct letter.

Questions 32-36: Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO, or NOT GIVEN.

Questions 37-40: Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD OR PHRASE from the box for each answer.',
    'A. In contemporary meta-ethics, philosophical logic, and the theory of normativity, the debate over the objective reality of reasons represents one of the most foundational inquiries in analytical philosophy. Normative Realism is the philosophical thesis that there are objective, stance-independent normative facts and reasons: propositions asserting that an agent ought to believe a scientifically verified claim, or that an agent has reason to avoid needless suffering, are objectively true independently of what any human individual, culture, or biological species happens to desire or believe. Realists claim that human rationality is an objective discovery process: when humans deliberate about science, logic, or ethics, we are tracking real normative truths woven into the logical architecture of reality.

B. Over the past two decades, however, this realist conviction has confronted a formidable, existential challenge: Evolutionary Anti-Realism about Reasons. Pioneered by philosophers such as Sharon Street, Matthew Bedke, and Richard Joyce, evolutionary debunking arguments have expanded beyond the boundaries of moral philosophy into the foundational domain of epistemic and practical reasons. The debunkeurs advance a radical skeptical proposition: our fundamental cognitive faculties for evaluating reasons, logic, and justification were directly shaped by blind Darwinian natural selection. Our normative intuitions were not selected to track abstract metaphysical truths; rather, they were selected because they promoted the biological reproductive fitness and survival of our ancestral hominin forebears.

C. In her landmark meta-normative critique, Sharon Street presented the "Darwinian Dilemma for Normative Realism," arguing that the evolutionary origins of human cognition plunge all normative realism into insurmountable skepticism: If natural selection operated independently of stance-independent normative truths, then it would be a miraculous coincidence if human normative beliefs happened to align with true normative reality. Alternatively, if realists claim that natural selection favored human normative beliefs because they are objectively true (the tracking account), that claim is scientifically redundant. Natural selection explains our normative tendencies—such as believing that survival is good, that consistency is desirable, or that pain is bad—purely through biological fitness, without ever needing to posit the existence of independent normative facts.

D. In response, prominent normative realists—most notably David Enoch, Thomas Nagel, and Derek Parfit—attempted to rescue realism by drawing a sharp boundary between moral reasons and epistemic reasons (the "Parity Strategy"). Realists argued that while evolutionary debunking might challenge moral values, it cannot possibly undermine our epistemic reasons for belief (e.g., our reasons to believe the logical conclusions of mathematics, geometry, and empirical science). Realists asserted that if an evolutionary argument were to undermine our epistemic reasons to believe logical arguments, the evolutionary argument itself—which relies on biological logic, mathematics, and scientific evidence—would be completely self-defeating and intellectually bankrupt.

E. However, evolutionary anti-realists (most prominently Matthew Bedke and Sharon Street) demonstrated that the Parity Strategy fails because evolutionary forces shaped our epistemic intuitions just as thoroughly as our moral intuitions. Consider our foundational epistemic judgments: why do humans believe that logical consistency is good, that contradictions are false, or that empirical evidence should update belief? The evolutionary answer is straightforward: ancestors whose cognitive systems followed basic rules of spatio-temporal inference and predictive consistency survived and reproduced far more successfully than ancestors whose brains accepted contradictions (e.g., believing that a tiger is both in the cave and not in the cave). Natural selection hardwired basic cognitive heuristics into our brains because of their biological survival utility, not because our brains were perceiving non-natural, abstract normative facts.

F. To illustrate this cognitive contingency, debunkeurs argue that if evolutionary pressures had favored cognitive heuristics that promoted survival through systematic falsehoods (such as evolved adaptive fitness payoffs in evolutionary game theory, formalizing Donald Hoffman''s "Fitness-Beats-Truth" theorem), human cognition would be systematically unhitched from metaphysical truth. Because the survival utility of an epistemic cognitive mechanism is determined strictly by its reproductive fitness payoffs rather than its alignment with mind- independent metaphysical facts, claiming that our reasoning faculties track absolute cosmic truth represents an ungrounded form of biological vanity.

G. To avoid collapsing into radical, self-undermining cognitive nihilism, anti-realists advocate Normative Constructivism. Constructivists (such as Sharon Street and Christine Korsgaard) argue that normative reasons are not mysterious, non-natural objects discovered in the external universe; rather, reasons are practical constructs forged from within the internal perspective of a valuing agent. A proposition is a genuine reason for an agent if and only if it withstands reflective equilibrium and scrutiny from the perspective of all the other evaluative commitments that the agent already holds.

H. In synthesizing the modern meta-normative landscape, the challenge of evolutionary anti- realism forces philosophy to confront the living biological foundations of human reason. It reveals that our search for truth, meaning, and justification is not an infallible direct transmission from an abstract metaphysical realm, but a living, evolved human enterprise. By reconstructing rationality as an active, reflective human construct, philosophy preserves the profound validity of science and ethics, demonstrating that reason is not a cosmic given, but humanity’s most brilliant, collaborative achievement in navigating our shared place in the physical cosmos.'
);

